window.OMMQ={"meta":{"lede":"The material taught since Written Exam 1: lymphatics and the three lumbar-spine weeks. Each lecture is split into numbered sections and parts. Every part starts with notes that explain the material in plain sentences and end with a short summary, and then come that part's practice questions. The questions are every one the 2026 OMM 1 Canvas course posted for these weeks (interactive polls and cases, pre-lab and lecture quizzes, faculty practice questions, the lumbar review package) plus past-year TA reviews and practice questions from the SGA drive. Similar questions sit together in a shaded box. Tap an answer to see why it is right, why the others are wrong, and the slides and TA-review pictures that teach it.","feat":1,"fold":1,"tbl":"TA pictures","tbn":"TA-review picture"},"lectures":[{"id":"OMM-W11","t":"Lymphatics & Diaphragms"},{"id":"OMM-W12","t":"Functional Lumbar Spine"},{"id":"OMM-W13","t":"Lumbar Muscles (Psoas, QL, Piriformis), Trigger Points & Chapman's"},{"id":"OMM-W14","t":"Clinical Lumbar Spine"}],"groups":[{"l":"OMM-W11","u":0,"label":"Spleen: largest mass of lymphoid tissue","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-9.jpg","cap":"Spleen is the largest lymphoid mass, under left ribs 9–11","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 9"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-2-1","s":"What organ is the largest mass of lymphoid tissue?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Liver","C. Thymus","D. Lymph Nodes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"","why":"<b>A. Spleen.</b> The spleen is the <b>largest mass of lymphoid tissue</b>. It sits under left ribs 9–11, below the diaphragm. It removes damaged red cells, makes antibodies and clears encapsulated bacteria.<br>B. Liver: with the intestines it makes half to two-thirds of all lymph fluid, but it is not the largest lymphoid mass.<br>C. Thymus: T-cell processing site in the superior mediastinum; it shrinks in adults.<br>D. Lymph nodes: many small filters along the vessels, not one single mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":2,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":0,"label":"Lymph channels skip CNS, epidermis, endomysium","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-12.jpg","cap":"Lymph channels reach every tissue except CNS, epidermis and endomysium","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 12"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-65-1","s":"Lymph channels traverse which tissues of the body?","o":["A. CNS","B. Endomysium","C. Epidermis","D. Lungs"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lungs","a":"","why":"<b>D. Lungs.</b> Lymph channels run through <b>every tissue except three</b>: the <b>CNS (central nervous system), epidermis and endomysium</b>. Those three are drained by small interstitial conduits instead. The lungs have rich lymph channels; pulmonary lymphoid tissue filters inhaled toxins.<br>A. CNS: one of the three exceptions.<br>B. Endomysium: the sheath around each muscle fiber; an exception.<br>C. Epidermis: the outer skin layer has no lymph vessels; an exception.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":65,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":0,"label":"Lymph flow: one-way valves, unidirectional","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-14.jpg","cap":"Lymphangion valves; segments contract in sequence for one-way flow","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 14"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-8-1","s":"Which statement is correct regarding lymphatic flow?","o":["A. It is bidirectional","B. It is unidirectional","C. There are no valves involved in the lymphatic vessels","D. The different segments of lymph vessels contract at the same time"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It is unidirectional","a":"","why":"<b>B. It is unidirectional.</b> Lymph vessels have a <b>muscular wall and one-way valves</b>. Each segment (lymphangion) contracts <b>one after the other</b>, pushing lymph forward. Once lymph reaches the main vessels, the vessel itself propels it.<br>A. Bidirectional: the valves stop backflow.<br>C. No valves: lymph vessels do have one-way valves.<br>D. Segments contract at the same time: they contract in sequence, like a wave.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":8,"f":"O096"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":1,"label":"Thoracic duct starts at cisterna chyli, L1–L2","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-19.jpg","cap":"Cisterna chyli: anterior and right of L1/L2, behind the diaphragm crus","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 19"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-12-1.png","cap":"Thoracic duct passes the aortic hiatus at T12","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":424},{"src":"sl/O065-2.jpg","cap":"Duct runs from cisterna chyli through T12, crosses inlet twice","where":"2026 week 11 Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13), page 2"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-17-1","s":"The cisterna chyli is found anterior and to the right of:","o":["A. T4-T5","B. T10-T12","C. L1-L2","D. L4-L5"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1-L2","a":"","why":"<b>C. L1–L2</b> (1st and 2nd lumbar vertebrae). The <b>cisterna chyli</b> is the sac at the start of the thoracic duct. It lies <b>anterior and to the right of the L1/L2 bodies</b>, behind the crus of the diaphragm. Trunks from the abdomen, pelvis and legs drain into it.<br>A. T4–T5 (4th–5th thoracic vertebrae): about where the thoracic duct crosses from right to left in the chest.<br>B. T10–T12: T12 is where the duct passes the aortic hiatus, above the cisterna.<br>D. L4–L5: level of the aortic bifurcation, below the cisterna.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":17,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":18,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-9-1","s":"Select the true statement regarding the left lymphatic duct","o":["A. Lymph from only the left side of the body flows through the LLD","B. Lymph from the cisterna chyli flows into the LLD","C. As with the right lymphatic duct, it passes only once through the diaphragm at the thoracic inlet","D. It carries the same amount of lymph as the right lymphatic duct","E. Anatomically, it begins at the level of T4"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lymph from the cisterna chyli flows into the LLD","a":"","why":"<b>B. Lymph from the cisterna chyli flows into it.</b> The left lymphatic duct (LLD) is the <b>thoracic duct</b>, and it <b>begins as the cisterna chyli</b> at L1–L2 (1st–2nd lumbar vertebrae).<br>A. Only the left side: it also drains both legs, the pelvis and the abdomen.<br>C. Passes the inlet once: the thoracic duct crosses Sibson's fascia <b>twice</b> (up to C7, the 7th cervical vertebra, then back down); only the right duct crosses once.<br>D. Same amount as the right duct: it drains most of the body, so it carries far more.<br>E. Begins at T4 (4th thoracic vertebra): it begins at the cisterna chyli in the abdomen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":9,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":10,"f":"O098"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":1,"label":"Lymph route: capillary, vessel, trunk, node, duct, vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-17.jpg","cap":"Lymph transport sequence, terminal lymphatic to the major veins","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 17"},{"src":"sl/ta-O097-8-1.png","cap":"Thoracic duct to left, right duct to right subclavian vein","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 8","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":483},{"src":"sl/O082-42.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct reaching the venous angles","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 42"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-69-1","s":"What is the sequence of lymph transport?","o":["A. Leaky terminal lymphatic in right lower leg, lymph vessel, lymph trunk, lymph node, left (Thoracic) duct, junction of right Internal Jugular and right Subclavian veins.","B. Lymph trunk, leaky terminal lymphatic in right lower leg, lymph vessel, lymph node, left (Thoracic) duct, junction of left Internal Jugular and left Subclavian veins.","C. Leaky terminal lympathic in right lower leg, lymph vessel, lymph trunk, lymph node, right lymphatic duct, junction of right Internal Jugular and right Subclavian veins.","D. Leaky terminal lymphatic in right lower leg, lymph vessel, lymph trunk, lymph node, left (Thoracic) duct, junction of left Internal Jugular and left Subclavian veins."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Leaky terminal lymphatic in right lower leg, lymph vessel, lymph trunk, lymph node, left (Thoracic) duct, junction of left Internal Jugular and left Subclavian veins.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> The order: <b>leaky terminal lymphatic, lymph vessel (lymphangion), trunk, lymph node, duct, vein</b>. The right lower leg drains to the <b>thoracic (left) duct</b>, which empties at the junction of the <b>left internal jugular and left subclavian veins</b>.<br>A. Right jugular–subclavian junction: that is where the right duct ends; the thoracic duct ends on the left.<br>B. Starts with the trunk: lymph starts in the leaky terminal lymphatic.<br>C. Right lymphatic duct: the leg drains to the thoracic duct, not the right duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":69,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":1,"label":"Right duct vs thoracic duct drainage","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O097-8-1.png","cap":"Right duct territory (dark blue) vs thoracic duct territory (green)","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 8","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":483},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-6-1.jpg","cap":"Right duct drains only right head, arm and chest","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 6","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":216},{"src":"sl/O064-15.jpg","cap":"Heart lymph drains mainly to the right lymphatic duct","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-75-1","s":"Which of the following does not drain into the Thoracic duct?","o":["A. Lymph from the left eye","B. Lymph from the right leg","C. Lymph from the left upper lobe of the lung","D. Lymph from the myocardium"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lymph from the myocardium","a":"","why":"<b>D. Lymph from the myocardium.</b> Heart lymph drains <b>mainly to the right lymphatic duct</b>. The right duct takes the right arm, right head and neck, the <b>heart</b> and the lungs except the left upper lobe. Everything else goes to the thoracic duct.<br>A. Left eye: left side of the head, so thoracic duct.<br>B. Right leg: both legs drain to the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct.<br>C. Left upper lobe of the lung: the one lobe that drains to the thoracic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":75,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O064-17-1","s":"Which of the following structures would be least affected by obstruction of the thoracic duct?","o":["A. Right foot","B. Spleen","C. Left kidney","D. Right hand","E. Left ear"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hand","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right hand.</b> The <b>right lymphatic duct</b> drains only the <b>right arm, right side of the head and neck, right chest, heart</b> and most of the lungs. Everything else drains to the <b>thoracic duct</b>. A thoracic duct block spares the right hand.<br>A. Right foot: everything below the diaphragm, both legs included, drains via the cisterna chyli to the thoracic duct.<br>B. Spleen: abdominal organ, so thoracic duct.<br>C. Left kidney: abdominal, so thoracic duct.<br>E. Left ear: the left side of the head drains to the thoracic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":17,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":32,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":2,"f":"O091"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":6,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":4,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":7,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":8,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O065-9-1","s":"A post-doctoral researcher is studying the drainage of lymphatic fluid with radionucleotide imaging in attempts to identify the structures which drain into the left (major) duct. Results of the study will most likely reveal fluid coming from the","o":["A. myocardium","B. right eye","C. right hemicranium","D. right leg","E. right upper lobe of the lung"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"right leg","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right leg.</b> The left (major) duct is the <b>thoracic duct</b>. It drains <b>both legs</b>, the abdomen and the <b>left</b> arm, head, neck and chest. The right (minor) duct drains the right arm, right head and neck, the <b>heart</b>, and the lungs except the left upper lobe.<br>A. Myocardium: heart lymph goes mainly to the right duct.<br>B. Right eye: right side of the head, so right duct.<br>C. Right hemicranium: right half of the head, so right duct.<br>E. Right upper lobe of the lung: every lobe except the left upper lobe drains to the right duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":9,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":149,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":9,"f":"O101"}]},{"id":"O065-11-4","s":"A 40-year-old female presents with chronic chest discomfort. Structural examination reveals a severe restriction to the right thoracic inlet. Which of the following is most likely to have inadequate lymphatic homeostasis in this patient?","o":["A. cisterna chyli","B. right lower extremity","C. right lumbar lymphatic trunk","D. right upper extremity","E. small intestine and ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"right upper extremity","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right upper extremity.</b> The <b>right lymphatic duct</b> empties into the right subclavian–internal jugular junction inside the <b>right</b> thoracic inlet. A right inlet restriction backs up its territory: right arm, right head and neck, heart, most of the lungs.<br>A. Cisterna chyli: start of the thoracic duct at L1–L2 (first and second lumbar vertebrae); that duct ends on the left.<br>B. Right lower extremity: both legs drain to the thoracic duct.<br>C. Right lumbar lymphatic trunk: feeds the cisterna chyli, so thoracic duct.<br>E. Small intestine and ascending colon: gut lymph goes to the cisterna chyli, so thoracic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":171,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":11,"f":"O101"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":14,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":2,"label":"Fluid into lymph capillaries; above 0 mmHg collapses","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-21-1.png","cap":"Starling forces across the capillary wall","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 21","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":876},{"src":"sl/O082-22.jpg","cap":"Fluid entry into lymph capillaries vs pressure above 0 mmHg","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 22"},{"src":"sl/O082-21.jpg","cap":"Interstitial pressure about -6.3 mmHg; above 0 mmHg capillaries collapse","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 21"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-20-1","s":"Which of the following promotes extracellular fluid flow into the lymphatic capillaries?","o":["A. Increased plasma colloidal osmotic pressure","B. Decreased arterial capillary pressure","C. Increased interstitial colloidal osmotic pressure","D. Decreased capillary permeability","E. Systemic Hypertension"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased interstitial colloidal osmotic pressure","a":"","why":"<b>C. Increased interstitial colloidal osmotic pressure.</b> More <b>protein in the interstitium</b> holds fluid outside the blood capillaries, and that fluid enters the lymph capillaries. The entry factors: higher arterial capillary pressure, <b>lower</b> plasma colloid osmotic pressure, more interstitial protein, higher capillary permeability, muscle movement, pulsations and breathing.<br>A. Increased plasma colloidal osmotic pressure: pulls fluid back into the blood; the list has decreased.<br>B. Decreased arterial capillary pressure: less fluid filters out; the list has increased.<br>D. Decreased capillary permeability: less leaks out; the list has increased.<br>E. Systemic hypertension: an example of pushing interstitial pressure <b>above 0 mmHg</b>, which <b>collapses</b> lymph capillaries and cuts flow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":20,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":20,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":15,"f":"O097"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-73-1","s":"Which of these is a factor that increases interstitial pressure?","o":["A. Cirrhosis","B. Muscular movement","C. Fluid fluctuations from respiration","D. Increased capillary pressure"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cirrhosis","a":"","why":"<b>A. Cirrhosis.</b> Four causes push interstitial pressure <b>above 0 mmHg</b>: systemic hypertension, <b>cirrhosis</b> (the liver makes less plasma protein), hypoalbuminemia from starvation, and toxins. Above 0 mmHg the lymph capillaries <b>collapse</b> and lymph flow falls.<br>B. Muscular movement: on the other list, the normal factors that let fluid enter lymph capillaries.<br>C. Fluid fluctuations from respiration: also on that normal-entry list.<br>D. Increased capillary pressure: also on the normal-entry list; its disease form on the above-0 list is systemic hypertension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":73,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":3,"label":"Intrinsic forces: smooth muscle, interstitial pressure","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-20.jpg","cap":"Extrinsic vs intrinsic forces on lymph flow","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 20"},{"src":"sl/O064-51.jpg","cap":"Acute sympathetic stimulation speeds lymph vessel peristalsis via alpha receptors","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-71-1","s":"Which of these in an intrinsic force affecting lymphatic flow?","o":["A. Interstitial fluid pressure","B. OMT","C. Exercise","D. Skeletal muscle contraction","E. Respiration"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Interstitial fluid pressure","a":"","why":"<b>A. Interstitial fluid pressure.</b> There are only <b>two intrinsic forces</b>: <b>smooth muscle contraction</b> of the lymph vessel wall and <b>interstitial fluid pressure</b>. Everything acting from outside the vessel is extrinsic.<br>B. OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment): extrinsic.<br>C. Exercise: extrinsic.<br>D. Skeletal muscle contraction: squeezes the vessels from outside, so extrinsic.<br>E. Respiration: negative chest pressure pulls lymph from outside, so extrinsic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":71,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O096-18-1","s":"Which of the following promotes lymphatic flow via an intrinsic force?","o":["A. Taking a deep breath","B. Increasing sympathetic tone","C. Going for a run","D. Pedal Pump","E. Flexing a muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increasing sympathetic tone","a":"","why":"<b>B. Increasing sympathetic tone.</b> Sympathetic nerves make the <b>smooth muscle</b> of the lymph vessel wall contract. This peristalsis is the vessel's own active pump, an <b>intrinsic</b> force. A short burst raises flow (via alpha receptors); only <b>chronic</b> high tone slows it.<br>A. Taking a deep breath: negative intrathoracic pressure is extrinsic.<br>C. Going for a run: exercise is extrinsic.<br>D. Pedal pump: OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) is extrinsic.<br>E. Flexing a muscle: skeletal muscle squeezes from outside, so extrinsic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":18,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":88,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":3,"label":"Physical activity beats any pump technique","slides":[{"src":"sl/O064-59.jpg","cap":"Dog study: exercise raised thoracic duct flow more than lymphatic pump","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/O064-52.jpg","cap":"Physical activity causes the greatest increase in lymphatic return","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-54-1","s":"Which of the following causes the greatest increase lymphatic return?","o":["A. Dalrymple Pedal pump","B. Miller thoracic pump","C. Effleurage","D. Physical activity","E. Splenic pump"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Physical activity","a":"","why":"<b>D. Physical activity.</b> In dogs, a lymphatic pump raised thoracic duct flow from 1.57 to 4.8 mL/min, but <b>exercise raised it from 1.47 to 5.81 mL/min</b>. Physical activity gives the <b>greatest increase in lymph return</b>.<br>A. Dalrymple pedal pump: raises flow, but less than exercise.<br>B. Miller thoracic pump: raises flow, but less than exercise.<br>C. Effleurage: light local stroking; small effect.<br>E. Splenic pump: mainly an immune effect; less than exercise.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":54,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":36,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":8,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":2,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":4,"label":"Five transverse diaphragms, popliteal included","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-8-1.png","cap":"The five diaphragms and their lymphatic function","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 8","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":438},{"src":"sl/O082-24.jpg","cap":"Definitions of the abdominal, pelvic and tentorium cerebelli diaphragms","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 24"},{"src":"sl/O082-44.jpg","cap":"Tentorium cerebelli and diaphragma sellae, the uppermost diaphragm","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 44"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-12-1","s":"Which of the following is not considered a diaphragm of the body?","o":["A. Popliteal Diaphragm","B. Abdominal Diaphragm","C. Axillary Diaphragm","D. Pelvic Diaphragm","E. Cervico-thoracic inlet","F. Tentorium Cerebelli"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Axillary Diaphragm","a":"","why":"<b>C. Axillary diaphragm.</b> There is no axillary diaphragm. The five transverse diaphragms: <b>tentorium cerebelli</b> (with the diaphragma sellae), <b>thoracic inlet</b>, <b>abdominal</b> (respiratory, the largest), <b>pelvic</b> and <b>popliteal</b>.<br>A. Popliteal: the fifth diaphragm; the soleus arch can crowd its lymph vessels.<br>B. Abdominal: the largest, the main respiratory pump.<br>D. Pelvic: levator ani and coccygeus; works in step with the abdominal diaphragm.<br>E. Cervico-thoracic inlet: the terminal drain for both ducts.<br>F. Tentorium cerebelli: the uppermost diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":12,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-7-1","s":"Name the diaphragms of the human body? What is their function? (Hint: There are 5 diaphragms)","o":["A. Falx cerebri, thoracic inlet, abdominal, pelvic, popliteal; they create pressure gradients that move lymph and steady vertical fascia","B. Tentorium cerebelli, thoracic inlet, abdominal, pelvic, axillary; they create pressure gradients that move lymph and steady vertical fascia","C. Tentorium cerebelli, thoracic inlet, abdominal, pelvic, popliteal; they pump lymph forward by their own smooth muscle peristalsis","D. Tentorium cerebelli, thoracic inlet, abdominal, urogenital, popliteal; they create pressure gradients that move lymph and steady vertical fascia","E. Tentorium cerebelli, thoracic inlet, abdominal, pelvic, popliteal; they create pressure gradients that move lymph and steady vertical fascia"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Five: 1. Tentorium cerebelli (diaphragma sella); 2. Cervico-thoracic/thoracic inlet; 3. Abdominal (respiratory) diaphragm, the largest; 4. Pelvic diaphragm; 5. Popliteal. Function: transverse diaphragms generate pressure differentials and tissue movement for lymphatic flow, and stabilize vertically oriented myofascial structures.","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> The <b>five</b> transverse diaphragms: <b>tentorium cerebelli</b> (with the diaphragma sellae), <b>thoracic inlet</b>, <b>abdominal</b> (respiratory, the largest), <b>pelvic</b> and <b>popliteal</b>. They create <b>pressure differences and tissue motion that move lymph</b>, and they steady the vertically running fascia and muscles.<br>A. Falx cerebri: a vertical dural fold between the hemispheres, not a transverse diaphragm.<br>B. Axillary: not one of the five; the fifth is the popliteal.<br>C. Own smooth muscle peristalsis: that is the lymph vessel's intrinsic pump; diaphragms work through pressure gradients.<br>D. Urogenital diaphragm: the perineal membrane; the fourth diaphragm is the pelvic diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":7,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O065-11-5","s":"Which of the following structures is considered to be a physiologic diaphragm?","o":["A. broad ligament of the uterus","B. greater omentum","C. hard and soft palate","D. mediastinum","E. tentorium cerebelli"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"tentorium cerebelli","a":"","why":"<b>E. Tentorium cerebelli.</b> There are <b>five</b> transverse diaphragms: <b>tentorium cerebelli</b> (with the diaphragma sellae), thoracic inlet, abdominal (respiratory) diaphragm, pelvic diaphragm and <b>popliteal</b>.<br>A. Broad ligament of the uterus: a peritoneal fold, not a diaphragm.<br>B. Greater omentum: a peritoneal apron hanging from the stomach.<br>C. Hard and soft palate: the roof of the mouth; not on the list.<br>D. Mediastinum: a vertical space between the lungs, not a transverse partition.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":173,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":11,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":4,"label":"Pelvic diaphragm: levator ani plus coccygeus","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-45.jpg","cap":"Pelvic diaphragm anatomy: levator ani and coccygeus","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 45"},{"src":"sl/O082-24.jpg","cap":"Definition: pelvic diaphragm is the paired levator ani and coccygeus","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 24"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-10-1","s":"The pelvic diaphragm is made up of the…","o":["A. Pubococcygeus and Iliococcygeus","B. Levator ani and the pubococcygeus","C. Levator ani and the ischiococcygeus","D. Coccygeus and levator ani"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Coccygeus and levator ani","a":"","why":"<b>C and D.</b> The pelvic diaphragm is the paired <b>levator ani and coccygeus</b> with their fascia. The coccygeus is also called the <b>ischiococcygeus</b>, so C names the same two muscles. The levator ani itself is the pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus (with puborectalis).<br>A. Pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus: together these are only the levator ani; the coccygeus is missing.<br>B. Levator ani and pubococcygeus: pubococcygeus is part of the levator ani, so the coccygeus is missing.","ex":"","fl":["C is also correct: the coccygeus is the ischiococcygeus, so C names the same two muscles as D."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":10,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":5,"label":"Diaphragm apertures: T8 IVC, T10 esophagus, T12 aorta","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-12-1.png","cap":"Diaphragm openings at T8, T10 and T12","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":424},{"src":"sl/O082-31.jpg","cap":"T8 vena cava, T10 esophagus, T12 aorta, with inspiration effects","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 31"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-26-1","s":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at which vertebral level?","o":["A. T6","B. T8","C. T10","D. T12","E. L2"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T10","a":"","why":"<b>C. T10</b> (10th thoracic vertebra). The diaphragm has three apertures: <b>T8 inferior vena cava</b>, <b>T10 esophagus</b>, <b>T12 aorta</b> (the thoracic duct passes with it). Memory aid: \"I 8 10 Eggs At 12\".<br>A. T6: no diaphragm aperture at this level.<br>B. T8: the inferior vena cava opening, in the central tendon.<br>D. T12: the aortic hiatus, which also carries the thoracic duct.<br>E. L2 (2nd lumbar vertebra): crus attachment and cisterna chyli level, not an aperture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":26,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":26,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":1,"f":"O091"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":3,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-11-1","s":"At what level does the thoracic duct pass through the diaphragm and what does it accompany?","o":["A. T10 with the esophagus","B. T12 with the IVC","C. T12 with the aorta","D. T8 - with the IVC","E. T12 with the Esophagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T12 with the aorta","a":"","why":"<b>C. T12 with the aorta.</b> The thoracic duct passes through the <b>aortic hiatus at T12</b> (12th thoracic vertebra), <b>behind the diaphragm</b> with the aorta, which protects both. The three openings: T8 inferior vena cava, T10 esophagus, T12 aorta.<br>A. T10 with the esophagus: the esophageal hiatus; no thoracic duct.<br>B. T12 with the IVC (inferior vena cava): wrong level; the vena cava passes at T8.<br>D. T8 with the IVC: a true pair, but not the duct's opening.<br>E. T12 with the esophagus: the esophagus passes at T10.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":11,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":77,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":5,"label":"Arcuate ligaments: QL lateral, psoas medial","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-12-1.png","cap":"Lateral arcuate over QL, medial arcuate over psoas","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":424},{"src":"sl/O082-36.jpg","cap":"Psoas to medial, QL to lateral arcuate ligament; both reach rib 12","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 36"},{"src":"sl/O064-30.jpg","cap":"Arcuate ligaments, crura and median arcuate ligament","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-32-1","s":"The Quadratus lumborum passes beneath which structure?","o":["A. the lateral arcuate ligament","B. the medial arcuate ligament","C. the median arcuate ligament","D. the central tendon","E. the deltoid ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"the lateral arcuate ligament","a":"","why":"<b>A. Lateral arcuate ligament.</b> The diaphragm's back attachments arch over two muscles: the <b>quadratus lumborum</b> (QL) runs under the <b>lateral</b> arcuate ligament; the <b>psoas</b> runs under the <b>medial</b> arcuate ligament. Both fasciae tie to the tip of rib 12.<br>B. Medial arcuate ligament: arches over the psoas.<br>C. Median arcuate ligament: joins the two crura over the aorta at T12 (12th thoracic vertebra).<br>D. Central tendon: top of the dome; no bony attachment and no muscle passes under it.<br>E. Deltoid ligament: medial ankle ligament; nothing to do with the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":32,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":28,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":4,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":1,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":6,"label":"Inhalation: diaphragm descends, chest pressure negative","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-34.jpg","cap":"Inspiration: diaphragm descends, chest pressure negative, abdominal pressure rises","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 34"},{"src":"sl/O082-27.jpg","cap":"The respiratory diaphragm acts as a piston","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 27"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-10-3","s":"Respiration causes lymphatic movement centrally by the creation of","o":["A. negative intrathoracic pressure during exhalation","B. negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation","C. positive intrathoracic pressure during exhalation","D. positive intrathoracic pressure during inhalation","E. thoracic duct smooth muscle spasms"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation","a":"","why":"<b>B. Negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation.</b> On inhalation the diaphragm <b>contracts and descends</b>. Chest pressure becomes more negative while abdominal pressure rises. This gradient <b>pulls lymph and venous blood toward the chest</b> and heart.<br>A. Negative pressure during exhalation: exhalation raises chest pressure; it does not pull lymph in.<br>C. Positive pressure during exhalation: higher chest pressure does not draw lymph centrally.<br>D. Positive pressure during inhalation: inhalation makes chest pressure negative, not positive.<br>E. Thoracic duct smooth muscle spasms: smooth muscle contraction is an intrinsic force, not a respiratory one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":159,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":10,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":6,"label":"Diaphragm dysfunction: curves, hypertonicity slow lymph","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-37.jpg","cap":"Lateral curves pull the diaphragm unevenly, slowing thoracic duct flow","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 37"},{"src":"sl/O082-38.jpg","cap":"Hypertonic diaphragm flattens; accessory scalenes take over breathing","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 38"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-83-1","s":"What additional physical finding might you observe on a patient with an abdominal diaphragm dysfunction?","o":["A. Use of scalene muscles to breathe","B. Hypertonic piriformis on the right","C. Hypertonic piriformis on the left","D. Flat back posture"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Use of scalene muscles to breathe","a":"","why":"<b>A. Use of scalene muscles to breathe.</b> A <b>hypertonic diaphragm flattens</b>. The chest's transverse diameter shrinks, breathing gets less efficient and the pressure gradient falls (less lymph and venous return). The patient recruits <b>accessory muscles</b>, especially the <b>scalenes</b>.<br>B. Hypertonic right piriformis: a psoas syndrome finding, not diaphragm dysfunction.<br>C. Hypertonic left piriformis: same; a psoas syndrome finding.<br>D. Flat back posture: a flattened diaphragm goes with <b>increased</b> lumbar lordosis, not a flat back.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":83,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-81-1","s":"You perform a structural exam on your patient, and you find a Type 1 SD from T7-T12 SLRR. There is a positive Red Reflex on the left from T7-T12, and you find boggy edema along the left erector spinae. What could be causing the edema?","o":["A. The lateral curve to the left is causing an asymmetric pull on the patient’s diaphragm, increasing diaphragm contraction and increasing lymphatic flow through the Thoracic duct.","B. The lateral curve to the left is causing an asymmetric pull on the patient’s diaphragm, reducing diaphragm contraction and lymphatic flow through the Thoracic duct.","C. The lateral curve to the left is causing an asymmetric pull on the patient’s diaphragm, reducing diaphragm contraction and lymphatic flow through the Right lymphatic duct.","D. The rotation to the right caused the TA to not be able to think of any other distractor choices"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The lateral curve to the left is causing an asymmetric pull on the patient’s diaphragm, reducing diaphragm contraction and lymphatic flow through the Thoracic duct.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> A <b>lateral curve</b> (here a Type 1, or neutral, group curve at T7–T12, the 7th to 12th thoracic vertebrae, sidebent left and rotated right) puts an <b>asymmetric pull on the diaphragm</b>. That <b>reduces effective contraction</b>, the pressure pump weakens, and flow through the <b>thoracic duct</b> drops. Boggy edema follows.<br>A. Increasing contraction and flow: the asymmetric pull weakens contraction.<br>C. Right lymphatic duct: the duct that passes the diaphragm (aortic hiatus, T12) is the thoracic duct.<br>D. A joke distractor; rotation alone does not explain the edema.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":81,"f":"O100"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":7,"label":"Sibson's fascia: makeup and both ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O097-12-1.png","cap":"Sibson's fascia from C7 transverse process to first rib","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":680},{"src":"sl/O082-41.jpg","cap":"Inlet borders; Sibson's fascia; thoracic duct rises to C7","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 41"},{"src":"sl/O064-37.jpg","cap":"Longus colli and Sibson's fascia at the thoracic inlet","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"O064-11-1","s":"Which fascia is associated with the thoracic inlet?","o":["A. Scarpa’s fascia","B. Buck’s fascia","C. Colle’s fascia","D. Sibson’s fascia","E. Tensor fascia lata"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sibson’s fascia","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sibson's fascia</b> (suprapleural membrane). It caps the lung apex at the thoracic inlet. It is made of <b>prevertebral fascia and scalene fascia</b>, and both lymph ducts pass through it.<br>A. Scarpa's fascia: membranous layer of the lower abdominal wall.<br>B. Buck's fascia: deep fascia of the penis.<br>C. Colles' fascia: superficial perineal fascia, continuous with Scarpa's.<br>E. Tensor fascia lata: a hip muscle that pulls on the iliotibial band.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":11,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":24,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":2,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":11,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":12,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O064-39-1","s":"Fascia from which of the following structures could directly impede lymphatic flow through the right lymphatic duct?","o":["A. Longus colli muscle","B. Right crus of abdominal diaphragm","C. Levator ani muscle","D. Pyrimidalis muscle","E. Pronator teres muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Longus colli muscle","a":"","why":"<b>A. Longus colli.</b> Sibson's fascia, the fascial roof of the thoracic inlet, is built from the <b>prevertebral fascia over the longus colli</b> and the <b>scalene fascia</b>. The right lymphatic duct passes through this fascia, so a tight longus colli or scalene fascia can block it.<br>B. Right crus of the diaphragm: at L1–L3 (lumbar vertebrae), far below the right duct.<br>C. Levator ani: part of the pelvic diaphragm.<br>D. Pyramidalis: a small lower abdominal wall muscle.<br>E. Pronator teres: a forearm muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":39,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":34,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":7,"f":"O104"}]},{"id":"O065-9-2","s":"Restrictions within Sibson's fascia could produce edema in","o":["A. both lower extremities","B. head and neck structures","C. the entire body","D. the left upper and both lower extremities","E. the right upper extremity"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"the entire body","a":"","why":"<b>C. The entire body.</b> <b>Both</b> ducts pass through Sibson's fascia at the thoracic inlet. The thoracic duct climbs through it to C7 (7th cervical vertebra) before turning down to the left veins; the right duct crosses it once. A restriction here can back up lymph from <b>everywhere</b>.<br>A. Both lower extremities: only part of the thoracic duct's territory.<br>B. Head and neck structures: only part of the picture.<br>D. Left upper and both lower extremities: thoracic duct only; ignores the right duct.<br>E. Right upper extremity: right duct only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":9,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":151,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":9,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":7,"label":"Cervical fascia: posterior on inhalation","slides":[{"src":"sl/O082-55.jpg","cap":"Cervical fascia: posterior with inhalation, anterior with exhalation","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 55"},{"src":"sl/O082-53.jpg","cap":"Anterior cervical fascia attachments; pretracheal fascia joins pericardium","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 53"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-22-1","s":"OMT to the cervical fascia facilitates lymphatic drainage of the head and neck. How does the the cervical fascia move with inhalation and exhalation?","o":["A. Superior with inhalation and inferior with exhalation","B. Anterior with inhalation and posterior with exhalation","C. Inferior with Inhalation and superior with exhalation","D. Posterior with inhalation and anterior with exhalation","E. Superior with inhalation and anterior with exhalation"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior with inhalation and anterior with exhalation","a":"","why":"<b>D. Posterior with inhalation, anterior with exhalation.</b> The cervical fascia moves <b>posterior (toward the spine) on inhalation</b> and <b>anterior on exhalation</b>. OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) to this fascia eases chest congestion and helps head and neck lymph drain.<br>A. Superior, then inferior: wrong axis; the motion is front to back.<br>B. Anterior with inhalation: reversed.<br>C. Inferior, then superior: wrong axis.<br>E. Superior, then anterior: mixes two axes; the motion is anterior–posterior only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":22,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":8,"label":"Popliteal diaphragm: soleus arch, fossa release","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-10-1.png","cap":"Popliteal fossa; soleus arch crowds the lymph capillaries","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 10","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":303},{"src":"sl/ta-O097-14-1.png","cap":"Popliteal fossa borders and contents","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 14","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":300},{"src":"sl/O082-47.jpg","cap":"Popliteal diaphragm and the soleus arch","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 47"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-13-1","s":"When considering treating the popliteal diaphragm of a patient, you recall that the lymphatic vessels there often get crowded by which of the following structures?","o":["A. Popliteal artery","B. Biceps femoris","C. Posterior cruciate ligament","D. Soleus arch","E. Gastrocnemius tendon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Soleus arch","a":"","why":"<b>D. Soleus arch.</b> At the <b>popliteal diaphragm</b> (the popliteal fossa) the lymph capillaries <b>can be crowded by the soleus arch</b>. A tight soleus slows lymph drainage from the leg and foot.<br>A. Popliteal artery: runs through the fossa beside the lymph vessels; its pulsation helps lymph flow.<br>B. Biceps femoris: forms the upper lateral border of the fossa; not the crowding structure.<br>C. Posterior cruciate ligament: inside the knee joint, not in the fossa.<br>E. Gastrocnemius tendon: its heads form the lower borders, but the soleus arch is the crowding point.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":13,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":14,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":9,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O065-10-4","s":"A 65-year-old diabetic presents with cellulitis of the lower extremity. Lymphatic treatment using the popliteal spread technique is applied. The primary objective of this treatment is to","o":["A. Release restrictions in the popliteal fossa","B. Decrease restrictions in the joint capsule of the knee","C. Loosen medial and lateral collateral ligaments","D. Release the gastrocnemius through effluage","E. Normalize the sympathetic tone of the popliteal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Release restrictions in the popliteal fossa","a":"","why":"<b>A. Release restrictions in the popliteal fossa.</b> The popliteal fossa is the <b>popliteal diaphragm</b>, the fifth transverse diaphragm. Its fascia and the soleus arch can crowd the lymph vessels. Popliteal spread is a <b>direct myofascial release</b> that spreads this fascia to drain the leg.<br>B. Knee joint capsule: target of articulatory technique, not this lymphatic one.<br>C. Collateral ligaments: not involved.<br>D. Gastrocnemius effleurage: effleurage is light stroking; popliteal spread spreads fascia instead.<br>E. Popliteal artery sympathetic tone: not the goal; the target is fascia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":161,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":10,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":9,"label":"Lab sequence: rib raising first, effleurage late","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-3-1.png","cap":"The nine-step sequence, rib raising to self-techniques","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 3","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":986},{"src":"sl/ta-O093-4-1.png","cap":"Seated rib raising, the first technique","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 4","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":712},{"src":"sl/O066-15.jpg","cap":"Cervical effleurage: gentle stroking in the direction of lymph flow","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-15-1","s":"What is the sequence of techniques when treating the lymphatic system? (Hint: There are 9 techniques)","o":["A. Pedal pump, thoracic pump, pectoral lift, re-doming, thoracic inlet release, rib raising, cervical effleurage, sinus drainage, self-techniques","B. Thoracic inlet release, rib raising, re-doming, pectoral lift, thoracic pump, cervical effleurage, sinus drainage, pedal pump, self-techniques","C. Rib raising, thoracic inlet release, re-doming, thoracic pump, pectoral lift, sinus drainage, cervical effleurage, pedal pump, self-techniques","D. Rib raising, re-doming, thoracic inlet release, pectoral lift, thoracic pump, cervical effleurage, sinus drainage, pedal pump, self-techniques","E. Rib raising, thoracic inlet release, re-doming, pectoral lift, thoracic pump, cervical effleurage, sinus drainage, pedal pump, self-techniques"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"1. Rib raising (seated patient); 2. Thoracic inlet myofascial release; 3. Re-doming the diaphragm; 4. Pectoral lift; 5. Thoracic pump; 6. Cervical lymphatic effleurage; 7. Air sinus drainage; 8. Pedal pump; 9. Self-lymphatic techniques.","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> The order: <b>rib raising</b> (seated), <b>thoracic inlet</b> myofascial release, <b>re-doming</b> the diaphragm, pectoral lift, thoracic pump, cervical effleurage, sinus drainage, <b>pedal pump</b>, then self-lymphatic techniques. Open the central drain first, then work out to the periphery.<br>A. Pumps first: pumping before the pathways are open just backs fluid up.<br>B. Inlet before rib raising: the sequence starts with seated rib raising, which calms the sympathetic ganglia beside the thoracic spine.<br>C. Thoracic pump before pectoral lift, sinus drainage before effleurage: both pairs are swapped.<br>D. Re-doming before the inlet: the inlet, the drain, opens before the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":15,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O100-94-1","s":"At what point would you perform effleurage in your treatment of a lymphatic patient?","o":["A. After treating the thoracic inlet and re-doming the diaphragm","B. Before any other lymphatic treatment","C. After doming the diaphragm, but before treating the thoracic inlet","D. Effleurage is contra-indicated in a patient with lymphatic congestion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"After treating the thoracic inlet and re-doming the diaphragm","a":"","why":"<b>A. After the thoracic inlet and re-doming the diaphragm.</b> Effleurage is <b>gentle stroking in the direction of lymph flow</b>. It moves peripheral lymph, so it comes <b>after</b> the central drain is open. Order: rib raising, inlet, re-doming, pectoral lift, thoracic pump, then <b>cervical effleurage</b>.<br>B. Before any other treatment: start centrally; effleurage is a peripheral technique.<br>C. After doming but before the inlet: the inlet is always opened first.<br>D. Contraindicated in congestion: congestion is exactly what it treats.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":94,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":9,"label":"Treat central first: inlet, diaphragm, then pump","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-2-1.png","cap":"Treat central drainage first, then work toward the periphery","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 2","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1218},{"src":"sl/O082-57.jpg","cap":"Plan: inlet, re-dome diaphragm, pelvic diaphragm, then lymphatic pump","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 57"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-10-2","s":"In an attempt to facilitate lymphatic return to the thorax, prior to attempting lymphatic pump techniques, the physician should","o":["A. articulate the sternal angle","B. articulate the sternocostal joints","C. measure diaphragmatic excursion","D. treat peripheral restrictions","E. treat thoracic inlet restrictions"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"treat thoracic inlet restrictions","a":"","why":"<b>E. Treat thoracic inlet restrictions.</b> The inlet is the <b>terminal drainage site</b> where both ducts enter the veins. Open it <b>first</b>, then the diaphragms, then pump.<br>A. Articulate the sternal angle: does not open the terminal drain.<br>B. Articulate the sternocostal joints: same; not the first step.<br>C. Measure diaphragmatic excursion: an assessment, not a treatment.<br>D. Treat peripheral restrictions: the periphery comes <b>last</b>; work central to peripheral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":157,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":10,"f":"O101"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":5,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":6,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":4,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":4,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":3,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":19,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":3,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O065-10-1","s":"A patient presents with a mild viral syndrome. Home symptomatic care is recommended in conjunction with osteopathic manipulative treatment. Which of the following correctly outlines the correct typical sequence for lymphatic treatment?","o":["A. thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, thoracic inlet release, lymphatic pump","B. lymphatic pump, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, thoracic inlet release","C. thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, lymphatic pump, thoracic inlet release","D. thoracic inlet release, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, lymphatic pump","E. thoracic inlet release, lymphatic pump, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"thoracic inlet release, thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release, lymphatic pump","a":"","why":"<b>D. Thoracic inlet, then thoraco-abdominal diaphragm, then lymphatic pump.</b> Rule: <b>remove restrictions first, then augment flow</b>, working <b>central to peripheral</b>. Open the drain (inlet), restore the pressure gradient (diaphragm), then pump.<br>A. Diaphragm first: the inlet, where the ducts empty, must open first.<br>B. Pump first: pumping against a closed inlet just backs fluid up.<br>C. Inlet last: the drain has to be opened first.<br>E. Pump before diaphragm: free the diaphragm before you pump.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":155,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":10,"f":"O101"}],"t":1},{"id":"O091-3-1","s":"A 65-year-old man presents to the office with a several-month history of bilateral hip pain. Physical examination reveals 2+ pitting edema in both lower extremities. Osteopathic examination reveals bilateral myofascial restriction of the pelvis. A full cardiopulmonary workup is unremarkable. The physician decides to begin treatment with lymphatic techniques by placing their hands posteriorly on the cervicothoracic junction and anteriorly on the sternoclavicular joints. The tissues are assessed in all directions, and upon identifying a restriction, the physician engages it indirectly. After approximately 20 seconds, a palpable release is felt. The physician elects to continue lymphatic techniques. The next most appropriate area to assess and treat is the","o":["A. pelvic diaphragm","B. tentorium cerebelli","C. thoracic diaphragm","D. thoracic inlet","E. tongue"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"thoracic diaphragm","a":"","why":"<b>C. Thoracic diaphragm.</b> Hands on the cervicothoracic junction and sternoclavicular joints, indirect, release in 20 seconds: that is <b>thoracic inlet myofascial release</b>, already done. The order: inlet, then <b>re-dome the thoraco-abdominal diaphragm</b>, then pelvic diaphragm, then pumps.<br>A. Pelvic diaphragm: tempting with leg edema and a tight pelvis, but it comes after the thoraco-abdominal diaphragm.<br>B. Tentorium cerebelli: cranial diaphragm; not the next step for leg edema.<br>D. Thoracic inlet: just treated.<br>E. Tongue: not one of the five diaphragms.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":3,"f":"O091"}]},{"id":"O111-21-1","s":"A patient presents after \"rolling her ankle\" while playing volley ball. She was seen in the urgent care after the injury and had an XR there which was negative. However, she still has pain and mild edema around her lateral ankle. Which of the following is the first technique you should perform to best help with her ankle swelling?","o":["A. Pedal pump","B. Thoracic inlet myofascial release","C. Diaphragm doming","D. Ankle joint muscle energy"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic inlet myofascial release","a":"","why":"<b>B. Thoracic inlet myofascial release.</b> Lymphatic treatment always starts at the <b>central terminal drainage site</b>, the thoracic inlet, where both ducts enter the veins, even when the swelling is in an ankle. Ankle lymph reaches the inlet through the thoracic duct.<br>A. Pedal pump: it augments flow, so it comes after the drains are open; an acute ankle sprain is also a pedal pump caution.<br>C. Diaphragm doming: the second step, after the inlet.<br>D. Ankle joint muscle energy: a local, peripheral technique; the periphery is treated last.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":21,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":10,"label":"Inlet release: thumbs on T1, fingers on clavicles","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-10.jpg","cap":"Thumbs on T1 and its transverse process, fingers on both clavicles","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/ta-O093-5-1.png","cap":"Inlet release tests rotation, sidebending, hands together or apart","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":998}],"q":[{"id":"O111-5-1","s":"When performing a thoracic inlet myofascial release, which of the following describes the correct hand placement of the physician?","o":["A. Thumbs along the inferior margin of the rib cage assessing diaphragm excursion","B. Thumbs along T1 and fingers along clavicles bilaterally","C. Hands posterior to the scapulae assessing rib excursion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thumbs along T1 and fingers along clavicles bilaterally","a":"","why":"<b>B. Thumbs along T1, fingers along both clavicles.</b> The thumbs sit on <b>T1</b> (first thoracic vertebra) and its transverse process, and the fingers rest on the <b>clavicles</b>, framing the inlet (rib 1, T1, manubrium, clavicles).<br>A. Thumbs along the lower rib margin checking diaphragm excursion: a diaphragm assessment; in re-doming the fingers go under the costal margin.<br>C. Hands behind the scapulae checking rib excursion: a rib cage assessment, not the inlet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":5,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":10,"label":"Thoracic inlet release: rotation, indirect","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-5-1.png","cap":"Inlet release tests rotation, sidebending, hands together/apart","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":998},{"src":"sl/O066-10.jpg","cap":"Inlet release with thumbs at T1, fingers on clavicles","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-17-1","s":"Which of the following motions is correct when performing a Thoracic inlet myofascial release?","o":["A. Anterior and posterior","B. Rotation clockwise and counterclockwise","C. Superior and inferior","D. Caudad and cephalad"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Rotation clockwise and counterclockwise","a":"","why":"<b>B. Rotation clockwise and counterclockwise.</b> The physician tests the inlet in <b>three motion pairs</b>: <b>clockwise/anticlockwise rotation</b>, sidebending left/right, and bringing the hands together or pushing/pulling them apart. The tissue is taken toward its ease (indirect), the patient breathes, then you reassess.<br>A. Anterior and posterior: not one of the three motion pairs.<br>C. Superior and inferior: not on the list.<br>D. Caudad and cephalad: the same pair as C in other words, so neither can be the answer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":17,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O100-90-1","s":"You diagnose your patient’s thoracic inlet as sidebent left, rotated left, and medial. You treat this by sidebending their fascia to the left, rotating it left, and moving your thumbs medially. What type of technique is this?","o":["A. Direct technique","B. Indirect technique","C. HVLA","D. Balanced ligamentous tension","E. Balanced membranous tension"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect technique","a":"","why":"<b>B. Indirect technique.</b> The inlet's <b>ease</b> is sidebent left, rotated left and medial. Moving the fascia <b>into those same directions</b>, away from the barrier, is <b>indirect</b>. Inlet release is done this way: find the direction of freedom in each plane and hold it.<br>A. Direct: would move into the barrier (sidebend right, rotate right, lateral).<br>C. HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude): a direct thrust through the barrier.<br>D. BLT (balanced ligamentous tension): balances ligament tension at a joint, not this fascial release.<br>E. Balanced membranous tension: a cranial technique for the dural membranes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":90,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O111-7-1","s":"When assessing the thoracic inlet for restriction, which of the following is NOT a direction of motion that is assessed?","o":["A. Sidebending","B. Flexion/extension","C. Compression/distraction (hands together and away)","D. Rotation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion/extension","a":"","why":"<b>B. Flexion/extension.</b> The physician tests the inlet in <b>three motion pairs</b>: rotation (clockwise and counterclockwise), sidebending (left and right), and compression/distraction (hands brought together, then pushed or pulled apart). Flexion/extension is not one of them. Each pair is held toward its ease (indirect), the patient breathes, then you reassess.<br>A. Sidebending: assessed.<br>C. Compression/distraction: assessed.<br>D. Rotation: assessed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":7,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":10,"label":"Re-doming: fingers under the costal margin","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-6-1.png","cap":"Re-doming, fingers under costal margin, follow exhalation up","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 6","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1018}],"q":[{"id":"O065-9-3","s":"A patient presents with generalized edema and requests osteopathic therapy. With the hands around the thoracic cage and underneath the costal margin, the physician holds the facial tissues during patient inhalation and exhalation. This technique is called the","o":["A. facilitated positional release","B. rib raising","C. thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release","D. thoracic inlet release","E. thoracic outlet release"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release","a":"","why":"<b>C. Thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release</b> (re-doming). Fingers <b>under the costal margin</b>, pointing up. <b>Follow the diaphragm up on exhalation, resist its descent on inhalation</b>, and repeat until it releases. This restores the dome and the pressure gradient.<br>A. Facilitated positional release: position of ease plus a compression or torsion, held a few seconds.<br>B. Rib raising: fingertips at the rib angles beside the spine, lifting up and out.<br>D. Thoracic inlet release: hands on T1 (1st thoracic vertebra) and the clavicles.<br>E. Thoracic outlet release: the same neck-base region as the inlet; hands are not under the ribs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":9,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":153,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":9,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":10,"label":"Re-doming goal: upward diaphragm motion in exhalation","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-6-1.png","cap":"Re-doming, follow the diaphragm up in exhalation, resist descent","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 6","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1018},{"src":"sl/O082-38.jpg","cap":"Hypertonic diaphragm flattens: smaller gradient, less lymph and venous return","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 38"}],"q":[{"id":"O111-9-1","s":"For the technique depicted below, which of the following is a goal of the treatment?","o":["A. Reduce myofascial tension across the thoracic inlet","B. Decrease the overall excursion of the diaphragm","C. Improve downward movement of the diaphragm in inhalation","D. Improve upward movement of the diaphragm in exhalation"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Improve upward movement of the diaphragm in exhalation","a":"","why":"<b>D. Improve upward movement of the diaphragm in exhalation.</b> The picture shows <b>re-doming</b>: fingers under the costal margin, pointing toward the head. A hypertonic diaphragm stays <b>flattened</b>, low as in inhalation. The physician follows it up on each exhalation and resists its descent on inhalation, so the dome and the pressure gradient return.<br>A. Reduce tension across the thoracic inlet: thoracic inlet release, hands on T1 and the clavicles.<br>B. Decrease overall excursion: the goal is more excursion, not less.<br>C. Improve downward movement in inhalation: descent is resisted, not helped; the flat diaphragm already sits low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-9-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":9,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":10,"label":"Thoracic inlet release: which patient benefits","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-5-1.png","cap":"Thoracic inlet myofascial release, hands at the inlet","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":998},{"src":"sl/O064-6.jpg","cap":"Inlet borders: rib 1, T1, manubrium, clavicles; both ducts empty here","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-12-1","s":"Thoracic inlet myofascial release would be useful for a patient who has","o":["A. functional scoliotic curve","B. inhalation dysfunction of rib 6","C. sacroiliac restriction with a tight piriformis muscle","D. thoracolumbar extended dysfunction","E. upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, and rib 1 dysfunction"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, and rib 1 dysfunction","a":"","why":"<b>E. Upper respiratory infection, tight scalenes, rib 1 dysfunction.</b> Thoracic inlet myofascial release works on <b>rib 1, T1 (1st thoracic vertebra), manubrium, clavicles</b> and Sibson's fascia, which includes the <b>scalene</b> fascia. Opening it lets head and neck lymph drain, so it comes first in infections.<br>A. Functional scoliotic curve: a spinal curve; treated along the spine.<br>B. Inhalation dysfunction of rib 6: treat rib 6 itself (e.g., muscle energy).<br>C. Sacroiliac restriction with tight piriformis: a pelvic problem.<br>D. Thoracolumbar extended dysfunction: thoracolumbar junction, near the abdominal diaphragm, not the inlet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":12,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":175,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":11,"f":"O101"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":11,"label":"Pectoral traction: anterior axillary folds","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-7-1.png","cap":"Pectoral traction grasping the anterior axillary folds","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 7","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":963}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-1","s":"Pectoral traction affects lymphatic flow because the","o":["A. anterior axillary fold is directly released","B. cisterna chyli is massaged","C. clavicles are articulated","D. posterior axillary fold is directly released","E. sternum is released using the hypothenar eminence"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"anterior axillary fold is directly released","a":"","why":"<b>A. Anterior axillary fold is directly released.</b> In pectoral traction the physician, at the head of the table, grasps the <b>pectoralis muscles at the anterior axillary folds</b> and leans back. This lifts the chest into inhalation; deep breaths then work the diaphragm and boost lymph return.<br>B. Cisterna chyli massaged: it lies deep at L1–L2 (lumbar vertebrae); not touched.<br>C. Clavicles articulated: the hands hold the pectorals, not the clavicles.<br>D. Posterior axillary fold: that is a separate upper-limb drainage technique.<br>E. Sternum released with the hypothenar eminence: describes sternal pressure, not pectoral traction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":165,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":11,"f":"O101"}],"t":1},{"id":"O111-19-1","s":"A 39-year-old pregnant patient presents with bilateral lower extremity edema that has progressed during her pregnancy. You decide to perform the technique depicted below to help with her overall lymphatic drainage. For this technique, which of the following physiologic mechanisms is being utilized?","o":["A. Oculocephalogyric reflex","B. Reciprocal inhibition","C. Respiratory assist","D. Crossed extensor reflex"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Respiratory assist","a":"","why":"<b>C. Respiratory assist.</b> The picture shows <b>pectoral traction</b> (pectoral lift): hands on the pectorals at the anterior axillary folds, physician leaning back. The patient's own <b>deep breathing</b> does the work: traction holds the front chest in inhalation, so each deep breath drives the diaphragm harder and boosts lymph return. Pregnancy edema is an indication.<br>A. Oculocephalogyric reflex: eye movement that helps neck or trunk muscles contract, used in muscle energy.<br>B. Reciprocal inhibition: contracting one muscle relaxes its opposite, a muscle energy principle.<br>D. Crossed extensor reflex: contracting the opposite limb relaxes the treated muscle, also muscle energy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-19-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":19,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":11,"label":"Pedal pump: lymph return, prone or supine","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-11-1.png","cap":"Pedal pump, rhythmic dorsiflexion of both feet","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 11","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1074},{"src":"sl/O064-62.jpg","cap":"Pedal pump encourages lymph return, prone or supine","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 62"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-6-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding the technique pictured?","o":["A. It is a part of the straight leg raise","B. It is assessing the patient’s muscle strength","C. It encourages lymphatic return","D. It can only be done with the patient supine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It encourages lymphatic return","a":"","why":"<b>C. It encourages lymphatic return.</b> The picture shows the <b>Dalrymple pedal pump</b>: physician at the foot of the table, rhythmically dorsiflexing both feet. The motion travels up the body and moves lymph from the <b>whole body</b>.<br>A. Part of the SLR (straight leg raise): the SLR lifts one straight leg to stretch the nerve roots; here both feet are pumped.<br>B. Assessing muscle strength: the patient is passive; nothing is resisted.<br>D. Only supine: it may be done <b>prone or supine</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O096-6-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":6,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":11,"label":"Miller thoracic pump: supine, sudden release","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-8-1.png","cap":"Thoracic pump supine, hands on upper chest, rebound release","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 8","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1002},{"src":"sl/O082-64.jpg","cap":"1918 influenza protocol: thoracic pump to increase lymph drainage","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 64"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-92-1","s":"Which statement correctly describes the Miller thoracic pump?","o":["A. With the patient laying supine, manually dorsiflex their ankles and gently provide rhythmic force to generate lymphatic flow","B. Standing at the head of a supine patient, lay your hands on their chest, using force to restrict inhalation, and providing rhythmic force downwards during exhalation to increase lymphatic return","C. Standing at the head of a supine patient, lay your hands on their chest, using force to restrict exhalation, and providing rhythmic force downwards during inhalation to increase lymphatic return","D. Standing at the head of a prone patient, lay your hands on their back, using force to restrict inhalation, and providing rhythmic force downwards during exhalation to increase lymphatic return"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Standing at the head of a supine patient, lay your hands on their chest, using force to restrict inhalation, and providing rhythmic force downwards during exhalation to increase lymphatic return","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> Miller thoracic pump: patient <b>supine</b>, physician at the <b>head</b>, hands on the upper chest. Push down rhythmically during <b>exhalation</b>, <b>resist inhalation</b>, then let go suddenly on a final inhalation. It is done supine only.<br>A. Dorsiflexing the ankles: that is the pedal (Dalrymple) pump.<br>C. Restrict exhalation, push during inhalation: the phases are reversed.<br>D. Prone, hands on the back: the thoracic pump is done supine only; prone-or-supine is the pedal pump.","ex":"","fl":["The correct answer is B only: the thoracic pump is done supine, with the hands on the upper chest; the prone option belongs to the pedal pump."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":92,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O065-10-5","s":"A middle aged female presents with body aches, fever, and head congestion. She is assessed as having influenza and osteopathic manipulative treatment is provided. During treatment the physician utilizes the elastic recoil of the thoracic cage to create an abrupt change in intra-thoracic pressure. What is the most appropriate name of this technique?","o":["A. redoming the diaphragm","B. indirect rib release","C. pectoral traction","D. thoracic lymphatic pump of Miller","E. pedal fascial lymphatic pump"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"thoracic lymphatic pump of Miller","a":"","why":"<b>D. Thoracic lymphatic pump of Miller.</b> Hands on the upper chest push during exhalation and hold during inhalation, then <b>let go suddenly</b>. The chest's <b>elastic recoil</b> makes an abrupt negative intrathoracic pressure that pulls lymph centrally. It was the 1918 influenza protocol.<br>A. Redoming the diaphragm: slow, fingers under the costal margin; no sudden release.<br>B. Indirect rib release: moves the rib toward ease; no pressure swing.<br>C. Pectoral traction: steady pull on the anterior axillary folds.<br>E. Pedal fascial lymphatic pump: rhythmic dorsiflexion at the feet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":10,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":163,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":10,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":11,"label":"Thoracic pump: lung cancer contraindicated, pneumonia indicated","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-8-1.png","cap":"Thoracic pump indications, contraindications and technique","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 8","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1002}],"q":[{"id":"O111-11-1","s":"A patient presents with several weeks of feeling shortness of breath and noticing a decrease in their exercise capacity. Which of the following would be a contraindication to performing the thoracic pump technique?","o":["A. Lung cancer","B. Pneumonia","C. Atelectasis","D. Bronchitis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lung cancer","a":"","why":"<b>A. Lung cancer.</b> The thoracic pump compresses the chest rhythmically. Its contraindications include <b>lung cancer</b>, acute rib fracture, severe osteoporosis, aspiration, pulmonary embolism, acute heart failure, peritonitis and recent abdominal surgery.<br>B. Pneumonia: an <b>indication</b>; the pump was the core of the 1918 influenza and pneumonia protocol.<br>C. Atelectasis (collapsed areas of lung): an indication; the sudden-release version encourages rapid lung expansion.<br>D. Bronchitis: an indication.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":11,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":12,"label":"Jugulodigastric node: head drainage, cervical effleurage","slides":[{"src":"sl/O066-15.jpg","cap":"Cervical effleurage follows lymph flow toward head and neck nodes","where":"2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic & Sinus Drainage, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"O111-13-1","s":"Which of the following lymph nodes (situated just posterior and inferior to the angle of the mandible) is a major site for lymphatic drainage from the head and a target of the cervical effleurage technique?","o":["A. Mastoid","B. Submental","C. Jugulodigastric","D. Occipital"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jugulodigastric","a":"","why":"<b>C. Jugulodigastric node.</b> It sits just <b>below and behind the angle of the mandible</b>, where the digastric muscle crosses the internal jugular vein. It is the main upper node of the deep cervical chain, so lymph from the sinuses, middle ear and throat passes through it; cervical effleurage strokes toward it and down the neck.<br>A. Mastoid: behind the ear; drains the scalp behind the ear.<br>B. Submental: under the chin; drains the chin, lower lip and tongue tip.<br>D. Occipital: at the back of the skull base; drains the back of the scalp.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":13,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":12,"label":"Sinus inhibitory pressure: trigeminal foramina","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-10-1.png","cap":"Sinus drainage sweeps over frontal, orbital, mandibular areas","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 10","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1006}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-3","s":"A manipulative treatment that involves physician contact at the patient's supraorbital, mental, and infraorbital foramina is","o":["A. anterior cervical counterstrain","B. frontal lift","C. pterygoid fossa decongestion","D. sinus inhibitory pressure","E. vomer pump technique"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"sinus inhibitory pressure","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sinus inhibitory pressure.</b> Steady pressure on the three <b>trigeminal exit foramina</b>: supraorbital (V1, ophthalmic), infraorbital (V2, maxillary) and mental (V3, mandibular). It reflexly eases sinus congestion. Stroking the face is <b>sinus drainage effleurage</b>, a different technique.<br>A. Anterior cervical counterstrain: tender points on the front of the neck.<br>B. Frontal lift: cranial technique lifting the frontal bone.<br>C. Pterygoid fossa decongestion: targets the deep pterygoid region, not the facial foramina.<br>E. Vomer pump: intraoral contact on the hard palate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":169,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":11,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":12,"label":"Galbreath technique: otitis media","slides":[{"src":"sl/O065-6.jpg","cap":"Facial sinus pressure and Galbreath drain toward the jugulodigastric node","where":"2026 week 11 Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13), page 6"}],"q":[{"id":"O065-11-2","s":"The Galbreath technique is useful for treating those with","o":["A. bacterial conjunctivitis","B. exudative tonsillitis","C. nasal polyposis","D. recurrent otitis media","E. trigeminal neuralgia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"recurrent otitis media","a":"","why":"<b>D. Recurrent otitis media.</b> The Galbreath technique (mandibular drainage) is a slow rhythmic <b>downward and medial pull on the mandible</b>. It repeatedly opens the <b>Eustachian tube</b> so the middle ear can drain.<br>A. Bacterial conjunctivitis: an eye infection; not a Eustachian tube problem.<br>B. Exudative tonsillitis: a throat infection; not what Galbreath targets.<br>C. Nasal polyposis: a structural growth; drainage does not remove it.<br>E. Trigeminal neuralgia: a nerve pain disorder, not fluid congestion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Lymphatics (Capobianco Ch 13)","sl":11,"f":"O065"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":167,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 1 Lymphatics (Capobianco)","sl":11,"f":"O101"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":12,"label":"Sinus effleurage: six sweep regions","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-10-1.png","cap":"Sinus drainage sweeps; step 4 is submandibular, fingers under jaw","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 10","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1006}],"q":[{"id":"O111-15-1","s":"In the image depicted below, which of the following regions is being treated in the sinus effleurage technique?","o":["A. Supraorbital","B. Infraorbital","C. Submandibular","D. Frontal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular","a":"","why":"<b>C. Submandibular.</b> One hand steadies the head at the forehead while the other hand's fingers sit <b>under the jaw</b>, stroking toward the neck. Sinus drainage effleurage makes 3–4 sweeps over six areas in order: frontal, supraorbital, infraorbital, inframalar, mandibular, submandibular.<br>A. Supraorbital: fingers sweep along the brow, above the eyes.<br>B. Infraorbital: fingers sweep below the eyes, beside the nose.<br>D. Frontal: fingers sweep across the forehead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-15-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":15,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":13,"label":"Contraindications: absolute vs relative","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-14-1.png","cap":"Absolute contraindications to lymphatic techniques","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 14","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":452},{"src":"sl/O082-60.jpg","cap":"Relative: unstaged cancer, coagulopathies or anticoagulants, abscess","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 60"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-22-1.png","cap":"Spleen pump contraindicated in acute infectious mononucleosis","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 22","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":603}],"q":[{"id":"O100-79-1","s":"Which of these is an absolute contraindication to lympathic techniques?","o":["A. Flu","B. DVT","C. Cancer","D. Abscess","E. Productive cough"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"DVT","a":"","why":"<b>B. DVT</b> (deep vein thrombosis). The <b>absolute</b> contraindications: <b>DVT</b> (pumping could dislodge the clot), <b>splenic pump in infectious mononucleosis</b>, <b>local fracture</b>, and <b>anuria in a patient not on dialysis</b>.<br>A. Flu: an indication; lymphatic pumps were the 1918 influenza treatment.<br>C. Cancer: unstaged cancer is only a relative contraindication.<br>D. Abscess: a relative contraindication.<br>E. Productive cough: an indication (bronchitis, pneumonia).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":79,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O095-13-1","s":"Which of the following is not an absolute contraindication for lymphatic techniques?","o":["A. DVT","B. Mono","C. Local fracture","D. Unstaged cancer","E. Anuria in pt not on dialysis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Unstaged cancer","a":"","why":"<b>D. Unstaged cancer.</b> It is a <b>relative</b> contraindication, like coagulopathies, anticoagulant use and abscess. The <b>absolute</b> list: DVT (deep vein thrombosis), splenic pump in mono, local fracture, anuria not on dialysis.<br>A. DVT: absolute; pumping can send the clot to the lungs.<br>B. Mono (infectious mononucleosis): absolute for the splenic pump; the swollen spleen can rupture.<br>C. Local fracture: absolute.<br>E. Anuria not on dialysis: absolute; fluid moved into the blood cannot leave as urine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":13,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":16,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-16-1","s":"Which of the following is not a relative contraindication to lymphatic techniques?","o":["A. Unstaged cancer","B. Coagulopathies","C. Local Fracture","D. Abscess","E. Patients on anticoagulants"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Local Fracture","a":"","why":"<b>C. Local fracture.</b> It is an <b>absolute</b> contraindication, so it is not a relative one. The <b>relative</b> list: <b>unstaged cancer</b>, <b>coagulopathies, including patients on anticoagulants</b>, and <b>abscess</b>.<br>A. Unstaged cancer: relative.<br>B. Coagulopathies: relative.<br>D. Abscess: relative.<br>E. Patients on anticoagulants: relative, grouped with coagulopathies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":16,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O095-21-1","s":"Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) is estimated to infect greater than 98% of adults worldwide and is one of the most common human viruses. EBV infection can lead to acute infectious mononucleosis characterized by fever, fatigue, malaise, sore throat, and lymphadenopathy. Elevated liver function tests (LFTs) and hepatosplenomegaly may also be present. This infection usually lasts over a period of weeks or months and is self-limited. What is contraindicated in someone with mono?","o":["A. Lymphatic Splenic pump","B. Counterstrain L5","C. Muscle energy QL","D. Myofascial Trapezius"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lymphatic Splenic pump","a":"","why":"<b>A. Lymphatic splenic pump.</b> Infectious mononucleosis (from Epstein-Barr virus) swells the spleen and makes it fragile. Pumping it risks <b>splenic rupture</b>, so the <b>splenic pump in mono is an absolute contraindication</b>.<br>B. Counterstrain to L5 (5th lumbar vertebra): nowhere near the spleen.<br>C. Muscle energy to the QL (quadratus lumborum): does not compress the spleen.<br>D. Myofascial release of the trapezius: far from the spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":21,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O111-17-1","s":"An elderly patient presents for a routine follow-up appointment and medication refill. He has a history of chronic swelling in both lower extremities. Today he denies any acute changes in his edema, or other/new complaints. Which of the following would be a contraindication to performing the pedal pump technique?","o":["A. Concern for a DVT","B. Lower extremity edema","C. A history of CHF","D. Pain in his lower extremities"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Concern for a DVT","a":"","why":"<b>A. Concern for a DVT</b> (deep vein thrombosis). DVT is an <b>absolute</b> contraindication: pumping the legs could dislodge the clot and send it to the lungs.<br>B. Lower extremity edema: an <b>indication</b>; moving that fluid is the purpose of the pump.<br>C. A history of CHF (congestive heart failure): caution applies only in <b>acute</b> heart failure; stable chronic swelling is not a contraindication.<br>D. Leg pain: not a contraindication by itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":17,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":13,"label":"Contraindication: pedal pump on infected foot","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O093-11-1.png","cap":"Pedal pump grips and pumps the feet directly","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 11","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1074},{"src":"sl/O082-60.jpg","cap":"Relative contraindications include abscess","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 60"},{"src":"sl/O082-61.jpg","cap":"Absolute contraindications to lymphatic techniques","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 61"}],"q":[{"id":"O091-2-3","s":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with a 2-week history of right foot swelling. He states that the swelling started after he found a nail in his shoe. Past medical history reveals poorly controlled type 2 diabetes. Vital signs reveal a temperature of 37.8°C (100.1°F) and a heart rate of 102/min. Physical examination reveals decreased pain and vibration sensation in both feet. A probe-to-bone test is positive. A photograph of the right foot is obtained, as shown in the exhibit. Which of the following is contraindicated in the management of this patient?","o":["A. Dalrymple pump","B. Galbreath technique","C. liver pump","D. Miller pump","E. splenic pump"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Dalrymple pump","a":"","why":"<b>A. Dalrymple (pedal) pump.</b> A positive probe-to-bone test in a diabetic foot means <b>osteomyelitis</b> of the right foot. The pedal pump grips and rhythmically pumps <b>the infected foot itself</b>. Local infection (abscess, cellulitis, osteomyelitis) rules out techniques applied at that site.<br>B. Galbreath technique: jaw technique for ear drainage; far from the infection.<br>C. Liver pump: over the right lower ribs; can aid the immune response.<br>D. Miller pump: thoracic pump on the chest; fine here.<br>E. Splenic pump: absolutely contraindicated in infectious <b>mononucleosis</b> (spleen rupture risk), not here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":2,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W11","u":14,"label":"Post-mastectomy lymphedema: sympathetic tone and plan","slides":[{"src":"sl/O064-48.jpg","cap":"Post-mastectomy lymphedema: chronic sympathetic tone decreases lymph movement","where":"2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/O082-57.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic plan: inlet, abdominal diaphragm, pelvic diaphragm, then pump","where":"2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System & Diaphragms, page 57"},{"src":"sl/ta-O093-2-1.png","cap":"Open central drainage sites first","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 2","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1218}],"q":[{"id":"O100-67-1","s":"A 68 year old female with breast cancer has just undergone a mastectomy to remove her right breast. She is now complaining of a swollen, edematous right upper extremity. How will prolonged hypersympathetic tone affect lymph vessels?","o":["A. Hypersympathetic tone will cause peristalsis of the lymph vessels, increasing lymphatic movement","B. Hypersympathetic tone will cause constriction of the lymph vessels, decreasing lymphatic movement","C. Lymphatic channels are only innervated by parasympathetic nerve fibers, so there is no change","D. Because lymph is propelled by muscular contractions through one-way valves, the nervous system has no effect on lymph movement"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypersympathetic tone will cause constriction of the lymph vessels, decreasing lymphatic movement","a":"","why":"<b>B. Constriction, decreasing lymphatic movement.</b> Sympathetic nerves make lymph vessels constrict and contract. A <b>short burst raises flow</b>, but <b>prolonged hypersympathetic tone</b> keeps them constricted and <b>reduces lymph movement</b>. The classic example is post-mastectomy lymphedema.<br>A. Peristalsis increasing flow: that is the acute effect, not the prolonged one.<br>C. Only parasympathetic fibers: lymphatics are under sympathetic control; parasympathetic effects are barely known.<br>D. No nervous effect: sympathetic tone changes the rate of peristalsis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":67,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O064-43-1","s":"A 45-year-old female presents to your office for Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment. Her past medical history is significant for breast cancer. She had a mastectomy and has under gone chemotherapy. Physical exam reveals lymph-edema of the upper right extremity. What would you include in the treatment plan of this patient?","o":["A. Reduce hyper-sympathetic activity","B. Diagnose and treat the thoracic inlet","C. Dome the abdominal diaphragm","D. Utilize lymphatic pump techniques","E. All of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"","why":"<b>E. All of the above.</b> The plan for lymphedema: <b>reduce hyper-sympathetic activity</b>, <b>treat the thoracic inlet</b>, <b>re-dome the abdominal diaphragm</b>, treat the pelvic diaphragm, then apply a <b>lymphatic pump</b>.<br>A. Reduce hyper-sympathetic activity: right on its own; chronic sympathetic tone constricts lymph vessels and slows flow.<br>B. Treat the thoracic inlet: right on its own; both lymph ducts empty there, so it is opened first.<br>C. Dome the diaphragm: right on its own; it restores the pressure gradient.<br>D. Lymphatic pumps: right on its own; they push lymph through the opened path.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Lymphatics","sl":43,"f":"O064"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":30,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":5,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O091-1-3","s":"A 45-year-old female with a past medical history of breast cancer presents to your office for OMT. She had a right-sided mastectomy and has been treated with chemotherapy. She currently complains of lymphedema of the right upper extremity. All of the following should be included in the treatment plan for this patient except for:","o":["A. Utilize lymphatic pump techniques","B. Remove myofascial dysfunction of the thoracic inlet","C. Dome the abdominal diaphragm","D. Increase sympathetic tone"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase sympathetic tone","a":"","why":"<b>D. Increase sympathetic tone</b> does not belong. A short burst of sympathetic firing speeds lymph vessel contraction, but <b>chronic hypersympathetic tone</b> keeps the vessels constricted and <b>reduces lymph flow</b>. Post-mastectomy lymphedema is the classic example. The plan <b>lowers</b> sympathetic tone.<br>A. Lymphatic pump techniques: belong; they push lymph once the path is open.<br>B. Thoracic inlet myofascial dysfunction: belongs; both ducts drain there, so it is treated first.<br>C. Doming the abdominal diaphragm: belongs; it restores the pressure gradient that moves lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Lecture 11 Quiz - Lymphatics (questions)","sl":1,"f":"O091"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":15,"label":"Lumbar lordosis develops in childhood","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-6.jpg","cap":"Spinal curves from newborn to age 10: lumbar lordosis forms in childhood","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-4-1","s":"When does lumbar lordosis develop?","o":["A. neonatal","B. infant","C. childhood","D. adult"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"childhood","a":"","why":"<b>C. Childhood.</b> The newborn spine is one long C-shaped (kyphotic) curve, and <b>lumbar lordosis develops during childhood</b> as the child stands and the spine extends against an iliopsoas that does not lengthen enough.<br>A. Neonatal: the newborn has no lumbar lordosis, only the primary kyphotic curve.<br>B. Infant: head control first builds the cervical lordosis; the lumbar curve follows with standing and walking.<br>D. Adult: the curve is already formed by then.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":4,"f":"O100"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":15,"label":"Lumbar ROM: flexion 40–50°, extension 20–30°","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-62-1.png","cap":"Most lumbar flexion happens at L5–S1 and L4–L5","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 62","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":486}],"q":[{"id":"O100-2-1","s":"What is the normal range of motion for flexion of the lumbar?","o":["A. 20-30","B. 30-40","C. 40-50","D. 50-60"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"40-50","a":"","why":"<b>C. 40–50°.</b> Normal lumbar range of motion (ROM) is <b>flexion 40–50°</b>, <b>extension 20–30°</b>, sidebending 20–25° per side and rotation only 5–7° per side.<br>A. 20–30°: that is lumbar extension.<br>B. 30–40°: too little; flexion is the largest lumbar motion.<br>D. 50–60°: above the normal of 40–50°.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":2,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-27-1","s":"Which of the following are the correct range of motion in the lumbar spine?","o":["A. Flexion 15-20 degrees","B. Extension 45-50 degrees","C. Lateral Flexion 25 degrees","D. Lateral Flexion 35 degrees"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral Flexion 25 degrees","a":"","why":"<b>C. Lateral flexion (sidebending) about 25°.</b> Normal lumbar sidebending (SB) is <b>20–25° per side</b>, the same normal used in the standing sidebending screen.<br>A. Flexion 15–20°: far too low; flexion is the largest motion, 40–50°.<br>B. Extension 45–50°: too high; extension is 20–30°.<br>D. Lateral flexion 35°: more than the normal 20–25°.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":27,"f":"O096"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":15,"label":"Lumbar flexion: mostly L5–S1, plus pelvic rotation","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-62-1.png","cap":"Flexion by segment: L5–S1 60–75%, L4–L5 20–25%, L1–L4 5–10%","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 62","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":486},{"src":"sl/O069-37.jpg","cap":"75% of flexion at L5–S1; the pelvis rotates about the hips for full flexion","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-47-1","s":"Lumbar flexion ROM: where does the majority of flexion occur? What else is happening?","o":["A. L3–L4 (60–75%), at the apex of the lumbar lordosis","B. L4–L5 (60–75%), with the pelvis held still","C. L1–L2 (60–75%), with the thoracic spine extending","D. Spread evenly from L1 to L5, with no pelvic motion","E. L5–S1 (60–75%), with the pelvis rotating about the hip joints"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"At L5–S1 (about 60–75% of lumbar flexion; L4–L5 plus L5–S1 give 80–90%). To reach full flexion the pelvis also rotates proportionately about the hip joints.","a":"","why":"<b>E. L5–S1, plus pelvic rotation.</b> Lumbar flexion totals about 40–50°. <b>L5–S1 gives 60–75%</b>, L4–L5 20–25% (together 80–90%), and L1–L4 only 5–10%. To reach full flexion the <b>pelvis also rotates about the hip joints</b>, so hip or hamstring restriction limits forward bending.<br>A. L3–L4: also in the 5–10% share.<br>B. L4–L5: second largest, only 20–25%, and the pelvis does move.<br>C. L1–L2: part of the small 5–10% upper share.<br>D. Even spread: no, motion concentrates at the lumbosacral junction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":47,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":61,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":26,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":27,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":32,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O111-55-1","s":"A 63-year-old female presents with a history of chronic low back pain. On examination, you notice that she has significantly limited lumbar flexion compared to the other planes of motion. You order an XR of her lumbar spine. Which vertebral level are you most likely to see pathology related to her limited range of motion?","o":["A. L4-5","B. L5-S1","C. L3-4","D. L1-2"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"L5-S1","a":"","why":"<b>B. L5–S1.</b> About <b>75% (60–75%) of lumbar flexion happens at L5–S1</b>, and L5 also has the most congenital anomalies, so limited flexion points there first. Full flexion also needs the pelvis to rotate about the hip joints.<br>A. L4–5: the second largest share, only 20–25%.<br>C. L3–4 and D. L1–2: the upper lumbar levels together give only 5–10% of flexion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":55,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":16,"label":"Facets: superior concave, back-up-medial; inferior convex","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O097-25-1.png","cap":"Superior facets concave, face medially and backward; inferior convex, face laterally","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 25","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":755},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-34-1.png","cap":"Inferior articular process of the vertebra above sits inside the superior process","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 34","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":481},{"src":"sl/O069-41.jpg","cap":"BUM mnemonic: lumbar superior facets face back, up and medial","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-33-1","s":"Superior Articular Facets are _____ and face ____:","o":["A. Convex and face laterally and forward","B. Concave and face primarily medially and backwards","C. Convex and face primarily medially and backwards","D. Concave and face laterally and forward."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Concave and face primarily medially and backwards","a":"","why":"<b>B. Concave, facing medially and backward.</b> Lumbar <b>superior articular facets are concave</b> and face <b>medially and backward</b> (about 45° from the sagittal plane). They cup the convex inferior facets of the vertebra above.<br>A. Convex, facing laterally and forward: this is the inferior facet.<br>C. Convex, medially and backward: right direction, wrong shape; superior facets are concave.<br>D. Concave, laterally and forward: right shape, wrong direction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":33,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":6,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O097-23-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the position of the inferior facet joints of lumbar vertebrae?","o":["A. Concave, facing backward and medially","B. Convex, facing backward and laterally","C. Concave, facing forward and medially","D. Convex, facing forward and laterally"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Convex, facing forward and laterally","a":"","why":"<b>D. Convex, facing forward and laterally.</b> Lumbar <b>inferior articular facets are convex</b> and face <b>laterally and forward</b>, fitting into the concave superior facets below, which face medially and backward.<br>A. Concave, backward and medially: this describes the superior facet.<br>B. Convex, backward and laterally: right shape, but inferior facets face forward.<br>C. Concave, forward and medially: wrong shape and wrong side; inferior facets are convex and face laterally.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":23,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":25,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":8,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O092-9-1","s":"A 42-year-old man presents to the office with a 20-year history of chronic low back pain that began after a fall. His pain does not radiate and is localized to the lumbar spine region. Physical examination reveals no muscle weakness or sensory changes of the lumbar spine, pelvis, or lower extremities. Osteopathic examination reveals L1–L5 sidebent right and rotated left. The orientation of the superior facet of the affected vertebrae is","o":["A. anterior, superior, and lateral","B. anterior, superior, and medial","C. posterior, inferior, and medial","D. posterior, superior, and lateral","E. posterior, superior, and medial"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"posterior, superior, and medial","a":"","why":"<b>E. Posterior, superior and medial.</b> The mnemonic is <b>BUM</b>: lumbar superior facets face <b>Back, Up and Medial</b> (cervical is also BUM; thoracic is BUL, back, up and lateral). The L1–L5 group curve in the stem does not change this anatomy.<br>A and B. Anterior: the inferior facets face forward.<br>C. Posterior, inferior, medial: superior facets face up, not down.<br>D. Posterior, superior, lateral: the thoracic pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":12,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":24,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":43,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":16,"label":"Sagittal facets: flexion–extension most, rotation least","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-9.jpg","cap":"Sagittal facets: flexion–extension greatest, rotation least, sidebending in between","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/ta-O097-27-1.png","cap":"Lumbar range chart: flexion largest, axial rotation smallest at 5°","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 27","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":359},{"src":"sl/O071-6.jpg","cap":"Lumbar motion test 1: patient bends forward, lordosis should flatten","where":"2026 week 12 Lab Lumbar Spine Diagnosis & HVLA, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"O069-42-1","s":"The orientation of the facets in the lumbar region most significantly permit which of the following?","o":["A. Flexion","B. Abduction","C. Sidebending","D. Rotation","E. Lateral translation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion","a":"","why":"<b>A. Flexion.</b> Lumbar facets lie close to the <b>sagittal plane</b> (superior facets face back, up and medial: BUM). Joint surfaces in that plane glide forward and back, so <b>flexion–extension</b> is the biggest lumbar motion; sidebending is in between and <b>rotation is the least</b>.<br>B. Abduction: a limb motion at the hip or shoulder, not a spinal motion.<br>C. Sidebending: moderate (about 20–25° per side), not the main motion.<br>D. Rotation: the smallest lumbar motion (about 5–7° per side) because sagittal facets block it.<br>E. Lateral translation: a small passive glide used in testing, not a main motion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":39,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":62,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":11,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-32-1","s":"The orientation of the facet in the lumbar region most significantly limits which of the following:","o":["A. Flexion","B. Abduction","C. Sidebending","D. Rotation","E. Translation"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rotation","a":"","why":"<b>D. Rotation.</b> Lumbar facets lie close to the <b>sagittal plane</b> and face each other, so they glide freely in flexion–extension but <b>jam against each other in rotation</b>. Rotation is the smallest lumbar motion, about 5–7° per side.<br>A. Flexion: the facets permit it most (40–50°).<br>B. Abduction: a limb motion, not a spinal one.<br>C. Sidebending: moderate, about 20–25° per side.<br>E. Translation: a small accessory glide, not what the facets mainly block.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":32,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":33,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":10,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O097-30-1","s":"The standing lumbar motion test will usually give the most amount of movement in:","o":["A. Rotation","B. Flexion","C. Extension","D. Sidebending","E. Compression"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion","a":"","why":"<b>B. Flexion.</b> In the standing lumbar motion test the patient <b>bends forward</b> while you palpate the lordosis, which should flatten. Flexion is the largest lumbar motion (<b>40–50°</b>) because the facets sit near the sagittal plane.<br>A. Rotation: the smallest lumbar motion, about 5–7° per side.<br>C. Extension: only 20–30°.<br>D. Sidebending: about 20–25° per side, between the two.<br>E. Compression: an axial load, not a motion you test.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":30,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":31,"f":"O098"}]},{"id":"O080-12-1","s":"A young male presents for a routine physical. His structural examination is unremarkable. You evaluate the patient's lumbar range of motion and note the main motion to be","o":["A. flexion and extension","B. rotation","C. sidebending","D. sidebending and rotation","E. translation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"flexion and extension","a":"","why":"<b>A. Flexion and extension.</b> The lumbar facets face mostly in the <b>sagittal plane</b>, so the <b>main lumbar motion is flexion–extension</b> (flexion about 40–50°, extension 20–30°).<br>B. Rotation: the least lumbar motion; the sagittal facets lock it.<br>C. Sidebending: moderate (about 20–25° per side), not the main motion.<br>D. Sidebending and rotation: coupled motions that follow Fryette's laws, but both are small here.<br>E. Translation: a small accessory glide, not a main motion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":12,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":221,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":14,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":26,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":17,"label":"Ferguson's angle: sacral base vs horizontal, 25–35°","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O099-15-1.png","cap":"Lumbosacral angle: a line along the sacral base meets a horizontal line","where":"Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 15","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":632},{"src":"sl/O068-10.jpg","cap":"Normal Ferguson's angle 25–35°; larger angle, more L/S shear","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/O069-90.jpg","cap":"Top-of-sacrum line versus horizontal; 45° or more is hyperlordotic","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 90"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-28-1","s":"What is the normal range for Ferguson’s Angle?","o":["A. 10-25%","B. 25-35%","C. 35-55%","D. 55-60%","E. 60-70%"],"g":["B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"35-55%","a":"","why":"<b>B. 25–35°.</b> The normal Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle is <b>25–35°</b>, measured between the <b>sacral base</b> and a <b>horizontal</b> line. A larger angle means more lordosis and more shear at L5–S1.<br>A. 10–25°: below normal, a flattened (hypolordotic) curve.<br>C. 35–55°: above normal; 45° or more is hyperlordotic.<br>D and E. 55–70°: far into hyperlordosis.","ex":"","fl":["The correct answer is B: the normal Ferguson's angle is 25–35° (the options show % but mean degrees)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":28,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":29,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":12,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-29-1","s":"A Ferguson’s angle greater than or equal to 45 degrees is indicative of _________. The Ferguson’s angle is an angle made by drawing a line parallel with the ________ and having it bisect with an imaginary ______ line.","o":["A. Hyperkyphosis, sacral base, horizontal line","B. Hyperlordosis, sacral base, vertical line","C. Hyperlordosis, L5, vertical line","D. Hyperkyphosis, L5, diagonal line","E. Hyperlordosis, sacral base, horizontal line"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hyperlordosis, sacral base, horizontal line","a":"","why":"<b>E. Hyperlordosis, sacral base, horizontal line.</b> Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle is drawn with one line along the <b>top of the sacrum (sacral base)</b> and one <b>horizontal</b> line. Normal is <b>25–35°</b>, so an angle of <b>45° or more is hyperlordotic</b>.<br>A and D. Hyperkyphosis: a big angle means more lumbar lordosis, not thoracic kyphosis.<br>B. Vertical line: the reference line is horizontal.<br>C. L5 and a vertical line: wrong on both lines; the sacral base is used.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":29,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":41,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O099-14-1","s":"A 65 year old woman comes to the office complaining of chronic low back pain. The patient states the pain began several years ago. You notice and increase in lumbar lordosis and measure her Ferguson’s angle. What is true of the Ferguson’s angle?","o":["A. The lumbosacral angle is normally between 15 and 35 degrees","B. An increase in shear stress can cause a decrease in lumbar lordosis","C. Shear forces cause the most degeneration at the lumbosacral angle causing an increase in lumbar lordosis","D. It is something only D.O.’s use to determine causes of back pain"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Shear forces cause the most degeneration at the lumbosacral angle causing an increase in lumbar lordosis","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> A <b>larger Ferguson's angle</b> means a steeper sacral base, more <b>lumbar lordosis</b> and more <b>shear force at the L/S (lumbosacral) junction</b>, the forces that cause the most degeneration there and back pain.<br>A. 15–35°: the normal is 25–35°.<br>B. Shear rises with more lordosis, not less.<br>D. Only DOs: it is a standard measurement on a standing lateral X-ray that any physician uses.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":14,"f":"O099"}]},{"id":"O111-45-1","s":"Which of the following angles is depicted in the image below?","o":["A. Q angle","B. Ferguson angle","C. Cobb angle","D. Carrying angle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferguson angle","a":"","why":"<b>B. Ferguson angle.</b> The drawing shows a line along the <b>top of the sacrum (sacral base)</b> meeting a <b>horizontal line</b>: Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle. Normal is 25–35°; this one is increased, with increased lumbar lordosis.<br>A. Q angle: at the knee, between the quadriceps line of pull and the patellar tendon.<br>C. Cobb angle: measures a scoliosis curve on a front-to-back X-ray.<br>D. Carrying angle: at the elbow, between the arm and the forearm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-45-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":45,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":17,"label":"Bigger Ferguson's angle means hyperlordosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O097-29-1.png","cap":"Steeper sacral base: increased lumbosacral angle with increased lumbar lordosis","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 29","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":590},{"src":"sl/O068-18.jpg","cap":"Curve B kypholordotic, curve C flattened: compare their Ferguson angles","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/O068-11.jpg","cap":"Pelvic rotation changes the lumbosacral angle: normal, steeper and flatter sacral bases","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-14-1","s":"Wearing high heels:","o":["A. increases the Ferguson angle","B. decreases the Ferguson angle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"increases the Ferguson angle","a":"","why":"<b>A. Increases it.</b> High heels tip the pelvis forward (anterior tilt). The sacral base gets steeper, so the <b>lumbosacral (Ferguson's) angle and the lumbar lordosis increase</b>. That is why patients with spondylolisthesis should avoid high heels.<br>B. Decreases it: a posterior pelvic tilt does this, as from the gluteus maximus, hamstrings or abdominal muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":14,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O068-18-1","s":"Look at curve B how has this changed the Ferguson angle and the A/P curves of the spine? Compare this to curve C. Which structures will be compressed?","o":["A. Angle decreased, curves flattened; anterior discs and bodies compressed, posterior ligaments stretched","B. Angle increased, curves deepened; anterior discs and bodies compressed, posterior ligaments stretched","C. Angle increased, curves deepened; spinous processes and facets compressed, anterior longitudinal ligament stretched","D. Angle decreased, curves flattened; spinous processes and facets compressed, anterior longitudinal ligament stretched"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Curve B increases the Ferguson (lumbosacral) angle and exaggerates the A/P curves (more lumbar lordosis and thoracic kyphosis), whereas curve C flattens them with a smaller angle. With B's hyperlordosis the posterior elements are compressed (spinous processes and zygapophyseal/facet joints) while the anterior longitudinal ligament is stretched.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Curve B is the <b>kypholordotic</b> posture: deeper lumbar lordosis and thoracic kyphosis and a steeper sacral base, so <b>Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle increases</b>. Curve C is the flattened posture with a smaller angle. In B's hyperlordosis the back of each segment closes: <b>spinous processes and zygapophyseal (facet) joints are compressed</b> and the <b>anterior longitudinal ligament is stretched</b>.<br>A. This describes curve C, the flattened posture.<br>B. Front compression with stretched posterior ligaments is the flexed, flat-back pattern, not lordosis.<br>D. Curve B's angle rises; a smaller angle with flat curves is curve C.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O068-18-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":18,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":14,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-92-1","s":"A 48-year-old female presents to your office with complaints of lower back pain. The pain has been progressive over the past 2 years. Physical exam reveals an abnormality of the A/P curve that is most significant at the lower lumbar spine. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the abnormal PE finding?","o":["A. A positive hip drop test","B. Loss of range of motion during side bending","C. Ferguson's angle being increased","D. Higher iliac crest on the left due to compensatory changes","E. None of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferguson's angle being increased","a":"","why":"<b>C. Increased Ferguson's angle.</b> Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle lies between a <b>horizontal line</b> and a line along the <b>top of the sacrum</b>. Normal is 25–35°. A bigger angle means lower lumbar <b>hyperlordosis</b>, an A/P (front-to-back) curve change, and more <b>shear at L5–S1</b>.<br>A. Positive hip drop test: restricted sidebending, a side-to-side finding.<br>B. Lost sidebending: also side-to-side, not an A/P curve.<br>D. Higher iliac crest: a coronal asymmetry.<br>E. None of the above: C explains it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":92,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":95,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":60,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":10,"f":"O104"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":18,"label":"Psoas, quads deepen lordosis; glutes, hamstrings flatten","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-39-1.png","cap":"Psoas and quadriceps increase lordosis; gluteals, hamstrings, abdominals flatten it","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 39","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":2048},{"src":"sl/O069-66.jpg","cap":"Shortened psoas pulls the lumbar spine into extension and tips the pelvis forward","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-24-1","s":"Gluteus maximus m. Let try contracting this muscle. How will this effect the L/S angle?","o":["A. Decreases it: pulls the pelvis posteriorly and reduces lordosis","B. Increases it: pulls the upper lumbar vertebrae forward","C. No change: it acts only on the hip, not the pelvis","D. Increases it: tilts the pelvis anteriorly and deepens lordosis"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"It decreases the lumbosacral (Ferguson) angle: gluteus maximus pulls the pelvis posteriorly (posterior tilt) and reduces lumbar lordosis.","a":"","why":"<b>A. It decreases the L/S (lumbosacral, Ferguson's) angle.</b> Gluteus maximus and the hamstrings <b>pull the pelvis posteriorly</b> (posterior tilt), the sacral base levels out and the lumbar spine becomes <b>hypolordotic</b>.<br>B. Pulling the upper lumbar vertebrae forward: the psoas, which increases lordosis (think arching the back).<br>C. No change: wrong; gluteus maximus arises from the ilium and sacrum, so it moves the pelvis and the angle.<br>D. Anterior tilt with more lordosis: what a hypertonic quadriceps (rectus femoris) does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":24,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-35-1","s":"A patient presents to your office complaining of muscle tightness. On examination, you determine a Ferguson’s angle of 45°. Which of the following active muscles might be causing the lordotic curve you notice in the patient? Select all that apply.","o":["A. Quadriceps","B. Rectus abdominus","C. Gluteus maximus","D. Psoas muscle","E. Hamstrings"],"g":["A","D"],"k":["A","D"],"rv":false,"at":"Quadriceps; Psoas muscle","a":"","why":"<b>A and D. Quadriceps and psoas.</b> A Ferguson's angle of 45° means <b>hyperlordosis</b>. The lordosis-raisers: the <b>psoas</b> pulls the upper lumbar vertebrae forward, and <b>hypertonic quadriceps</b> (rectus femoris, from the anterior inferior iliac spine) tilt the pelvis forward.<br>B. Rectus abdominis: the abdominals flatten lordosis.<br>C. Gluteus maximus and E. Hamstrings: pull the pelvis posteriorly and reduce lordosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":35,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O099-16-1","s":"An instagram famous model comes into your office complaining that, “his back looks weird.” You inquire about his workout routine and find out he does 5000 crunches a day to maintain his sweet 8 pack. What muscle would you recommend he works out to reduce his flattened back?","o":["A. Psoas","B. Gluteus Maximus","C. Hamstrings","D. Pectoralis Major"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Psoas","a":"","why":"<b>A. Psoas.</b> Thousands of crunches build the <b>abdominals, which flatten lordosis</b>. To bring the curve back he needs a muscle that <b>increases lordosis</b>: the psoas pulls the upper lumbar vertebrae forward (think arching the back).<br>B. Gluteus maximus and C. Hamstrings: both pull the pelvis posteriorly and flatten the curve further.<br>D. Pectoralis major: a chest and arm muscle with no effect on the lumbar curve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":16,"f":"O099"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-37-1","s":"A patient comes in to your clinic after doing 1,000,000 squats for one dollar a squat. The patient informs you that money is not an issue, and that her legs and back are killing her. She says that her leg hurts right “there” when you are palpating the rectus femoris. After completing your examination, you note extremely hypertonic quadricep muscles. Which of the following would you expect to find on osteopathic examination?","o":["A. A hypertonic quadratus lumborum","B. An increased ferguson angle","C. A decreased ferguson angle","D. A hypolordotic lumbar spine","E. A hyperlordotic lumbar spine","F. E and B are correct","G. C and D are correct"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"E and B are correct","a":"","why":"<b>F. B and E: increased Ferguson's angle and a hyperlordotic spine.</b> Hypertonic <b>quadriceps</b> (rectus femoris pulls on the anterior inferior iliac spine) <b>tilt the pelvis anteriorly</b>, which steepens the sacral base and <b>deepens lordosis</b>.<br>A. Hypertonic quadratus lumborum: sidebends the trunk and hikes the hip; the quads do not cause it.<br>B and E alone: each is true, but F names both.<br>C, D and G. Decreased angle, hypolordosis: the effect of gluteus maximus and hamstrings.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":37,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":19,"label":"Long erector spinae type I; short rotatores type II","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-36-1.png","cap":"Long erector spinae columns beside short rotatores and intertransversarii","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 36","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":900},{"src":"sl/O079-19.jpg","cap":"Rotatores and multifidus span one or two levels: single-segment control","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/O068-34.jpg","cap":"Multifidi and rotatores rotate individual vertebrae","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-31-1","s":"Erector spinae group (spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis): bilateral contraction extends the L-spine; unilateral contraction causes ipsilateral extension and side bending. What type of somatic dysfunction do these muscles cause?","o":["A. Type II group curve: three or more segments rotated into the concavity","B. Pure flexion of one segment with no rotation or sidebending","C. Type II: single flexed or extended segment, rotation and sidebending same side","D. Type I: neutral group curve, sidebending and rotation to opposite sides"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Type 1 (neutral, group-curve) somatic dysfunction.","a":"","why":"<b>D. Type I (neutral) somatic dysfunction (SD).</b> The erector spinae are <b>long muscles spanning many segments</b>. Their hypertonia pulls a <b>group</b> of vertebrae into a sidebent curve, and in neutral the group rotates the opposite way (Fryette's first principle; e.g. L1–4 NSlRr = neutral, sidebent left, rotated right).<br>A. Type II is a single segment, never a group; a group curve is type I.<br>B. Pure one-plane flexion: not the pattern of a long sidebending muscle.<br>C. Single-segment type II: from <b>short deep muscles</b> (multifidus, rotatores) that control one vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":31,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O068-33-1","s":"Multifidus and rotatores (small, deep, postural back muscles that control individual vertebrae): what type of somatic dysfunction would you see as a result of hypertonia of these muscles?","o":["A. Single-segment type II, rotation and sidebending to the same side","B. Flexion of the entire region with loss of lordosis","C. Type I group curve, sidebending and rotation to opposite sides","D. Type I curve of the whole lumbar spine with sacral torsion"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Single-segment (segmental) somatic dysfunction, i.e. type II / non-neutral dysfunction of an individual vertebra.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Single-segment (segmental) type II somatic dysfunction.</b> <b>Short, deep muscles</b> drive <b>segmental</b> dysfunction because they get innervation from the fewest spinal segments and control one vertebra. A non-neutral (flexed or extended) segment rotates and sidebends to the <b>same side</b> (Fryette's second principle).<br>B. Regional flexion: multifidus actually extends locally.<br>C. Type I group curve: from long muscles such as the erector spinae.<br>D. A whole-region curve with sacral torsion: a long-restrictor pattern, not a one-segment muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":33,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-84-1","s":"Which of the following muscles are most likely to maintain a Type II somatic dysfunction?","o":["A. Spinalis","B. Iliocostalis","C. Longissimus","D. Rotatores brevis","E. Quadratus lumborum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rotatores brevis","a":"","why":"<b>D. Rotatores brevis.</b> Short, deep muscles (rotatores, multifidus, intertransversarii) span <b>one or two segments</b>, control single vertebrae and get innervation from the fewest spinal segments, so their hypertonia holds a <b>single-segment type II</b> (non-neutral) somatic dysfunction. Rotatores brevis cross just one level.<br>A. Spinalis, B. Iliocostalis, C. Longissimus: the three <b>erector spinae</b> columns are long, multi-segment muscles; they make <b>type I group</b> curves.<br>E. Quadratus lumborum: a long muscle from the iliac crest to rib 12 and the L1–L4 transverse processes; it sidebends a group (type I).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":118,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":128,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":75,"f":"O108"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":30,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":177,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-34-1","s":"A muscle deep to the spinalis became extremely hypertonic. You remember from anatomy lab that this muscle is considered an intrinsic muscle of the back, however, you cannot remember the name. What is the name of this muscle and what type of somatic dysfunction may this hypertonicity cause?","o":["A. Longissimus, Type 1","B. Multifidus, Type 2","C. Latissimus dorsi, Type 2","D. Serratus posterior, Type 2","E. Iliocostalis, Type 1"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Multifidus, Type 2","a":"","why":"<b>B. Multifidus, type II.</b> The <b>multifidus</b> lies <b>deep to the erector spinae</b> (spinalis is their medial column). It is a short, deep muscle that controls single vertebrae, so its hypertonia holds a <b>single-segment type II</b> (non-neutral) somatic dysfunction (SD).<br>A. Longissimus, type I: correctly paired, but it is an erector spinae column beside spinalis, not deep to it.<br>C. Latissimus dorsi and D. Serratus posterior: superficial (extrinsic) back muscles, not segment controllers.<br>E. Iliocostalis, type I: correctly paired, but it is the lateral erector spinae column.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":34,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":35,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":20,"label":"ALL limits extension; posterior ligaments limit flexion","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-48-1.png","cap":"ALL broad, limits extension; PLL narrow, limits flexion; interspinous and supraspinous ligaments","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 48","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":960},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-42-2.png","cap":"Side view: ALL down the front; interspinous and supraspinous ligaments behind","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":631}],"q":[{"id":"O095-46-1","s":"Your partner missed your question and scoffed! The nerve of your partner! They fire back with a final question for all the marbles. They ask you “which spinal ligament limits flexion of the spine?” You scoff back and ask “is this the first anatomy exam all over again? The answer is obviously _______!”","o":["A. Intraspinal ligament","B. Supraspinous ligament","C. Posterior longitudinal ligament","D. Anterior longitudinal ligament","E. Lumbosacral ligament"],"g":["A","B","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior longitudinal ligament","a":"","why":"<b>A, B and C.</b> Every ligament <b>behind the disc</b> is stretched when you bend forward, so all three limit flexion: the <b>posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL)</b>, the <b>interspinous</b> ('intraspinal') and the <b>supraspinous</b> ligaments. <b>PLL = narrow, limits flexion</b>, while <b>ALL (anterior longitudinal ligament) = broad, limits extension</b>; on a PLL-versus-ALL question, PLL is the flexion answer.<br>D. ALL: in front of the vertebral bodies; it limits extension.<br>E. Lumbosacral ligament: from the L5 transverse process to the sacral ala; it anchors L5, it is not a flexion check.","ex":"","fl":["A and B are also correct, not only C (posterior longitudinal ligament): the interspinous ('intraspinal') and supraspinous ligaments also lie behind the disc and are stretched by flexion, so A, B and C all limit it."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":46,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O099-6-1","s":"What anatomical structure prevents most of us from being able to perform the motion pictured below, thus landing that  guy a sweet gig in an old-timey circus?","o":["A. Posterior longitudinal ligament","B. Anterior longitudinal ligament","C. Intervertebral disc","D. Vertebral transverse processes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior longitudinal ligament","a":"","why":"<b>B. Anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL).</b> The contortionist is in extreme <b>hyperextension</b>. The ALL runs down the front of the vertebral bodies; it is <b>broad and limits extension</b>, so it stops most of us from bending back this far.<br>A. Posterior longitudinal ligament: narrow, behind the bodies; it limits flexion and goes slack in extension.<br>C. Intervertebral disc: it lets the segment move; extension just squeezes its back part.<br>D. Transverse processes: lateral levers for muscles; they do not block extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O099-6-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":6,"f":"O099"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":20,"label":"Midline spinous tenderness: possible disc","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-42-2.png","cap":"Interspinous and supraspinous ligaments lie between spinous processes, behind the discs","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":631},{"src":"sl/O068-36.jpg","cap":"Multifidus triangle map: interspinous ligament (ISL) over the L4–L5 level","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-20-1","s":"Segmental tenderness to palpation of the spinous process and interspinous ligament: a positive test would give you what information?","o":["A. Iliolumbar ligament strain at L4–L5","B. A hypertonic psoas pulling on the segment","C. Sacroiliac joint somatic dysfunction","D. Facet tropism at that level","E. A possible disc problem at that segment"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Possible disc issue at that segment.","a":"","why":"<b>E. Possible disc problem.</b> Tenderness over one <b>spinous process and its interspinous ligament</b> points to a possible <b>intervertebral disc</b> issue at that segment. (Pain on percussion of a spinous process is a red flag for fracture or infection.)<br>A. Iliolumbar ligament strain: tender lateral to the spine, between the L4–L5 transverse processes and iliac crest.<br>B. Psoas: lies anterior; checked with the Thomas test.<br>C. Sacroiliac joint: tender over the sacral sulcus beside the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine), not the midline.<br>D. Facet tropism: an anatomic facet asymmetry, not a tenderness finding.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":20,"f":"O068"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":21,"label":"Multifidus triangle: what lies inside","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-42-1.png","cap":"Multifidus triangle holds iliolumbar ligament, multifidus, SI ligaments; QL lies above","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":908},{"src":"sl/ta-O094-3-1.png","cap":"Triangle map: interspinous ligament midline, iliolumbar ligament lateral, L4–L5 discs, SI joint","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 3","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1084}],"q":[{"id":"O096-37-1","s":"Your friend comes to visit and tells you they have been experiencing some lower back pain. Knowing that the multifidus triangle might be involved, you explain that all of the following could be causes except for:","o":["A. Problem with the intertransverse ligaments","B. Ruptured L4 disc","C. Problem with the sacroiliac joint","D. Facet syndrome","E. Problem with the quadratus lumborum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Problem with the quadratus lumborum","a":"","why":"<b>E. Quadratus lumborum (QL).</b> The multifidus triangle is formed by the <b>L4 spinous process, the iliac crest and the tip of the sacrum</b>. It holds the multifidi, the most often ruptured discs (L4, L5), intertransverse ligaments, iliolumbar ligaments, facet syndromes and the sacroiliac (SI) joint. The QL runs above it, from iliac crest to rib 12.<br>A. Intertransverse ligaments, B. L4 disc, C. SI joint, D. Facet syndrome: all found in the triangle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":37,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O095-40-1","s":"You are studying for your upcoming practical with a friend and state “OK I am going to begin palpating structures found in your multifidus triangle.” Your friend sensed the nervousness in your voice and said “At its most lateral attachment, which ligament is found most medially in the multifidus triangle?” Annoyed by your friend who is trying to be a future anatomy TA, you tell them that the ligament they are referring to is:","o":["A. Iliolumbar ligament","B. Sacroiliac ligament","C. Interspinous ligament","D. Lumbosacral ligament","E. Ask your friend to relax because it’s the first time you looked at the rubric since your last lab"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Interspinous ligament","a":"","why":"<b>C. Interspinous ligament.</b> Of these, only the <b>interspinous ligament</b> sits in the <b>midline</b>, between neighboring spinous processes (ISL at L4–L5 on the multifidus-triangle map). Tenderness over it suggests a disc problem at that level.<br>A. Iliolumbar ligament: lateral, from the L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crest.<br>B. Sacroiliac ligaments: at the lateral edges of the sacrum.<br>D. Lumbosacral ligament: lateral, from the L5 transverse process to the sacral ala.<br>E. A joke choice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":40,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O111-49-1","s":"You are evaluating a 29-year-old pregnant patient who has been complaining of low back pain during her pregnancy. She denies any radiation of pain down her legs, or any numbness/tingling, or weakness associated with the pain. She describes the pain as encompassing the area from the top of her waist (pointing to her iliac crests) bilaterally and extending to her tailbone (sacrum). What is the name of the anatomic region where she describes her pain?","o":["A. Thoracic inlet","B. Multifidus triangle","C. Sacral plexus","D. Anatomic snuffbox"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Multifidus triangle","a":"","why":"<b>B. Multifidus triangle.</b> Its corners are the <b>L4 spinous process and the iliac crests</b> above and the <b>tip of the sacrum</b> below, the area she outlines. Most low back problems arise here: multifidi, L4 and L5 discs, intertransverse and iliolumbar ligaments, facets and the sacroiliac joint.<br>A. Thoracic inlet: the top opening of the chest, at the first ribs.<br>C. Sacral plexus: a nerve network inside the pelvis, not a surface region.<br>D. Anatomic snuffbox: at the wrist, over the scaphoid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":49,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":21,"label":"Iliolumbar ligament: first strained in lumbosacral imbalance","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-43-1.png","cap":"Iliolumbar ligaments circled: L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crests","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 43","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1000},{"src":"sl/O069-48.jpg","cap":"Iliolumbar ligament stabilizes L5 and blends with the anterior SI ligament","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-60-1.png","cap":"A diseased disc compresses more than a normal disc under the same load","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 60","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":805}],"q":[{"id":"O092-1-1","s":"Which of the following ligaments is the first to become tender when there is lumbosacral postural decompensation?","o":["A. Annular","B. Posterior longitudinal","C. Anterior longitudinal","D. Sacrospinous","E. Iliolumbar"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliolumbar","a":"","why":"<b>E. Iliolumbar.</b> The iliolumbar ligament runs from the <b>L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crest</b> and stabilizes L5 on the pelvis. It is the <b>first ligament to become tender (strained) in lumbosacral postural decompensation</b>.<br>A. Annular: the annulus fibrosus is part of the disc, not the first ligament strained.<br>B. Posterior longitudinal: limits flexion; tied to disc problems, not postural strain.<br>C. Anterior longitudinal: limits extension.<br>D. Sacrospinous: a pelvic ligament from the sacrum to the ischial spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":31,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":119,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":130,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-39-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding the lumbar spine?","o":["A. Extension increases pressure on the posterior annulus and the posterior longitudinal ligament","B. A diseased lumbar disc can be less compressible than a healthy disc","C. Iliolumbar ligament is the first ligament to be strained in a lumbosacral imbalance","D. The anterior longitudinal ligament is broad and limits flexion","E. All of the above are true"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliolumbar ligament is the first ligament to be strained in a lumbosacral imbalance","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>iliolumbar ligament is the first ligament strained</b> in a lumbosacral imbalance.<br>A. False: <b>flexion</b> loads the posterior annulus and posterior longitudinal ligament; extension loads the anterior annulus and anterior longitudinal ligament.<br>B. False: a diseased or aging disc is <b>more</b> compressible (and its annulus tears), which shifts weight onto the facets.<br>D. False: the anterior longitudinal ligament is broad and limits <b>extension</b>.<br>E. All of the above: only C is true.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":39,"f":"O096"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":21,"label":"Iliolumbar irritation: spring test, groin pain, mimics SI","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-45-1.png","cap":"Iliolumbar ligament: spring test, refers to the groin, mimics SI joint dysfunction","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 45","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":960},{"src":"sl/O068-45.jpg","cap":"Iliolumbar syndrome: groin referral, tenderness, same-side lumbosacral or SI dysfunction","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-42-1.png","cap":"The iliolumbar ligament lies inside the multifidus triangle","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":908}],"q":[{"id":"O068-42-1","s":"Iliolumbar ligament (iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS to the transverse processes of L4 and L5; first ligament to be strained in a lumbosacral imbalance). How would you know the ligament is irritated and the cause of pain in your patient?","o":["A. Spring test over the ligament reproduces the tenderness","B. Thomas test shows the knee lifting off the table","C. Hip drop test shows less than 20° of iliac crest drop","D. Straight leg raise reproduces leg pain at 30–70°"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Spring test (pain reproduced on springing; the ligament is tender to palpation).","a":"","why":"<b>A. Spring test.</b> You locate the ligament between the <b>L4–L5 transverse processes and the iliac crest</b> and spring it on both sides; reproduced <b>tenderness</b> marks it as the cause of the pain. It is the <b>first ligament strained</b> in a lumbosacral imbalance.<br>B. Thomas test: the knee lifting off the table means a tight iliopsoas.<br>C. Hip drop test: screens lumbar sidebending; a drop under 20° means lumbar somatic dysfunction.<br>D. Straight leg raise: radiating pain at 30–70° means L5/S1 nerve-root irritation, as from a disc.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O068-42-2","s":"Iliolumbar ligament (iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS to the transverse processes of L4 and L5). Where does the irritation of this ligament refer pain to?","o":["A. Anteriorly into the groin on the same side","B. To the arm and shoulder, with dysesthesias","C. Down the posterior thigh and calf to the heel","D. To the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Anteriorly to the groin area (on the same side).","a":"","why":"<b>A. The groin, same side.</b> The iliolumbar ligament runs from the <b>L4–L5 transverse processes</b> to the iliac crest just above and lateral to the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine). Palpating an irritated ligament often <b>refers pain anteriorly into the ipsilateral groin</b>.<br>B. Arm and shoulder dysesthesias: the referral pattern of latissimus dorsi.<br>C. Posterior thigh and calf to the heel: an S1 nerve-root (sciatic) pattern.<br>D. Lateral leg and dorsum of the foot: the L5 dermatome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":43,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O068-42-3","s":"Iliolumbar ligament irritation: what condition will it mimic?","o":["A. L5 radiculopathy from a disc herniation","B. Sacroiliac joint somatic dysfunction or inflammation","C. Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","D. Cauda equina syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"SI joint somatic dysfunction or inflammation.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Sacroiliac (SI) joint somatic dysfunction or inflammation.</b> The iliolumbar ligament attaches to the iliac crest beside the PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine) and <b>blends with the anterior SI ligament</b>, so its pain sits over the SI region. Same-side lumbosacral and SI dysfunction often come with it.<br>A. L5 radiculopathy: dermatomal pain to the dorsal foot with weak big-toe extension.<br>C. Spinal stenosis: elderly patient, leg pain on walking or standing, relieved by bending forward.<br>D. Cauda equina syndrome: saddle anesthesia and bladder or bowel dysfunction, a surgical emergency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-20-1","s":"43 y/o female patient comes to the office complaining about low back pain. On palpating the multifidus triangle, she says that she has pain in the groin. [Which is the most likely diagnosis?]","o":["A. Piriformis syndrome","B. Iliopsoas syndrome","C. Iliolumbar ligament syndrome","D. Hamstring syndrome","E. Quadratus lumborum syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliolumbar ligament syndrome","a":"","why":"<b>C. Iliolumbar ligament syndrome.</b> The iliolumbar ligament lies inside the <b>multifidus triangle</b> (L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crest). Palpating an irritated ligament <b>refers pain to the groin on the same side</b>, the classic clue.<br>A. Piriformis syndrome: buttock pain down the back of the thigh, worse after sitting.<br>B. Iliopsoas (psoas) syndrome: flexed posture, pain to the anterior thigh, positive Thomas test.<br>D. Hamstring syndrome: posterior thigh pain.<br>E. Quadratus lumborum syndrome: pain along the iliac crest, hip and buttock.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":20,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O111-51-1","s":"A 23-year-old hockey player presents for left-sided low back pain that started after a hip check during a game last week. He describes the pain radiating to his left groin when most exacerbated, but not down his leg. He denies any lower extremity numbness or weakness, or any bladder or bowel changes since the injury. Which of the following ligamentous structures is likely involved in his pain?","o":["A. Iliolumbar ligament","B. Anterior longitudinal ligament","C. Intraspinous ligament","D. Posterior longitudinal ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliolumbar ligament","a":"","why":"<b>A. Iliolumbar ligament.</b> It runs from the <b>L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crest</b>, is the first ligament strained in a lumbosacral imbalance, and when irritated <b>refers pain anteriorly into the same-side groin</b>, with no leg radiation or neurologic signs.<br>B. Anterior longitudinal ligament: front of the vertebral bodies; limits extension.<br>C. Interspinous ('intraspinal') ligament: midline between spinous processes; tenderness suggests a disc problem.<br>D. Posterior longitudinal ligament: behind the bodies; limits flexion and guards the disc.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":51,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":22,"label":"Fryette's three principles","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-61.jpg","cap":"Fryette II: non-neutral spine, rotation precedes sidebending, both to the same side","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 61"},{"src":"sl/O068-63.jpg","cap":"Fryette I: neutral spine, sidebending precedes rotation to the opposite side","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/O068-66.jpg","cap":"Fryette III: motion in one plane reduces motion in the other planes","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-45-1","s":"How does Fryette's second principle describe spinal motion?","o":["A. Side bending and rotation occur to the same side in a neutral spine.","B. Side bending and rotation to opposite sides happen in a flexed or extended spine.","C. Rotation and side bending are independent in a neutral spine.","D. Side bending is followed by rotation to the same side in a flexed or extended spine"],"g":["D"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Side bending and rotation to opposite sides happen in a flexed or extended spine.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> Fryette's <b>second principle (type II)</b>: in a <b>non-neutral spine (flexed or extended)</b>, rotation and sidebending occur to the <b>same side</b>. Rotation comes first; D is still the only choice with same side plus a flexed or extended spine.<br>A. Same side in a neutral spine: neutral is type I, opposite sides.<br>B. Opposite sides is type I, which needs a neutral spine.<br>C. Independent: in neutral they are coupled; sidebending comes first, with rotation to the opposite side.","ex":"","fl":["The correct answer is D: in a non-neutral (flexed or extended) spine, rotation and sidebending go to the same side; opposite sides (B) is type I."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":45,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-49-1","s":"You kicked that old partner to the curb because you deserve better. You start practicing OMM with a new, perhaps even better, partner. This new partner was confused on Freyette’s third principle. In layman's terms, you explained that:","o":["A. Freyette’s third principle just means type I curves side bend before they rotate to the opposite side","B. Freyette’s third principle just means that type II somatic dysfunctions occur when sidebending is introduced into a region of the spine that is either flexed or extended.","C. Freyette’s third principle just means that if any motion is induced into a spinal segment, all other motions are reduced"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Freyette’s third principle just means that if any motion is induced into a spinal segment, all other motions are reduced","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Fryette's <b>third principle</b>: motion in one plane <b>changes (reduces) motion in all the other planes</b>. For example, a flexed vertebral unit has less sidebending and rotation. It applies to both type I and type II.<br>A. Sidebending before rotation to the opposite side: the <b>first</b> principle (type I, neutral).<br>B. Type II from sidebending a flexed or extended spine: the <b>second</b> principle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":49,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":22,"label":"Type II: restrictions are opposite the freedoms","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-64.jpg","cap":"Non-neutral RrSr segment rotates into the concavity; neutral mechanics above and below","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 64"},{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Rotation named for the vertebral body: right TP posterior, spinous process left","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-62-1","s":"Diagram of a non-neutral RRSR vertebra (body rotated right, right transverse process posterior, spinous process deviated left). Restricted motion?","o":["A. Rotation left and sidebending left","B. Rotation left and sidebending right","C. Rotation right and sidebending right","D. Rotation right and sidebending left"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Left rotation and left sidebending (plus the opposite of its flexion/extension component); somatic dysfunction is named for its freedoms, so the restrictions are the opposite directions.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Rotation left and sidebending left.</b> Somatic dysfunction is <b>named for its freedoms</b>, the position it moves into easily. A non-neutral RRSR segment rotates and sidebends <b>right</b> freely (type II: same side), so its <b>barriers are the opposites</b>: left rotation and left sidebending, plus the opposite of its flexion or extension component.<br>C. Right rotation and right sidebending: these are the freedoms.<br>C and D. Mixed-side pairs: opposite-side coupling is type I (neutral); a non-neutral segment never does it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":62,"f":"O068"}]},{"id":"O069-117-1","s":"A 31-year-old female presents with complaints of lower back pain. The pain started yesterday when she bent forwards and to the right to pick up her 9-month-old son who was playing on the floor. Which of the following motions are most likely to be found restricted on physical examination:","o":["A. Flexion, rotation left, sidebending left","B. Flexion, rotation right, sidebending right","C. Extension, rotation left, sidebending left","D. Extension, rotation right, sidebending right","E. Neutral, rotation right, side bending left"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Extension, rotation left, sidebending left","a":"","why":"<b>C. Extension, rotation left, sidebending left.</b> She bent <b>forward and to the right</b>, so the segment stuck <b>flexed, rotated right, sidebent right</b>: a type II (non-neutral) FRSR. In type II, rotation and sidebending (SB) go to the same side. Somatic dysfunction is named for its freedoms, so the <b>restrictions are the opposites</b>.<br>A. Flexion, left rotation, left SB: the barriers of an ERSR (extended, rotated and sidebent right); flexion is free here.<br>B. Flexion, right rotation, right SB: these are her freedoms.<br>D. Extension, right rotation, right SB: right motions are free.<br>E. Neutral with opposite-side rotation: a type I group pattern, not this single-segment flexion injury.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":117,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":128,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":66,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":13,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":31,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":23,"label":"Neutral group: posterior TP marks rotation, SB opposite","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Right rotation: right transverse process goes posterior, spinous process swings left","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/O068-65.jpg","cap":"Neutral SrRl: sidebending and rotation to opposite sides","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 65"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-33-1.png","cap":"Iliac crests: the landmark for the L4 level","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 33","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1920}],"q":[{"id":"O096-140-1","s":"You are told to diagnose the lumbar spine. You find L1 by going inferior to transverse process located by the ____ rib or you can find ____ by palpating the iliac crest and following it posteriorly. You feel that L1-L5 transverse process is more posterior on the right side. Your diagnosis is:","o":["A. 10th rib, L4 , L1-L5 N sRrL","B. 12th rib, L4, L1-L5 N sLrR","C. 10th rib, L5, L1-L5 N sLrL","D. 12th rib, L5 , L1-L5 N sRrR"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"12th rib, L4, L1-L5 N sLrR","a":"","why":"<b>B. 12th rib, L4, L1–L5 N SlRr.</b> The <b>L1 transverse process (TP) lies just below the 12th rib</b>, and the <b>iliac crest marks L4</b>. Right TPs posterior from L1 to L5 means a <b>group rotated right</b>; a group is neutral (type I), so it is <b>sidebent left</b> (N SlRr: neutral, sidebent left, rotated right).<br>A. 10th rib, and sidebent right, rotated left: wrong rib and the mirror diagnosis.<br>C. 10th rib, L5, same-side coupling: wrong landmarks, and a neutral group never pairs same sides.<br>D. L5 at the crest, N SrRr: the crest marks L4, and neutral needs opposite sides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":140,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O092-10-1","s":"A 55-year-old man presents to the office with chronic low back pain. The pain began shortly after he started work as an accountant several years ago. His job requires prolonged sitting at a desk. Osteopathic examination reveals: ropy tissue texture changes in the L2–L4 region; L3 left transverse process is posteriorly displaced; L3 left transverse process does not change with lumbar flexion or extension. The most likely diagnosis is","o":["A. L2–L4 flexed, rotated left, sidebent right","B. L2–L4 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right","C. L2–L4 neutral, rotated right, sidebent left","D. L3 neutral, rotated right, sidebent left","E. L3 extended, rotated right, sidebent right"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"L2–L4 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right","a":"","why":"<b>B. L2–L4 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right.</b> The <b>left transverse process (TP) of L3 is posterior</b>, so the group is rotated left. It <b>does not change with flexion or extension</b>, so it is neutral (type I), and the ropy tissue spans L2–L4, a group. Type I: sidebending goes the <b>opposite</b> way, right.<br>A. Flexed: a non-neutral finding would change with flexion or extension.<br>C. Rotated right: the left TP is posterior, so rotation is left.<br>D. Single L3, rotated right: the finding is a group, and rotation is left.<br>E. Extended, same side: type II, which would change with flexion or extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":23,"label":"Hip drop test: crest drops 20–25°, spine bends away","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-65-1.png","cap":"Hip drop: bend one knee, crest drops 20–25°, spine sidebends away","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 65","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":796},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-105-1.png","cap":"Left hip drop test: the lumbar spine sidebends right","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 105","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1384},{"src":"sl/O069-58.jpg","cap":"Right and left hip drops: 20–25° crest drop, positive criteria","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-65-1","s":"Recall that the hip drop test is a screening test to determine the thoracolumbar spine’s ability to side bend. How many degrees does the patient bend their knee to induce the side bending?","o":["A. The knee is bent 20–25°; the iliac crest drop is not measured","B. No set knee angle: the patient unlocks one knee; the iliac crest should drop 20–25°","C. The knee is bent 90°; the iliac crest should drop about 45°","D. The knee is bent 45°; the iliac crest should drop about 5–10°"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"20 to 25º","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> No knee angle is set: the standing patient simply <b>unlocks one knee without lifting the heel</b>. The number to know is the <b>iliac crest drop of 20–25°</b> on the bent-knee side, with a <b>smooth lumbar sidebending curve away</b> from that side. Less drop, or a non-smooth curve, is a positive test.<br>A. 20–25° belongs to the crest drop, not the knee.<br>C. A 90° knee bend and 45° drop: far beyond the test; the drop is 20–25°.<br>D. A 5–10° drop would be a positive (restricted) test, not the normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":65,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O104-27-1","s":"A patient has a positive hip drop on the right. The patient’s lumbar spine will freely sidebend to the:","o":["A. Right","B. Left","C. Both sides equally"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right","a":"","why":"<b>A. Right.</b> A <b>right hip drop</b> makes the lumbar spine sidebend <b>left</b> (away from the dropped side). <b>Positive on the right</b> means the right crest drops less, so <b>left sidebending is restricted</b>. The spine sits in, and moves freely into, <b>right sidebending</b>: the positive side is the side it is sidebent toward.<br>B. Left: that is the restricted direction.<br>C. Both equally: a positive test means one direction is restricted.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":27,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":126,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":64,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":116,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":122,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":23,"label":"Positive hip drop: spine sidebent toward that side","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-107-1.png","cap":"L1–5 N SrRl spine: positive hip drop expected on the right","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 107","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1404},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-127-1.png","cap":"Tight left QL holds the left ilium up: left hip drop is the barrier","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 127","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1057},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-193-1.png","cap":"Same tight left QL: right hip drop is free (left lumbar sidebending)","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 193","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1458}],"q":[{"id":"O069-59-1","s":"What are the results of the Hip Drop Test in a patient whose had the diagnosis of L1-5NSrRL?","o":["A. Positive on the right","B. Positive on the left","C. Normal","D. Patient will be unable to perform the test due to low back restriction."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Positive on the right","a":"","why":"<b>A. Positive on the right.</b> In the hip drop test the patient bends one knee; that iliac crest should drop <b>20–25°</b> and the lumbar spine should <b>sidebend away</b> from the dropped side. So a right hip drop tests <b>left sidebending</b>. L1–5 NSrRL is a neutral (type I) group <b>sidebent right</b>, rotated left, so it resists left sidebending and the right crest drops less. <b>Positive is the side that drops less.</b><br>B. A left drop tests right sidebending, which this spine does freely.<br>C. Normal: no, one direction is restricted.<br>D. Unable to perform: the test still works; it is just abnormal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":59,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":58,"f":"O107"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":17,"f":"O104"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":28,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":114,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":124,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2021 lumbar review (SGA)","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Fall 2021","sl":8,"f":"O105"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-106-1","s":"In a patient with a positive hip drop test on the right, in which direction would the patient be side bent and rotated towards? (assume it is a group curve)","o":["A. Side bent Left, Rotated Left","B. Side bent Right, Rotated Right","C. Side bent Left, Rotated Right","D. Side bent Right, Rotated Left"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Side bent Right, Rotated Left","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sidebent right, rotated left.</b> Positive right hip drop: the right crest drops less because <b>left sidebending is restricted</b>, so the spine is <b>sidebent right</b> (often a tight right QL, quadratus lumborum, holding the right ilium up). A group curve follows type I (neutral) mechanics: <b>rotation opposite</b> to sidebending, so rotated left (N SrRl).<br>A. Sidebent left, rotated left: wrong side, and same-side coupling is type II.<br>B. Sidebent right, rotated right: same side is type II, not a group.<br>C. Sidebent left, rotated right: the result of a positive LEFT hip drop.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":106,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":22,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O099-10-1","s":"While dancing and moving your hips, you noticed that you can’t drop that hip on the left. What else would you notice?","o":["A. Positive hip drop test on the right","B. Hypertonic right QL","C. Diagnosis L1-L5 N SrRl","D. Lumbar Convexity on the right","E. Restriction in lumbar sidebending left"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar Convexity on the right","a":"","why":"<b>D. Lumbar convexity on the right.</b> If the <b>left hip cannot drop</b>, the hip drop test is <b>positive on the left</b>: right sidebending is restricted, so the spine lives in <b>left sidebending</b>. Sidebent left means concave left, <b>convex right</b>.<br>A. Positive on the right: positive is the side that drops less, the left.<br>B. Hypertonic right QL (quadratus lumborum): a tight <b>left</b> QL holds the left ilium up.<br>C. L1–L5 N SrRl: that spine is sidebent right; this one is N SlRr.<br>E. Restricted left sidebending: the restriction is right sidebending.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":10,"f":"O099"}]},{"id":"O111-204-1","s":"A 28-year-old male presents to the clinic with acute lower back pain following a weightlifting session. Structural examination reveals that when the patient flexes his right knee in the standing position, his right pelvic side drops properly, but when he flexes his left knee, his left pelvis fails to drop smoothly and resists lateral displacement. What sidebending restricted motion is present in the lumbar spine, and what is the status of the Hip Drop Test?","o":["A. Restricted right lumbar sidebending; Positive right Hip Drop Test","B. Restricted left lumbar sidebending; Positive left Hip Drop Test","C. Restricted right lumbar sidebending; Positive left Hip Drop Test","D. Restricted left lumbar sidebending; Positive right Hip Drop Test"],"g":["C"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Restricted left lumbar sidebending; Positive right Hip Drop Test","a":"","why":"<b>C. Restricted right sidebending; positive left hip drop.</b> Flexing one knee should drop that iliac crest 20–25° and sidebend the lumbar spine <b>away</b> from it, so a <b>left drop asks for right lumbar sidebending</b>. The left pelvis fails to drop: the test is <b>positive on the left</b> (the side that drops less), and right sidebending is restricted.<br>A. Positive right: the right side dropped normally.<br>B. Restricted left sidebending: the right drop tests it, and that was normal.<br>D. The mirror image: it describes a failed right drop.","ex":"","fl":["The correct answer is C: the left pelvis fails to drop, so the hip drop test is positive on the left and right lumbar sidebending is restricted."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":204,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O111-53-1","s":"A 35-year-old male presents with new episodes of left-sided low back pain. He notes he recently became a father and is spending a lot of time carrying his daughter in his left arm so that he can use his right hand for tasks around the house. You perform the hip drop test and discover that his right hip drops lower than his left does. What does this indicate about his lumbar range of motion?","o":["A. He has an increased ferguson angle","B. He has limited lumbar flexion","C. His lumbar spine is sidebent to the right","D. His lumbar spine is sidebent to the left"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"His lumbar spine is sidebent to the left","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sidebent left.</b> The right hip drops lower, so the <b>left drops less: a positive left hip drop</b>. A left drop asks the lumbar spine to sidebend right; that motion is restricted, so the spine rests <b>sidebent left</b> (concave left), for example from a tight left quadratus lumborum holding the left ilium up.<br>A. Increased Ferguson angle: a front-to-back (lordosis) finding; the hip drop tests side-to-side motion.<br>B. Limited flexion: the hip drop test does not test flexion.<br>C. Sidebent right: that spine would block a right hip drop instead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":53,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":24,"label":"FRS/ERS: asymmetry worse toward the barrier","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-40.jpg","cap":"Facets open in flexion and close in extension: the motion a stuck segment lacks","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"The posterior transverse process marks the side the body rotates toward","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-61-1","s":"Fryette's second principle example: L4 F RrSr (L4 FRSr). What changes would you palpate at the TP and SP of L4 during extension?","o":["A. TPs become level and the SP returns to the midline","B. Right TP more posterior, SP deviated to the left; asymmetry increases","C. No change in TP or SP position in any position","D. Left TP more posterior, SP deviated to the right; asymmetry increases","E. Right TP more posterior, SP deviated to the right; asymmetry increases"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The asymmetry gets worse in extension: the right transverse process becomes more prominent (posterior) and the spinous process stays deviated to the left (it does not approximate the one above), because a flexed (FRS) segment cannot extend. In flexion the TPs become more symmetric.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> L4 FRSr is flexed, rotated right, sidebent right (type II). Rotation is named for the <b>front of the body</b>: body right, so the <b>right TP (transverse process) is posterior</b> and the <b>SP (spinous process) points left</b>. A flexed segment <b>cannot extend</b>, so in extension the right TP gets <b>more prominent</b>; in flexion the TPs even out.<br>A. TPs level: happens in flexion, the freedom of a flexed segment.<br>C. No change: describes a neutral (type I) group.<br>D. Left TP posterior: that would be left rotation.<br>E. The SP swings opposite to the body, so it goes left, not right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":61,"f":"O068"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-118-1","s":"If L4 is ERRSR, which of the following would be most correct:","o":["A. L5 and L3 would also be dysfunctional","B. The side bending component is exacerbated with the patient in flexion","C. The rotational component is exacerbated with the patient in extension.","D. The spinous processes will rotate to the side of the rotational component.","E. The position of the posterior transverse process will not change with flexion nor extension."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The side bending component is exacerbated with the patient in flexion","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> L4 ERRSR (extended, rotated right, sidebent right) is a type II (non-neutral) dysfunction. Its barriers are <b>flexion</b>, left rotation and left sidebending. Moving the segment <b>toward its barrier (flexion)</b> makes its rotation and sidebending asymmetry <b>more obvious</b>; extension makes it more symmetric.<br>A. Type II is a <b>single segment</b>; the levels above and below usually show neutral mechanics.<br>C. Extension is the freedom, so the asymmetry lessens in extension.<br>D. The spinous process moves <b>opposite</b> to the body: body right, spinous process left.<br>E. The posterior TP (transverse process) does change: more prominent in flexion, less in extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":132,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":68,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":20,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-36-1","s":"A 56-year-old male is complaining of low back pain. On examination you notice transverse process of L5 is posterior on the right. Extending L5 worsens the asymmetry while flexion restores rotational symmetry. Which one of the following is the best statement regarding the patient’s somatic dysfunction?","o":["A. Due to the non-neutral L5 somatic dysfunction, a group dysfunction of the high lumbar region will be present","B. This patient has a right-sided psoas syndrome","C. L5 will resist left rotation in the extending position","D. When employing a muscle energy (post-isometric relaxation)technique the patient would be asked to rotate his torso to the left against isometric contraction","E. Extending L5 will greatly limit its sidebending to the right"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L5 will resist left rotation in the extending position","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The right transverse process (TP) of L5 is posterior (rotated right); the asymmetry is <b>worse in extension and gone in flexion</b>, so L5 is <b>flexed: L5 FRSR</b> (flexed, rotated right, sidebent right). Its barriers are extension, <b>left rotation</b> and left sidebending, so in extension it <b>resists left rotation</b>.<br>A. One non-neutral segment does not force a high lumbar group curve.<br>B. Psoas syndrome centers on a flexed L1 or L2, not L5.<br>D. Muscle energy (ME): the patient pushes toward the freedom (right), not left.<br>E. Right sidebending is L5's freedom; extension does not limit it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":36,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":37,"f":"O098"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":24,"label":"Name type II: posterior TP plus flexion test","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Posterior transverse process marks rotation; spinous process swings the opposite way","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-33-1.png","cap":"Iliac crests mark L4, the level just above the crest line","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 33","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1920},{"src":"sl/O069-40.jpg","cap":"Flexion opens and extension closes the facets: test which one evens the TPs","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-13-5","s":"A 25-year-old male complains of low back pain. History reveals it is worse with forward bending. Structural examination reveals the right transverse process of L2 becomes more posterior with flexion. The most appropriate nomenclature for this dysfunction is","o":["A. ERLSL","B. ERRSR","C. FRLSL","D. FRRSR","E. NRRSL"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ERRSR","a":"","why":"<b>B. ERRSR (extended, rotated right, sidebent right).</b> The <b>right transverse process (TP) is posterior</b>, so L2 is rotated right. The asymmetry gets <b>worse with flexion</b>, so flexion is the barrier and the segment is <b>extended</b>. An asymmetry that changes with flexion or extension means type II (non-neutral), so <b>sidebending is to the same side</b> as rotation.<br>A. ERLSL: wrong side; the right TP is posterior.<br>C. FRLSL and D. FRRSR: a flexed segment worsens in extension, not flexion.<br>E. NRRSL: a neutral (type I) asymmetry does not change with flexion or extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":229,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":15,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":56,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":72,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":72,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-43-1","s":"Your friend complains their back hurts and asks you to fix them. As you are trying to diagnose them, you place your hands at the level of the iliac crest and proceed to the transverse processes. The left transverse process feels more posterior. You ask them to flex and extend and notice the transverse process becomes more symmetrical in flexion. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. L4ESrRl","B. L2ERlSl","C. L4ERlSl","D. L5FRrSr","E. L4FRlSl"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"L4FRlSl","a":"","why":"<b>E. L4 FRlSl (flexed, rotated left, sidebent left).</b> The <b>iliac crest marks L4</b>. The <b>left transverse process (TP) is posterior</b>, so L4 is rotated left. It becomes <b>symmetric in flexion</b>, so flexion is free: the segment is <b>flexed</b>. A flexed segment follows type II mechanics: sidebending to the <b>same side</b>.<br>A. L4 ESrRl: extended, and opposite-side coupling is type I.<br>B. L2: wrong level; the crest is L4.<br>C. L4 ERlSl: an extended segment gets more asymmetric in flexion.<br>D. L5 FRrSr: wrong level and wrong side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":43,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O095-32-1","s":"In one of your last labs of the semester, you found that your partner had a somatic dysfunction slightly above the level of the iliac crest with the transverse process posterior on the right. When you rolled your fingers cephalad and anteriorly, you noticed that the right transverse process remained posterior on the right. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. L4NSlRr","B. L1-5NSlRr","C. L4FRrSr","D. L4ERrSr","E. L4ERlSl","F. L5FRrSr","G. L5ERrSr"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L4ERrSr","a":"","why":"<b>D. L4 ERrSr (extended, rotated right, sidebent right).</b> Just above the iliac crest is <b>L4</b>. Right transverse process (TP) posterior means <b>rotated right</b>. Rolling your thumbs <b>cephalad and anteriorly induces flexion</b>; the right TP <b>stays posterior</b>, so flexion is the barrier and L4 is <b>extended</b>. Type II: sidebending to the same side.<br>A and B. Neutral (N SlRr): group mechanics; this is one segment that reacts to flexion.<br>C. L4 FRrSr: a flexed segment evens out in flexion.<br>E. L4 ERlSl: left rotation would put the left TP posterior.<br>F and G. L5: lies below the iliac crest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":32,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":25,"label":"Injury story to diagnosis: named where it's stuck","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-61.jpg","cap":"Fryette II example L4 FRrSr: flexed, rotated and sidebent to the same side","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 61"},{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Left rotation puts the left transverse process posterior","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-176-1","s":"Timmy’s pencil rolls off the left side of his desk during an exam, and he bends forward quickly to pick up his pencil off the floor. He has pain in his lower back. Noted tenderness and warmth directly below 12th rib. What’s Timmy’s Diagnosis (SD)?","o":["A. L1 FRSL","B. L1 ERSL","C. L1 FRSR","D. L4 FRSL","E. L1–L5 NSrRl"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Type 2 L1 FRSL","a":"","why":"<b>A. L1 FRSL (flexed, rotated left, sidebent left).</b> 'Directly below the 12th rib' is <b>L1</b>. He bent <b>forward and to the left</b> and got stuck there; somatic dysfunction is <b>named for the position it is stuck in</b> (its freedom). Flexed means type II (non-neutral), so rotation and sidebending go to the <b>same side</b>, left.<br>B. L1 ERSL: he bent forward, so flexion, not extension.<br>C. L1 FRSR: he reached to the left, not the right.<br>D. L4 FRSL: L4 sits at the iliac crest, not below rib 12.<br>E. L1–L5 NSrRl: a neutral group, not a single flexion injury.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":176,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-175-1","s":"Timmy’s pencil rolls off the left side of his desk during an exam, and he bends forward quickly to pick up his pencil off the floor. He has pain in his lower back. Noted tenderness and warmth directly below 12th rib. What diagnostic findings may you find?","o":["A. Post TP L1 on right, segment TPs symmetrical in flexion","B. Post TP L1 on left, segment TPs symmetrical in flexion","C. Post TP L1 on left, segment TPs asymmetrical in flexion","D. Post TP L4 on right, segment TPs symmetrical in flexion","E. Post TP L4 on left, segment TPs asymmetrical in flexion","F. Post TP L4 on left, segment TPs symmetrical in flexion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Post TP L1 on left, segment TPs symmetrical in flexion","a":"","why":"<b>B. Left L1 transverse process (TP) posterior; TPs symmetric in flexion.</b> Timmy is <b>L1 FRSL</b> (flexed, rotated left, sidebent left): rotated left puts the <b>left TP posterior</b>, and flexion is his freedom, so the TPs <b>even out in flexion</b> and worsen in extension.<br>A. Right TP posterior: that would be right rotation.<br>C. Asymmetric in flexion: an extended segment.<br>D, E and F. L4: at the iliac crest, not directly below rib 12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":175,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":25,"label":"Springing findings name the dysfunction","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-60.jpg","cap":"Anterior push on one transverse process rotates the body the opposite way","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-33-1.png","cap":"Iliac crests: the landmark for finding L4","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 33","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1920}],"q":[{"id":"O080-13-7","s":"A 55-year-old plumber presents with chronic low back pain. Structural examination in the prone position reveals the following: • Boggy area over the paravertebral tissue at L1 right • Resistance to anterior motion over the right transverse process of L1 • Resistance to anterior motion over the spinous process of L1. The most likely diagnosis is","o":["A. L1 ERSL","B. L1 ERSR","C. L1 FRSL","D. L1 FRSR","E. L1 NRLSR"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L1 FRSR","a":"","why":"<b>D. L1 FRSR (flexed, rotated right, sidebent right).</b> Anterior pressure on the <b>spinous process</b> induces <b>extension</b>; resistance means L1 is <b>flexed</b>. Anterior pressure on the <b>right transverse process</b> induces <b>left rotation</b>; resistance means L1 is <b>rotated right</b>. A flexed segment follows type II mechanics: <b>sidebending same side</b> as rotation. The boggy tissue is the T of TART (tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, tenderness).<br>A. ERSL and B. ERSR: extended segments move freely into extension.<br>C. FRSL: rotated left would resist pressure on the left TP.<br>E. NRLSR: a neutral group would not resist extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":233,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":15,"f":"O103"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-179-1","s":"Mr.Jones comes in with Lower Back Pain. You note warmth and hypertonicity close to the level of the Iliac crest. Upon springing on the segment you note stiffness. With your thumbs, the left Transverse process is stiff and resistant to springing. You push the segment cranially, noting relative resistance and difficulty of movement. What is the most possible diagnosis?","o":["A. L1 FRSR","B. L5 FRSL","C. L4 ERSL","D. L1-L4 NRLSR","E. L4 FRSL","F. L4 ERSR"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L4 ERSL","a":"","why":"<b>C. L4 ERSL (extended, rotated left, sidebent left).</b> Near the iliac crest is <b>L4</b>. Springing the <b>left transverse process (TP)</b> anteriorly induces right rotation; it resists, so L4 is <b>rotated left</b>. Pushing the segment <b>cranially induces flexion</b>; it resists, so L4 is <b>extended</b>. Type II: sidebending to the same side.<br>A. L1 FRSR: wrong level, side and flexion.<br>B. L5 FRSL: L5 is below the crest, and flexion was resisted.<br>D. L1–L4 NRLSR: a group curve, not one stiff segment.<br>E. L4 FRSL: a flexed segment moves freely into flexion.<br>F. L4 ERSR: then the right TP would resist.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":179,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":26,"label":"Nucleus moves away from the compressed side","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-56-1.png","cap":"Flexion squeezes the front of the disc; the nucleus is pushed posteriorly","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 56","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":327},{"src":"sl/O069-109.jpg","cap":"Squish and squirt: extension squeezes the back, nucleus moves anteriorly","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 109"},{"src":"sl/O068-74.jpg","cap":"Sidebending right loads the right side; the nucleus shifts left, to the convexity","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 74"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-55-1","s":"When a patient flexes forward, which way does the nucleus pulposus shifted as well as which ligament is stretched?","o":["A. NP moves anterior, Posterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","B. NP moves posterior, Posterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","C. NP moves anterior, Anterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","D. NP moves posterior, Anterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"NP moves posterior, Posterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Nucleus moves posteriorly; posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL) stressed.</b> Squish and squirt: <b>flexion squeezes the front of the disc</b>, so the nucleus pulposus (NP) <b>squirts posteriorly</b>, loading the posterior annulus and PLL. That is how flexion worsens a posterior disc bulge.<br>A. Anterior NP with PLL stress: the nucleus moves back, not forward.<br>C. Anterior NP, anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL): the extension pattern.<br>D. Posterior NP with ALL: mixes flexion and extension.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":55,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O095-57-1","s":"When a patient extends back, which way does the nucleus pulposus shift as well as which ligament is stretched?","o":["A. NP moves anterior, Posterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","B. NP moves posterior, Posterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","C. NP moves anterior, Anterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","D. NP moves posterior, Anterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"NP moves anterior, Anterior Longitudinal Ligament stressed.","a":"","why":"<b>C. Nucleus moves anteriorly; anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL) stressed.</b> <b>Extension squeezes the back of the disc</b>, so the nucleus pulposus (NP) <b>squirts anteriorly</b>; extension increases pressure on the <b>anterior annulus and ALL</b>.<br>A. Anterior NP with posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL): the PLL goes slack in extension.<br>B. Posterior NP, PLL: the flexion pattern.<br>D. Posterior NP with ALL: the nucleus moves away from the squeezed back.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":57,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O068-74-1","s":"During side bending to the right the nucleus moves toward the concavity or convexity?","o":["A. Concavity","B. Convexity"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Convexity – the nucleus pulposus shifts away from the loaded, compressed side (to the left when side bending right).","a":"","why":"<b>B. Convexity.</b> The nucleus pulposus moves <b>away from the compressed side</b>, like toothpaste in a squeezed tube. Sidebending right compresses the <b>right (concave)</b> side of the disc, so the nucleus shifts <b>left, toward the convexity</b>. The same rule: flexion pushes the nucleus <b>posteriorly</b>, extension pushes it <b>anteriorly</b>.<br>A. Concavity: that side is being squeezed, so the nucleus leaves it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":74,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":58,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":26,"label":"Disc herniation: posterolateral, L4–L5 and L5–S1","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-67-1.png","cap":"Posterolateral disc herniation bulging toward the exiting nerve root","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 67","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":415},{"src":"sl/O068-79.jpg","cap":"Posterolateral herniation from a narrow PLL; L4–L5 and L5–S1 most common","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 79"},{"src":"sl/O069-81.jpg","cap":"Bulging versus herniated disc; posterolateral is the usual direction","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 81"}],"q":[{"id":"O068-41-1","s":"At which level are you most likely to have disc injuries?","o":["A. L4–L5 and L5–S1, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrowest","B. T12–L1, at the thoracolumbar junction where the curves change","C. L3–L4, at the apex of the lumbar lordosis","D. L1–L2 and L2–L3, where the posterior longitudinal ligament is widest"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The lower lumbar discs, L4–L5 and L5–S1 (most common ruptured discs L4 and L5), where the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrowest, so herniations go posterolateral.","a":"","why":"<b>A. L4–L5 and L5–S1.</b> The <b>posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL) narrows</b> as it descends; at L4–L5 it is about half its L1 width. The <b>posterolateral</b> disc is left unsupported, so herniations go posterolateral, and <b>L4–L5 and L5–S1</b> make up about 95% of lumbar herniations. Both lie in the <b>multifidus triangle</b>.<br>B. T12–L1: a transition zone for somatic dysfunction, not the common disc level.<br>C. L3–L4: herniates far less often.<br>D. Upper lumbar levels: the ligament is widest there, so herniation is rare.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":41,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":18,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-66-1","s":"A 23 year old man (he/him) presents to your clinic with complaints of excruciating low back pain with numbness and tingling radiation to the legs. Upon taking a good history and physical, you tell him your most likely diagnosis is a disc herniation. Where is the most common place for disc herniation in the lumbar spine?","o":["A. L1 - L3","B. L2 - L4","C. L4 - L5","D. L5 - S1"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L4 - L5","a":"","why":"<b>C. L4–L5.</b> <b>L4–L5</b> is the most common level; with L5–S1 it makes up about <b>95% of lumbar herniations</b>. The <b>posterior longitudinal ligament narrows</b> low in the lumbar spine (half its L1 width at L4–L5), leaving the posterolateral disc unsupported.<br>A. L1–L3 and B. L2–L4: upper levels, where the ligament is wide; herniations are uncommon.<br>D. L5–S1: the second most common level, after L4–L5.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":66,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":27,"label":"Disc load: laughing, sitting and flexion worst","slides":[{"src":"sl/O068-82.jpg","cap":"L3 load in newtons: laughing 1200, sitting 1000, standing 700, supine 300","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 82"},{"src":"sl/O069-111.jpg","cap":"Disc load by position: supine lowest, seated flexed highest","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 111"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-56-1.png","cap":"Flexion pushes the nucleus posteriorly, toward the nerve root","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 56","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":327}],"q":[{"id":"O100-26-1","s":"Which of the following will exacerbate a herniated L3 disk the most?","o":["A. laughing","B. lying supine","C. standing","D. sitting","E. lying lateral recumbent"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"laughing","a":"","why":"<b>A. Laughing.</b> Of these choices, load on L3 (in newtons) is highest with laughing: <b>laughing 1200 N</b>, coughing 1100, upright sitting 1000, standing 700, supine 300. Laughing, coughing and straining raise abdominal and disc pressure, like a Valsalva.<br>B. Lying supine: the lowest load, 300 N.<br>C. Standing: 700 N.<br>D. Sitting: 1000 N, still below laughing.<br>E. Lateral recumbent: lower than standing (about 75 versus 100 kg of disc load).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":26,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O104-39-1","s":"Which of the following is the best statement regarding the biomechanics of the lumbar spine?","o":["A. The lumbar spine moves easiest about the sagittal axis.","B. Lumbarization increase the flexibility of the lumbosacral unit and decreasing the likelihood of degenerative joint disease.","C. The S1 nerve root will exit between the L5 and S1 vertebrae.","D. Central disc herniations are more common that postero-lateral disc herniations.","E. Radicular symptoms from a herniated disc will often worsen when the lumbar spine is flexed."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Radicular symptoms from a herniated disc will often worsen when the lumbar spine is flexed.","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>Flexion</b> squeezes the front of the disc and pushes the nucleus <b>posteriorly</b> against the nerve root while stretching the root, so <b>radicular symptoms worsen in flexion</b> (sitting, bending).<br>A. Flexion–extension, the easiest motion, turns around a <b>transverse</b> (left–right) axis; the sagittal (front–back) axis carries sidebending.<br>B. Lumbarization (S1 acting as a sixth lumbar) alters the lumbosacral junction and leads to <b>earlier</b> degeneration.<br>C. Lumbar roots exit below their vertebra: <b>L5</b> exits between L5 and S1.<br>D. <b>Posterolateral</b> herniations are more common; central ones are rare (cauda equina risk).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":39,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":186,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":146,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O069-113-1","s":"A 51-year-old non-binary patient presents with sensory changes in the left lower extremity due to a disc herniation. The positions that would most likely aggravate their symptoms the most are:","o":["A. Extension and sidebending to the right while sitting","B. Extension and sidebending to the right while standing","C. Flexion and rotation to the right while sitting","D. Flexion and sidebending to the left when standing","E. Left lateral recumbent","F. Right lateral recumbent"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion and rotation to the right while sitting","a":"","why":"<b>C. Flexion and rotation right while sitting.</b> Disc load is highest <b>sitting and flexed</b> (about 185 kg on L3 in a 70 kg person, versus about 25 kg supine). <b>Flexion</b> squeezes the front of the disc and pushes the nucleus <b>posteriorly</b> toward the nerve root, and twisting adds load (900 versus 700 newtons standing). It is the only choice joining sitting with flexion.<br>A. Extension while sitting: extension moves the nucleus anteriorly, away from the root.<br>B. Extension standing: lower load and an anterior nucleus shift.<br>D. Flexion standing: about 150 kg, less than seated flexion.<br>E and F. Lateral recumbent: low load, about 75 kg.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":113,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":127,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":64,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":12,"f":"O104"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":27,"label":"Hyperlordosis: facets, foramina, nerve roots","slides":[{"src":"sl/O069-40.jpg","cap":"Extension closes the foramina and approximates the facets; flexion opens them","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/O068-84.jpg","cap":"Lordotic low back pain: facet degeneration, foraminal narrowing, irritable nerve roots","where":"2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 84"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-26-1.png","cap":"Side view of lumbar spine: the intervertebral foramen between neighboring vertebrae","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 26","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":792}],"q":[{"id":"O068-84-1","s":"A patient is referred to as having a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this impact the facet joints?","o":["A. The facets gap open and become unloaded","B. Degenerative changes from increased facet loading","C. The facets reorient into the coronal plane","D. The facets develop tropism (left–right angle mismatch)"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Degenerative changes.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Degenerative changes.</b> Hyperlordosis holds the lumbar segments in <b>extension</b>, which <b>approximates the facet (zygapophyseal) joints</b> and shifts weight onto them. The result is <b>facet degeneration</b> (facet syndrome: arthritis-like breakdown of the joint cartilage).<br>A. Gapping and unloading: happen in flexion, not lordosis.<br>C. Coronal reorientation: facet orientation is fixed anatomy; lumbar facets stay near the sagittal plane.<br>D. Facet tropism: a developmental mismatch of left and right facet angles, the most common lumbar anomaly; posture does not cause it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":197,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O068-84-2","s":"A patient is referred to as having a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this impact the intervertebral foramen?","o":["A. Widening","B. Widening on the concave side only","C. Narrowing","D. No change; only the central canal is affected"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Narrowing.","a":"","why":"<b>C. Narrowing.</b> Extension brings the pedicles and facets of neighboring vertebrae together and <b>closes the intervertebral foramina</b>; flexion opens them. A hyperlordotic spine sits in extension, so its foramina are <b>narrowed</b>.<br>A. Widening: happens with flexion, which is why flexion exercises ease foraminal and stenosis pain.<br>B. Widening on the concave side: sidebending narrows the foramina on the concave side; it never widens them.<br>D. No change: wrong; foraminal size changes with every flexion–extension movement.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":197,"f":"O107"}]},{"id":"O068-84-3","s":"A patient is referred to as having a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this impact the nerve roots and their sheaths?","o":["A. Protected, because the foramina widen","B. Stretched over a posterior disc bulge","C. Unaffected, because the roots exit above the disc","D. More prone to irritation"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"More prone to irritation.","a":"","why":"<b>D. More prone to irritation.</b> Each nerve root and its sheath pass through an intervertebral foramen. In hyperlordosis the foramina <b>narrow</b> and the loaded facets crowd them, so the roots are <b>more easily irritated</b>.<br>A. Protected by wider foramina: lordosis narrows the foramina, it does not widen them.<br>B. Stretched over a posterior disc bulge: the flexed, flat-back problem, where the nucleus moves posteriorly.<br>C. Unaffected: exiting above the disc does not protect a root from a narrowed foramen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":84,"f":"O068"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":197,"f":"O107"}]},{"id":"O107-197-4","s":"Discussion: Let us consider a patient who presents with a lordotic lumbar spine. How does this presentation impact the following areas: Nucleus pulposus?","o":["A. Pushed posteriorly toward the nerve roots, loading the posterior longitudinal ligament","B. Stays centered while only the facet joints take the extra load","C. Pushed toward the convex side, stretching the lateral annulus","D. Pushed anteriorly, stretching the anterior longitudinal ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Extension squeezes the posterior disc, so the nucleus pulposus is pushed ('squirts') anteriorly, away from the nerve roots.","a":"","why":"<b>D. Pushed anteriorly.</b> A lordotic spine sits in <b>extension</b>. Extension squeezes the <b>back of the disc</b>, and the nucleus pulposus squirts away from the squeezed side, <b>anteriorly</b>, like toothpaste in a tube; the anterior annulus and anterior longitudinal ligament take the strain.<br>A. Posteriorly toward the nerve roots: the flexed, flat-back pattern.<br>B. Stays centered: the nucleus shifts with every flexion or extension.<br>C. Toward the convex side: what happens in sidebending.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":197,"f":"O107"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":27,"label":"Excess lordosis: pelvic tilt and flexion exercises","slides":[{"src":"sl/O107-200.jpg","cap":"Supine pelvic tilt, active resistive flexion; extension for flat back","where":"2026 week 14 IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final), slide 200"}],"q":[{"id":"O107-197-5","s":"Discussion: Let us consider a patient who presents with a lordotic lumbar spine. Which exercises would help this patient.","o":["A. Strengthening exercises for the psoas","B. Supine pelvic tilt and active resistive lumbar flexion","C. Strengthening exercises for the quadriceps","D. Prone extension-inducing exercises"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Exercises that decrease the lordosis: supine pelvic tilt and active resistive lumbar flexion (the patient rolls into a ball and pushes the knees up). Extension-inducing exercise is for the opposite problem, loss of lumbar lordosis.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Supine pelvic tilt and active resistive flexion.</b> Excess lordosis is reduced by exercises that <b>tilt the pelvis posteriorly and flex the lumbar spine</b>: the supine pelvic tilt, and rolling into a ball while pushing the knees up against resistance.<br>A. Psoas strengthening: the psoas pulls the lumbar spine forward and deepens the lordosis.<br>C. Quadriceps strengthening: hypertonic quadriceps tilt the pelvis anteriorly and also deepen it.<br>D. Prone extension-inducing exercises: for the opposite problem, loss of lordosis (flat back).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":197,"f":"O107"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":28,"label":"Lumbar HVLA: posterior TP up, into the barrier","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O094-7-2.png","cap":"Lumbar roll HVLA: patient lateral recumbent, posterior transverse process up","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 7","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":900},{"src":"sl/ta-O094-7-1.png","cap":"HVLA steps: monitor posterior TP, flex hips, rotate torso, thrust pelvis","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 7","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1770},{"src":"sl/O071-3.jpg","cap":"Lumbar HVLA set-up: posterior transverse process up","where":"2026 week 12 Lab Lumbar Spine Diagnosis & HVLA, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-3-8","s":"With the HVLA set up in the figure below, where is the posterior transverse process?","o":["A. Cephalad","B. Caudad","C. Left","D. Right"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right","a":"","why":"<b>D. Right.</b> Lumbar HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude) is set up with the <b>posterior transverse process (TP) up</b>. Here the patient lies on the <b>left side</b>, right side up, so the posterior TP, the side the vertebra is rotated toward, is on the <b>right</b>.<br>A. Cephalad and B. Caudad: directions along the spine, not the side a TP is on.<br>C. Left: the down side, against the table.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O077-3-8.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":3,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":192,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-51-1","s":"A patient presents with lower back pain and upon osteopathic maniupulative examination you find a type II SD at L4ERrSr. You decide to treat the patient with HVLA. Which of the following is the correct setup for the treatment?","o":["A. Patient lies prone with the dysfunctional facet joint away from you","B. Patient lies on their left side with dysfunctional facet joint facing up.","C. Patient lies on their right side with dysfunctional facet joint facing down.","D. Patient lies supine with the dysfunctional facet joint towards you"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Patient lies on their left side with dysfunctional facet joint facing up.","a":"","why":"<b>B. On the left side, dysfunctional facet up.</b> Lumbar HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude) is done <b>lateral recumbent with the posterior transverse process (TP) up</b>. L4 ERrSr (extended, rotated right, sidebent right) has its <b>right TP posterior</b>, so the right side goes up and the patient <b>lies on the left side</b>.<br>A. Prone: not the lumbar roll position.<br>C. On the right side, facet down: the posterior TP must face up.<br>D. Supine: not used for the lumbar roll.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":51,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-53-1","s":"With respect to the patient from the previous case (L4ERrSr treated with HVLA), your attending asks you what position is the vertebra placed in?","o":["A. L4 is rotated and sidebent left with respect to L5, placing it in its barrier","B. L4 is rotated and sidebent left with respect to L3, placing it in its barrier","C. L4 is rotated and sidebent right with respect to L5, placing it in its freedom","D. L4 is rotated and sidebent right with respect to L3, placing it in its freedom"],"g":["A"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"L4 is rotated and sidebent left with respect to L3, placing it in its barrier","a":"","why":"<b>A. Rotated and sidebent left with respect to L5, into its barrier.</b> L4 ERrSr means L4 is extended, rotated right and sidebent right <b>on L5</b>: a segment is named <b>relative to the vertebra below</b> (for example, T6 ERlSl is named in relation to T7). HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude) is <b>direct</b>, so L4 is taken into its <b>barrier</b> (flexion, left rotation, left sidebending) relative to L5 before the thrust.<br>B. Relative to L3: that is the joint above, not the dysfunctional L4–L5 unit.<br>C and D. Right rotation and sidebending: the freedom, an indirect position.","ex":"","fl":["The correct answer is A: a segment's motion and barrier are named relative to the vertebra below, so L4 ERrSr is engaged in flexion, left rotation and left sidebending relative to L5."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":53,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W12","u":29,"label":"NEJM 1999: same outcomes, far less medication","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-206-1.png","cap":"NEJM 1999 summary: similar outcomes and satisfaction, far less medication with OMT","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 206","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1404},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-206-2.png","cap":"NSAID harms: about 103,000 hospitalizations and 16,500 deaths a year","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 206","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1696},{"src":"sl/O079-119.jpg","cap":"The 1999 NEJM trial of OMT for low back pain","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 119"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-87-1","s":"In the NJEM 1999 article, “A comparison of Osteopathic spinal manipulation with standard care for patients with low back pain”, what were the outcomes when comparing the control group that received medications and physical therapy versus the group that received individualized Osteopathic manipulative treatment(OMT)?","o":["A. The control group used far less medication than the OMT group","B. The OMT group used far less medication than the control group","C. Medication use was similar between both groups","D. There was no clinical satisfaction with the OMT group"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The OMT group used far less medication than the control group","a":"","why":"<b>B. The OMT group used far less medication.</b> The 1999 NEJM (New England Journal of Medicine) trial of 155 patients with <b>subacute low back pain</b> compared 8 individualized OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) sessions over 12 weeks with standard care (analgesics including opioids, anti-inflammatories, physical therapy). Result: <b>similar clinical outcomes and satisfaction, far less medication with OMT</b>.<br>A. Reversed: the control group used more.<br>C. Similar: medication use differed significantly.<br>D. No satisfaction: over 90% in both groups were satisfied.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":87,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":204,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-169-1","s":"In reference to the 1999 NEJM article “A COMPARISON OF OSTEOPATHIC SPINAL MANIPULATION WITH STANDARD CARE FOR PATIENTS WITH LOW BACK PAIN”, what’s its significance?","o":["A. osteopathic treatment was considered to not be significantly different in outcome when compared to standard care practices that are more reliant on medication for LBP.","B. Paves a future to which Osteopathy can be used to reduce medication use, helping to avoid long term side effects of NSAIDs","C. Osteopathy has the ability to play a role in helping to control the Opioid abuse epidemic","D. All of the Above"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the Above","a":"","why":"<b>D. All of the above.</b> In the 1999 NEJM (New England Journal of Medicine) trial, osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) gave <b>outcomes no different from standard care</b> (A) while patients used <b>far less medication</b> (B), sparing NSAID (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug) harms of about 103,000 hospitalizations and 16,500 deaths a year. Early studies of OMT in chronic back pain with <b>opioid</b> use also look promising (C).<br>A, B or C alone: each is true, so all of the above is best.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":169,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O111-39-1","s":"What has one randomized controlled trial of low back pain (published in the NEJM) demonstrated regarding OMT compared to the standard of care at the time?","o":["A. Patients receiving standard of care were largely dissatisfied with their care","B. There were no statistic differences in any finding between the two groups","C. Patients receiving standard of care reported worse back pain at the end of the trial","D. Patients receiving OMT required significantly less analgesic medication"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Patients receiving OMT required significantly less analgesic medication","a":"","why":"<b>D. Less analgesic medication with OMT.</b> In the 1999 NEJM (New England Journal of Medicine) trial, osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and standard care gave <b>similar outcomes</b>, but the OMT group used <b>significantly less medication</b> and physical therapy.<br>A. Dissatisfied: over 90% in both groups were satisfied.<br>B. No differences in any finding: medication use differed significantly.<br>C. Worse pain with standard care: pain outcomes did not differ.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":39,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":30,"label":"Diaphragm attachments: crura on L1–L3","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-12-1.png","cap":"Diaphragm from below: crura on L1–L3, arcuate ligaments over psoas and QL","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":424},{"src":"sl/O074-3.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm from below, crura reaching the lumbar spine","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-4-1","s":"Where does the diaphragm insert?","o":["A. Lower ribs and sternum; crura anchor on T10–T12","B. T12–L5 bodies and discs, via tendinous arches","C. Lower ribs and sternum; crura anchor on L1–L3","D. Lower ribs and xiphoid; crura anchor on L4–L5","E. 12th rib and the transverse processes of L1–L4"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"It attaches to the lower ribs and sternum; the crura anchor at L1, L2, L3.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The diaphragm attaches around the <b>lower ribs</b> and the <b>sternum (xiphoid)</b>. Its <b>crura</b> anchor on the bodies of <b>L1, L2 and L3</b> (right crus to L3, left to L2). That lumbar anchor is why upper lumbar somatic dysfunction (SD) can flatten the diaphragm.<br>A. T10–T12 is too high; the crura reach the lumbar bodies.<br>B. T12–L5 bodies, discs and transverse processes are the psoas major origin.<br>D. L4–L5 is too low; the crura stop at L2–L3.<br>E. Rib 12 and the L1–L4 transverse processes are the quadratus lumborum attachments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O074"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":30,"label":"Diaphragm innervation: phrenic C3–C5","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-4.jpg","cap":"Phrenic C3–C5 motor; intercostals T5–T11, subcostal T12 sensory","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 4"},{"src":"sl/O043-34.jpg","cap":"Muscles of inspiration: diaphragm supplied by the phrenic nerve, C3–C5","where":"2026 week 7 Lecture 7 Clinical Ribs, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-4-2","s":"What is the innervation of the diaphragm?","o":["A. Intercostals T5–T11 + subcostal T12: motor; phrenic: peripheral sensory only","B. Phrenic: motor + central sensory; intercostals T5–T11 + subcostal T12: peripheral sensory","C. Vagus: motor; phrenic: all sensory","D. Lumbar plexus L1–L3 (via the crura): motor; phrenic: sensory","E. Phrenic: motor; vagus: all sensory"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (motor and central sensory); the peripheral diaphragm sends sensory fibers via the intercostals T5–T11 and subcostal T12.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>phrenic nerve</b> is the only motor nerve to the diaphragm, and it also carries sensation from the <b>central</b> part. The <b>peripheral rim</b> sends sensation through the lower <b>intercostal nerves (T5–T11)</b> and the <b>subcostal nerve (T12)</b>.<br>A. Reversed: the intercostals give the diaphragm no motor supply; they carry only rim sensation.<br>C. The vagus passes through the diaphragm (esophageal hiatus) but does not move it.<br>D. The crura anchor on L1–L3, but lumbar nerves do not supply the diaphragm.<br>E. Diaphragm sensation returns by phrenic and intercostal nerves, not the vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O074"}]},{"id":"O074-4-3","s":"Where do the nerves (phrenic) exit?","o":["A. C1–C3","B. T5–T11","C. C5–C7","D. C3–C5","E. L1–L3"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"C3–C5.","a":"","why":"<b>D. C3–C5.</b> The phrenic nerve forms from the <b>C3, C4 and C5</b> ventral rami: “<b>C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive</b>.” It runs down the neck on the anterior scalene, inside the prevertebral fascia, to the diaphragm.<br>A. C1–C3 is too high; only C3 contributes.<br>B. T5–T11 intercostals carry only sensation from the diaphragm’s rim.<br>C. C5–C7 is the long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior), a common mix-up.<br>E. L1–L3 is where the crura anchor, not where the nerve comes from.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O074"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":30,"label":"Inhalation straightens the spine: lordosis decreases","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O097-45-1.png","cap":"Spine on inhalation (curves straighten) vs exhalation (curves deepen)","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 45","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":998},{"src":"sl/O074-5.jpg","cap":"Spinal motion in inhalation and exhalation","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-44-1","s":"Lumbar lordosis ______________ upon inhalation.","o":["A. Increases","B. Decreases","C. Remains the same"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreases","a":"","why":"<b>B. Decreases.</b> On <b>inhalation</b> the spinal curves <b>straighten</b>: the thoracic kyphosis and the lumbar lordosis both decrease, and the sacral base moves posteriorly. On exhalation the curves deepen again. That link is why a tight psoas or quadratus lumborum (QL), both pulling on the lumbar spine, can restrict breathing.<br>A. Increases: that happens on exhalation.<br>C. Remains the same: the lumbar curve moves with every breath.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":44,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":44,"f":"O098"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":31,"label":"QL attachments: rib 12, L1–L4 TPs, iliac crest","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-52-1.png","cap":"QL attachments (rib 12, L1–L4 TPs, iliac crest) and actions","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 52","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1214},{"src":"sl/O074-9.jpg","cap":"QL origin, insertion and actions","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-51-1","s":"Which of the following is not an attachment of the quadratus lumborum muscle?","o":["A. 12th rib","B. Posterior iliac crest","C. Iliolumbar ligament","D. Transverse processes of L1-L4","E. ASIS"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"ASIS","a":"","why":"<b>E. ASIS.</b> The quadratus lumborum (QL) attaches to the <b>12th rib</b>, the <b>transverse processes of L1–L4</b>, the <b>iliolumbar ligament</b> and the <b>posterior iliac crest</b>. The ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) is not on that list; the sartorius and iliacus attach there. (The crest and ligament are the origin; rib 12 and the transverse processes are the insertion.)<br>A. 12th rib: QL attachment (it stabilizes rib 12).<br>B. Posterior iliac crest: QL attachment.<br>C. Iliolumbar ligament: QL attachment.<br>D. Transverse processes of L1–L4: QL attachment.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":51,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O077-1-2","s":"Between what two structures can you palpate the quadratus lumborum muscle?","o":["A. Lateral edge of the sacrum and the greater trocanter","B. Transverse processes of the upper lumbar and the lesser trocanter","C. Rib 12 and the posterior aspect of the iliac crest","D. Inferior to the iliac crest and lateral thigh"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Rib 12 and the posterior aspect of the iliac crest","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The quadratus lumborum (QL) runs from the <b>12th rib</b> and L1–L4 transverse processes to the <b>posterior iliac crest</b> and iliolumbar ligament. Palpate in the gap between rib 12 and the crest, lateral to the erector spinae; its trigger points sit at the <b>lateral edge</b>.<br>A. Lateral sacrum to greater trochanter is the piriformis.<br>B. Upper lumbar transverse processes to lesser trochanter is the psoas.<br>D. Below the iliac crest to the lateral thigh is gluteus medius / tensor fasciae latae territory.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":180,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":85,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":31,"label":"QL action: side-bends (one), extends (both)","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-52-1.png","cap":"QL actions — side-bending one side, extension both sides","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 52","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1214},{"src":"sl/O069-51.jpg","cap":"QL actions — side-bending, hip hiking, lumbar stability","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-83-1","s":"What is the function of the QL muscle?","o":["A. External rotation of the hip","B. Hip flexion","C. Sidebending of lumbar (unilateral), extension of lumbar (bilateral)","D. Knee flexion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sidebending of lumbar (unilateral), extension of lumbar (bilateral)","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> One quadratus lumborum (QL) contracting <b>side-bends the lumbar spine</b> toward itself (and hikes that hip); both together <b>extend</b> the lumbar spine. It also stabilizes the 12th rib.<br>A. External rotation of the hip: piriformis.<br>B. Hip flexion: psoas (iliopsoas).<br>D. Knee flexion: hamstrings; the QL crosses neither the hip nor the knee.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":83,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-132-1","s":"Which muscle is the key muscle in stabilizing the spine in a side to side direction?","o":["A. Erector spinae","B. Quadratus Lumborum","C. Iliopsoas","D. Rotatores"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Quadratus Lumborum","a":"","why":"<b>B. Quadratus lumborum (QL).</b> Running from the iliac crest up to rib 12 and the L1–L4 transverse processes on each side, the QL is the key <b>side-to-side (lateral) stabilizer</b> of the lumbar spine: one side side-bends and hikes the hip, both sides extend and steady the lumbosacral junction.<br>A. Erector spinae: mainly extend the spine (front-to-back control).<br>C. Iliopsoas: flexes the hip and the lumbar spine on the pelvis.<br>D. Rotatores: tiny segmental rotators, not a lateral stabilizer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":132,"f":"O100"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":32,"label":"QL trigger points refer to hip and buttock","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-114-1.png","cap":"Travell QL trigger points: superficial refer to crest and hip, deep to SI/buttock","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 114","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1057},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-87-2.png","cap":"Psoas trigger-point referral: lumbar paraspinal strip and front of thigh","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 87","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":680},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-87-3.png","cap":"Piriformis trigger-point referral: sacrum, buttock, down the back of thigh","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 87","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":450},{"src":"sl/O074-10.jpg","cap":"QL trigger points and referred pain pattern","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-87-1","s":"What is the referred pain pattern for the QL? (Three referral-pattern drawings are shown.)","o":["A. Top drawings: pain along the lumbar paraspinal strip and the anterior thigh/groin","B. Bottom-left drawings: pain along the iliac crest, sacroiliac region, lower buttock and greater trochanter","C. Bottom-right drawings: pain over the sacrum and buttock spreading down the posterior thigh"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bottom-left drawings: pain along the iliac crest, sacroiliac region, lower buttock and greater trochanter","a":"","why":"<b>B. Bottom-left.</b> Quadratus lumborum (QL) trigger points (TrPs) refer along the <b>iliac crest</b>, to the <b>sacroiliac (SI) region and lower buttock</b>, and to the <b>greater trochanter</b> (lateral hip), sometimes wrapping toward the groin.<br>A. Top: the <b>psoas</b> pattern — a vertical strip beside the lumbar spine plus the <b>front of the thigh</b>.<br>C. Bottom-right: the <b>piriformis</b> pattern — sacrum and buttock spreading down the <b>back of the thigh</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O095-87-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":87,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":130,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O097-57-1","s":"After sleeping on the couch one night, Ryan woke up with severe muscle spasm in his left lower back. Upon assessment, he reveals that the pain radiates down to his hip and around into his groin. On PE, which of the following trigger points would you most likely find and need to treat?","o":["A. QL TrP","B. Piriformis TrP","C. Gluteus Medius TrP","D. Psoas TrP"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"QL TrP","a":"","why":"<b>A. QL TrP.</b> A low back spasm whose pain <b>wraps around the hip into the groin</b> is the quadratus lumborum (QL) trigger point (TrP) pattern: iliac crest, lateral hip and greater trochanter, buttock, groin. Lying twisted on a couch all night overloads the QL.<br>B. Piriformis TrP: buttock pain running down the back of the thigh.<br>C. Gluteus medius TrP: posterior iliac crest, sacrum, buttock and upper lateral thigh — no groin.<br>D. Psoas TrP: a vertical low back strip plus the front of the thigh, without the wrap around the hip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":57,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":57,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":44,"f":"O099"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":32,"label":"Hypertonic QL: side-bend toward, same-side short leg","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-54-1.png","cap":"Tight right QL makes the right leg look short, side-bent right","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 54","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1064},{"src":"sl/ta-O100-136-1.png","cap":"QL spasm: unlevel pelvis, trunk lean, short leg on spasm side","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 136","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1131},{"src":"sl/O075-38.jpg","cap":"Right QL spasm hikes the innominate; right leg looks short","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-1-3","s":"Your patient presents with complaints of right sided low back pain. On palpation you find the right Quadratus Lumborum to be hypertonic with some trigger points to the lateral edge of the muscle. Upon further examination what might you expect to find?","o":["A. Decreased side-bending to the left","B. Decreased side-bending to the right","C. Increased forward flexion","D. Increased forward extension"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased side-bending to the left","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> A tight right quadratus lumborum (QL) pulls rib 12 and the right iliac crest together, so the lumbar spine is held <b>side-bent right</b> (a type I group curve, concave right). Bending the other way stretches the tight muscle, so <b>side-bending left is decreased</b>.<br>B. Bending right is toward the tight muscle — the free direction.<br>C. QL hypertonicity decreases (not increases) side-bending and/or flexion.<br>D. A hypertonic muscle restricts motion; it does not increase range.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":182,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-123-1","s":"A 50 year old male presents to your clinical for back pain and after performing and osteopathic structural examination you note a right short leg. If this short leg is due to a hypertonic muscle this patient most likely has a trigger point in which muscle?","o":["A. Gluteus medius","B. Piriformis","C. Gluteus maximus","D. Quadratus lumborum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Quadratus lumborum","a":"","why":"<b>D. Quadratus lumborum (QL).</b> A QL spasm (with trigger points, TrPs) <b>hikes the innominate</b> on its own side, so that leg <b>looks short</b> — a functional, not a true, short leg. Treating the QL lets the innominate drop and reveals the true leg length.<br>A. Gluteus medius TrPs refer pain to the buttock and trochanter; they do not hike the pelvis.<br>B. Piriformis spasm can make an apparent length change only indirectly, through a sacral torsion; it is not the classic short-leg TrP.<br>C. Gluteus maximus TrPs refer to the buttock; no pelvic hike.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":123,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":134,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O069-54-1","s":"A patient presents with a chief complaint of low back pain that radiates to the hip and groin. Examination reveals that the lumbar spine is side bent to the right with the right leg appearing shorter in the supine position. When you apply a gentle traction to the legs you find restriction on the right. Which muscle is hypertonic?","o":["A. Right psoas","B. Right quadratus lumborum","C. Left quadratus lumborum","D. Left piriformis","E. Right hemi-diaphragm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right quadratus lumborum","a":"","why":"<b>B. Right QL.</b> A hypertonic quadratus lumborum (QL) side-bends the lumbar spine <b>toward itself</b> and <b>hikes the innominate</b> on that side, so the right leg <b>looks short</b> (functional, not true). Gentle leg traction in supine is <b>restricted on the tight side</b>. QL trigger points refer to the hip and buttock.<br>A. Right psoas: flexed posture, positive Thomas test, anterior thigh pain; traction is not its test.<br>C. A left QL would side-bend left and make the left leg look short.<br>D. Left piriformis: buttock pain and an externally rotated leg.<br>E. The hemi-diaphragm does not hike the pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":54,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":56,"f":"O107"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":16,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":67,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-46-1","s":"A 52 y/o male present to your office with lower left back pain that radiates to his left hip and groin. When pulling traction on the leg, you notice that the left side is more restricted. Which of the following diagnoses might you expect to find and which muscle is most likely hypertonic?","o":["A. L1-L5 N SlRr; Piriformis","B. L2 F SlRl; Psoas","C. L2-L4 N SrRl; Quadratus Lumborum","D. L3-L5 N SrRl; Psoas","E. L1-L5 N SlRr; Quadratus Lumborum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"L1-L5 N SlRr; Quadratus Lumborum","a":"","why":"<b>E. L1–L5 N SlRr; quadratus lumborum (QL).</b> Restricted left leg traction plus low back pain wrapping to the hip and groin = <b>hypertonic left QL</b>. It side-bends the lumbar spine <b>toward itself</b>: a type I group curve, neutral (N), side-bent left (Sl), rotated right (Rr) — opposite sides.<br>A. Right curve, wrong muscle: the piriformis does not side-bend the lumbar spine.<br>B. L2 flexed, side-bent and rotated left with the psoas: psoas syndrome (Thomas test, front-of-thigh pain).<br>C. Side-bending right would come from a right QL.<br>D. L3–L5 N SrRl with the psoas: wrong side and wrong muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":46,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":46,"f":"O098"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":32,"label":"QL pulls rib 12 down: breathing restricted","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O105-23-1.png","cap":"Diagnosis of QL: 12th rib inferior, restricted breathing, hip drop","where":"Fall 2021 lumbar review (SGA drive), slide 23","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":793},{"src":"sl/O078-5.jpg","cap":"QL diagnosis — 12th rib dropped, restriction in breathing","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-119-1","s":"A 46 year old male presents complaining of right sided low back pain. The patient exhibits bad posture when sitting in a chair. The hip drop test is positive on the right and the iliac crest on the right is elevated. Sidebending to the left is limited during ROM testing. All other neurological testing is normal. What other dysfunction would you expect to see?","o":["A. Quadratus lumborum spasm on the left","B. Superior 11th and 12th ribs on the right","C. Piriformis spasm on the right","D. Inferior 12th rib on the left","E. Inferior 12th rib on the right"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior 12th rib on the right","a":"","why":"<b>E. Inferior 12th rib on the right.</b> Positive right hip drop, high right iliac crest and limited left side-bending = <b>right quadratus lumborum (QL) spasm</b> (slumped sitting often starts it). The QL attaches to rib 12, so its spasm pulls the <b>12th rib inferiorly</b> on the same side and restricts breathing.<br>A. The QL spasm is on the right, not the left.<br>B. The QL pulls ribs 11–12 down, not up.<br>C. A piriformis spasm goes with psoas syndrome (opposite side), not QL spasm.<br>D. Wrong side: the rib drops on the QL side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":119,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O099-19-1","s":"Dr. Burke comes in for an osteopathic assessment and treatment. After introducing yourself, washing your hands, and asking for permission, you begin your exam and find the following:  right shoulder is depressed, right inferior angle of the scapula is depressed, and right leg appears shorter than the left. Red reflex is positive on the right between T12 and L2, and hypertonia is found in the same region. What else might you expect to find?","o":["A. Positive hip drop on the left","B. Restriction in breathing","C. Patient slouched forward when sitting","D. Pain referral to the umbilicus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Restriction in breathing","a":"","why":"<b>B. Restriction in breathing.</b> Right shoulder and scapula low, right leg short and hypertonia near T12–L2 point to a <b>tight right quadratus lumborum (QL)</b>. The QL attaches to <b>rib 12</b>; its pull holds rib 12 down (inferior) and <b>restricts breathing</b>.<br>A. The hip drop would be positive on the <b>right</b>, the QL side.<br>C. Slumped sitting can start QL problems, but it is history, not an exam finding; the QL picture is a lateral curve.<br>D. Pain referral to the umbilicus is not a QL pattern; QL pain goes to the iliac crest, hip and buttock.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":19,"f":"O099"}]},{"id":"O111-81-1","s":"A 33-year-old female presents for left-sided low back pain. She is a new mother to a now 4-month-old baby. Both her pregnancy and delivery were uncomplicated, however over the last several weeks, she has had increasingly persistent low back pain. She describes the pain as \"achy, sore\" and points to the area between the left side of her lumbar spine and the posterior/lateral aspect of her iliac crest. She notes intermittent radiation of pain to her left groin when the pain is most significant. She denies bladder or bowel incontinence associated with the pain. She denies any inciting event, but notes she does spend a lot of time carrying her daughter around \"on my left hip\". She has not tried any analgesic medication and would like to avoid that due to breastfeeding. On examination, neurologic testing of her lower extremities is normal including SLR, strength, sensation, reflexes. Which of the following findings is additionally likely to be present?","o":["A. Increased traction of her left leg in supine position","B. Decreased traction of her right leg in supine position","C. Left 12th rib inhalation somatic dysfunction","D. L1-5 NSrRl"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left 12th rib inhalation somatic dysfunction","a":"","why":"<b>C. Left 12th rib inhalation somatic dysfunction (SD).</b> Pain between the left lumbar spine and iliac crest, spreading to the groin, after carrying a baby on the left hip = <b>hypertonic left quadratus lumborum (QL)</b>. The QL pulls rib 12 <b>inferiorly</b>, which is the inhalation direction for the caliper ribs 11–12, so the rib is held in inhalation and breathing is restricted.<br>A. A tight left QL makes left leg traction <b>restricted</b> (decreased), not increased.<br>B. The right side is the normal side.<br>D. A left QL side-bends the spine left: L1–L5 N Sl Rr (neutral, side-bent left, rotated right), not N Sr Rl.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":81,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":33,"label":"Hip drop test: positive on the tight-QL side","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-105-1.png","cap":"Hip drop test steps; positive when the hip drops less than 20°","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 105","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1384},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-127-1.png","cap":"Tight left QL holds the left ilium up: left hip drop restricted","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 127","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1057},{"src":"sl/O078-7.jpg","cap":"Hip drop test — lack of a 20–25° drop is positive","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-104-1","s":"A patient comes in with right lower back pain. You suspect they have a tight right QL. What would you observe when you do the Hip Drop test to confirm your suspicion?","o":["A. You ask the patient to bend their left leg and will see their right hip drop indicating a positive right hip drop","B. You ask the patient to bend their right leg and you will see their right hip drop indicating a positive right hip drop","C. You ask the patient to bend their right leg and you will see their right hip stay up (not drop) indicating a positive right hip drop","D. You will ask the patient to bend their left leg and you will see their left hip drop indicating a positive left hip drop"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"You ask the patient to bend their right leg and you will see their right hip stay up (not drop) indicating a positive right hip drop","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> In the hip drop test the patient bends <b>one knee</b>, and that same-side hip should drop <b>20–25°</b>, side-bending the lumbar spine away from it. A <b>tight right quadratus lumborum (QL)</b> holds the right iliac crest up, so with the <b>right knee bent the right hip stays up</b>: a positive right hip drop.<br>A. Bending the left knee tests the left side, and a right hip cannot drop when the left knee bends.<br>B. A right hip that drops normally is a negative test.<br>D. A left hip that drops is a normal (negative) left test, and it does not test the suspected right QL.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":104,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":63,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-89-1","s":"You have a patient with a short leg on the right. What are the special test findings?","o":["A. Positive hip drop on the right, hypertonic left QL","B. Positive hip drop on the right, hypertonic right QL","C. Positive hip drop on the left, hypertonic left QL","D. Positive hip drop on the left, hypertonic right QL"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Positive hip drop on the right, hypertonic right QL","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> A hypertonic quadratus lumborum (QL) hikes its own innominate, so the <b>short-looking leg is on the QL side</b>. The same tight QL keeps that hip from dropping, so the <b>hip drop test is positive on that side</b>. Right short leg → right QL → positive right hip drop.<br>A. A left QL would hike the left side and shorten the left leg.<br>C. Left-sided findings fit a left short leg.<br>D. Mixed sides: the positive hip drop and the tight QL are always on the same side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":89,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":192,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":46,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":79,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-53-1","s":"A patient comes to your practice complaining of lower back pain. On examination the patient is side bent to the right and has a right leg that is shorter than the left. What is a test that you could perform to help confirm your most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Trendelenburg test","B. Thomas test","C. Hip drop test","D. Straight leg raise test"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hip drop test","a":"","why":"<b>C. Hip drop test.</b> Side-bent right with a short-looking right leg suggests a <b>hypertonic right quadratus lumborum (QL)</b>. The QL tests are the <b>hip drop test</b> (that hip drops less than 20–25°) and gentle leg traction in supine.<br>A. Trendelenburg test: hip abductor (gluteus medius) weakness — the opposite hip sags in single-leg stance.<br>B. Thomas test: tight iliopsoas.<br>D. Straight leg raise (SLR): nerve root irritation or piriformis syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":53,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O097-20-1","s":"A 25 y/o patient presents to your office with low back pain. In order to assess her, you perform the hip drop test. When bending the left knee, you note that her left hip drops down 25 degrees, but when bending her right knee, the right hip only drops down 10 degrees. Which of the following would be seen in this patient?","o":["A. Hypertonic QL on the left and lumbar convexity to the right","B. Hypertonic QL on the right and lumbar convexity to the left","C. Hypertonic QL on the left and lumbar concavity to the right","D. Hypertonic QL on the right and lumbar convexity to the right"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic QL on the right and lumbar convexity to the left","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> A normal hip drop is about <b>20–25°</b>. The right hip drops only 10°, so the test is <b>positive on the right</b>: a tight <b>right quadratus lumborum (QL)</b> holds the crest up. That QL side-bends the lumbar spine <b>right</b>, so the curve is concave right and <b>convex left</b>.<br>A. The left hip dropped a normal 25°, so the left QL is fine.<br>C. Concave right is true, but the tight muscle is the right QL, not the left.<br>D. Right QL is correct, but its curve is convex left, not right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":20,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":23,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-24-1","s":"A 57 y/o patient presents with significant pain on the right greater trochanter and slightly inferior to the iliac crest. The hip drop test is positive on the right. What is the most likely somatic dysfunction?","o":["A. Right short leg","B. Left short leg","C. L2-4 NSlRr","D. L5FRrSr","E. L5ERrSr"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right short leg","a":"","why":"<b>A. Right short leg.</b> Right trochanter pain fits the quadratus lumborum (QL) trigger point pattern, and a <b>positive right hip drop</b> means the right QL holds the right hip up. That hiked innominate makes the <b>right leg look short</b>, with the lumbar spine side-bent right.<br>B. Left short leg: the tight QL is on the right.<br>C. L2–4 N SlRr (neutral, side-bent left, rotated right): a right QL side-bends right (N SrRl).<br>D. L5 FRrSr (flexed, rotated and side-bent right): one segment; it does not hike the pelvis.<br>E. L5 ERrSr (extended, rotated and side-bent right): same problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":24,"f":"O100"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":34,"label":"QL muscle energy: tight side up, hike hip","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-124-1.png","cap":"Side-lying QL muscle energy: tight side up, hands on hip and trunk","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 124","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1510},{"src":"sl/O078-9.jpg","cap":"Passive myofascial release of QL, prone — not muscle energy","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-3-9","s":"You decide to treat a left hypertonic quadratus lumborum muscle on the left with muscle energy. You have the patient on their side and apply a longitudinal stretch to the quadratus lumborum muscle. What direction do you then give to the patient for the active component of the muscle energy treatment?","o":["A. Ask the patient to push their hip posteriorly","B. Ask the patient to raise their hip towards their head","C. Ask the patient to push the top leg into your pelvis","D. Ask the patient to raise their top leg into your forearm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ask the patient to raise their hip towards their head","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> In muscle energy (ME) you hold the muscle at its barrier while the patient gently contracts it against your equal counterforce. The longitudinal stretch separates rib 12 from the iliac crest (the barrier). The quadratus lumborum (QL) <b>hikes the hip</b>, so the patient <b>pulls the hip toward the head</b> while you hold still.<br>A. Pushing the hip back is hip extension (gluteus maximus), not QL.<br>C. Pushing the leg into your pelvis uses hip muscles; it does not bring rib 12 toward the crest.<br>D. Raising the top leg is hip abduction (gluteus medius), not QL.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":3,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":194,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-92-1","s":"How would you set up to treat a hypertonic right QL with muscle energy?","o":["A. Patient prone with their knee flexed, operator places leg into hip internal rotation and resists patient’s external rotation of the hip.","B. Patient supine with one knee flexed up to their chest, operator places hip into extension and resists patient’s flexion of the hip.","C. Patient right lateral recumbent, position similar to thoracolumbar bidirectional stretch, and tell patient “try to bring your shoulder and hip together”","D. Patient left lateral recumbent, position similar to thoracolumbar bidirectional stretch, and tell patient “try to bring your shoulder and hip together”"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Patient left lateral recumbent, position similar to thoracolumbar bidirectional stretch, and tell patient “try to bring your shoulder and hip together”","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> For quadratus lumborum (QL) muscle energy (ME) the <b>tight QL faces up</b>, so a right QL means the patient lies on the <b>left side</b>, set up like the thoracolumbar bidirectional stretch. You lengthen the QL to its barrier, then the patient <b>tries to bring shoulder and hip together</b> against your counterforce.<br>A. Prone, knee flexed, hip into internal rotation: piriformis ME.<br>B. Supine, one knee to chest, other hip into extension: psoas ME.<br>C. Right side down puts the tight right QL against the table, where it cannot be stretched.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":92,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-123-1","s":"A 45 year old woman comes into your office with low back pain and a positive hip drop test on the left? How can you treat this patient with muscle energy?","o":["A. With the patient in a prone position, the physician will make contact with the patient’s 11th and 12th ribs and their ASIS. The physician will pull the patients hip towards themselves in a rhythmic pattern until the muscle softens and relaxes.","B. With the patient supine and one knee brought to their chest. The physician pushes one leg off the table into the barrier and has the patient push up against resistance three consecutive times with a relaxation phase in between and a passive stretch at the end.","C. With the patient on their side with their legs bent 90 degrees, the physician place their forearms on the patient’s shoulders and hip while using their hands to pull the muscle upwards. The pt adds resistance by bringing their hip and shoulders towards each other three times with a relaxation phase x3 and a passive stretch.","D. With the patient supine, the physician hold sthe pt’s ankle and slightly abducts the leg. The physician adds traction to the leg and has the pt hike their hip towards their ear x3 with relaxation phases in between and a passive stretch at the end.","E. With the patient prone. The physician bring the pt’s bent leg to their axilla and internally rotates the leg to the barrier. The patient resists 3x with a relaxation phase and passive stretch at the end."],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"With the patient on their side with their legs bent 90 degrees, the physician place their forearms on the patient’s shoulders and hip while using their hands to pull the muscle upwards. The pt adds resistance by bringing their hip and shoulders towards each other three times with a relaxation phase x3 and a passive stretch.","a":"","why":"<b>C and D.</b> Positive left hip drop = <b>tight left quadratus lumborum (QL)</b>. Both are QL muscle energy (ME): lengthen the QL to its barrier, then the patient <b>hikes the hip toward the shoulder</b> against your counterforce, 3 times, then a passive stretch. C does it side-lying (tight side up); D does it supine with traction on the leg.<br>A. Prone, rhythmic pull on the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) with a hand on rib 12: QL passive myofascial release, not ME.<br>B. Supine, leg pushed off the table: psoas ME.<br>E. Prone, bent leg internally rotated: piriformis ME.","ex":"","fl":["D is also correct: the supine version, with traction on the leg while the patient hikes the hip toward the head, is also quadratus lumborum muscle energy."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":123,"f":"O096"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":34,"label":"QL hypertonicity, no red flags: OMT plus home exercise","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-16.jpg","cap":"Treat QL trigger points, 12th rib, passive myofascial release","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-81-1.png","cap":"Low back pain red flags; no imaging without one","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 81","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1230},{"src":"sl/O075-29.jpg","cap":"Self-care, home stretching, ergonomics, postural strengthening","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"O111-75-1","s":"A 33-year-old female presents for left-sided low back pain. She is a new mother to a now 4-month-old baby. Both her pregnancy and delivery were uncomplicated, however over the last several weeks, she has had increasingly persistent low back pain. She describes the pain as \"achy, sore\" and points to the area between the left side of her lumbar spine and the posterior/lateral aspect of her iliac crest. She notes intermittent radiation of pain to her left groin when the pain is most significant. She denies bladder or bowel incontinence associated with the pain. She denies any inciting event, but notes she does spend a lot of time carrying her daughter around \"on my left hip\". She has not tried any analgesic medication and would like to avoid that due to breastfeeding. On examination, neurologic testing of her lower extremities is normal including SLR, strength, sensation, reflexes. Which of the following is the appropriate next step for management?","o":["A. Lumbar corticosteroid injection","B. Lumbar imaging to rule out compression fracture","C. STAT referral to the ED for concern of cauda equina syndrome","D. In-office OMT and a home exercise program"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"In-office OMT and a home exercise program","a":"","why":"<b>D. In-office osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and a home exercise program.</b> A <b>hypertonic left quadratus lumborum (QL)</b> from carrying the baby on one hip, with a normal neuro exam and <b>no red flags</b>, is a muscular problem. Treat it with OMT (passive myofascial release, 12th rib technique, muscle energy) plus stretching and self-care at home; no drug is needed.<br>A. A corticosteroid injection is not first-line care for a tight muscle.<br>B. Imaging is for red flags such as trauma, cancer, steroid use or age over 50; none are present.<br>C. Cauda equina syndrome brings bladder or bowel loss and saddle numbness; she has neither.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":75,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":35,"label":"Psoas: T12–L5 to lesser trochanter; hip flexor","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-121-1.png","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall: psoas beside the spine, QL lateral, kidney on top","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 121","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":622},{"src":"sl/O074-20.jpg","cap":"Psoas major along the lumbar spine with the lumbar plexus","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/O074-29.jpg","cap":"Iliopsoas functions — walking, flexion, erect posture, pelvic balance","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-72-1","s":"Where is the origin & insertion of the Psoas Major?","o":["A. T11-L7 vertebral bodies & greater trochanter of the femur","B. L1-L4 vertebral bodies & lesser trochanter of the femur","C. T12-L5 vertebral bodies & lesser trochanter of the femur","D. 12th rib & posterior iliac crest"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T12-L5 vertebral bodies & lesser trochanter of the femur","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Psoas major arises from the transverse processes, bodies and discs of <b>T12–L5</b> and joins the iliacus to insert on the <b>lesser trochanter</b> by a common tendon. Nerve supply: lumbar nerves L1–L2 (some L3).<br>A. There is no L7, and the greater trochanter takes the piriformis and gluteal muscles.<br>B. Right insertion, but the origin starts at T12 and runs to L5.<br>D. Rib 12 and posterior iliac crest: quadratus lumborum (QL).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":72,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-70-1","s":"What is the function of the Psoas?","o":["A. Hip abduction","B. Knee Flexion","C. Lumbar spine extension","D. Hip flexion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hip flexion","a":"","why":"<b>D. Hip flexion.</b> The psoas (with the iliacus, the iliopsoas) is the <b>strongest flexor of the thigh</b>; it also adds some external rotation and flexes the spine on the pelvis.<br>A. Hip abduction: gluteus medius and minimus.<br>B. Knee flexion: hamstrings; the psoas does not cross the knee.<br>C. Lumbar extension is not its function; a <b>short</b> psoas in standing pulls the lumbar spine into extension (more lordosis), but that is a dysfunction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":70,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-127-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the psoas major?","o":["A. Flexion of the hip.","B. Maintaining erect posture at the hip joint","C. Lateral flexion of the lumbar spine","D. Walking"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral flexion of the lumbar spine","a":"","why":"<b>C. Lateral flexion of the lumbar spine.</b> The iliopsoas functions are <b>walking</b>, <b>flexion</b> of the femur, pelvis and lumbar spine, <b>maintaining erect posture at the hip</b>, and keeping the pelvis balanced in the midline. Side-bending is the one not listed. (The psoas can side-bend the trunk weakly — a spasm bends the spine toward it in psoas syndrome — but it is not one of its listed functions.)<br>A. Flexion of the hip: its main action.<br>B. Erect posture at the hip joint: listed function.<br>D. Walking: listed function.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":127,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":35,"label":"Short psoas pulls lumbar into lordosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O099-61-1.png","cap":"Short psoas with the femur fixed pulls the lumbar spine into lordosis","where":"Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 61","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":545},{"src":"sl/O074-31.jpg","cap":"Psoas hypertonicity is a major cause of increased lordosis","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-30-1","s":"What happens to the lumbar spine when the psoas is shortened?","o":["A. Loses its lordosis; pelvis rotates posteriorly, as with short hamstrings","B. Pulled into extension (more lordosis); sacral base forward, pelvis rotates anteriorly","C. Pulled into extension (more lordosis); sacral base backward, pelvis rotates posteriorly","D. Unaffected, because the psoas only moves the femur","E. Side-bends away from the short psoas, convex on the psoas side"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"When the person stands the femur is fixed, so the shortened psoas pulls the lumbar spine into extension (increased lordosis) and can pull the sacral base forward / rotate the pelvis anteriorly.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> Standing fixes the femur, so a short psoas pulls its origin (T12–L5) down and forward: the lumbar spine goes into <b>extension (increased lordosis)</b>, the <b>sacral base moves forward</b> and the <b>pelvis rotates anteriorly</b>.<br>A. Flattened lordosis with posterior pelvic tilt is the short hamstring / gluteus maximus pattern.<br>C. Half right: the psoas tips the pelvis anteriorly, not posteriorly.<br>D. The psoas attaches to the lumbar spine, so it moves it whenever the femur is fixed.<br>E. Psoas spasm side-bends the spine <b>toward</b> the tight side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":30,"f":"O074"}]},{"id":"O069-65-1","s":"Upon observation of a patient with low back pain that radiates to the anterior thigh, you find increased lumbar lordosis. In this patient, which of the following is a major cause of extension in this area of the spine?","o":["A. Hypertonic quadricep femoris muscle","B. Weakened abdominal muscles","C. Hypertonic psoas","D. Shortened hamstring muscles","E. Weakened erector spinae muscles"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic psoas","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The psoas attaches directly to the lumbar vertebrae. When it is tight and the femur is fixed in standing, it pulls the lumbar spine into <b>extension (increased lordosis)</b> — one of the major causes. Its trigger points refer to the low back and <b>anterior thigh</b>, matching this patient.<br>A. Tight quadriceps can tip the pelvis forward, but they never attach to the spine; not the major cause.<br>B. Weak abdominals allow lordosis but do not pull the spine into extension.<br>D. Short hamstrings tilt the pelvis back and flatten lordosis.<br>E. Weak erector spinae would reduce extension, not increase it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":65,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":61,"f":"O107"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":15,"f":"O104"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":36,"label":"Psoas pain: low back, front of thigh to knee","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-76-1.png","cap":"Psoas TrPs near umbilicus and groin; pain to low back and front thigh","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 76","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1176},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-199-2.png","cap":"Psoas syndrome pain: low back plus lateral/anterior thigh, not past knee","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 199","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1014}],"q":[{"id":"O095-129-1","s":"Low back pain with pain on the lateral/anterior aspect of the lower extremity extending no further than the knee is characteristic of what condition?","o":["A. Piriformis syndrome","B. IT Band Syndrome","C. Hypertonic QL","D. Psoas syndrome"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Psoas syndrome","a":"","why":"<b>D. Psoas syndrome.</b> Its typical pain is <b>low back pain</b> with pain on the <b>lateral/anterior thigh</b> that stops <b>at the knee</b>.<br>A. Piriformis syndrome: buttock pain down the <b>back</b> of the thigh to the knee, worse after sitting.<br>B. Iliotibial (IT) band syndrome: lateral thigh and knee pain from running, without low back pain.<br>C. Hypertonic quadratus lumborum (QL): low back pain to the iliac crest, hip and buttock, with a short-looking leg.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":129,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":28,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O099-21-1","s":"Dr. Torrents comes in stating “the front of my upper thigh hurts”. Even before introducing yourself, washing your hands, and asking for permission, where might you expect to find a hyperirritable, hypersensitive, palpable nodule?","o":["A. Near Rib 12","B. Along the iliotibial band","C. Suprapubic","D. Peri-umbilical"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Peri-umbilical","a":"","why":"<b>D. Peri-umbilical.</b> Pain in the <b>front of the upper thigh</b> is the psoas trigger point (TrP) referral pattern. One psoas TrP is reached with deep pressure through the abdominal wall <b>beside the umbilicus</b> (lateral to the rectus abdominis); another sits in the upper thigh near its insertion.<br>A. Near rib 12: quadratus lumborum (QL) TrPs, which refer to the hip and buttock.<br>B. Along the iliotibial band: lateral thigh, not a psoas site.<br>C. Suprapubic: not a psoas TrP site.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":21,"f":"O099"}]},{"id":"O111-47-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient presents to the clinic while you are on your family medicine rotation as a 3rd-year medical student. The patient reports back pain over the last few weeks that started after a weekend where he spent a lot of time raking leaves. He notes the pain has not improved since then and describes it affecting the areas in the image below. Which of the following muscles should you recommend stretching for a HEP (home exercise plan)?","o":["A. Piriformis","B. Quadratus lumborum","C. Iliopsoas","D. Multifidus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliopsoas","a":"","why":"<b>C. Iliopsoas.</b> The drawing shows the <b>psoas trigger point (TrP) pattern</b>: a vertical strip beside the lumbar spine plus the <b>front of the thigh</b>. Raking keeps the trunk and hips flexed, so the psoas is held short. Once the pain is no longer acute, the home plan stretches the psoas with hip extension (the Lancelot lunge).<br>A. Piriformis: sacrum and buttock, spreading down the back of the thigh.<br>B. Quadratus lumborum: iliac crest, greater trochanter, sacroiliac region and lower buttock.<br>D. Multifidus: deep pain beside the spine and over the sacrum, without the front-of-thigh zone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-47-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":47,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":36,"label":"Recognizing psoas syndrome: posture and exam","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-56-1.png","cap":"Psoas syndrome exam: L1–L2 flexed, L5 extended, contralateral piriformis tenderpoints","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 56","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1028},{"src":"sl/O078-18.jpg","cap":"Psoas syndrome definition — posture, gait, pain, associated lesions","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-55-1","s":"Which of the following is not a somatic dysfunction associated with psoas syndrome?","o":["A. L5 extension dysfunction","B. Increased hip extension","C. L1 flexion dysfunction","D. Sacrum and innominate dysfunctions","E. Contralateral piriformis tenderpoint"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased hip extension","a":"","why":"<b>B. Increased hip extension.</b> A short, tight psoas <b>limits hip extension</b>: range of motion shows increased hip flexion and <b>decreased extension</b>, so increased extension does not fit.<br>A. L5 extension somatic dysfunction: typical of psoas syndrome.<br>C. L1 flexion somatic dysfunction: the key lesion.<br>D. Sacral and innominate dysfunctions: variable but associated.<br>E. Contralateral piriformis tender point: associated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":55,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O080-13-6","s":"A young male presents with back pain. Observation reveals he is standing in a flexed, sidebent right posture. Physical examination findings include resisted hip extension on the right, a tenderpoint medial to the right ASIS. The most likely cause is","o":["A. piriformis tension on the side of the discomfort","B. S1 nerve root compression","C. sciatic nerve compression under the inguinal ligament","D. spondylolysis of the L3-L4 segments","E. hypertonic right psoas muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"hypertonic right psoas muscle","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> Psoas syndrome: <b>flexed posture side-bent toward</b> the tight psoas, <b>restricted hip extension</b> on that side, and a tender point just <b>medial to the ASIS</b> (anterior superior iliac spine; the ipsilateral iliacus point) — all on the right.<br>A. In psoas syndrome the piriformis spasm is on the <b>opposite</b> side and does not cause this posture.<br>B. S1 root compression gives neuro deficits (ankle reflex, lateral foot); psoas syndrome has a normal neuro exam.<br>C. The sciatic nerve runs through the buttock, not under the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Spondylolysis is a pars defect, usually at L5, with no tender point medial to the ASIS.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":231,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":15,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":58,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":61,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":61,"f":"O098"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":36,"label":"Psoas syndrome cause: long flexion, sudden stretch","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-199-1.png","cap":"Initial positions of flexion: tucked-under sitting, desk work, weeding, sit-ups","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 199","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1018},{"src":"sl/O074-33.jpg","cap":"Etiology — long shortening of psoas, then sudden lengthening","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-130-1","s":"After the Thanksgiving Break, Brandon wanted to workout to get rid of his gut. He read online that doing a lot of sits up will help him achieve this goal. After a week of doing this, Brandon started experiencing lower back pain. What is the most likely diagnosis for Brandon and what special test would you perform to confirm it?","o":["A. No pain No gain Syndrome and No Test Needed Bro","B. Tight QL and Straight Leg Test","C. Piriformis Syndrome and Thomas Test","D. Piriformis Syndrome and Hip Drop Test","E. Psoas Syndrome and Thomas Test"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Psoas Syndrome and Thomas Test","a":"","why":"<b>E. Psoas syndrome and Thomas test.</b> Repetitive <b>sit-ups</b> hold the psoas short (a flexion position), and the spasm brings low back pain. The test for a tight psoas is the <b>Thomas test</b>. Match them: psoas = Thomas, quadratus lumborum (QL) = hip drop, piriformis = straight leg raise (SLR).<br>A. Joke option: new back pain after a new activity needs a diagnosis.<br>B. A tight QL is tested with the hip drop or leg traction, not the SLR.<br>C. Piriformis syndrome follows buttock trauma or sitting on a wallet; its test is the SLR.<br>D. Wrong muscle, and the hip drop tests the QL.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":130,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O095-197-1","s":"Which of the following is an expected finding in a left psoas syndrome?","o":["A. + Thomas test on right","B. Hypertonic piriformis on left","C. Pelvic side shift to the left","D. Continued sitting from desk work","E. Upper back pain","F. L1-L2 Extended","G. L5 Flexed"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Continued sitting from desk work","a":"","why":"<b>D. Continued sitting from desk work.</b> Psoas syndrome starts with the psoas held <b>short for a long time</b> (desk work, slouched sitting, weeding, sit-ups), then a sudden stretch. For a <b>left</b> psoas syndrome the findings are: Thomas test positive <b>left</b>, piriformis spasm <b>right</b>, pelvic shift <b>right</b>, <b>low</b> back pain, <b>L1–L2 flexed</b>, <b>L5 extended</b>.<br>A. The Thomas test is positive on the psoas side (left).<br>B. The piriformis spasm is contralateral (right).<br>C. The pelvis shifts to the opposite side (right).<br>E. It causes low back, not upper back, pain.<br>F. L1–L2 are flexed, not extended.<br>G. L5 is extended, not flexed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":197,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":37,"label":"Thomas test: lifted thigh = tight psoas","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-58-1.png","cap":"Positive Thomas test: knee hugged, opposite straight leg lifts off the mat","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 58","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":976},{"src":"sl/O078-19.jpg","cap":"Thomas test steps — gap under the tested knee is positive","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/O074-41.jpg","cap":"Space under the knee means iliopsoas spasm on that side","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"O077-1-1","s":"What is the name of the special test used to diagnose a hypertonic Psoas muscle?","o":["A. Thompson","B. Spurling's","C. Phalen's","D. Thomas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thomas","a":"","why":"<b>D. Thomas test.</b> Supine, the patient pulls one knee to the chest (flattening the lordosis). If the other thigh lifts off the table, that <b>iliopsoas</b> is tight (positive). Test both sides.<br>A. Thompson test: calf squeeze for Achilles tendon rupture.<br>B. Spurling’s test: neck compression for cervical radiculopathy.<br>C. Phalen’s test: wrist flexion for carpal tunnel syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":178,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":74,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-58-1","s":"You conduct a special test to evaluate your patient with lower back pain. The result of the test is shown below. What is the most likely underlying somatic dysfunction causing this positive test?","o":["A. Hypertonic psoas muscle on the left","B. Hypertonic hamstring muscles on the left","C. Hypertonic QL on the right","D. Hypertonic piriformis on the left"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic psoas muscle on the left","a":"","why":"<b>A. Hypertonic psoas on the left.</b> This is a <b>positive Thomas test</b>: the patient hugs one knee to the chest, flattening the lordosis, and the <b>other, straight leg lifts off the table</b>. A lifted thigh marks a tight <b>iliopsoas on that side</b> (here the left).<br>B. Tight hamstrings do not lift the thigh off the table; they limit the straight leg raise.<br>C. A tight quadratus lumborum (QL) shows on the hip drop test or leg traction.<br>D. A tight piriformis gives an externally rotated leg with restricted internal rotation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O096-58-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":58,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":71,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":37,"label":"Psoas key lesion: L1–L2 flexed toward psoas","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-114-1.png","cap":"L1/L2 side-bent and rotated to psoas side; opposite piriformis and pelvic shift","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 114","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":500},{"src":"sl/O074-46.jpg","cap":"Full-blown left psoas syndrome, key non-neutral L1 or L2","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-111-1","s":"If a patient has left psoas syndrome, which vertebrae is the key non-neutral SD?","o":["A. L1 or L2","B. L1 or L5","C. L3 or L4","D. L3 or L5"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"L1 or L2","a":"","why":"<b>A. L1 or L2.</b> The <b>key lesion</b> of psoas syndrome is a non-neutral (Fryette type II) <b>flexion</b> somatic dysfunction (SD) of <b>L1 or L2</b> (sometimes T12 or L3), side-bent and rotated toward the tight psoas. Treat it first.<br>B. L5 is involved, but it is <b>extended</b> and secondary.<br>C. L3 or L4: not the key level.<br>D. L3 or L5: not the key level, and L5 is extended.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":111,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":131,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O080-14-9","s":"A 65-year-old machinist presents with chronic low back pain. History reveals he must spend several hours in a forward bent position while working. Structural examination reveals a hypertonic left psoas. The most likely associated somatic dysfunction is","o":["A. L1 FRSL","B. L2 ERSR","C. L3 FRSR","D. L5 FRSL","E. posterior sacrum left"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"L1 FRSL","a":"","why":"<b>A. L1 FRSL</b> (flexed, rotated and side-bent left). The key lesion of psoas syndrome is a <b>non-neutral (type II) flexion</b> somatic dysfunction of <b>L1 or L2</b>, rotated and side-bent <b>toward the tight psoas</b>. Type II = one segment, flexed or extended, rotation and side-bending to the same side; rotation is named for the posterior transverse process.<br>B. L2 ERSR (extended, rotated and side-bent right): wrong motion and side.<br>C. L3 FRSR (flexed, rotated and side-bent right): wrong level and side.<br>D. L5 is <b>extended</b> in psoas syndrome.<br>E. A posterior left sacrum is not the reported pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":237,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":16,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":23,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":79,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":30,"f":"O100"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":40,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":39,"f":"O097"}],"t":1},{"id":"O077-1-4","s":"Your patient presents with non-radiating left side back and hip pain. On observation you notice their posture to be slightly flexed and side-bent left. There is a positive Thomas test and negative neurologic findings. On Osteopathic Structural exam what might you expect to find?","o":["A. Group curve with concavity to the right","B. Piriformis spasm on the ipsilateral side of psoas spasm","C. L1-2 side-bent and rotated to the ipsilateral side of psoas spasm","D. Sciatic Nerve pain on the left"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1-2 side-bent and rotated to the ipsilateral side of psoas spasm","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Flexed, side-bent left, positive Thomas test and a normal neuro exam = <b>left psoas syndrome</b>. Its key finding is a <b>non-neutral L1–L2</b> somatic dysfunction (flexed), <b>side-bent and rotated toward the psoas side</b> (left).<br>A. A group curve (type I, neutral) is not the psoas pattern; also, side-bent left means concave left.<br>B. The piriformis spasm is on the <b>contralateral</b> side.<br>D. Pain here is non-radiating with normal neuro findings; any thigh pain would be on the opposite (piriformis) side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":1,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":184,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O092-11-1","s":"A 20-year-old male presents to the office with a 2-month history of worsening low back pain. He is a kicker for his college football team. Physical examination reveals hypertonic lumbar paraspinal muscles with no vertebral tenderness or step-off. The straight leg test is negative bilaterally. When the patient is instructed to lay supine while pulling his flexed left knee to his chest, his right leg lifts off the examination table. Radiographs of the lumbar spine and pelvis are normal. Which of the following additional findings is most likely in this patient?","o":["A. left iliacus tender point","B. L5 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right","C. L1 flexed, rotated right, sidebent right","D. positive pelvic side shift test to the right","E. right piriformis spasm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1 flexed, rotated right, sidebent right","a":"","why":"<b>C. L1 flexed, rotated right, side-bent right.</b> Pulling the left knee to the chest lifted the <b>right</b> leg: a positive right Thomas test = <b>right psoas</b> spasm (a kicker flexes that hip over and over). Psoas syndrome brings a non-neutral <b>L1–L2 flexion</b> lesion, side-bent and rotated <b>toward the psoas</b>.<br>A. The iliacus tender point is <b>ipsilateral</b> — right, not left.<br>B. L5 is <b>extended</b> in psoas syndrome, not neutral.<br>D. The pelvis shifts to the <b>opposite</b> side (left).<br>E. The piriformis spasm is <b>contralateral</b> (left).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"}]},{"id":"O105-9-1","s":"Which of the following is an expected finding in a right psoas syndrome?","o":["A. + Thomas test on left","B. Hypertonic piriformis on right","C. Pelvic side shift to the right","D. L2 F Rr Sr"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L2 F Rr Sr","a":"","why":"<b>D. L2 F Rr Sr</b> (flexed, rotated right, side-bent right). In a <b>right</b> psoas syndrome the key lesion is L1 or L2 <b>flexed</b>, rotated and side-bent <b>toward the psoas</b>; the other findings go to the <b>other side</b>.<br>A. The Thomas test is positive on the psoas side — right.<br>B. The piriformis spasm is contralateral — left.<br>C. The pelvis shifts to the opposite side — left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 lumbar review (SGA)","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Fall 2021","sl":9,"f":"O105"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":38,"label":"Psoas syndrome: opposite piriformis, opposite pelvic shift","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-57-1.png","cap":"Full-blown left psoas: right pelvic shift, right piriformis spasm, right leg pain","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 57","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1438},{"src":"sl/O074-45.jpg","cap":"Stages — bilateral spasm, torsion, contralateral piriformis, sciatica","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-113-1","s":"What do you expect to see in a patient with the psoas syndrome on the right?","o":["A. Left pelvic shift and right piriformis spasm with tender point","B. Right pelvic shift and right piriformis spasm with tender point","C. Left pelvic shift and left piriformis spasm with tender point","D. Right pelvic shift and left piriformis spasm with tender point"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left pelvic shift and left piriformis spasm with tender point","a":"","why":"<b>C. Left pelvic shift and left piriformis spasm.</b> In a <b>right</b> psoas syndrome everything below the lumbar key lesion goes to the <b>opposite side</b>: the pelvis shifts <b>left</b>, and the <b>left piriformis</b> spasms (with a tender point), often irritating the left sciatic nerve.<br>A. Left shift is right, but a right piriformis is the psoas side.<br>B. Both findings on the psoas side.<br>D. Left piriformis is right, but the pelvis shifts away from the psoas (left).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":113,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O080-14-8","s":"A 65-year-old machinist presents with chronic low back pain. History reveals he must spend several hours in a forward bent position while working. Structural examination reveals a hypertonic left psoas. The most likely associated postural screening finding is","o":["A. elevated right iliac crest","B. flexed L5 dysfunction","C. sacral shear","D. pelvic side shift right","E. posterior left sacrum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"pelvic side shift right","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> A tight <b>left</b> psoas side-bends the lumbar spine left, so the pelvis <b>shifts right</b>. The pelvic shift test, like every somatic dysfunction, is named for the side of freer motion, so it is <b>positive right</b> (contralateral).<br>A. An elevated right iliac crest is not part of psoas syndrome.<br>B. L5 is <b>extended</b>; the flexed segment is L1 or L2.<br>C. Sacral shear is not reported with psoas syndrome.<br>E. The sacrum tends to rotate forward on an oblique axis; a posterior sacrum is not the pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":235,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":15,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":77,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":109,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-73-1","s":"A 28-year-old medical student presents with a 2 week history of low back pain that radiates down his left leg just to his knee with no numbness or tingling below. He states that the low back pain began after standing up quickly. In standing observation, you find that he is bend forward over his right leg. On examination, which of the findings would you expect to find?","o":["A. L1-L5 NSlRr","B. Hypertonic piriformis on the left","C. Thomas test positive on the left","D. L5 flexed","E. Sacral rotated backwards"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertonic piriformis on the left","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> He leans forward over his <b>right</b> leg: flexed and side-bent right = <b>right psoas</b> spasm. Psoas syndrome brings <b>contralateral piriformis</b> spasm, so the <b>left</b> piriformis is tight — matching pain down the left leg that stops at the knee.<br>A. L1–L5 NSLRR (neutral, side-bent left, rotated right) is a type I group curve; psoas syndrome gives a single non-neutral flexed somatic dysfunction at L1 or L2, side-bent right here.<br>C. The Thomas test is positive on the psoas side (right).<br>D. L5 is <b>extended</b> in psoas syndrome.<br>E. The sacrum tends to rotate forward on an oblique axis, not backward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":73,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":75,"f":"O107"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":19,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":144,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-48-1","s":"A 46 y/o female presents to your office with lower left back pain radiating to the anterior thigh on the left side. When performing the Thomas Test, you note that the left popliteal fossa is raised six inches off of the table. Which of the following is NOT a finding on the rest of the exam?","o":["A. L1 F RlSl","B. Right pelvic shift","C. Left piriformis spasm","D. Pain in right hip down back of right leg","E. Rightward facing convexity of the lumbar spine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left piriformis spasm","a":"","why":"<b>C. Left piriformis spasm.</b> The left popliteal fossa lifting in the Thomas test = <b>left psoas</b> spasm. Its piriformis spasm is <b>contralateral</b> — the <b>right</b> piriformis — so a left piriformis spasm is not expected.<br>A. L1 F RlSl (flexed, rotated and side-bent left): the key lesion, toward the psoas.<br>B. Right pelvic shift: the pelvis shifts away from the psoas.<br>D. Pain in the right hip down the back of the right leg: the right piriformis irritating the sciatic nerve.<br>E. Rightward convexity: the spine side-bends left toward the psoas, so it is concave left, convex right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":48,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":48,"f":"O098"}]},{"id":"O111-73-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents for low back pain that started over the weekend. He reports feeling some discomfort after a lot of yard work (indicating frequently bending down to the side to pick up heavy objects), however he notes the pain became much more significant the following day (after sleeping on his stomach). He currently describes pain that is mostly on the left side of his low back with some radiation to his groin, however today he has started to feel some pain in his right gluteal region and upper hamstring as well. Neurologic testing including SLR, strength, sensation, and DTR in the lower extremities is intact. He has a positive Thomas test on the left. Which of the following findings are likely additionally present?","o":["A. L5 FRSl","B. Left pelvic side shift","C. L1-2 ERSl","D. Restricted IR of the right hip"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Restricted IR of the right hip","a":"","why":"<b>D. Restricted internal rotation of the right hip.</b> A positive left Thomas test with left low back and groin pain = <b>left psoas syndrome</b>. The <b>opposite (right) piriformis</b> spasms, causing the right buttock and upper hamstring pain; a tight piriformis holds the hip turned out, so <b>internal rotation is restricted</b>.<br>A. L5 is <b>extended</b> in psoas syndrome, not flexed, rotated and side-bent left (FRSl).<br>B. The pelvis shifts <b>away</b> from the psoas: to the right, not the left.<br>C. L1–L2 is <b>flexed</b> (FRSl), not extended (ERSl).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":73,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":39,"label":"Kidney stone irritates the psoas (ureter over it)","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-80-1.png","cap":"Kidney and ureter running down over the psoas major","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 80","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":354},{"src":"sl/O074-44.jpg","cap":"Ureter crosses the psoas; kidney stones cause psoas dysfunction","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/O074-43.jpg","cap":"Organic causes to rule out in psoas syndrome","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-34-1","s":"[Same patient: psoas syndrome after a long sit.] After fixing the lumbar vertebral somatic dysfunction, she says that the pain is now in her lower back. What would NOT be a differential diagnosis?","o":["A. Kidney stone","B. Crohn's disease","C. Fibroid tumor","D. Ovarian disease","E. Nutcracker syndrome"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Nutcracker syndrome","a":"","why":"<b>E. Nutcracker syndrome.</b> The organic causes of psoas dysfunction: <b>kidney stones</b>, irritable bowel, <b>Crohn's disease</b>, ulcerative colitis, <b>ovarian disease</b>, <b>fibroid tumors</b>, plus colon, appendix, prostate, kidney, disc and hip problems. Nutcracker syndrome (left renal vein squeezed between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery) is not on it.<br>A. Kidney stone: the ureter runs over the psoas.<br>B. Crohn's disease: listed.<br>C. Fibroid tumor: listed.<br>D. Ovarian disease: listed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":34,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O097-50-1","s":"A patient presents to the ED complaining of severe lower right back pain that started ten hours earlier. The patient reports having a small amount of blood in his urine as well. You suspect that he has a kidney stone. Which of the following dysfunctions would you expect to find in this patient?","o":["A. Right psoas spasm","B. Right QL spasm","C. Left Piriformis spasm","D. Left psoas spasm","E. Left QL spasm"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right psoas spasm","a":"","why":"<b>A. Right psoas spasm.</b> The <b>ureter runs down over the psoas</b>, so a passing kidney stone often irritates the <b>same-side psoas</b> (expect a positive Thomas test on that side). Right flank pain with blood in the urine → right psoas spasm.<br>B. Right quadratus lumborum (QL) spasm: the kidney rests on the QL, but stones are linked to the psoas.<br>C. Left piriformis spasm: can come later in a right psoas syndrome, not from the stone itself.<br>D. Left psoas: wrong side.<br>E. Left QL: wrong muscle and side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":50,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":50,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":121,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":125,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O069-61-1","s":"Your patient with a psoas spasm has a paraspinal area at T10-T12 that is warm and boggy on the right. You suspect which of the following?","o":["A. Infection of the quadratus lumborum","B. Irritable bowel syndrome","C. Kidney stone","D. Piriformis muscle spasm","E. Poor posture and mechanics in lifting"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Kidney stone","a":"","why":"<b>C. Kidney stone.</b> Warm, boggy paraspinal tissue is an <b>acute viscerosomatic reflex</b>. The kidney and upper ureter get sympathetics from about <b>T10–T11</b> (lower ureter T12–L2), here on the right. The <b>ureter runs over the psoas</b>, so a passing stone often triggers psoas spasm.<br>A. QL (quadratus lumborum) infection is not a recognized cause of a T10–T12 visceral reflex.<br>B. Irritable bowel can upset the psoas, but it is chronic; chronic reflexes feel ropy and cool, not warm and boggy.<br>D. Piriformis spasm is somatic and sits in the buttock.<br>E. Poor lifting strains muscle; it does not make a segmental visceral reflex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA Functional Lumbar Spine","sl":61,"f":"O069"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":69,"f":"O107"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":18,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O099-25-1","s":"Student Dr. Augustin comes to you complaining of back pain on the right. Since he is currently studying hard for his cardiac module exam, you know the pain must be pretty bad. After assessing him, you find a piriformis tender point on the left, positive Thomas Test on the right, and increased hip flexion on the right during range of motion testing. You notice that he is sweating, and he says he really needs to pee. Which of the following could be the root cause of his symptoms?","o":["A. Urinary tract infection","B. Thoracic radiculitis","C. Kidney stone","D. T3-T6NRrSl"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Kidney stone","a":"","why":"<b>C. Kidney stone.</b> Left piriformis tender point, positive right Thomas test and increased right hip flexion = <b>right psoas spasm</b>. Add sweating and urinary urgency, and the root cause is a <b>stone in the ureter</b>, which runs over the psoas and irritates it.<br>A. A urinary tract infection (UTI) gives urgency but does not cause psoas spasm.<br>B. Thoracic radiculitis: band-like chest-wall pain, no psoas signs.<br>D. T3–T6 NRrSl (neutral, rotated right, side-bent left): an upper thoracic group curve, unrelated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":25,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":63,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":40,"label":"Psoas OMT: L1–L2 first, then psoas ME","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-49.jpg","cap":"Treat the upper-lumbar flexion key lesion first; no HVLA acutely","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 49"},{"src":"sl/O074-52.jpg","cap":"Passive extension stretch over a roll for flexed L1–L2","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"O074-48-1","s":"Full-blown left psoas syndrome: where to begin the OMT treatment?","o":["A. The sacral torsion on the left oblique axis","B. The key upper lumbar (L1–L2) flexion somatic dysfunction","C. The lumbosacral junction (L5–S1), with HVLA","D. The left psoas itself, with muscle energy","E. The right piriformis tender point, with counterstrain"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The key somatic dysfunction at L1–L2 (upper lumbar flexion / non-neutral dysfunction).","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> In osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) of psoas syndrome, treat the <b>key lesion first</b>: the upper lumbar (<b>L1–L2</b>, sometimes T12 or L3) <b>flexion</b> somatic dysfunction. This alone may restore function. Acutely, use only techniques that bring the upper lumbar into <b>extension</b>.<br>A. The sacral torsion is secondary and often resolves once L1–L2 is treated.<br>C. Avoid the unstable lumbosacral area; HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude) is not tolerated acutely.<br>D. Psoas muscle energy comes after the key lesion.<br>E. The right piriformis spasm is a downstream compensation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas","sl":48,"f":"O074"}],"t":1},{"id":"O077-2-7","s":"How do you position your patient when performing Muscle Energy for the Psoas?","o":["A. Prone with the hip in marked Flexion","B. Supine with the hip in marked Flexion","C. Prone with the effected side/leg off the table","D. Supine with the effected side/leg off the table"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Supine with the effected side/leg off the table","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> The psoas is mainly a <b>hip flexor</b>, so its barrier is <b>hip extension</b>. With the patient <b>supine</b> and the affected leg hanging <b>off the table</b>, the hip extends; the patient then lifts the knee toward the ceiling (hip flexion) against your counterforce. This is muscle energy (ME).<br>A and B. Marked hip flexion shortens the psoas — that is its ease, not its barrier.<br>C. Prone with the leg off the side drops the hip into flexion — the wrong direction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":2,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":190,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O111-77-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents for low back pain that started over the weekend. He reports feeling some discomfort after a lot of yard work (indicating frequently bending down to the side to pick up heavy objects), however he notes the pain became much more significant the following day (after sleeping on his stomach). He currently describes pain that is mostly on the left side of his low back with some radiation to his groin, however today he has started to feel some pain in his right gluteal region and upper hamstring as well. Neurologic testing including SLR, strength, sensation, and DTR in the lower extremities is intact. He has a positive Thomas test on the left. Which of the following treatments would likely be most beneficial for this patient?","o":["A. Muscle energy for L3 ERSr","B. Muscle energy for the 12th rib","C. HVLA for L1-2 FRSl","D. Passive myofascial for the QL in prone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"HVLA for L1-2 FRSl","a":"","why":"<b>C. High-velocity low-amplitude (HVLA) thrust to L1–2 FRSl.</b> Left groin pain, new right buttock pain and a <b>positive left Thomas test</b> = left psoas syndrome. Its <b>key lesion</b>, L1–L2 flexed, rotated and side-bent left (FRSl), is <b>treated first</b>, and this is the only option aimed at it. Caution: HVLA may not be tolerated in the acute stage, when gentle extension techniques (muscle energy, counterstrain, passive extension) are used first.<br>A. L3 extended, rotated and side-bent right (ERSr): wrong level and motion.<br>B and D. The 12th rib technique and prone myofascial release treat the quadratus lumborum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":77,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":40,"label":"Home stretches: Lancelot for psoas; hamstring stretch","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O100-33-1.jpg","cap":"Lancelot stretch and psoas stretch photos: back hip in extension","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 33","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":240},{"src":"sl/O074-54.jpg","cap":"Psoas stretches, including the Lancelot lunge","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-32-1","s":"[Same patient: 23 y/o with leg pain after getting up suddenly from a Lord of the Rings movie marathon (psoas syndrome).] What home exercise would you give to this patient?","o":["A. Sit ups","B. Downward dog","C. Lancelot stretch","D. Cat - cow stretch","E. Planks"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lancelot stretch","a":"","why":"<b>C. Lancelot stretch.</b> This kneeling lunge takes the back hip into <b>extension</b>, lengthening the tight psoas — the home stretch for psoas syndrome once it is no longer acute (extension exercises are indicated).<br>A. Sit-ups shorten the psoas; they are on the 'don'ts' list.<br>B. Downward dog flexes the hips, stretching the hamstrings and calves.<br>D. Cat-cow moves the spine; it does not extend the hip.<br>E. Planks strengthen the core; they do not stretch the psoas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":32,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O077-4-10","s":"What muscle is being stretched in the figure below?","o":["A. Rectus Abdominus","B. Quadriceps","C. Hamstrings","D. Psoas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hamstrings","a":"","why":"<b>C. Hamstrings.</b> The heel rests on the table with the <b>knee straight</b> while she <b>bends forward at the hips</b>. Hip flexion plus knee extension lengthens the hamstrings, which cross the back of both joints — the same position as a straight leg raise.<br>A. Rectus abdominis is stretched by trunk extension (arching back).<br>B. Quadriceps are stretched by knee flexion (heel to buttock) with the hip extended.<br>D. Psoas is stretched by hip extension, as in a lunge.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O077-4-10.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":4,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":196,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":41,"label":"Piriformis anatomy: S2–S4 sacrum to greater trochanter","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-96-2.png","cap":"Piriformis from sacrum to greater trochanter, sciatic nerve emerging below","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 96","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1120},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-96-1.png","cap":"Sacrum: piriformis origin around the anterior S2–S4 foramina (red outline)","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 96","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":580},{"src":"sl/O074-61.jpg","cap":"Piriformis origin, insertion and S1–S2 innervation","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 61"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-95-1","s":"What is the origin and insertion of the piriformis?","o":["A. Spinous processes of L4-L5 to the superior-medial aspect of the greater trochanter","B. Anterior-lateral surface of S2-S4 at the SI joint capsule/ greater sciatic foramen/ sacrotuberous ligament to the superior-medial aspect of the greater trochanter","C. Anterior-lateral surface of S2-S4 at the SI joint capsule/ greater sciatic foramen/ sacrotuberous ligament to the inferior-medial aspect of the greater trochanter","D. Spinous processes of L4-L5 to the inferior-medial aspect of the greater trochanter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior-lateral surface of S2-S4 at the SI joint capsule/ greater sciatic foramen/ sacrotuberous ligament to the superior-medial aspect of the greater trochanter","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> The piriformis arises from the <b>anterior-lateral sacrum at S2–S4</b> (near the sacroiliac joint capsule, greater sciatic foramen and sacrotuberous ligament) and inserts on the <b>superior-medial greater trochanter</b> by a round tendon.<br>A. Lumbar spinous processes are not a piriformis attachment.<br>C. Right origin, but the tendon reaches the <b>superior</b>, not inferior, part of the trochanter.<br>D. Wrong at both ends.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":95,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O095-97-1","s":"What is the innervation of the piriformis?","o":["A. Sciatic nerve (L4–S3)","B. Femoral nerve branches (L2–L4)","C. Spinal nerves S1 and S2 (occasionally L5)","D. Superior gluteal nerve (L4–S1)","E. Inferior gluteal nerve (L5–S2)"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Spinal nerves S1 and S2, occasionally L5.","a":"","why":"<b>C. S1 and S2 (occasionally L5).</b> The piriformis is supplied by small direct branches of the <b>S1 and S2</b> spinal nerves, sometimes L5.<br>A. The sciatic nerve passes <b>under</b> (anterior to) the piriformis but does not supply it.<br>B. Femoral nerve branches supply the iliacus, not the piriformis.<br>D. Superior gluteal nerve: gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae.<br>E. Inferior gluteal nerve: gluteus maximus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":97,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":41,"label":"Sciatic nerve: L4–S3, passes under the piriformis","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-102-1.png","cap":"Sciatic nerve vs piriformis: 88% below, 11% split, rare variants","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 102","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1874},{"src":"sl/O074-65.jpg","cap":"Sciatic nerve from ventral rami L4–S3, on piriformis","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 65"},{"src":"sl/O074-66.jpg","cap":"MRI: sciatic nerve running deep to the piriformis","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-98-1","s":"The sciatic nerve is formed by the ___ rami of ___ – ___ and converges on the anterior surface of the piriformis. Fill in the blanks.","o":["A. Ventral rami of L5–S2","B. Ventral rami of L2–L4","C. Dorsal rami of L4–S3","D. Ventral rami of L4–S3","E. Ventral rami of S2–S4"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Ventral rami of L4 – S3.","a":"","why":"<b>D. Ventral rami of L4–S3.</b> The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body (about 2 cm wide), forms from the <b>ventral rami of L4–S3</b> and gathers on the <b>anterior surface of the piriformis</b>.<br>A. L5–S2 is the inferior gluteal nerve (to gluteus maximus).<br>B. Ventral rami L2–L4 form the femoral and obturator nerves.<br>C. Dorsal rami supply the back muscles and skin, not the limb.<br>E. Ventral rami S2–S4 form the pudendal nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":98,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O095-97-3","s":"What nerve is commonly affected by piriformis action?","o":["A. Obturator nerve","B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","C. Femoral nerve","D. Sciatic nerve","E. Genitofemoral nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"The sciatic nerve.","a":"","why":"<b>D. Sciatic nerve.</b> Piriformis syndrome is a <b>peripheral neuritis of the sciatic nerve</b>: the nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen <b>right under (anterior to) the piriformis</b>, so spasm or a split muscle squeezes it.<br>A. Obturator nerve: leaves through the obturator foramen to the medial thigh.<br>B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve: trapped under the inguinal ligament (meralgia paresthetica).<br>C. Femoral nerve: runs between psoas and iliacus to the front of the thigh.<br>E. Genitofemoral nerve: pierces the psoas, not the piriformis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":97,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":133,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":42,"label":"Piriformis syndrome causes: split nerve vs trauma","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-129-1.png","cap":"Piriformis history: buttock trauma, long sitting, back-pocket items, overuse","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 129","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":766},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-102-1.png","cap":"Anatomic variants: sciatic nerve splitting or piercing the piriformis","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 102","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1874},{"src":"sl/O074-67.jpg","cap":"Primary (anatomic, under 15%) vs secondary (trauma) piriformis syndrome","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 67"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-100-1","s":"What is an example of primary piriformis syndrome?","o":["A. Inflammation and muscle spasm creating nerve compression","B. Overuse (running/walking)","C. Split sciatic nerve","D. Direct compression - “wallet neuritis”"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Split sciatic nerve","a":"","why":"<b>C. Split sciatic nerve.</b> <b>Primary</b> piriformis syndrome (under 15%) has an <b>anatomical</b> cause: a split piriformis, a split sciatic nerve or an unusual sciatic nerve path. <b>Secondary</b> piriformis syndrome (more common) has a precipitating cause such as trauma.<br>A. Inflammation and spasm after a blow to the buttock: secondary (macrotrauma).<br>B. Overuse from running or walking: secondary (microtrauma).<br>D. Wallet neuritis from sitting on a wallet: secondary (microtrauma by compression).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":100,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O099-34-1","s":"Student Dr. Perfetto comes to see you, complaining of a sciatica flare up. Because of your impeccable knowledge of OMM, you know that there is a trigger point commonly mistaken for sciatica. If student Dr. Perfetto truly has just a trigger point, what else might you expect to find?","o":["A. Ipsilateral positive Thomas test","B. Ipsilateral short leg","C. History of trauma to the ipsilateral buttock","D. Ipsilateral internal rotation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"History of trauma to the ipsilateral buttock","a":"","why":"<b>C. History of trauma to the ipsilateral buttock.</b> The trigger point most often mistaken for sciatica is the <b>piriformis</b>. Piriformis syndrome usually follows <b>buttock trauma</b>: a fall, long sitting, a wallet in the back pocket, or overuse (running, golf).<br>A. Ipsilateral positive Thomas test: psoas spasm.<br>B. Ipsilateral short leg: quadratus lumborum (QL) spasm.<br>D. The leg is held in <b>external</b> rotation, so internal rotation is restricted, not present.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":34,"f":"O099"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":42,"label":"Piriformis syndrome exam: leg turned out, SLR painful","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-129-2.png","cap":"Piriformis exam: neuro normal; straight leg raise painful throughout","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 129","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":587},{"src":"sl/ta-O105-15-1.jpg","cap":"Piriformis syndrome: buttock and posterior leg pain, patient bent forward","where":"Fall 2021 lumbar review (SGA drive), slide 15","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":400},{"src":"sl/O078-12.jpg","cap":"Piriformis tender point between sacrum and trochanter, sciatic below","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/O074-75.jpg","cap":"Observation — painful gait, leg externally rotated, flexed spine","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 75"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-142-1","s":"In Piriformis syndrome you will find:","o":["A. Ipsilateral internal rotation","B. Increased extension of the lumbar spine","C. Contralateral SI pain","D. No symptoms with straight leg raising"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Contralateral SI pain","a":"","why":"<b>C. Contralateral SI pain.</b> Piriformis syndrome history: pain after sitting 15–20 minutes, pain over the sacrum and greater trochanter radiating to the knee, pain rising from sitting, and <b>contralateral sacroiliac (SI) pain</b> (the spasm can pull the sacrum into a forward torsion to the opposite side).<br>A. The leg is held in <b>external</b> rotation on the same side.<br>B. The lumbar spine is restricted, <b>especially in extension</b>.<br>D. The straight leg raise is <b>painful throughout</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":142,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O096-128-1","s":"Patient comes in with lower back pain that radiates throughout the back of her leg. Upon further history taking, you find out that she is a truck driver and sits with her wallet in her back pocket. What is the most likely diagnosis of this patient and what special test would you perform?","o":["A. Quadratus Lumborum and Hip Drop Test","B. Psoas and Thomas Test","C. Piriformis and Straight Leg Test","D. Piriformis and Thomas Test"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Piriformis and Straight Leg Test","a":"","why":"<b>C. Piriformis syndrome and straight leg raise (SLR).</b> Pain down the back of the leg in a driver who sits on a <b>back-pocket wallet</b> (wallet neuritis) = the piriformis compressing the sciatic nerve. Its special test is the <b>SLR, painful throughout</b> the range, with a normal neuro exam.<br>A. Quadratus lumborum (QL) with hip drop: low back to hip pain and a short-looking leg.<br>B. Psoas with Thomas: flexed posture and front-of-thigh pain.<br>D. Right muscle, wrong test: the Thomas test checks the psoas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":128,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":106,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O095-104-1","s":"A transgender woman came into your office complaining of pain in her buttock that is most intense after sitting for 15-20 minutes. She explains that when she takes long drives, she likes to store everything in her back jean pockets. What would you expect to find on observation/ palpation?","o":["A. Tenderness without radiation","B. Contralateral external rotation of the leg","C. Abnormal sensory/ motor/ DTR","D. Increased flexion of the spine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased flexion of the spine","a":"","why":"<b>D. Increased flexion of the spine.</b> Buttock pain after 15–20 minutes of sitting with items in the back pockets = <b>piriformis syndrome</b>. On observation: altered painful gait, <b>leg externally rotated</b>, <b>increased spinal flexion</b> (the lumbar spine is restricted, especially in extension).<br>A. The pain radiates (low back, hip, down the leg to the knee).<br>B. The external rotation is on the <b>same</b> side.<br>C. Sensory, motor and DTR (deep tendon reflex) exams are <b>normal</b>, which separates it from a disc herniation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":104,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O100-40-1","s":"[Previous question: 32 y/o with pain radiating down one leg that is worse when he sits; a well leg raise test was done.] The result from the previous test was negative. What patient observation would you most likely see?","o":["A. Inability to stand up straight","B. Externally rotated leg","C. Abnormal neuro exam","D. increased extension of the spine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Externally rotated leg (Piriformis syndrome)","a":"","why":"<b>B. Externally rotated leg.</b> Leg pain worse with sitting and a negative well-leg raise (no disc or nerve root sign) points to <b>piriformis syndrome</b>. The tight piriformis is an external rotator, so the leg rests <b>turned out</b>.<br>A. Inability to stand up straight (flexed, side-bent posture): psoas syndrome.<br>C. The neuro exam is normal in piriformis syndrome.<br>D. The spine shows increased <b>flexion</b>; extension is restricted.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":40,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O100-16-1","s":"30 y/o male patient comes to the office with pain on his left buttock. He notes that there is the most pain when you press the left greater trochanter and and left PSIS. Which is the most likely problem?","o":["A. left piriformis","B. right piriformis","C. left psoas","D. right gluteus medius","E. rotatores"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"left piriformis","a":"","why":"<b>A. Left piriformis.</b> Left buttock pain worst over the <b>left greater trochanter and sacral/PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine) area</b> traces the piriformis, which runs from the sacrum to the greater trochanter; its tender points sit at the midpole sacrum and the posteromedial trochanter.<br>B. Right piriformis: wrong side.<br>C. Left psoas: low back and front-of-thigh pain.<br>D. Right gluteus medius: wrong side; pain along the posterior iliac crest and buttock.<br>E. Rotatores: deep segmental pain beside the spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":16,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O111-85-1","s":"A 52-year-old truck driver presents for recurrent episodes of right-sided low back pain. He jokingly describes the pain as \"a pain in my butt\" pointing to his mid gluteal region on the right. He notes the pain is worst after long drives, and will occasionally be associated with radiation down the back of his right leg, stopping above the knee. His other medical history is significant for diabetes that is well managed. He denies any other associated symptoms including: weight loss, changes in bladder or bowel function, night sweats. On exam, he is standing with an overtly externally rotated right lower extremity. Neurologic testing for his lower extremities including strength, sensation, and reflexes is intact bilaterally. Which of the following findings is likely present?","o":["A. Positive babinski reflex","B. Apparent difference in leg length","C. Right sided hip flexion","D. A palpable mass above the iliac crest"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Apparent difference in leg length","a":"","why":"<b>B. Apparent difference in leg length.</b> Buttock pain worse after long drives, spreading down the back of the thigh, a leg turned out and a normal neuro exam = <b>right piriformis syndrome</b>. The spasm pulls the sacrum into a <b>forward torsion to the opposite side</b>, which makes the legs look unequal.<br>A. A positive Babinski reflex is an upper motor neuron sign; the neuro exam here is normal.<br>C. A hip-flexed posture belongs to psoas syndrome; piriformis syndrome flexes the spine and turns the leg out.<br>D. The piriformis mass lies in the buttock, below the iliac crest; the quadratus lumborum lies above it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":85,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":42,"label":"Piriformis tender points: lateral sacral border, trochanter","slides":[{"src":"sl/O078-12.jpg","cap":"Piriformis tender point between lateral sacrum and greater trochanter","where":"2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas & Quadratus, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/O074-82.jpg","cap":"Counterstrain points at midpole sacrum, piriformis, posteromedial trochanter","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"O111-83-1","s":"A 52-year-old truck driver presents for recurrent episodes of right-sided low back pain. He jokingly describes the pain as \"a pain in my butt\" pointing to his mid gluteal region on the right. He notes the pain is worst after long drives, and will occasionally be associated with radiation down the back of his right leg, stopping above the knee. His other medical history is significant for diabetes that is well managed. He denies any other associated symptoms including: weight loss, changes in bladder or bowel function, night sweats. On exam, he is standing with an overtly externally rotated right lower extremity. Neurologic testing for his lower extremities including strength, sensation, and reflexes is intact bilaterally. Where are you most likely to palpate tender points for this patient?","o":["A. Lateral sacral border","B. Superior to the PSIS","C. Anterior hip inferior to the inguinal ligament","D. Inferior to the 12th rib"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral sacral border","a":"","why":"<b>A. Lateral sacral border.</b> In right piriformis syndrome the tender points lie along the piriformis: at the <b>midpole of the lateral sacral border</b>, in the belly of the muscle and at the <b>posteromedial greater trochanter</b>. These are its counterstrain points.<br>B. Above the posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS) is the iliac crest, where quadratus lumborum and gluteus medius points lie.<br>C. The anterior hip below the inguinal ligament is iliopsoas territory.<br>D. Below the 12th rib lies the quadratus lumborum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":83,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":42,"label":"Psoas vs piriformis syndrome: Thomas test decides","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-58-1.png","cap":"Positive Thomas test: knee hugged, opposite straight leg lifts off the mat","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 58","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":976},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-129-2.png","cap":"Piriformis exam: neuro normal, straight leg raise painful throughout","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 129","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":587},{"src":"sl/O074-75.jpg","cap":"Piriformis syndrome observation, leg externally rotated, spine flexed","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 75"}],"q":[{"id":"O110-1-1","s":"What is a key physical exam finding that would differentiate psoas syndrome from piriformis syndrome?","o":["A. Positive Thomas test: the straight thigh lifts off the table","B. Positive hip drop test on the painful side","C. Thigh pain that stops at the knee","D. Normal lower-extremity strength, sensation and reflexes","E. Tender piriformis with restricted hip internal rotation"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"A positive Thomas test (hip flexion contracture from the hypertonic psoas) points to psoas syndrome. Piriformis syndrome causes no hip flexion contracture; it shows an externally rotated leg on the affected side with a tender piriformis and buttock.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Positive Thomas test.</b> Hugging one knee flattens the lordosis; if the other thigh lifts off the table, that <b>iliopsoas is tight</b>. In piriformis syndrome the Thomas test is negative; the leg rests <b>externally rotated</b> and the straight leg raise is painful throughout.<br>B. A positive hip drop points to the quadratus lumborum.<br>C. Both send thigh pain that stops at the knee (front or side for psoas, back for piriformis).<br>D. The neuro exam is normal in both.<br>E. A tender piriformis with restricted internal rotation occurs in both: same side in piriformis syndrome, opposite side in psoas syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 study guide (SGA)","n":"Montana OMM Exam 2 Study Guide 2024 (sample questions)","sl":null,"f":"O110"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":43,"label":"Piriformis: external rotator; ME barrier internal rotation","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-116-1.png","cap":"Prone piriformis muscle energy: knee bent, leg turned into internal rotation","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 116","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":794},{"src":"sl/O069-72.jpg","cap":"Piriformis is one of six lateral (external) rotators of the hip","where":"2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 72"},{"src":"sl/O074-63.jpg","cap":"Action — external rotation straight, abduction at 90° flexion","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 63"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-65-1","s":"The action of the piriformis muscle is","o":["A. External rotation of the thigh at the hip and some thigh extension","B. Adduction of the thigh at the hip","C. Hip lateral rotator when the hip is being flexed at more than 90 degrees","D. Medially rotates the femur during hip extension"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"External rotation of the thigh at the hip and some thigh extension","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> With the lower limb <b>straight</b>, the piriformis <b>externally rotates</b> the thigh and may help extend it. With the hip <b>flexed 90°</b> it <b>abducts</b> the thigh and may <b>internally rotate</b> it.<br>B. It abducts, not adducts, the thigh.<br>C. Past 90° of hip flexion it becomes a <b>medial</b> (internal) rotator, not a lateral one.<br>D. In hip extension it rotates the femur <b>laterally</b> (externally), not medially.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":65,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":97,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O077-2-5","s":"You evaluate your patient with right sided low back and hip pain and discover a hypertonic piriformis on the right. What freedom might you expect to find when motion testing?","o":["A. External Rotation","B. Internal Rotation","C. Hip Flexion","D. Hip Extension"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"External Rotation","a":"","why":"<b>A. External rotation.</b> The piriformis is a hip <b>external rotator</b> (and an abductor when the hip is flexed 90°). A hypertonic piriformis holds the hip turned out, so external rotation (ER) is the <b>freedom</b> and internal rotation (IR) is <b>restricted</b>.<br>B. Internal rotation is the restricted direction (the barrier).<br>C. Hip flexion is mainly the iliopsoas.<br>D. Hip extension is mainly gluteus maximus and hamstrings.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":2,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":186,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O077-2-6","s":"When using Piriformis Muscle Energy on your patient, what position will you place your patient in to engage the barrier?","o":["A. External Rotation","B. Internal Rotation","C. Abduction","D. Hip Flexion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal Rotation","a":"","why":"<b>B. Internal rotation.</b> Muscle energy (ME) starts at the barrier — the motion that stretches the tight muscle. The piriformis externally rotates the hip, so you position into <b>internal rotation</b> (with the hip flexed, also adduction); the patient then pushes toward external rotation against you.<br>A. External rotation is the freedom — the direction the patient pushes, not the setup.<br>C. Abduction shortens the piriformis (it abducts the flexed hip).<br>D. Hip flexion alone does not engage the piriformis barrier; rotation does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Study Guide and Questions - Lumbar QL & Psoas","sl":2,"f":"O077"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":188,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-115-1","s":"A 65-year-old truck driver presents after a long drive with the right-sided low back pain. After taking a thorough history, you find out that the pain radiates to the knee and is usually associated with rising from a seating position. Upon checking his range of motion, you notice a restriction in internal rotation of the right hip. What would be the correct placement and technique steps in order to improve patient’s condition?","o":["A. Pt. is supine and at the edge of the table. Dr. bends pt’s knee on the unaffected side and brings it to the pt’s chest, whereas the leg on the side of a dysfunction is dangling off the table. Dr. stabilizes flexed leg, and presses down on dysfx leg toward floor as pt. resists toward ceiling. (3-5 seconds, 3x w/passive stretch)","B. Pt. is prone. You bend pt’s leg and bring out toward you placing in axilla. Passively internal rotate leg while monitoring the affected muscle and asking pt to resist. (3-5 seconds, 3x w/passive stretch)","C. Pt. is on side (lateral recumbent position), with the affected muscle facing up. Place your forearms on hip and shoulder. Stretch the affected muscle asking pt. to the resist brings the hip and shoulder together. (3-5 seconds, 3x w/passive stretch)","D. Pt. is prone. You stand on the opposite side having the cephalad hand stabilized at the 12th rib. Your caudad hand grasps the ASIS, and you bring it towards yourself. Repeat in slow, rhythmic motions. Reassess the affected muscle’s tone at completion."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pt. is prone. You bend pt’s leg and bring out toward you placing in axilla. Passively internal rotate leg while monitoring the affected muscle and asking pt to resist. (3-5 seconds, 3x w/passive stretch)","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> Pain to the knee after long sitting with <b>restricted right hip internal rotation</b> = right piriformis syndrome. Piriformis muscle energy (ME): patient <b>prone</b>, knee bent; you <b>internally rotate</b> the hip to the barrier (foot swings out) while monitoring the muscle; the patient pushes back toward external rotation 3–5 seconds, 3 times, then a passive stretch.<br>A. Supine, other knee to chest, leg off the table: psoas ME.<br>C. Side-lying, bringing hip and shoulder together: quadratus lumborum (QL) ME.<br>D. Prone, hand on rib 12, rhythmic pull on the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine): QL passive myofascial release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":115,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":87,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":43,"label":"Piriformis OMT: indirect CS/FPR; direct ME/HVLA","slides":[{"src":"sl/O074-82.jpg","cap":"Piriformis OMT — indirect counterstrain/FPR; direct ME and HVLA","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 82"},{"src":"sl/O074-79.jpg","cap":"Conservative treatment — NSAIDs, ice, rest, stretch, OMT","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 79"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-108-1","s":"OMT for piriformis syndrome: What are your indirect techniques?","o":["A. Counterstrain and high-velocity low-amplitude (HVLA)","B. Muscle energy (ME) and facilitated positional release (FPR)","C. Muscle energy (ME) and high-velocity low-amplitude (HVLA)","D. Counterstrain and facilitated positional release (FPR)"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Counterstrain (tender points at the midpole of the sacrum, the piriformis muscle and the posteromedial trochanter) and facilitated positional release (FPR).","a":"","why":"<b>D. Counterstrain and FPR.</b> The indirect techniques reported most often for piriformis syndrome are <b>counterstrain</b> (tender points at the <b>midpole sacrum</b>, the <b>piriformis</b> and the <b>posteromedial trochanter</b>) and <b>facilitated positional release (FPR)</b>. Both move the tissue into its ease.<br>A. Counterstrain is indirect, but HVLA is direct.<br>B. FPR is indirect, but ME is direct.<br>C. ME (muscle energy) and HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude): the direct pair.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":108,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O095-108-2","s":"OMT for piriformis syndrome: What are your direct techniques?","o":["A. Muscle energy (ME) and high-velocity low-amplitude (HVLA)","B. Counterstrain and facilitated positional release (FPR)","C. Muscle energy (ME) and facilitated positional release (FPR)","D. Counterstrain and high-velocity low-amplitude (HVLA)"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Muscle energy, and HVLA for sacral and iliac somatic dysfunction.","a":"","why":"<b>A. Muscle energy and HVLA.</b> The direct techniques for piriformis syndrome are <b>muscle energy (ME)</b> — no absolute contraindication — and <b>high-velocity low-amplitude (HVLA)</b> for the sacral and iliac somatic dysfunction (caution with osteoporosis).<br>B. Counterstrain and FPR (facilitated positional release): the indirect pair.<br>C. ME is direct, but FPR is indirect.<br>D. HVLA is direct, but counterstrain is indirect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":108,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O099-27-1","s":"Student Dr. Smith comes to you a few days after making an eight hour drive back to Middletown from his hometown in Pennsylvania. He complains of pain in his right hip, down the back of his leg to the knee. He’s very into fitness, but notes that he hasn’t been able to work out because most hip movements exacerbate the pain. You ask him where his wallet was during the trip, and he said that he keeps it in his back right pocket. As he walks into your office, you see him grimacing slightly. What treatment option would be the best?","o":["A. Opioids","B. Gentle, indirect OMT techniques","C. Surgical decompression","D. Heating pad and rest"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gentle, indirect OMT techniques","a":"","why":"<b>B. Gentle, indirect OMT.</b> A painful, acute piriformis syndrome is best met with <b>gentle indirect osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT)</b> — counterstrain or facilitated positional release — the techniques reported most often for it. Early conservative care (NSAIDs, ice, rest, muscle relaxants, stretching) helps about 79% of patients.<br>A. Opioids: not first line; NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) are.<br>C. Surgical decompression: only if all else fails.<br>D. Rest helps, but ice is used, not heat, and OMT is the key treatment.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":27,"f":"O099"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":44,"label":"Trigger point: tender nodule in a taut muscle band","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-70-1.png","cap":"Trigger point vs counterstrain tender point","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 70","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1580},{"src":"sl/O076-2.jpg","cap":"Table: Chapman's points vs trigger points vs tender points","where":"2026 week 13 Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco), page 2"},{"src":"sl/O075-5.jpg","cap":"TrP = hyperirritable spot, nodule in a taut band","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-55-1","s":"Which of the following best describes a trigger point?","o":["A. Nodular, 2-3 mm and located in deep fascia","B. A taut band located in muscle","C. Discrete, tender points located in tendons, muscles, or ligaments","D. No referral of pain"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A taut band located in muscle","a":"","why":"<b>B. A taut band located in muscle.</b> A trigger point (TrP) is a hyperirritable spot in skeletal muscle: a tender nodule in a <b>taut band</b> that, when pressed, gives <b>predictable referred pain</b>, motor and autonomic effects, and a local twitch response.<br>A. Nodular, 2–3 mm, in deep fascia: a <b>Chapman's</b> reflex point.<br>C. Discrete tender points in tendon, muscle or ligament: Jones counterstrain <b>tender points</b>.<br>D. No referral of pain: also tender points; TrPs refer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":55,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":55,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":36,"f":"O099"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":44,"label":"Trigger point vs tender point: referral, band, twitch","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O100-109-1.png","cap":"Trigger point vs counterstrain tender point: band, twitch, referral, location","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 109","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1091},{"src":"sl/O075-21.jpg","cap":"Trigger point vs tender point table","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-117-1","s":"Which of these statement is characteristic of a trigger point?","o":["A. Typically no characteristic pain pattern","B. Taut band not present","C. Elicits twitch response with snapping palpation","D. Nontender","E. Located in muscle, tendon, ligament or fascia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Elicits twitch response with snapping palpation","a":"","why":"<b>C. Twitch response with snapping palpation.</b> Only a trigger point (TrP) has a <b>taut band</b> that twitches when snapped and a <b>characteristic referral pattern</b>, and it lies in <b>muscle</b>. The other options describe a Jones counterstrain tender point.<br>A. No characteristic pain pattern: tender point.<br>B. Taut band not present: tender point.<br>D. Nontender: wrong for both; both are locally tender.<br>E. Muscle, tendon, ligament or fascia: tender point locations.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":117,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":107,"f":"O100"},{"t":"2024 study guide (SGA)","n":"Montana OMM Exam 2 Study Guide 2024 (sample questions)","sl":null,"f":"O110"}]},{"id":"O104-22-1","s":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of a myofascial trigger point?","o":["A. Referred pain","B. Twitch response","C. Associated with autonomic phenomenon","D. Located in ligaments","E. Tender palpable nodule"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Located in ligaments","a":"","why":"<b>D. Located in ligaments.</b> A myofascial trigger point (TrP) lies in <b>skeletal muscle</b>. Points in tendons, ligaments or fascia are Jones counterstrain tender points.<br>A. Referred pain: the TrP hallmark.<br>B. Twitch response: a TrP feature.<br>C. Autonomic phenomena (sweating, tearing, gooseflesh): a TrP feature.<br>E. Tender palpable nodule in a taut band: a TrP feature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":22,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":92,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O100-105-1","s":"Which characteristics describe a CS tenderpoint and not a trigger point?","o":["A. Pt presents with a characteristic pain pattern, with a taut band of tissue and the twitch response is not present","B. Pt presents with pain that has no characteristic pain pattern, is locally tender, and dermographia of skin over thoracic and lumbar are present","C. Taut band is not present, twitch response is not present and no radiating pain pattern when pressed","D. May elicit jump sign when pressed and is locally tender"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Taut band is not present, twitch response is not present and no radiating pain pattern when pressed","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> A counterstrain (CS) tender point has <b>no taut band</b>, <b>no twitch response</b> and <b>no radiating pain</b> when pressed — it is only locally tender.<br>A. A characteristic pain pattern with a taut band is a trigger point (TrP).<br>B. Dermographia of the skin over thoracic and lumbar points goes with TrPs, not tender points.<br>D. Jump sign and local tenderness occur with <b>both</b>, so they do not separate them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":105,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O076-3-8","s":"An elderly male presents with shortness of breath and palpitations. An electrocardiogram is obtained and reveals a supraventricular tachyarrhythmia. The tissue texture abnormality discovered is different from tenderpoints because this type","o":["A. is located in specific areas of the musculoskeletal system","B. is located on the torso and never on the extremities","C. is more tender when palpated","D. results in pain perceived in a distant site called a reference zone","E. usually lacks associated sympathetic dystonia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"results in pain perceived in a distant site called a reference zone","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> A trigger point (TrP) <b>refers pain to a distant reference zone</b> when pressed; a Jones tender point is only <b>locally</b> tender. Referral is the defining difference.<br>A. Both lie in specific, predictable locations.<br>B. Both occur on the trunk and the limbs.<br>C. Both are locally tender; tender points are often exquisitely tender.<br>E. TrPs <b>do</b> cause autonomic effects (sweating, tearing, gooseflesh), so they do not lack sympathetic signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco)","sl":3,"f":"O076"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":201,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 2 Trigger Points (Capobianco)","sl":3,"f":"O102"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":44,"label":"Right pectoralis TrP (ribs 5–6) and SVT","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-124.jpg","cap":"Right pectoralis major TrP between ribs 5–6 and SVT","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 124"},{"src":"sl/O076-1.jpg","cap":"Chapman's points and TrPs: right pectoralis TrP linked to SVT","where":"2026 week 13 Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco), page 1"}],"q":[{"id":"O076-3-7","s":"An elderly male presents with shortness of breath and palpitations. An electrocardiogram is obtained and reveals a supraventricular tachyarrhythmia. Structural examination will most likely reveal which of the following?","o":["A. A crossover point at T6 left","B. flattened thoracic kyphosis involving T5-T8","C. Jones' anterior right rib 3 tenderpoint","D. Travell's right pectoralis major myofascial trigger point","E. lateral curve convex left"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Travell's right pectoralis major myofascial trigger point","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> A Travell <b>trigger point in the right pectoralis major</b>, between the <b>5th and 6th ribs</b> near the sternum, is linked to <b>supraventricular tachyarrhythmia (SVT)</b> — a somatovisceral reflex. Treating it has stopped the arrhythmia in case reports.<br>A. A crossover (where curves or fascial patterns switch direction) at T6 is not tied to SVT.<br>B. A flattened thoracic kyphosis (straight back) is linked to mitral valve prolapse, not SVT.<br>C. Jones counterstrain tender points reflect local somatic dysfunction, not this visceral reflex.<br>E. A lateral (scoliotic) curve is not an arrhythmia sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Practice Questions & Review - Chapman's & Trigger Points (Capobianco)","sl":3,"f":"O076"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 2 Trigger Points (Capobianco)","sl":3,"f":"O102"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":199,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":45,"label":"TrP formation: excess ACh, contraction knots","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-69-2.png","cap":"Trigger point up close: contracted knots between stretched muscle fibers","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 69","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":784},{"src":"sl/O075-9.jpg","cap":"Sarcomere contraction knots form the TrP nodule","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/O075-11.jpg","cap":"Dysfunctional motor end-plates release excess acetylcholine","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-97-1","s":"What forms the characteristic palpable nodule of a muscle trigger point?","o":["A. Formation of a trigger point involves contraction of a sarcomere that normally relaxes","B. Formation of a trigger point that involves the contraction of a sarcomere that forms a contraction knot instead of relaxing","C. Lack of a twitch response","D. Caused by imbalance, dizziness and tinnitus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Formation of a trigger point that involves the contraction of a sarcomere that forms a contraction knot instead of relaxing","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> A trigger point (TrP) forms when sarcomeres <b>contract and fail to relax</b>, making <b>contraction knots</b>; a group of knots close together is the palpable nodule.<br>A. A sarcomere that contracts and then relaxes normally is ordinary muscle work, not a knot.<br>C. TrPs <b>do</b> give a local twitch response.<br>D. Imbalance, dizziness and tinnitus are proprioceptive effects of TrPs, not what forms the nodule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":97,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O099-39-1","s":"[Previous question: a construction worker with an active trigger point (taut band) in the left levator scapulae.] Considering the patient in the previous question, which of the following contributes to the their etiology.","o":["A. Hyper-lengthened muscles leading to fatigue and strain","B. Excess ACh release causing uncontrolled calcium release and sustained muscle contraction","C. Increased ACh-esterase activity","D. Decreased O2/nutrient demand of the affected muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Excess ACh release causing uncontrolled calcium release and sustained muscle contraction","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> The proposed mechanism: <b>dysfunctional motor end-plates release excess acetylcholine (ACh)</b>, leading to calcium release and <b>sustained contraction</b> (contraction knots) with local ischemia.<br>A. Trigger point muscle is <b>shortened</b> and contracted, not hyper-lengthened.<br>C. More acetylcholinesterase would clear ACh faster and calm the end-plate — the opposite.<br>D. Oxygen and nutrient demand <b>rise</b> while circulation falls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":39,"f":"O099"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":45,"label":"TrP stages: tenderness, ischemia, taut band, satellites","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O100-103-1.png","cap":"Stages 1–2 of trigger point development, with local twitch drawing","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 103","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1122},{"src":"sl/O075-13.jpg","cap":"Stage 2 — increased metabolism, reduced circulation, local ischemia","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/O075-15.jpg","cap":"Stage 4 — secondary effects and satellite trigger points","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-99-1","s":"Cycle of increased metabolic demand from contracted muscle, with diminished circulatory mechanisms from tight muscles compressing arteries and veins is which stage of TrP development?","o":["A. Stage 1","B. Stage 2","C. Stage 3"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stage 2 (increased metabolism/reduced circulation)","a":"","why":"<b>B. Stage 2</b> (increased metabolism / reduced circulation): the contracted muscle needs more energy while the tight fibers <b>squeeze arteries and veins</b>, so the trigger point (TrP) becomes a spot of <b>local ischemia</b>. The four stages: 1 hyperirritability (local tenderness, twitch); 2 increased metabolism / reduced circulation; 3 palpable taut band; 4 secondary effects with <b>satellite TrPs</b>.<br>A. Stage 1: hyperirritability and local tenderness only.<br>C. Stage 3: the palpable taut band (contracture from leaking calcium).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":99,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O111-89-1","s":"You are evaluating your partner in OMM lab and during palpation, discover a few areas that are significantly tender when pressed. These tender regions are located along the iliac crest, and below the 12th rib (as shown in the image below) and feel like a very taught band of tissue. When palpated deeply, your partner feels a radiation of pain to the lateral aspect of the hip, as well as the gluteal and groin regions. The rest of the exam is negative for any restrictions in range of motion of the hips or other regions. What type of points did you palpate on your partner?","o":["A. Counterstrain points","B. Grade IV trigger points","C. Chapman points","D. Grade III trigger points"],"g":["D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Counterstrain points","a":"","why":"<b>D. Grade (stage) III trigger points.</b> A <b>taut band</b> that <b>refers pain</b> when pressed is a trigger point (TrP), here in the quadratus lumborum. Stage 3 is the palpable taut band with referred pain; stage 4 adds secondary effects such as <b>restricted joint motion</b> and satellite TrPs, and motion here is full.<br>A. Counterstrain (Jones) tender points have no taut band and no referral.<br>B. Grade IV would show restricted range of motion or satellite TrPs.<br>C. Chapman points are 2–3 mm nodules in deep fascia that reflect organ disease, without referral.","ex":"","fl":["The correct answer is D: a taut band with referred pain is a trigger point, not a counterstrain point, and full range of motion places it at stage 3, not stage 4."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":89,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":45,"label":"Active vs latent trigger points","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O100-113-1.png","cap":"Active TrPs: pain complaint, patient-recognized referral, can spawn satellites","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 113","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1136},{"src":"sl/ta-O100-114-1.png","cap":"Latent TrPs: more common, painful only on palpation, cause stiffness","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 114","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1134},{"src":"sl/O075-19.jpg","cap":"Both active and latent TrPs cause autonomic, proprioceptive effects","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-69-1","s":"What are the characteristics of an Active trigger point","o":["A. Painful only when palpated","B. taut palpable band with a referred pain pattern and possible satellite trigger points in functionally similar muscles","C. stiffness in joints with autonomic and proprioceptive symptoms"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"taut palpable band with a referred pain pattern and possible satellite trigger points in functionally similar muscles","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> An <b>active</b> trigger point (TrP) is a taut, palpable band that refers a <b>patient-recognized pain pattern</b> and can spawn <b>satellite TrPs</b> in muscles lying in its referral zone.<br>A. Painful only when palpated: a <b>latent</b> TrP.<br>C. Joint stiffness is the hallmark of a <b>latent</b> TrP; autonomic and proprioceptive symptoms occur with both types, so they do not define an active one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":69,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O104-23-1","s":"Which of the following statements is accurate with regards to active and latent trigger points?","o":["A. An active trigger point elicits a twitch response when palpated, whereas a latent trigger point does not.","B. A latent trigger point is associated with a pain complaint the patient has, but an active does not.","C. A taut band is only found in active trigger points.","D. Autonomic and proprioceptive symptoms can be associated with both active and latent trigger points.","E. Active trigger points are more common than latent."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Autonomic and proprioceptive symptoms can be associated with both active and latent trigger points.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Both</b> active and latent trigger points (TrPs) can cause <b>autonomic</b> effects (sweating, tearing, runny nose, salivation, gooseflesh) and <b>proprioceptive</b> effects (imbalance, dizziness, tinnitus, misjudging the weight of lifted objects).<br>A. Both types give a local twitch response.<br>B. Reversed: the <b>active</b> TrP causes the pain complaint.<br>C. Both types have a taut band.<br>E. <b>Latent</b> TrPs are more common.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":23,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":94,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-63-1","s":"A 27 y/o female presents to the OMM clinic complaining of pain in her right buttocks after cycling 96 miles the day before. Upon assessment you not that the patient has restricted ROM in right internal rotation at her hip. Pt reports the pain being there all day long which is reproduced by palpation. Upon palpation, you find a trigger point in her right buttocks. Which of the following best describes this TrP and what differentiates it from the other TrP type?","o":["A. Active; Pain only noted by patient upon TrP compression","B. Latent; Pain only noted by patient upon TrP compression","C. Active; Pain noted during movement and other activities and reproduced upon compression","D. Latent; Pain noted during movement and other activities and reproduced upon compression"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Active; Pain noted during movement and other activities and reproduced upon compression","a":"","why":"<b>C. Active; pain with activity, reproduced on compression.</b> An <b>active</b> trigger point (TrP) causes the patient's <b>own pain complaint</b> during the day, is always tender, weakens the muscle and, when pressed, refers a pattern the patient <b>recognizes</b>. A <b>latent</b> TrP hurts <b>only when palpated</b>, and the pain may not feel familiar.<br>A. Pain only on compression describes a latent TrP, not an active one.<br>B. That is the latent description, but this patient has pain all day.<br>D. Pain during daily activity belongs to an active TrP, not a latent one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":63,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":63,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":38,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":119,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":46,"label":"TrP aggravating factors and associated conditions","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O099-43-1.jpg","cap":"Perpetuating factors of TrPs and associated conditions","where":"Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 43","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":2126},{"src":"sl/O075-22.jpg","cap":"TrP aggravators incl. viral infection, cold, hypoglycemia","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/O075-23.jpg","cap":"TrP aggravators incl. leg length inequality, small hemipelvis","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/O075-25.jpg","cap":"Conditions associated with TrPs (thyroid, iron, uric acid)","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-116-1","s":"Which is not considered a perpetuating factor of TrPs?","o":["A. Viral infections","B. Hyperglycemia","C. Length Length Inequality","D. Small hemipelvis","E. Chronic postural or occupational strains"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hyperglycemia","a":"","why":"<b>B. Hyperglycemia.</b> <b>Hypoglycemia</b> (low sugar), not hyperglycemia, aggravates trigger points (TrPs). Other aggravators: strenuous use, pressure, prolonged shortening, cold damp weather or drafts, <b>viral infections</b>, nervous tension, <b>leg length inequality</b>, <b>small hemipelvis</b>, short upper arms, ergonomic factors, <b>chronic postural or occupational strain</b>, somatic dysfunction.<br>A. Viral infections: listed.<br>C. Leg length inequality: listed.<br>D. Small hemipelvis: listed.<br>E. Chronic postural or occupational strains: listed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":116,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-119-1","s":"What conditions are associated with TrPs/Myofascial Pain?","o":["A. Hypothyroidism, Hypoglycemia and Hypouricemia","B. Iron deficiency, Candida albicans infection and Hyperglycemia","C. Vitamin excess, hypoglycemia, hyperthyroidism","D. Hypothyroidism, Iron deficiency,, Hyperuricemia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypothyroidism, Iron deficiency, Hyperuricemia","a":"","why":"<b>D. Hypothyroidism, iron deficiency, hyperuricemia.</b> Conditions linked with trigger points (TrPs) and myofascial pain: vitamin deficiency (C, D, B12, folate), <b>hypothyroidism</b>, <b>hypoglycemia</b>, <b>hyperuricemia</b>, <b>iron deficiency</b>, Candida infection.<br>A. Hypouricemia is wrong; it is hyperuricemia.<br>B. Hyperglycemia is wrong; it is hypoglycemia.<br>C. Vitamin excess and hyperthyroidism are wrong; it is deficiency and hypothyroidism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":119,"f":"O100"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":46,"label":"Treating TrPs: spray and stretch, needling, OMT","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-72-1.png","cap":"Vapocoolant spray applied over the muscle before stretching","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 72","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":554},{"src":"sl/O075-27.jpg","cap":"Spray and stretch — spray over TrP and referral area","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/O075-28.jpg","cap":"Injection/dry needling, OMT, and stretching with self-care","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-121-1","s":"Treatment of a trigger points includes all of the following except?","o":["A. Steroid injections","B. Spray and stretch","C. Dry needling","D. OMT"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Steroid injections","a":"","why":"<b>A. Steroid injections.</b> The trigger point (TrP) treatments: <b>spray and stretch</b> (vapocoolant), <b>injection with local anesthetic or dry needling</b>, and <b>OMT</b> (osteopathic manipulative treatment: counterstrain, muscle energy), always with stretching and self-care. Steroid injection is for an inflamed bursa (trochanteric bursitis), not TrPs.<br>B. Spray and stretch: TrP treatment.<br>C. Dry needling: TrP treatment.<br>D. OMT: TrP treatment.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":121,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O104-24-1","s":"Which of the following is true with regards to treatment of myofascial trigger points?","o":["A. OMT should consist of only gentle counterstrain techniques.","B. Vapocoolant spray should be applied right over the trigger point, followed by rest.","C. Injection treatment involves injecting both the trigger point and the referral zone.","D. Stretching and home exercises are vital to the resolution and inactivation of the trigger points."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stretching and home exercises are vital to the resolution and inactivation of the trigger points.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> Treatment of trigger points (TrPs) <b>must include stretching of the muscle and self-care</b> (home stretching, ergonomics, posture strengthening, self-massage), or they return.<br>A. OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) is not limited to counterstrain; muscle energy and others are used.<br>B. Spray goes over the TrP <b>and</b> the referral zone and is followed by <b>stretch</b>, not rest.<br>C. Injection targets the TrP in the muscle, not the referral zone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":24,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":96,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":49,"f":"O099"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-65-1","s":"Upon attempting treatment for the patient in the previous question, she states that the pain is really bad and is afraid to try OMT. What should you offer in response to her concerns?","o":["A. Avoid doing OMT","B. Apply vapocoolant spray to the site of pain","C. Perform lidocaine/cortisone injections of the TrP","D. Perform OMT anyways because the benefits are more important than the immediate pain that the patient is experiencing"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Apply vapocoolant spray to the site of pain","a":"","why":"<b>B. Vapocoolant spray.</b> Ethyl chloride <b>vapocoolant spray</b> is a <b>distraction</b>: it breaks the pain cycle so you can stretch the muscle and do osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT). Spray over the trigger point (TrP) <b>and</b> its referral zone, stretch, then rewarm.<br>A. Avoiding OMT leaves the TrP untreated; treatment must include stretching.<br>C. Injection is an option, but it is more invasive and not the answer to a patient afraid of pain.<br>D. Forcing painful OMT on a frightened patient is never right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":65,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":65,"f":"O098"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W13","u":47,"label":"Greater trochanter pain: QL, gluteus medius, piriformis TrPs","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O094-5-1.png","cap":"Travell referral maps — QL, piriformis, iliopsoas, rotatores/multifidi","where":"2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1088},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-114-3.jpg","cap":"Gluteus medius TrP1–3 just below the iliac crest and their referral","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 114","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":574},{"src":"sl/ta-O100-127-1.png","cap":"Piriformis TrPs: mistaken for SIJ pain, trochanteric bursitis, sciatic impingement","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 127","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1087},{"src":"sl/O075-31.jpg","cap":"Trochanter pain without local tissue changes suggests referred pain","where":"2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"O100-125-1","s":"Trigger points in the piriformis muscle cause pain that can be mistaken for:","o":["A. Trochanteric bursitis","B. Inguinal lymphadenopathy","C. Lower back pain","D. Prostatitis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Trochanteric bursitis","a":"","why":"<b>A. Trochanteric bursitis.</b> Piriformis trigger points (TrPs) refer to the sacrum, buttock and <b>greater trochanter</b> region, so their pain can be mistaken for <b>trochanteric bursitis</b>, sacroiliac joint (SIJ) pain or sciatic nerve impingement.<br>B. Inguinal lymphadenopathy: a groin swelling, not a piriformis pattern.<br>C. Low back pain: not one of the classic piriformis TrP mimics.<br>D. Prostatitis: a pelvic organ problem, not a piriformis TrP mimic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":125,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O100-128-1","s":"This referred pain pattern refers to which trigger point?","o":["A. Quadratus Lumborum","B. Piriformis","C. Gluteus Medius","D. Hip osteoarthritis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gluteus Medius","a":"","why":"<b>C. Gluteus medius.</b> Three trigger points (TrP1–3) just <b>below the iliac crest</b> refer along the <b>posterior iliac crest, sacrum and buttock</b>; TrP2 reaches toward the <b>greater trochanter</b> and upper lateral thigh.<br>A. Quadratus lumborum (QL): TrPs between rib 12 and the crest; pain along the crest, lateral hip and lower buttock.<br>B. Piriformis: TrPs mid-buttock; pain over the sacrum and buttock and down the back of the thigh.<br>D. Hip osteoarthritis: front-of-groin pain with lost internal rotation and extension, not a TrP map.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O100-128-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":128,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O104-25-1","s":"A 42-year-old male presents with pain over the left greater trochanter after a long day of walking. There is mild tenderness to palpation over the greater trochanter, with no associated warmth or bogginess. There is no tenderness over the buttock, but the pain is reproduced by palpation cephalad to the posterior iliac crest. There is normal internal rotation and extension of the left hip. Which is the most consistent with the above scenario?","o":["A. Trochanteric bursitis","B. Trigger point in the quadratus lumborum muscle","C. Trigger point in the gluteus medius muscle","D. Trigger point in the piriformis muscle","E. Osteoarthritis of the hip"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Trigger point in the quadratus lumborum muscle","a":"","why":"<b>B. Quadratus lumborum (QL) trigger point.</b> Trochanter pain with little local tenderness and no warmth or bogginess is <b>referred</b> pain, not bursitis. Pressing <b>above the posterior iliac crest</b>, where the QL lies between the crest and rib 12, reproduces it.<br>A. Trochanteric bursitis: warm, boggy, very tender over the trochanter.<br>C. Gluteus medius TrP2 lies <b>below</b> the iliac crest, in the buttock (no buttock tenderness here).<br>D. Piriformis TrP: buttock tenderness and restricted internal rotation, both absent.<br>E. Hip osteoarthritis: lost internal rotation and extension, both normal here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":25,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":98,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O075-36-1","s":"How would you differentiate whether trochanteric hip pain is caused by a gluteus medius, quadratus lumborum, or piriformis trigger point?","o":["A. Order an MRI to see which of the three muscles is enlarged","B. Look in each muscle for a tender point that has no taut band","C. Use referral patterns to pick muscles, then palpate for a taut band with twitch that refers to the hip","D. Inject the trochanteric bursa; if pain persists, the source is a trigger point","E. Press each muscle for a jump sign, since only trigger points give one"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Use knowledge of each muscle's trigger-point referral pattern to pick which muscles to examine, then palpate them for a taut band that gives a local twitch response and refers pain to the patient's area of complaint.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Each trigger point (TrP) has a <b>predictable referral pattern</b>; quadratus lumborum, piriformis and <b>gluteus medius TrP 2</b> TrPs can all refer to the greater trochanter. Use the patterns to pick muscles, then palpate for a <b>taut band</b> with a <b>local twitch response</b> that <b>reproduces the hip pain</b>.<br>A. Imaging has little role in finding TrPs.<br>B. A tender point has no taut band and no referral — the opposite of a TrP.<br>D. A bursa injection cannot tell the three muscles apart; bursitis shows local tenderness and inflamed tissue.<br>E. Tender points also give a jump sign, so it does not separate them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points","sl":36,"f":"O075"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":48,"label":"LBP basics: 90% mechanical; acute, subacute, chronic","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-147-1.png","cap":"Low back pain differential by category; mechanical causes listed first","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 147","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":446},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-143-1.png","cap":"Acute under 6 weeks, subacute 6 to 12, chronic over 3 months","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 143","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":316},{"src":"sl/O079-35.jpg","cap":"Lumbar problems usually come from strain, wear or disc compression","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-82-1","s":"What is the most common cause of LBP?","o":["A. Viscerogenic","B. Infection","C. Neoplastic","D. Mechanical","E. Psychiatric"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Mechanical","a":"","why":"<b>D. Mechanical.</b> About <b>90%</b> of low back pain (LBP) is <b>mechanical</b>: muscle or ligament strain, disc, facet or age-related wear. Look for other causes only when red flags appear.<br>A. Viscerogenic (kidney, bowel, aorta, pelvic organs): referred pain; uncommon.<br>B. Infection (discitis, osteomyelitis): rare; think of it with fever or IV (intravenous) drug use.<br>C. Neoplastic (metastasis, myeloma): rare; think of it with a cancer history or weight loss.<br>E. Psychiatric (depression): a contributor, not the usual cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":82,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-134-1","s":"Patient comes into office c/o back pain, states that they have been experiencing it for the past 50 days. How would you classify this back pain?","o":["A. Acute","B. Chronic","C. Hyperacute","D. Subacute"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Subacute","a":"","why":"<b>D. Subacute.</b> 50 days is about <b>7 weeks</b>. <b>Acute</b> = under 6 weeks, <b>subacute</b> = 6 to 12 weeks, <b>chronic</b> = over 12 weeks (3 months).<br>A. Acute: needs under 6 weeks.<br>B. Chronic: needs more than 3 months.<br>C. Hyperacute: not a back pain category.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":134,"f":"O096"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":48,"label":"Red flags: when back pain needs imaging","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-34.jpg","cap":"Red flags for spinal pathology with low back pain","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-140-1.png","cap":"Red flags: sciatica, trauma, night pain, incontinence, cancer, steroids","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 140","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":698},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-84-2.png","cap":"Risk factors: age, IV drugs, steroids, cancer","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 84","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":942},{"src":"sl/O079-100.jpg","cap":"Contrast MRI: vertebral osteomyelitis and discitis in an IV drug user","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 100"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-139-1","s":"All of these are red flags for LBP except:","o":["A. Prostate Cancer","B. Trauma","C. Bladder Incontinence","D. Headache"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Headache","a":"","why":"<b>D. Headache.</b> Red flags point to a serious spine cause (cancer, infection, fracture, cauda equina) and call for imaging. Headache is not one; it is simply the most common pain complaint overall.<br>A. Prostate cancer: a cancer history is a red flag; prostate, breast, lung, thyroid and kidney cancers spread to the spine.<br>B. Trauma: significant trauma can fracture a vertebra.<br>C. Bladder incontinence: points to cauda equina syndrome, a surgical emergency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":139,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O079-100-1","s":"28-year-old white male with back pain for 8 weeks not helped with pain medication. No trauma. History of IV drug use. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis","B. Lumbar strain (mechanical back pain)","C. L5–S1 disc herniation","D. Isthmic spondylolisthesis","E. Lumbar spinal stenosis"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Osteomyelitis (infection, fever, erosion) and discitis","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis.</b> <b>IV (intravenous) drug use</b> is a red flag: bacteria in the blood seed the vertebral body and disc. Pain past <b>4–6 weeks</b> that medicine does not help is a second red flag. Order an <b>MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) with contrast</b>; it shows eroded endplates and an enhancing disc.<br>B. Lumbar strain: no red flags; it improves within weeks.<br>C. Disc herniation: dermatomal leg pain and a positive straight leg raise.<br>D. Isthmic spondylolisthesis: step-off, tight hamstrings, pain with extension.<br>E. Spinal stenosis: patients over 60; leg pain with standing and walking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":100,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":153,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":42,"f":"O108"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":212,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-102-1","s":"A 63-year-old female fell off a chair and landed on her buttocks. She complains of severe, unrelenting low back pain. There is point tenderness to percussion of the spinous process of L3. A lumbar spine X-ray confirms your diagnosis. The most likely etiology of the pain is:","o":["A. Tumor","B. Herniated nucleus pulposus","C. Cauda equina syndrome","D. Spinal stenosis","E. Compression fracture"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Compression fracture","a":"","why":"<b>E. Compression fracture.</b> A postmenopausal woman (osteoporosis risk) lands on her buttocks, then has severe pain and <b>point tenderness when the spinous process is tapped</b>. Trauma and age over 50 are red flags; the x-ray shows a wedged vertebral body.<br>A. Tumor: no link to trauma; night pain, weight loss, cancer history.<br>B. Herniated nucleus pulposus (HNP): dermatomal leg pain; not seen on x-ray.<br>C. Cauda equina syndrome: saddle numbness and bladder loss.<br>D. Spinal stenosis: chronic leg pain with walking; no point tenderness.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":102,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":101,"f":"O098"}]},{"id":"O096-79-1","s":"Upon taking the patient's history, they mention the pain gets better with forward flexion. What could this indicate and what would be another red flag from a patient with LBP ?","o":["A. Spinal Stenosis, age of onset before 50","B. Disc Herniation, history of cancer","C. Spinal Stenosis, bladder incontinence","D. Disc Herniation, history of steroid use"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Spinal Stenosis, bladder incontinence","a":"","why":"<b>C. Spinal stenosis, bladder incontinence.</b> Pain that <b>eases with forward flexion</b> means stenosis: flexion opens the canal. <b>Bladder incontinence</b> is a red flag for cauda equina syndrome.<br>A. Onset before 50 is not a red flag; first onset after 50 is.<br>B. A disc herniation gets <b>worse</b> with flexion; cancer history is a red flag, but the posture clue is wrong.<br>D. Same posture problem: disc pain worsens with flexion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":79,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O108-44-1","s":"A 72 year old woman presents to you with continued back pain. History is positive for hysterectomy in 1955 without hormone replacement therapy. X-Ray shows?","o":["A. Lytic, punched-out metastatic lesions","B. Spondylolysis of L5 (collar on the Scottie dog)","C. Endplate erosion from vertebral osteomyelitis","D. Degenerative spondylolisthesis of L4 on L5","E. Compression fracture secondary to osteoporosis"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Compression fracture secondary to osteoporosis.","a":"","why":"<b>E. Compression fracture secondary to osteoporosis.</b> A hysterectomy decades ago (surgical menopause) with no hormone replacement means long untreated <b>estrogen loss</b>, so she has <b>osteoporosis</b>. The front of each lumbar body is its weak spot; it collapses into a <b>wedge</b>, with pain out of proportion to the exam.<br>A. Lytic metastases: cancer history, night pain, weight loss.<br>B. Spondylolysis: a pars break in young athletes who hyperextend.<br>C. Osteomyelitis: fever or IV (intravenous) drug use.<br>D. Degenerative slip: step-off and neurogenic claudication; nothing here points to it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":44,"f":"O108"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":49,"label":"Babinski: upgoing big toe = upper motor neuron","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-82.jpg","cap":"Babinski: great toe dorsiflexes = upper motor neuron lesion","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 82"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-84-1","s":"Why does a positive Babinski Reflex cause the patient to extend their toes up, instead of curling them down like normal?","o":["A. Compression of the S1 root weakens the toe flexors, so the extensors win","B. It is the normal adult plantar response","C. Loss of upper motor neuron inhibition lets the toe extensors fire when the sole is stroked","D. Irritation of the L5 root overdrives the extensor hallucis longus","E. Compression of the cauda equina releases a primitive reflex"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"It signals an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion: UMNs normally inhibit the reflex, so without that inhibition the lower motor neurons drive the toe extensors and the great toe goes up.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> A positive Babinski (big toe goes <b>up</b> when the sole is stroked) means an <b>upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion</b> in the brain or cord: stroke, TBI (traumatic brain injury), multiple sclerosis, tumor. Descending UMN fibers normally suppress this reflex; without them the toe extensors take over. Normal adults curl the toe <b>down</b>.<br>A. An S1 root lesion is lower motor neuron (LMN): weak plantar flexion and a lost ankle reflex; the toe still goes down.<br>B. An upgoing toe is normal only in infants.<br>D. L5 root irritation gives pain and weak toe extension, not an upgoing toe.<br>E. The cauda equina is roots (LMN), so reflexes drop.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":84,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":83,"f":"O098"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":49,"label":"Cauda equina: saddle numbness, low reflexes, surgery","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O099-64-1.png","cap":"Massive central disc compressing the cauda equina roots","where":"Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 64","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":764},{"src":"sl/O079-38.jpg","cap":"Cauda equina red flags: saddle anesthesia, retention, bilateral sciatica","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/O079-114.jpg","cap":"Knee and ankle jerks lost; urgent neurosurgical decompression","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"O092-5-1","s":"What are the prominent signs and symptoms of cauda equina syndrome?","o":["A. Saddle anesthesia, brisk DTRs, loss of bladder control.","B. Saddle anesthesia, decrease DTRs, loss of bladder control.","C. Anterior groin anesthesia, decrease DTRs, loss of bladder control.","D. Anterior groin anesthesia, decreased DTRs, loss of bowl function"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Saddle anesthesia, decrease DTRs, loss of bladder control.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Saddle anesthesia, decreased DTRs, loss of bladder control.</b> Cauda equina syndrome squeezes many lumbosacral roots below the cord. Roots are lower motor neurons, so deep tendon reflexes (DTRs) <b>drop</b>. Numbness covers the <b>saddle</b> (perineum, S2 to S4), and bladder and bowel control fail.<br>A. Brisk DTRs: an upper motor neuron (cord or brain) sign.<br>C. Anterior groin numbness: L1 skin, not the saddle.<br>D. Groin numbness again; bowel loss does happen, but the skin area is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":176,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":138,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":35,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O080-12-3","s":"A 55-year-old male presents to your office with low back pain radiating to his lower extremities. He states that yesterday his feet felt numb and weak, and now this feeling has progressed into his thighs. Neuromuscular examination reveals 0+ deep tendon reflexes in the lower extremities bilaterally, 3/5 muscle strength in the ankle plantar flexors, 3/5 in ankle dorsiflexors, and 3/5 in knee flexors and extensors. Digital rectal examination reveals decreased rectal tone. The most likely cause is","o":["A. a spinal cord mass effect above the level of L1","B. compression of the thecal sac","C. epidural venous plexus congestion","D. meningeal inflammation","E. severe spondylolysis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"compression of the thecal sac","a":"","why":"<b>B. Compression of the thecal sac.</b> The cord ends at <b>L1–L2</b>. Below that the thecal (dural) sac holds only nerve roots, the <b>cauda equina</b>. Squeezing many roots gives <b>lower motor neuron</b> signs: absent reflexes, flaccid weakness, saddle numbness, low rectal tone. Treat with emergency MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) and decompression within 48 hours. Strictly, the roots are compressed here, not the cord.<br>A. Cord lesion above L1: upper motor neuron signs (brisk reflexes, Babinski).<br>C. Epidural venous congestion: does not lower rectal tone.<br>D. Meningeal inflammation: fever, stiff neck, Kernig sign; no root deficits.<br>E. Spondylolysis: pars fracture; back pain without neuro deficits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":12,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":225,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":14,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":62,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":74,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":74,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-89-1","s":"A man is brought in after a bad fall while skateboarding down a hill. He is sent in for an MRI which shows an L3 disc herniation. Shortly after the imaging, the man states he cannot feel his toes B/L. Then the patient states he cannot feel his lower legs. Soon after, the patient states he cannot control his bladder and then the doctor quickly calls for a surgical consult. What is the doctor most likely concerned about?","o":["A. Osteoporosis","B. Neoplasm","C. Lumbar spasm","D. Meralgia Paresthetica","E. Cauda Equina Syndrome"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cauda Equina Syndrome","a":"","why":"<b>E. Cauda equina syndrome.</b> After a disc herniation the numbness <b>ascends</b> (toes, then legs) and he <b>loses bladder control</b>. A large disc is compressing many roots of the cauda equina. He needs an urgent surgical consult and decompression, ideally within 48 hours.<br>A. Osteoporosis: fragile bone; no nerve signs.<br>B. Neoplasm: slow onset, cancer history, night pain.<br>C. Lumbar spasm: local pain; no numbness or bladder loss.<br>D. Meralgia paresthetica: lateral thigh numbness only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":89,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O100-57-1","s":"You educate the patient about his condition and tell him that he should no longer work on the farm. However, he does not heed your warning and comes back the following year complaining about intermittent bladder incontinence and constipation for the past 6 months. He is afebrile without chills. On physical exam, you note that both his knee reflexes are 1/4 and lower extremities strength are 3/5. What is the next best course of action?","o":["A. Surgical consult","B. OMT","C. Biopsy","D. Complete blood count","E. Complete metabolic panel"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Surgical consult","a":"","why":"<b>A. Surgical consult.</b> Bladder incontinence, constipation, low knee reflexes (1/4) and 3/5 leg strength on <b>both</b> sides mean <b>cauda equina syndrome</b>. Get an emergency MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) and a neurosurgical consult for decompression; delay can make the loss permanent.<br>B. OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment): not for compressed roots with bladder loss.<br>C. Biopsy: only if imaging shows a mass.<br>D. Complete blood count: helps look for infection, but is not the next step.<br>E. Metabolic panel: does not address compressed roots.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":57,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O111-216-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents with acute central lower back pain following a heavy lifting injury. He reports sudden onset of urinary incontinence, loss of bowel control, and numbness around his perineum (\"saddle anesthesia\"). Neurological exam demonstrates reduced anal sphincter tone and bilateral lower extremity motor weakness. What is the most appropriate immediate step in management?","o":["A. Perform High-Velocity, Low-Amplitude (HVLA) treatment to the lumbar spine","B. Prescribe oral muscle relaxants and outpatient physical therapy","C. Obtain urgent emergency MRI and neurosurgical consult for decompression","D. Administer intramuscular ketorolac and reassess in 24 hours"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Obtain urgent emergency MRI and neurosurgical consult for decompression","a":"","why":"<b>C. Urgent emergency MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) and neurosurgical consult for decompression.</b> Saddle anesthesia, bowel and bladder loss, low anal tone and weakness of both legs after a lift mean <b>cauda equina syndrome</b>, usually from a large central disc. It is a surgical emergency: decompress within <b>48 hours</b> to prevent permanent loss.<br>A. HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust) is contraindicated and could worsen the herniation.<br>B. Muscle relaxants and physical therapy only delay surgery.<br>D. Waiting 24 hours on ketorolac (an anti-inflammatory) risks permanent deficits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":216,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":50,"label":"Disc herniation: posterolateral, worse with flexion","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-67-1.png","cap":"Posterolateral disc herniation pressing on the nerve root","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 67","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":415},{"src":"sl/O079-58.jpg","cap":"Bulging vs herniated disc; herniation goes posterolateral","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 58"},{"src":"sl/O079-86.jpg","cap":"Squish and squirt: flexion pushes the nucleus backward","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 86"},{"src":"sl/O081-4.jpg","cap":"Root entrapment signs, including SLR and restricted hip drop","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"O099-71-1","s":"Match each of the following clinical correlations of the lumbar spine with its most relevant characteristic: Herniated Disc","o":["A. Spinous processes closer on palpation","B. Scotty dog separation","C. Pressure on the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","D. Narrowing of posterior longitudinal ligament","E. Stress fracture of the pars interarticularis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Narrowing of posterior longitudinal ligament","a":"","why":"<b>D. Narrowing of the posterior longitudinal ligament.</b> The posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL) is only <b>half as wide at L4 to L5</b> as at L1. It covers the disc in the midline but not at the sides, so lumbar discs herniate <b>posterolaterally</b> onto a nerve root.<br>A. Spinous processes closer: degenerative arthritis (lost disc height).<br>B. Scotty dog separation: spondylolisthesis.<br>C. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve pressure: meralgia paresthetica.<br>E. Pars stress fracture: spondylolysis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":71,"f":"O099"}]},{"id":"O092-8-1","s":"Which of the following is the best statement regarding the biomechanics of the lumbar spine?","o":["A. Radicular symptoms from a herniated disc will often worsen when the lumbar spine is flexed","B. The S1 nerve root will exit between the L5 and S1 vertebrae.","C. Lumbarization increase the flexibility of the lumbosacral unit and decreasing the likelihood of degenerative joint disease.","D. The lumbar spine moves easiest about the vertical axis.","E. Central disc herniations are more common that postero-lateral disc herniations."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Radicular symptoms from a herniated disc will often worsen when the lumbar spine is flexed","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Lumbar <b>flexion</b> squeezes the front of the disc and pushes the nucleus <b>backward</b> (the squish and squirt), into the tear and onto the root. That is why disc patients avoid flexion and sitting.<br>B. False: the <b>L5</b> root exits between L5 and S1; S1 exits through the first sacral foramen.<br>C. False: a transitional vertebra does not protect against degenerative joint disease; sacralization even speeds disc wear.<br>D. False: the lumbar spine moves most in flexion and extension (transverse axis); rotation is very small.<br>E. False: posterolateral herniations are more common, because the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"}],"t":1},{"id":"O104-38-1","s":"A 40-year-old male is complaining of severe right-sided low back pain radiating into the foot. The pain started yesterday and is associated with calf cramping. He describes a shooting pain into his right foot that is made worse with forward flexion of the lumbar spine. On examination, the pelvis is shifted to the left. There is an absence of the lumbar lordosis. The lumbar spine is side bent to the right. There is a positive straight leg raising test. Electromyography show abnormal findings in the anterior tibialis and extensor hallucis longus. [Which of the following is most accurate?]","o":["A. This patient’s injury would have been prevented if he received OMT.","B. The primary cause of this patient’s pain is due to a shortened psoas on the right side.","C. The patient is likely to have an asymmetry with the hip drop test","D. This patient will eventually need surgery due to the natural progression of his diagnosis."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The patient is likely to have an asymmetry with the hip drop test","a":"","why":"<b>C. Asymmetric hip drop test.</b> This is a disc herniation hitting <b>L5</b> (tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus abnormal on EMG, electromyography). Root entrapment brings protective spasm and a functional scoliosis (pelvic shift, sidebent spine), so lumbar sidebending is restricted and the <b>hip drop test is asymmetric</b>.<br>A. OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) cannot promise to prevent a disc injury.<br>B. A short psoas gives groin or thigh pain to the knee with a normal neuro exam; here EMG shows root damage.<br>D. About 90 to 95% of disc herniations settle with conservative care; surgery only if that fails.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":38,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":184,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":144,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":51,"label":"Foraminal stenosis: exiting root named for vertebra above","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O099-69-1.png","cap":"Each lumbar root exits below its own vertebra","where":"Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 69","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":526},{"src":"sl/O080-1.jpg","cap":"Roots exit below their vertebra, above the disc","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 1"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-13","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (affected nerve root) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. An MRI of the lumbar spine reveals severe stenosis of the intervertebral foramen affecting the nerve root between L3 and L4.","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"L3","a":"","why":"<b>B. L3.</b> Lumbar roots exit <b>below their own vertebra</b>, so the foramen between L3 and L4 carries the <b>L3 root</b>. <b>Foraminal</b> stenosis (or a far-lateral disc) squeezes this exiting root. A posterolateral L3–L4 disc would instead hit L4 (X+1 rule).<br>A. L2: exits between L2 and L3.<br>C. L4: exits between L4 and L5; at L3–L4 it is still inside the canal.<br>D. L5: exits between L5 and S1.<br>E. S1: exits through the first sacral foramen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":245,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":17,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":85,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":84,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O099-68-1","s":"An MRI of the lumbar spine reveals severa stenosis of the intervertebral foramen affecting the nerve root between L5 and S1, at which level would you find the affected nerve root?","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L5","a":"","why":"<b>D. L5.</b> Lumbar roots exit <b>below their own vertebra</b>, so the L5 to S1 foramen carries the <b>L5</b> root. Foraminal narrowing squeezes this exiting root.<br>Compare: a posterolateral <b>disc</b> at L5 to S1 hits <b>S1</b> (X+1 rule), because S1 crosses that disc inside the canal.<br>A. L2: exits at L2 to L3.<br>B. L3: exits at L3 to L4.<br>C. L4: exits at L4 to L5.<br>E. S1: exits through the first sacral foramen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":68,"f":"O099"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":51,"label":"X+1 rule: disc X compresses root X+1","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-60.jpg","cap":"Disc vs deficit: herniation at disc X hits root X+1","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/ta-O097-88-1.png","cap":"L4 and L5 roots exit above the discs they cross","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 88","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":332},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-191-1.png","cap":"Disc level, root, motor, sensory and reflex loss","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 191","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1126},{"src":"sl/O079-118.jpg","cap":"MRI: herniated nucleus pulposus at L5 to S1","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 118"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-15","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (affected nerve root) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. An MRI of the lumbar spine reveals a posteriolateral disc herniation of the intervertebral disc of L4-L5.","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L5","a":"","why":"<b>D. L5.</b> <b>X+1 rule</b>: a <b>posterolateral</b> herniation of the L4–L5 disc hits the root crossing that disc on its way down, <b>L5</b>. The L4 root already exited through the L4–L5 foramen, above the disc.<br>A. L2: exits at L2–L3, far above this disc.<br>B. L3: exits at L3–L4, above this disc.<br>C. L4: exits above the disc; only a far-lateral (foraminal) L4–L5 herniation hits it.<br>E. S1: hit by a posterolateral L5–S1 herniation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":249,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":17,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":87,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":86,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":67,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-118-1","s":"Disc vs. Deficit. Where is the problem?","o":["A. L4–L5 disc herniation compressing the L5 nerve root","B. L4–L5 disc herniation compressing the L4 nerve root","C. L5–S1 disc herniation compressing the L5 nerve root","D. L5 pars interarticularis fracture (spondylolysis)","E. L5–S1 disc herniation compressing the S1 nerve root"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"An L5-S1 disc herniation (HNP) compressing the S1 nerve root (X+1 rule: a herniation at disc X affects nerve root X+1).","a":"","why":"<b>E.</b> The MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) shows a herniated nucleus pulposus (HNP) at <b>L5–S1</b>. <b>X+1 rule</b>: a posterolateral herniation at disc X hits root <b>X+1</b>, because root X already left through its foramen above the disc. So L5–S1 hits <b>S1</b>.<br>A. Right rule, wrong level: the image marks L5–S1, not L4–L5.<br>B. An L4–L5 disc spares L4, which has already exited.<br>C. L5 exits above the L5–S1 disc; only a far-lateral (foraminal) herniation catches it.<br>D. Spondylolysis is a pars fracture; the arrow points at the disc.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O079-118-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":183,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":65,"f":"O108"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":52,"label":"L4 root: patellar reflex, medial leg, inversion","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-30-1.png","cap":"L4 level: tibialis anterior, patellar reflex, medial leg skin","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 30","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":315},{"src":"sl/O081-5.jpg","cap":"L4: foot inversion, patellar reflex, medial foot","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-118-1.png","cap":"L3 to L4 disc, L4 root, patellar reflex, medial malleolus","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 118","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":550}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-14","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (affected nerve root) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. Physical examination reveals an absent patellar reflex with associated sensory loss down the medial aspect of the leg.","o":["A. L2","B. L3","C. L4","D. L5","E. S1"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L4","a":"","why":"<b>C. L4.</b> L4 gives the <b>patellar reflex</b> and the skin of the <b>medial leg</b> and medial ankle. Its muscles are the quadriceps and tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion and inversion; heel-walk with the foot inverted).<br>A. L2: hip flexion and upper anterior thigh; no routine reflex.<br>B. L3: helps the patellar reflex, but its skin area stops at the medial knee.<br>D. L5: great-toe extension, dorsum of foot, no reliable reflex.<br>E. S1: Achilles reflex, lateral foot and sole, plantar flexion and eversion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":247,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":17,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":70,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":188,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O096-29-1","s":"Which of the following are correct locations for sensory examinations?","o":["A. L4: medial leg and foot","B. L5: medial leg and foot","C. S1: dorsum of foot","D. L4: lateral leg and dorsum of foot"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"L4: medial leg and foot","a":"","why":"<b>A. L4: medial leg and foot.</b> Sensory map: <b>L4 = medial</b> leg, ankle and foot; <b>L5 = dorsum (top)</b> of foot and lateral leg; <b>S1 = lateral foot and sole</b>.<br>B. Medial leg and foot belong to L4, not L5.<br>C. The dorsum of the foot is L5; S1 is the lateral foot.<br>D. Lateral leg and dorsum of foot are L5, not L4.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":29,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O092-12-1","s":"A 49-year-old male presents to the office with low back pain. History reveals an acute onset of sharp, burning pain radiating down the leg that started while he was working on his car in the garage. An MRI of the lumbar spine is obtained revealing a herniated disc between the L3 and L4 vertebrae. The most likely additional finding upon physical examination is","o":["A. absent Achilles reflex","B. absent patellar reflex","C. hyperreflexive Achilles reflex","D. hyperreflexive patellar reflex","E. positive Babinski sign"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"absent patellar reflex","a":"","why":"<b>B. Absent patellar reflex.</b> A posterolateral L3 to L4 disc compresses the <b>L4</b> root (X+1 rule). A root is a lower motor neuron, so its reflex <b>falls</b>; the patellar (knee) reflex is mainly L4.<br>A. Absent Achilles reflex: S1 root (L5 to S1 disc).<br>C. Hyperreflexive Achilles: an upper motor neuron sign.<br>D. Hyperreflexive patellar: also upper motor neuron; a root lesion lowers reflexes.<br>E. Positive Babinski: upper motor neuron lesion, not a disc.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"}]},{"id":"O092-7-1","s":"A 25-year-old male patient presents with a 2 week history of lower back pain with bilaterally pain radiations into the buttock. He reports decreased sensation on the medial aspect of the right foot and marked weakness of right foot inversion and weakness from rising from a squat. The straight leg test is positive on the right. What would be the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Herniated nucleus pulposus between vertebral levels L4 & L5.","B. Spondylolisthesis at L5 level","C. Herniated nucleus pulposus between vertebral levels L3 & L4.","D. Spinal stenosis between vertebral levels L5 & S1."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Herniated nucleus pulposus between vertebral levels L3 & L4.","a":"","why":"<b>C. Herniated nucleus pulposus between L3 and L4.</b> Numb <b>medial foot</b>, weak <b>inversion</b> (tibialis anterior) and weak rising from a squat (quadriceps) all point to the <b>L4 root</b>. A positive straight leg raise (SLR) means root irritation. By the X+1 rule, the L3 to L4 disc hits L4.<br>A. L4 to L5 disc hits L5: weak big-toe extension, numb dorsum of foot.<br>B. Spondylolisthesis at L5: step-off and tight hamstrings, usually no deficit.<br>D. Stenosis at L5 to S1: elderly patient; pain with standing and walking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":37,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":117,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":189,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":214,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":180,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":142,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":52,"label":"L5 root: big-toe extension, dorsal foot, no reflex","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-101-2.png","cap":"L5 level: extensor hallucis longus, no reflex, dorsum of foot","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 101","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1038},{"src":"sl/O081-6.jpg","cap":"L5: great toe extension, no reflex, top of foot","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-201-1.png","cap":"Radicular pain lines of S1, L5, L4 and L3","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 201","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":705}],"q":[{"id":"O096-99-1","s":"L5 reflex is great toe extension.","o":["A. True","B. False","C. Umm Im ready for finals to be ovah!"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"","why":"<b>B. False.</b> <b>L5 has no reliable reflex.</b> Great-toe extension (extensor hallucis longus) is the L5 <b>motor</b> test, not a reflex. L5 skin is the dorsum (top) of the foot. Reflex map: patellar = L4, Achilles = S1, L5 = none.<br>A. True is wrong: great-toe extension is a strength test.<br>C. Not a real answer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":99,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O097-79-1","s":"During your OSCE, you were assessing the SPs lower extremity. Upon PE, you noted that she was unable to lift her big toe up and was unable to feel you touching the top of her foot. Which nerve root is most likely affected in this patient?","o":["A. L3","B. L4","C. L5","D. S1","E. S2"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L5","a":"","why":"<b>C. L5.</b> L5 runs the <b>extensor hallucis longus</b> (lifts the big toe) and the skin of the <b>dorsum (top) of the foot</b>. It has no reliable reflex.<br>A. L3: thigh and medial knee skin; knee extension.<br>B. L4: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion and inversion), medial leg and foot, patellar reflex.<br>D. S1: plantar flexion and eversion, lateral foot, Achilles reflex.<br>E. S2: sole and back of thigh; small foot muscles and bladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":79,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":78,"f":"O098"}]},{"id":"O105-11-1","s":"Which of the following is consistent with an L4-5 herniated disc?","o":["A. Absent patellar DTR","B. Great toe dorsiflexion weakness","C. Pain in the lateral ankle","D. Diminished Achilles reflex","E. Weakness of knee extension"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Great toe dorsiflexion weakness","a":"","why":"<b>B. Great toe dorsiflexion weakness.</b> An L4 to L5 disc hits the <b>L5</b> root (X+1 rule). L5 drives the extensor hallucis longus, which lifts the big toe.<br>A. Absent patellar reflex: L4 root (L3 to L4 disc).<br>C. Lateral ankle and foot pain: S1 area.<br>D. Diminished Achilles reflex: S1 root (L5 to S1 disc).<br>E. Weak knee extension: quadriceps, L3 to L4.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 lumbar review (SGA)","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Fall 2021","sl":11,"f":"O105"}]},{"id":"O095-200-1","s":"Pt comes in with low back pain and tingling that radiates all the way down to the foot. Pt explains to you that they were doing intense deadlifts when their back blew out. You notice on your neurological examination that they have a weakness in dorsiflexing their big toe. Which of the following do you expect the pt has?","o":["A. L4-L5 Herniated Disc","B. L3-L4 Herniated Disc","C. Spondylosis","D. Spondylolisthesis","E. Piriformis Syndrome"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"L4-L5 Herniated Disc","a":"","why":"<b>A. L4 to L5 herniated disc.</b> Weak <b>big-toe dorsiflexion</b> is the <b>L5</b> root. A heavy lift plus pain shooting to the foot suggests a disc, and by the X+1 rule the L4 to L5 disc hits L5.<br>B. L3 to L4 disc: hits L4; patellar reflex and inversion.<br>C. Spondylosis: degenerative stiffness; no acute root deficit.<br>D. Spondylolisthesis: step-off and tight hamstrings; usually no deficit.<br>E. Piriformis syndrome: buttock pain from sciatic irritation; no single-root weakness.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":200,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":113,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O104-38-2","s":"A 40-year-old male is complaining of severe right-sided low back pain radiating into the foot. The pain started yesterday and is associated with calf cramping. He describes a shooting pain into his right foot that is made worse with forward flexion of the lumbar spine. On examination, the pelvis is shifted to the left. There is an absence of the lumbar lordosis. The lumbar spine is side bent to the right. There is a positive straight leg raising test. Electromyography show abnormal findings in the anterior tibialis and extensor hallucis longus. Which nerve root is most likely involved?","o":["A. S1","B. L5","C. S2","D. L3","E. L4"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"L5","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>L5.</b> Extensor hallucis longus is the classic <b>L5</b> muscle, and tibialis anterior gets L4 and L5; the root both share is L5. Pain worse with flexion, a positive straight leg raise and a protective pelvic shift fit an L4 to L5 disc.<br>A. S1: peroneus longus and calf; Achilles reflex.<br>C. S2: small foot muscles and bladder.<br>D. L3: quadriceps and hip adductors; neither muscle here.<br>E. L4: tibialis anterior, but not extensor hallucis longus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":38,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":185,"f":"O107"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":52,"label":"S1 root: Achilles reflex, lateral foot, toe walking","slides":[{"src":"sl/O081-7.jpg","cap":"S1: eversion, Achilles reflex, lateral foot sensation","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-191-2.png","cap":"Pain, numbness, weakness and reflexes for L4, L5, S1","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 191","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":643}],"q":[{"id":"O097-81-1","s":"When tapping your reflex hammer on the achilles tendon of your patient, you note that there was no responsive muscle contraction. Which nerve root normally plays a role in this reflex circuit?","o":["A. L3","B. L4","C. L5","D. S1","E. S2"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"S1","a":"","why":"<b>D. S1.</b> The <b>Achilles (ankle) reflex</b> is S1. S1 also runs eversion (peroneus longus) and plantar flexion (toe walking), with skin on the <b>lateral foot and sole</b>.<br>A. L3: helps the patellar reflex.<br>B. L4: patellar reflex.<br>C. L5: no reliable reflex.<br>E. S2: no routine reflex; sole and back of thigh.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":81,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":80,"f":"O098"}]},{"id":"O079-118-2","s":"Disc vs. Deficit. What are the T.A.R.T findings during your osteopathic findings? What will you find on physical exam?","o":["A. Weak quadriceps and inversion, reduced patellar reflex, numb medial leg; limited trunk flexion","B. Saddle anesthesia, low rectal tone, absent knee and ankle reflexes on both sides","C. Weak great-toe extension, no reflex change, numb dorsum of foot; limited trunk flexion","D. Weak plantar flexion and eversion, reduced Achilles reflex, numb lateral foot; limited trunk flexion","E. Brisk reflexes, upgoing Babinski toe and spastic weakness of both legs"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"S1 radiculopathy. Pain down the posterior leg to the lateral foot, numbness of the lateral foot/sole, weak plantar flexion of the great toe and foot (cannot walk on toes), weak eversion (peroneus longus), and a diminished ankle (Achilles) reflex. Osteopathic findings of root entrapment: limited trunk flexion, functional lumbar scoliosis from protective spasm, dermatomal hyperalgesia and multifidus triangle findings.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> An L5–S1 herniation hits <b>S1</b>: weak <b>plantar flexion</b> (cannot toe-walk) and eversion, a <b>reduced Achilles (ankle) reflex</b>, and numbness of the <b>lateral foot and sole</b>. TART (tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, tenderness) findings of root entrapment: limited trunk flexion, protective functional scoliosis, multifidus-triangle tenderness.<br>A. L4 root (L3–L4 disc).<br>B. Cauda equina: many roots on both sides.<br>C. L5 root (L4–L5 disc).<br>E. Upper motor neuron (cord) lesion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O079-118-2.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":65,"f":"O108"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":183,"f":"O107"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":53,"label":"Quick neuro screen: heel and toe walking","slides":[{"src":"sl/O081-8.jpg","cap":"Heels with foot inverted L4, heels L5, toes S1","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-157-1.png","cap":"Quick 60-second neuro exam by nerve root","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 157","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":556}],"q":[{"id":"O095-155-1","s":"Steve Harvey comes to your clinic with chronic low back pain. He states he has history of a herniated disc between L3/L4. What neurologic exam can you tell him to perform and what nerve root is being compressed?","o":["A. Walk on heels with abduction of hip, L4","B. Walk on toes with foot inversion S1","C. Walk in heels with foot inverted L5","D. Walk on heels with foot inverted L4"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Walk on heels with foot inverted L4","a":"","why":"<b>D. Walk on heels with foot inverted, L4.</b> An L3 to L4 disc compresses <b>L4</b> (X+1 rule). L4 powers tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion plus <b>inversion</b>), so heel-walking with the foot turned in tests it. Quick screen: L4 = heels, foot inverted; L5 = heels; S1 = toes.<br>A. Hip abduction is an L5 movement, not L4.<br>B. Toe walking is S1, and inversion is not part of it.<br>C. Right test, wrong root: an L3 to L4 disc spares L5.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":155,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":83,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O111-101-1","s":"You are on a rotation in a primary care office, evaluating a 48-year-old patient presenting for an annual exam. His past medical history is negative and he is in overall good health, however he mentions over the past few weeks having issues with left-sided low back pain that started after helping his son move in to his college dorm. He notes the trip included a five-hour drive to and from the college, and a lot of lifting during the process of moving. He notes the pain is worse with prolonged sitting, and has recently started to have radiation down his leg to the lateral aspect of his left foot. He denies any changes in bowel or bladder habits associated with the pain. Which of the following tasks is he likely to have difficulty doing in the office?","o":["A. Walking on his toes","B. Extending his lumbar spine","C. Walking with inverted feet","D. Walking on his heels"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Walking on his toes","a":"","why":"<b>A. Walking on his toes.</b> Pain worse with sitting that radiates to the <b>lateral foot</b> points to the <b>S1</b> root (L5–S1 disc). S1 drives the calf (plantar flexion), so toe walking is weak.<br>B. Extending the lumbar spine usually eases a disc; extension provokes stenosis.<br>C. Heel walking with the feet inverted tests L4 (tibialis anterior).<br>D. Heel walking tests L5 (great-toe and foot extensors).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":101,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":53,"label":"Straight leg raise, Bragard and well-leg raise","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-76.jpg","cap":"SLR: if pain starts under 80 degrees, lower 5 and dorsiflex","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-154-1.png","cap":"Hamstring pain vs nerve root pain on the straight leg raise","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 154","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":740},{"src":"sl/O079-77.jpg","cap":"Bragard test: dorsiflexion at hip-flexion limit confirms the SLR","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 77"},{"src":"sl/O079-78.jpg","cap":"Well (crossed) straight leg raise: large disc herniation","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 78"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-60-1","s":"The straight leg raise test can help you evaluate sciatic nerve compression with leg elevation and hip flexion. Upon pain, the doctor lowers the leg about 5 degrees and moves the foot to evaluate pain. What is considered a positive SLR?","o":["A. Pain with plantarflexion of the foot, which indicates sciatic nerve compression","B. Pain from leg lowering, which indicates a tight QL","C. Upon leg lowering and dorsiflexion, pain is felt all the way down the leg","D. If no pain is elicited, there is a positive test and indicates tight hamstrings"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Upon leg lowering and dorsiflexion, pain is felt all the way down the leg","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Straight leg raise (SLR): lift the straight leg until pain starts. If it starts below about 80 degrees, lower the leg <b>5 degrees</b> and <b>dorsiflex the foot</b> (Bragard). Pain shooting <b>down the leg</b> again = <b>positive</b>: nerve root irritation.<br>A. Plantar flexion slackens the sciatic nerve; dorsiflexion is the stretch.<br>B. Lowering the leg should ease pain; the quadratus lumborum is not tested by SLR.<br>D. No pain is a negative test; pain only in the back of the thigh means tight hamstrings.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":60,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":152,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O079-118-3","s":"Disc vs. Deficit. Which special test would you consider performing on your patient?","o":["A. Babinski reflex for an upper motor neuron lesion","B. ASIS compression (pelvic rock) test for SI joint motion","C. Straight leg raise, confirmed with Bragard's test (foot dorsiflexion)","D. Thomas test for iliopsoas contracture","E. Kernig's sign for meningeal irritation"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Straight leg raise (Lasègue) test: positive if radiating leg pain is reproduced at 30-70 degrees of hip flexion. Confirm with Bragaard's test (ankle dorsiflexion). The well-leg (crossed) SLR points to a large herniation, and Valsalva/Milgram raise intrathecal pressure.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>straight leg raise (SLR)</b> stretches the sciatic roots (L4–S3). Radiating pain below the knee before about 70–80° points to a root. Lower the leg 5° and <b>dorsiflex the foot (Bragard)</b>: pain again confirms root irritation, not hamstring tightness. Pain when lifting the <b>well leg</b> (crossed SLR) means a large herniation.<br>A. Babinski: upper motor neuron lesion; a disc hits lower motor roots.<br>B. ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) compression: SI (sacroiliac) joint restriction.<br>D. Thomas test: psoas (hip flexor) contracture.<br>E. Kernig sign: meningitis, not a root test.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O079-118-3.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":118,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":202,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":65,"f":"O108"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":183,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-38-1","s":"A 32 y/o patient presents with pain radiating down one leg. It gets worse when he sits down. What test would best help you narrow down your differential diagnosis?","o":["A. Thomas test","B. Thompson test","C. Hoover's sign","D. Leg traction test","E. Well leg raise"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Well leg raise","a":"","why":"<b>E. Well leg raise.</b> One-sided leg pain worse with <b>sitting</b> (flexion) suggests a disc. Lifting the <b>unaffected</b> leg (crossed straight leg raise) that brings pain down the <b>symptomatic</b> leg points to a <b>large disc herniation</b>.<br>A. Thomas test: tight psoas (hip flexor).<br>B. Thompson test: Achilles tendon rupture.<br>C. Hoover sign: effort during leg raising (non-organic weakness); not a root test.<br>D. Leg traction test: quadratus lumborum, not a nerve root.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":38,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O111-103-1","s":"You are on a rotation in a primary care office, evaluating a 48-year-old patient presenting for an annual exam. His past medical history is negative and he is in overall good health, however he mentions over the past few weeks having issues with left-sided low back pain that started after helping his son move in to his college dorm. He notes the trip included a five-hour drive to and from the college, and a lot of lifting during the process of moving. He notes the pain is worse with prolonged sitting, and has recently started to have radiation down his leg to the lateral aspect of his left foot. He denies any changes in bowel or bladder habits associated with the pain. Which of the following physical exam findings is likely to be present on further examination?","o":["A. Loss of patellar tendon reflex","B. Reproduction of pain with extension of his hip between 30-70 degrees","C. Diminished sensation in the L4 dermatome","D. Weakness of the extensor hallicus longus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Reproduction of pain with extension of his hip between 30-70 degrees","a":"","why":"<b>B. Pain reproduced by the straight leg raise, between 30° and 70°.</b> Pain worse with sitting that radiates to the <b>lateral foot</b> is an <b>S1</b> root irritated by a disc (L5–S1). Raising the straight leg stretches the root and reproduces the pain, usually between 30° and 70°. The option is worded as hip extension, but the test raises the leg into hip <b>flexion</b> with the knee extended; it is still the only finding that fits.<br>A. Lost patellar reflex: L4.<br>C. L4 dermatome: medial leg and foot.<br>D. Weak extensor hallucis longus (big-toe extension): L5.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":103,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O111-115-1","s":"A 40-year-old truck driver presents with increasingly painful right-sided low back pain. He reports the pain started as \"just an ache\", but has now progressed to pain that radiates down the back of his right leg to his foot. On examination, he has decreased sensation to light touch on the dorsum of his right foot and weakness when he tries to extend his right big toe. You decide to perform the Well Leg Raise. Which of the following would be considered positive findings for this test?","o":["A. Exacerbation of radiation/pain with extension of his left hip","B. Exacerbation of his radiation/pain with dorsiflexion of his right foot","C. Inferior movement of his left iliac crest when standing on his right leg","D. Decreased traction of his right lower extremity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Exacerbation of radiation/pain with extension of his left hip","a":"","why":"<b>A. Pain in the right (symptomatic) leg when the left leg is raised.</b> The well-leg (crossed) straight leg raise lifts the <b>unaffected</b> leg with the knee straight; pain shooting down the affected leg means a <b>large disc herniation</b>. The option is worded as hip extension, but raising the leg is hip <b>flexion</b>; it is still the only well-leg finding listed.<br>B. Dorsiflexing the painful leg's foot is Bragard's test, which confirms the straight leg raise on that side.<br>C. A dropping iliac crest during one-leg standing is a hip drop or Trendelenburg finding.<br>D. Restricted leg traction points to the quadratus lumborum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":115,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":53,"label":"Meralgia paresthetica: lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-56.jpg","cap":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (L2 to L3) skin area on the thigh","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"O096-138-1","s":"Patient comes in c/o inguinal pain and dysethesia in the anterolateral thigh. Pertinent history includes recent pregnancy with gestational DM, and habit of wearing skinny jeans 3 sizes too small. What nerve is most likely compressed?","o":["A. Sciatic nerve","B. Cutaneous gracilis nerve","C. Cutaneous popliteal nerve","D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","a":"","why":"<b>D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve.</b> Burning numbness of the <b>anterolateral thigh</b> = meralgia paresthetica. This sensory-only nerve (L2 to L3) runs under the inguinal ligament near the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine). Tight clothing, belts, pregnancy, obesity and diabetes compress it. No motor or reflex loss.<br>A. Sciatic nerve: posterior thigh and leg pain.<br>B. 'Cutaneous gracilis nerve': not a real nerve.<br>C. 'Cutaneous popliteal nerve': not a real nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":138,"f":"O096"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":54,"label":"Lumbar variants: tropism, sacralization, lumbarization","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-90.jpg","cap":"Lumbarization of S1 vs sacralization of L5","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 90"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-167-1.png","cap":"X-ray: enlarged L5 transverse processes meeting the sacrum","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 167","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":800},{"src":"sl/O079-89.jpg","cap":"Facet tropism: left and right facet angles differ","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 89"}],"q":[{"id":"O097-100-1","s":"Which of the following is considered to be the most common congenital anomaly in the lumbar spine?","o":["A. Sacralization","B. Lumbarization","C. Facet hypertrophy","D. Facet tropism","E. Spina bifida occulta"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Facet tropism","a":"","why":"<b>D. Facet tropism.</b> Tropism = the left and right facets at one level face different angles (8 degrees or more apart). It is the <b>most common</b> lumbar anomaly (about 30%) and favours early degeneration.<br>A. Sacralization (L5 fused to the sacrum): common, but less than tropism.<br>B. Lumbarization (S1 acting as a lumbar vertebra): rarer than sacralization.<br>C. Facet hypertrophy: degenerative, not congenital.<br>E. Spina bifida occulta: about 10%; a laminar closure defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":100,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":99,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-45-1","s":"An abdominal/pelvic X ray was done and sacralization was found. What describes this finding?","o":["A. L4 fused with L5","B. L5 fused with S1","C. S1-4 are fused together.","D. S1 independent of the rest of the sacrum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"L5 fused with S1","a":"","why":"<b>B. L5 fused with S1.</b> <b>Sacralization</b>: L5 joins the sacrum, often through a long transverse process (TP) that meets the sacrum. It is more common than lumbarization.<br>A. L4 fused with L5: a block vertebra, not sacralization.<br>C. S1 to S4 fused: the normal sacrum.<br>D. S1 separate from the sacrum: <b>lumbarization</b>, the opposite.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":45,"f":"O100"}]},{"id":"O095-167-1","s":"60 year old Male comes in for Lower Back Pain. He also has pain radiating to the right buttock and down the leg. You notice on physical exam a loss of spring in the L5-S1 region. Rigid abrupt endpoints in feel. The spinous processes are unusually close. You palpate unusual bony stiffness on the TP of L5.","o":["A. Spondylolisthesis","B. Spondylitis","C. Sacralization of L5","D. Lumbarization of S1","E. Piriformis Syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sacralization of L5","a":"","why":"<b>C. Sacralization of L5.</b> The <b>L5 transverse process (TP) feels bony and stiff</b> because it is enlarged and joined to the sacrum. L5 to S1 loses its spring and end feel is rigid; the fused level loads the disc above, which wears early.<br>A. Spondylolisthesis: step-off; tight hamstrings.<br>B. Spondylitis: inflammatory arthritis (ankylosing spondylitis); morning stiffness that eases with exercise.<br>D. Lumbarization of S1: an extra mobile segment, not a stiff L5.<br>E. Piriformis syndrome: buttock pain; no bony change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":167,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":54,"label":"Spina bifida: occulta, meningocele, meningomyelocele","slides":[{"src":"sl/O080-3.jpg","cap":"Spina bifida occulta, meningocele, meningomyelocele","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 3"},{"src":"sl/O079-13.jpg","cap":"Faun tail nevus, a hair tuft over hidden spina bifida","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-12-2","s":"A newborn is delivered with a posterior lumbar mass. Ultrasound revealed that the mass appears to be composed of meningeal and neural tissue. The most likely diagnosis is","o":["A. disc herniation","B. spina bifida meningocele","C. spina bifida meningomyelocele","D. spina bifida occulta","E. spondylolysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"spina bifida meningomyelocele","a":"","why":"<b>C. Spina bifida meningomyelocele.</b> The laminae fail to close and both the <b>meninges and neural tissue</b> (cord or roots) herniate into the sac; it usually causes neurologic deficits.<br>A. Disc herniation: goes into the canal, never out as a back mass.<br>B. Meningocele: a sac of <b>meninges only</b>, no neural tissue.<br>D. Occulta: no herniation; often only a hair tuft (faun tail nevus).<br>E. Spondylolysis: pars fracture, no mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":12,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":223,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":14,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":54,"f":"O099"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":55,"label":"Lumbar OA: spurs, narrow L4-S1 discs, no spring","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O100-50-1.png","cap":"X-ray: osteophytes and a narrowed disc in degenerative disc disease","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 50","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":565},{"src":"sl/O079-93.jpg","cap":"Lumbar OA: narrow L4 to S1 discs, no spring, ROM limited","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 93"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-151-1.png","cap":"Degenerative arthritis: loss of spring; labeled lateral x-ray","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 151","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":578}],"q":[{"id":"O099-71-2","s":"Match each of the following clinical correlations of the lumbar spine with its most relevant characteristic: Degenerative arthritis","o":["A. Spinous processes closer on palpation","B. Scotty dog separation","C. Pressure on the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","D. Narrowing of posterior longitudinal ligament","E. Stress fracture of the pars interarticularis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Spinous processes closer on palpation","a":"","why":"<b>A. Spinous processes closer on palpation.</b> Degenerative arthritis wears the discs (L4 to L5, L5 to S1). With <b>less disc height</b>, neighbouring vertebrae and their spinous processes sit <b>closer together</b>, with loss of spring.<br>B. Scotty dog separation: spondylolisthesis.<br>C. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve pressure: meralgia paresthetica.<br>D. Narrowed posterior longitudinal ligament: why discs herniate posterolaterally.<br>E. Pars stress fracture: spondylolysis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":71,"f":"O099"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-91-1","s":"Osteoarthritis: Degenerative Arthritis (Disc Degeneration). Disc Degeneration Narrowing of disc space L4/L5 &L5-S1. Osteoarthritic Spur. What would you find at this level when evaluation ROM ?","o":["A. Restricted active and passive ROM in all planes, loss of spring, abrupt end points","B. One-segment restriction that changes with flexion vs extension (Type II)","C. Restricted extension only; flexion free with a normal springy end feel","D. Full ROM; pain only on straight leg raise between 30° and 70°","E. Hypermobile segment with a step-off that persists in flexion"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Restriction of active and passive ROM in all planes, with loss of spring (rigid, abrupt end points); the spinous processes feel closer together on palpation because of loss of disc height.","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Worn discs at <b>L4–L5 and L5–S1</b> lose height, the facets wear and spurs form, so the segment turns stiff. <b>Active and passive ROM (range of motion) are limited in all planes</b>, the spring test is hard (<b>loss of spring</b>), and end points are rigid and abrupt. Spinous processes feel closer together.<br>B. A Type II (non-neutral) somatic dysfunction changes with flexion or extension; OA blocks every direction.<br>C. One-direction restriction with normal spring is a functional problem, not bony OA (osteoarthritis).<br>D. Isolated SLR (straight leg raise) pain: disc or root irritation.<br>E. Step-off: spondylolisthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":91,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":135,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":28,"f":"O108"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":150,"f":"O095"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-49-1","s":"In addition, you also see a bone spur at L4 and a narrowing of disc space L4/L5. This patient also has ____.","o":["A. Fibromyalgia","B. Pott's disease","C. Crystalline arthritis","D. Degenerative Arthritis","E. Rheumatoid Arthritis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Degenerative Arthritis (aka osteoarthritis)","a":"","why":"<b>D. Degenerative arthritis.</b> A <b>bone spur (osteophyte)</b> plus a <b>narrowed L4 to L5 disc space</b> is degenerative arthritis (osteoarthritis).<br>A. Fibromyalgia: widespread tender points; normal x-ray.<br>B. Pott disease: spinal tuberculosis destroying the vertebral body and disc (gibbus).<br>C. Crystalline arthritis (gout, pseudogout): peripheral joints.<br>E. Rheumatoid arthritis: erosions; spares the lumbar spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":49,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-85-1","s":"A 53 year old woman presents to her primary care doctor with increasing lower back pain. She describes her back as stiff and feels slower with movement. The doctor noticed on palpation loss of spring and having rigidity. The doctor also observed reduced range of motion in all planes of motion. A lumbar spine X-ray was ordered for the patient. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Fracture of pars interarticularis","B. Spinal stenosis","C. Spondylolysis","D. Osteoarthritis","E. Meralgia Paresthetica"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Osteoarthritis","a":"","why":"<b>D. Osteoarthritis.</b> Middle age, stiffness, <b>loss of spring with a rigid end feel</b> and <b>ROM (range of motion) limited in all planes</b> fit lumbar osteoarthritis (degenerative arthritis). The x-ray shows osteophytes.<br>A. Pars fracture: spondylolysis, usually a young athlete.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: leg pain with walking, eased by flexion.<br>C. Spondylolysis: same as A; young, pain with extension.<br>E. Meralgia paresthetica: lateral thigh numbness; normal back motion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":85,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O095-148-1","s":"A 50 y/o patient presents with low back pain for weeks. He states that he has morning stiffness that lasts for less than 30 minutes. He presents with Bouchards nodes and Heberdens nodes on his fingers. What palpatory findings do you expect to see in his lumbar spine x-ray?","o":["A. Osteoparthritic spurs and narrowing of the disc spaces usually between L4/L5 and L5-S1","B. Widening of the disc spaces","C. Narrowing of the thoracic spine","D. Felty’s syndrome"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Osteoparthritic spurs and narrowing of the disc spaces usually between L4/L5 and L5-S1","a":"","why":"<b>A. Osteophytic spurs and narrowed disc spaces, usually L4 to L5 and L5 to S1.</b> Heberden and Bouchard nodes with morning stiffness under 30 minutes mean <b>osteoarthritis (OA)</b>. The lumbar x-ray shows bone spurs and lost disc height at the lowest, most loaded levels.<br>B. Widened disc spaces: the opposite of degeneration.<br>C. 'Narrowing of the thoracic spine': not a finding.<br>D. Felty syndrome: rheumatoid arthritis with a big spleen and low white cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":148,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":56,"label":"Spinal stenosis: worse standing, eased by flexion","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-97.jpg","cap":"Stenosis: shopping-cart relief, Kemp test, stenotic vertebral foramen","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 97"},{"src":"sl/ta-O099-67-1.png","cap":"Normal vs stenotic vertebral foramen (bone specimens)","where":"Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 67","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":541},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-160-1.png","cap":"Stenosis = aging canal; neurogenic claudication","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 160","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":566}],"q":[{"id":"O079-97-1","s":"Spinal Stenosis: Narrowing of spinal canal. Why these patient have pain on walking and standing and flexing forward is a relieving factor?","o":["A. Standing and walking extend the spine, narrowing canal and foramina; flexion widens them","B. Standing and walking flex the spine, pushing the nucleus onto the roots; extension relieves it","C. Walking starves leg muscles of arterial blood; stopping in any posture relieves it","D. Standing raises intrathecal pressure like a Valsalva; flexing forward lowers it"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Standing and walking hold the lumbar spine in extension, which narrows the spinal canal and foramina and compresses the nerve roots (neurogenic claudication: back, buttock and thigh pain, leg weakness/heaviness). Flexing forward (sitting, leaning on a shopping cart) opens the canal and relieves symptoms.","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Upright posture keeps the lumbar spine in <b>extension</b>: the ligamentum flavum buckles in, the facets close, and the <b>canal and foramina narrow</b>. That causes <b>neurogenic claudication</b> (back, buttock and thigh pain, heavy legs). Leaning on a shopping cart flexes the spine and opens the space. Kemp's test (extension with rotation and sidebending to the painful side) reproduces it.<br>B. That is the disc \"squish and squirt\"; a disc worsens with flexion.<br>C. Vascular claudication: weak pulses; standing still relieves it.<br>D. Valsalva and Milgram raise intrathecal pressure to detect a disc or tumor; posture changes canal size, not this pressure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":97,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":141,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-98-1","s":"Which of the following is common in lumbar stenosis with neurogenic claudication, but is not typical in peripheral vascular disease with vascular claudication?","o":["A. Pain relief with sitting","B. Increased pain when treating a typical posterior lumbar tenderpoint","C. Absence of lower extremity weakness","D. Increased lower extremity pain with walking","E. Posterior calf pain"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased pain when treating a typical posterior lumbar tenderpoint","a":"","why":"<b>B. Increased pain when treating a typical posterior lumbar tender point.</b> Posterior lumbar counterstrain puts the spine in <b>extension</b>. Extension narrows the canal and foramina, so it hurts in <b>neurogenic</b> (stenosis) claudication. Vascular claudication depends on muscle work, and the patient is at rest.<br>A. Relief with sitting: rest eases both kinds.<br>C. No leg weakness: typical of vascular, not neurogenic.<br>D. Pain with walking: occurs in both.<br>E. Posterior calf pain: occurs in both.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":98,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":97,"f":"O098"}]},{"id":"O095-158-1","s":"An elderly aged women presents to you with Chronic back pain with radicular symptoms and intermittent neurogenic claudication. On X-ray it shows narrowing of the spinal canal with a hypertrophic posterior longitudinal ligament and thickening of the ligamentum flavum. What is the potential diagnosis?","o":["A. Compartment syndrome","B. Sciatica","C. Spondylolisthesis","D. Spinal Stenosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Spinal Stenosis","a":"","why":"<b>D. Spinal stenosis.</b> An elderly patient with chronic back pain, radicular symptoms and <b>neurogenic claudication</b>, plus a canal narrowed by a <b>buckled posterior longitudinal ligament and thick ligamentum flavum</b>. (These soft tissues show on MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; x-ray shows the bony changes.)<br>A. Compartment syndrome: tight, painful leg compartment after trauma.<br>B. Sciatica: a symptom (pain along the sciatic nerve), not the cause.<br>C. Spondylolisthesis: a forward slip; it can cause stenosis but is not described.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":158,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O080-13-4","s":"A 70-year-old male presents with chronic radicular low back pain associated with bilateral lower extremity pain and paresthesia. Which of the following additional findings is most closely associated with this clinical presentation?","o":["A. increased pain with standing and walking","B. extensor plantar response","C. fasciculations","D. increased deep tendon reflexes","E. symmetrical proximal hip girdle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"increased pain with standing and walking","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> Age 70 with bilateral radicular leg pain means <b>lumbar spinal stenosis</b>. Standing and walking hold the spine in <b>extension</b>, which narrows the canal and foramina. Sitting or leaning forward (shopping-cart sign) relieves it.<br>B. Extensor plantar response (Babinski): upper motor neuron lesion; stenosis squeezes roots.<br>C. Fasciculations: anterior horn cell disease such as ALS (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis); not a stenosis sign.<br>D. Brisk reflexes: upper motor neuron sign; root compression lowers reflexes.<br>E. Symmetric proximal hip-girdle weakness: suggests a myopathy; stenosis is usually low lumbar with distal, dermatomal signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":13,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":227,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":14,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":65,"f":"O099"}],"t":1},{"id":"O111-210-1","s":"A 72-year-old male presents with chronic bilateral leg cramping, numbness, and lower back discomfort that worsens after standing or walking for 15 minutes. He states he experiences immediate relief by sitting down or leaning forward over a shopping cart while walking. Ankle-brachial indices (ABI) and pedal pulses are normal bilaterally. What is the primary underlying cause of his symptoms, and what diagnostic modality confirms it?","o":["A. Peripheral Arterial Disease; Arterial Doppler Ultrasound","B. Lumbar Spinal Stenosis; Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)","C. Acute Herniated Nucleus Pulposus; Electromyography (EMG)","D. Degenerative Spondylolisthesis; Flexion-Extension Plain Radiographs"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar Spinal Stenosis; Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)","a":"","why":"<b>B. Lumbar spinal stenosis; MRI (magnetic resonance imaging).</b> Leg pain after standing or walking that eases at once with sitting or leaning on a shopping cart is <b>neurogenic claudication</b>: flexion opens the canal. <b>Normal pulses and ankle-brachial indices</b> rule out an arterial cause. MRI confirms the narrowed canal.<br>A. Peripheral arterial disease: weak pulses, a low ankle-brachial index, and relief by standing still.<br>C. Acute herniated nucleus pulposus: one-sided dermatomal pain, worse with flexion and sitting.<br>D. Degenerative spondylolisthesis can cause stenosis, but no step-off is described; flexion-extension films test stability, not canal size.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":210,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":57,"label":"Kemp's test reproduces stenosis leg pain","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-163-1.png","cap":"Kemp test: extension with rotation; pain radiates below buttocks","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 163","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1056},{"src":"sl/O079-96.jpg","cap":"Stenosis evaluation: worse walking or standing, relieved bending forward","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 96"}],"q":[{"id":"O095-161-1","s":"What test would you perform to support your diagnosis of spinal stenosis?","o":["A. Straight leg raise","B. Kemp’s test","C. Ober’s Test","D. Hip drop test"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Kemp’s test","a":"","why":"<b>B. Kemp's test.</b> The patient extends the lumbar spine with rotation and sidebending <b>toward the painful side</b> for about 30 seconds. Extension narrows the canal and foramina, so back, buttock and thigh pain (neurogenic claudication) supports <b>spinal stenosis</b>.<br>A. Straight leg raise: nerve root tension from a disc.<br>C. Ober test: tight iliotibial band; not a stenosis test.<br>D. Hip drop test: screens lumbar sidebending.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":161,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":53,"f":"O100"}],"t":1},{"id":"O100-51-1","s":"After resolving the GI problem, the 65 y/o male patient returns with restriction in walking and leg extension. His symptoms get better with 20 to 30 mins of rest when he sits down. You perform the Kemp's test. If positive, you would find ___.","o":["A. Pain radiating below the buttocks","B. Rebound tenderness","C. Pain radiating up to the head","D. Pain radiates down the opposite leg","E. Pain during inhalation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Pain radiating below the buttocks","a":"","why":"<b>A. Pain radiating below the buttocks.</b> Kemp's test (lumbar extension with rotation and sidebending to the painful side, 30 seconds) narrows the canal and foramina. In stenosis it reproduces <b>neurogenic claudication</b>: pain in the back, buttock and thigh, below the buttocks, with heavy legs.<br>B. Rebound tenderness: peritoneal irritation.<br>C. Pain up to the head: not a lumbar finding.<br>D. Pain down the opposite leg: crossed (well) straight leg raise, a large disc.<br>E. Pain on inhalation: rib or pleural problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":51,"f":"O100"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":57,"label":"Stenosis imaging: osteophytes, narrow discs (spondylosis)","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-95.jpg","cap":"MRI and drawings of a canal narrowed by osteophytes","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 95"},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-85-1.png","cap":"X-ray: degenerative lumbar spine with osteophytes","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 85","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":850},{"src":"sl/O080-7.jpg","cap":"Stenosis radiology: osteophytes, decreased disc space","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 7"}],"q":[{"id":"O092-2-1","s":"What radiographic findings are commonly present in spinal stenosis?","o":["A. Facet tropism","B. Osteophytes and decrease intervertebral disc space.","C. Fracture of the pars interarticularis without anterior displacement of the vertebral body.","D. Lumbarization"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Osteophytes and decrease intervertebral disc space.","a":"","why":"<b>B. Osteophytes and decreased disc space.</b> Stenosis usually comes from <b>degeneration</b>: bone spurs, lost disc height, big facets and a thick ligamentum flavum narrow the canal. X-ray shows the spurs and narrowed discs; MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) confirms the narrowed canal.<br>A. Facet tropism: a congenital facet-angle asymmetry.<br>C. Pars fracture without slip: spondylolysis.<br>D. Lumbarization: S1 acting as a sixth lumbar vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":145,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":132,"f":"O111"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":32,"f":"O104"}],"t":1},{"id":"O092-3-1","s":"A 50-year-old male comes to your office with chronic low back pain. His back pain is a dull ache that radiates into the buttock and thighs bilaterally. The pain is made worse with standing or walking 20 minutes or more and maintaining extension. Pain is relieved completely with sitting. Range of motion of his lumbar spine is decreased. Sensation is decreased in both feet in a stocking like distribution. All lumbar spinous processes are palpable and percussion on the spinous processes produce no pain. There is no fever, no weight loss and undisturbed sleep. What is the most likely finding this patient will have on radiological studies?","o":["A. Lytic (punched-out lesions) spinal metastases involving the lumbar vertebrae.","B. Loss of definition of a vertebral end-plate and elevation of the periosteum suggestive of vertebral osteomyelitis.","C. Spondylolisthesis of the L1 on L2","D. A herniated nucleus pulposus on magnetic resonance imaging.","E. Spondylosis of the lumbar spine."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spondylosis of the lumbar spine.","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylosis of the lumbar spine.</b> Buttock and thigh pain on both sides, <b>worse with standing, walking and extension</b> and gone with sitting, is <b>spinal stenosis</b>. Its usual cause is spondylosis: worn discs and spurs. No fever, weight loss or night pain, so no red flags.<br>A. Lytic metastases: cancer history, night pain, weight loss.<br>B. Osteomyelitis: fever, IV (intravenous) drug use, eroded endplates.<br>C. L1 on L2 slip: spondylolisthesis is usually L5 on S1 or L4 on L5.<br>D. Herniated disc: one-sided dermatomal pain, worse with flexion and sitting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":33,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":150,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":134,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":57,"label":"Stenosis/OA exercise: flexion and pelvic tilt","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-131.jpg","cap":"Lumbar exercises: pelvic tilt, resisted flexion, extension-inducing","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 131"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-91-2","s":"Osteoarthritis: Degenerative Arthritis (Disc Degeneration). Disc Degeneration Narrowing of disc space L4/L5 &L5-S1. Osteoarthritic Spur. Which exercise is best for this patient presentation?","o":["A. Strict bed rest until the pain has fully resolved","B. Standing extension with rotation and sidebending toward the painful side","C. Supine pelvic tilt and active resistive flexion (knees to chest) to flatten the lordosis","D. Prone extension-inducing exercises (press-ups) to increase the lordosis"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion-biased exercise that flattens or decreases the lordosis: supine pelvic tilt and active resistive lumbar flexion (hips and knees flexed to the chest, pushing the knees into the hands). Avoid extension.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Lumbar OA (osteoarthritis) brings <b>spinal stenosis</b> symptoms. <b>Extension narrows</b> the canal and foramina; <b>flexion opens</b> them. Treatment is <b>pelvic tilt</b> plus active resistive flexion (knees to chest, push the knees into your hands) to flatten the lordosis, with OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) for motion and blood flow.<br>A. Bed rest beyond 2 days weakens the patient and is not advised.<br>B. That is Kemp's position, a test that provokes stenosis pain.<br>D. Extension-inducing exercise is for a lost (flat) lordosis; here it worsens the narrowing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":91,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":135,"f":"O107"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":32,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":32,"f":"O108"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":145,"f":"O107"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":58,"label":"Spondylo- terms: osis, lysis, listhesis","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O100-48-1.png","cap":"Normal pars, spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis side by side","where":"Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 48","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":650},{"src":"sl/O079-103.jpg","cap":"Spondylolysis (pars break) vs spondylolisthesis (forward slip)","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 103"},{"src":"sl/O079-98.jpg","cap":"Spondylosis (degeneration) vs spondylitis (inflammation)","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 98"}],"q":[{"id":"O080-14-11","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (diagnosis) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. Degenerative changes within the intervertebral disc and ankylosing of adjacent vertebral bodies is known as","o":["A. spina bifida","B. spinal stenosis","C. spondylolisthesis","D. spondylolysis","E. spondylosis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"spondylosis","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylosis.</b> General term for <b>degenerative</b> change: disc narrowing, osteophytes and stiffening (<b>ankylosis</b>) of neighbouring vertebral bodies. Patients have constant low back and buttock pain and stiffness.<br>A. Spina bifida: the laminae fail to close.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: a narrowed canal or foramen; a result of degeneration, not its name.<br>C. Spondylolisthesis: forward slip.<br>D. Spondylolysis: pars fracture with no slip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":241,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":16,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":89,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":88,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-104-1","s":"What is spondylolysis?","o":["A. Fracture of the pars interarticularis without forward slip of the vertebral body","B. Disc degeneration with osteophytes and ankylosis of adjacent vertebral bodies","C. Failed laminar closure with the meninges herniating through the defect","D. Forward slip of a vertebral body on the one below it","E. Inflammatory arthritis of the spine and sacroiliac joints"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Fracture of the pars interarticularis without anterior displacement of the vertebral body (thought to be micro-fractures sustained over time). It usually gives rise to spondylolisthesis.","a":"","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Spondylolysis</b> (\"lysis\" = break) is a fracture of the <b>pars interarticularis</b>, the bony bridge between the upper and lower facets, <b>without anterior displacement</b>. It is thought to come from repeated micro-fractures, is most common at L5 (80% bilateral), and often leads to spondylolisthesis.<br>B. Degeneration with ankylosis: spondylosis.<br>C. Meninges through a laminar defect: spina bifida meningocele.<br>D. Forward slip: spondylolisthesis.<br>E. Inflammatory arthritis: spondylitis (e.g., ankylosing spondylitis).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":104,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":156,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":47,"f":"O100"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":71,"f":"O099"}],"t":1},{"id":"O080-14-10","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (diagnosis) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. The anterior displacement of one vertebral body in relation to the one below is known as","o":["A. spina bifida","B. spinal stenosis","C. spondylolisthesis","D. spondylolysis","E. spondylosis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"spondylolisthesis","a":"","why":"<b>C. Spondylolisthesis.</b> \"Listhesis\" means slip: <b>forward displacement</b> of a vertebral body on the one below, most often <b>L5 on S1</b> (isthmic, from pars defects, under 50) or L4 on L5 (degenerative, over 50). It shows on the lateral x-ray and is graded I–IV by percent slip.<br>A. Spina bifida: laminar closure defect.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: narrowed canal.<br>D. Spondylolysis: pars fracture without the slip.<br>E. Spondylosis: disc degeneration with osteophytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":14,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":239,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":16,"f":"O103"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-105-1","s":"What is spondylolisthesis?","o":["A. Pars interarticularis fracture with the vertebral body still in line","B. Backward displacement of a vertebral body on the one below it","C. Forward displacement of a vertebral body on the one below it, most often L5 on S1","D. Disc degeneration with osteophytes and ankylosis of adjacent vertebral bodies","E. Narrowing of the spinal canal by ligamentum flavum and facet hypertrophy"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior displacement (forward slip) of a vertebral body on the one below it, most commonly L5 slipping over S1. The isthmic type comes from a pars interarticularis defect.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Spondylolisthesis</b> (\"listhesis\" = slip) is <b>anterior displacement</b> of a vertebral body on the one below, most often <b>L5 over S1</b>. The isthmic type comes from bilateral pars defects. Clues: more lordosis, flattened gluteal creases, <b>hamstring spasm</b> (the most common dysfunction), a palpable step-off.<br>A. Fracture without slip: spondylolysis.<br>B. Backward slip is retrolisthesis, not spondylolisthesis.<br>D. Degeneration with ankylosis: spondylosis.<br>E. Narrowed canal: spinal stenosis (a slip can cause it, but it is not the slip).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":105,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":157,"f":"O107"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":59,"label":"Spondylo- x-rays: collar, slip, spurs","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-112.jpg","cap":"Scottie dog outlined; arrows at the collar (pars defect)","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 112"},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-91-1.png","cap":"X-rays: pars defect on lateral and oblique views","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 91","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":600},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-165-1.png","cap":"Lateral x-ray: L5 slipped forward on S1 (arrow)","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 165","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":263},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-166-3.png","cap":"Spondylolisthesis = Scottie dog neck separation; best on lateral film","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 166","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":455}],"q":[{"id":"O080-15-12","s":"For each numbered item (objective finding) select one heading (diagnosis) most closely associated with it. Each lettered heading may be selected once, more than once, or not at all. A lumbar spine roentgenogram reveals a 'collar around the neck of the Scotty dog' at L5.","o":["A. spina bifida","B. spinal stenosis","C. spondylolisthesis","D. spondylolysis","E. spondylosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"spondylolysis","a":"","why":"<b>D. Spondylolysis.</b> It is a defect (fracture) of the <b>pars interarticularis</b> without forward slip. On the <b>oblique</b> x-ray the pars is the Scottie dog's neck, and the dark break looks like a <b>collar</b>. It is most common at L5, in athletes who hyperextend.<br>A. Spina bifida: failed closure of the laminae.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: a narrowed canal, best seen on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging).<br>C. Spondylolisthesis: forward slip, seen on the lateral view.<br>E. Spondylosis: disc narrowing and osteophytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice questions","n":"Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions","sl":15,"f":"O080"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":243,"f":"O111"},{"t":"Capobianco questions (SGA)","n":"Practice Questions 3 Lumbar Spine (Capobianco)","sl":16,"f":"O103"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":70,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":70,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-104-2","s":"Spondylolysis (fracture of the pars interarticularis without anterior displacement of the vertebral body): Radiographic findings?","o":["A. Vertebral endplate erosion with an enhancing disc on contrast MRI","B. Disc space narrowing with anterior osteophytes (lipping) on the lateral view","C. Forward slip of L5 on S1, graded by percentage on the lateral view","D. Collar on the neck of the Scottie dog: a lucent pars defect on the oblique view"],"g":["D"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"\"Collar on the neck of a Scottie dog\": a lucent pars defect seen on the oblique lumbar x-ray.","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> On an <b>oblique</b> lumbar x-ray the posterior elements look like a Scottie dog: <b>nose = transverse process</b>, <b>eye = pedicle</b>, ear = superior articular process, front leg = inferior articular process, <b>neck = pars interarticularis</b>. A pars fracture is a dark <b>collar</b> on the neck. The lateral view misses it because nothing has slipped.<br>A. Endplate erosion and enhancement on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging): osteomyelitis with discitis.<br>B. Narrowing and lipping: spondylosis.<br>C. Slip graded on the lateral view: spondylolisthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":104,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":156,"f":"O107"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-102-1","s":"Spondylosis: Degenerative changes within intervertebral disc and ankylosis of adjacent vertebral bodies. Patient symptoms: Constant pain and stiffness in the low back, buttocks. Radiographic findings?","o":["A. Lucent \"collar\" on the neck of the Scottie dog on the oblique view","B. \"Bamboo spine\" with fused sacroiliac joints","C. Disc space narrowing, endplate sclerosis and osteophytes (anterior lipping)","D. Eroded vertebral endplates with an enhancing disc on contrast MRI","E. Forward slip of one vertebral body on the one below on the lateral view"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Degenerative changes: disc space narrowing, endplate sclerosis and osteophytes (anterior lipping of the vertebral bodies).","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Spondylosis</b> is age-related <b>degeneration</b> of discs and vertebrae. X-ray or MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) shows <b>disc space narrowing</b>, <b>endplate sclerosis</b> and <b>osteophytes</b> (anterior lipping), sometimes bridging (ankylosis).<br>A. Scottie-dog collar: spondylo<b>lysis</b> (pars fracture).<br>B. Bamboo spine: ankylosing spondylitis, an inflammatory spondylitis, not spondylosis.<br>D. Eroded, enhancing endplates and disc: osteomyelitis with discitis.<br>E. Forward slip: spondylo<b>listhesis</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":102,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":155,"f":"O107"}]},{"id":"O099-71-4","s":"Match each of the following clinical correlations of the lumbar spine with its most relevant characteristic: Spondylolisthesis","o":["A. Spinous processes closer on palpation","B. Scotty dog separation","C. Pressure on the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","D. Narrowing of posterior longitudinal ligament","E. Stress fracture of the pars interarticularis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Scotty dog separation","a":"","why":"<b>B. Scotty dog separation.</b> In spondylolisthesis the vertebra slips forward, so on the oblique film the Scottie dog's neck is not just collared but <b>pulled apart</b> (a 'decapitated' dog). That separation is the slip.<br>A. Spinous processes closer: degenerative arthritis.<br>C. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve pressure: meralgia paresthetica.<br>D. Narrowed posterior longitudinal ligament: posterolateral disc herniation.<br>E. Pars stress fracture alone (a collar, no slip): spondylolysis. It causes the isthmic type but is not the slip itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":71,"f":"O099"}]},{"id":"O097-68-1","s":"You are evaluating an X-ray of a patient’s L-spine. You notice a linear lucency through the L5 pars interarticularis and a forward shifting of L5 on S1. What is your diagnosis","o":["A. Ankylosing spondylitis","B. Somatic dysfunction L5 FRlSl","C. Spondylosis","D. Spondylolysis","E. Spondylolisthesis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spondylolisthesis","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylolisthesis.</b> A lucent line through the <b>pars interarticularis</b> (the fracture) <b>plus forward shift of L5 on S1</b> (the slip) = isthmic spondylolisthesis. The slip shows on the lateral view.<br>A. Ankylosing spondylitis: bamboo spine, fused sacroiliac joints.<br>B. L5 FRS (flexed, rotated, sidebent) somatic dysfunction: a motion finding, invisible on x-ray.<br>C. Spondylosis: disc narrowing and osteophytes.<br>D. Spondylolysis: the pars fracture <b>without</b> a slip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":68,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":91,"f":"O096"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":68,"f":"O098"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":208,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":60,"label":"Spondylolisthesis: step-off shelf, tight hamstrings","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-110.jpg","cap":"Pars defect without and with L5 slip; where the shelf is","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 110"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-166-2.png","cap":"Drawing: L5 slipped forward on the sacrum","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 166","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":249},{"src":"sl/O080-10.jpg","cap":"Spondylolisthesis on lateral x-ray, spondylolysis on oblique","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 10"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-105-2","s":"Spondylolisthesis (anterior displacement of the vertebral body on the one below it; most common L5 slip over S1): What would you palpate during your examination of the Lsp? What is a palpable shelf?","o":["A. Spinous processes closer together with loss of spring at every level","B. A tender point medial to the ASIS with restricted hip extension","C. A boggy area with a step-off (shelf) between spinous processes that stays in flexion","D. Tenderness from the L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crest","E. A tuft of hair over a lumbosacral laminar defect"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"A boggy area with a palpable shelf (step-off sign): the spinous processes no longer line up at the slip, and the step at L5 persists even with lumbar flexion. Hamstring spasm is the most common associated dysfunction.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> In spondylolisthesis you feel <b>bogginess</b> and a <b>palpable shelf (step-off)</b>: the spinous processes no longer line up, so one sits forward of its neighbour. The shelf stays when the patient <b>flexes</b>, so it is not just posture. Expect <b>hamstring spasm</b> too.<br>A. Close spinous processes with loss of spring: disc degeneration (OA, osteoarthritis).<br>B. Tender point medial to the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine): psoas or anterior L1 counterstrain point.<br>D. L4–L5 transverse processes to iliac crest: iliolumbar ligament strain.<br>E. Hair tuft (faun tail nevus): spina bifida occulta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":105,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":157,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2016 TA review","n":"OMM E2 TA Review Fall 2016","sl":55,"f":"O100"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":40,"f":"O108"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":41,"f":"O108"}],"t":1},{"id":"O079-110-1","s":"In B, a fracture or separation of the pars: Sliding of L5 on S1. Anterior slippage at body of L5 occurs, movement of the posterior arch of L5 is resisted by the facet surfaces of S1. The resulting shelf is palpable at which level?","o":["A. Between the L5 spinous process and the sacrum, because the L5 arch slides forward","B. Between the L4 and L5 spinous processes, because the L5 arch stays back","C. Between S1 and S2 on the median sacral crest","D. Between the L3 and L4 spinous processes, two levels above the slip"],"g":["B"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"At the L5 spinous process, as a step-off between the L4 and L5 spinous processes (the level just above the slip). The L5 posterior arch stays back while L4 rides forward with the L5 body.","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> With a <b>pars fracture</b> the L5 body slides forward and carries L4 and everything above with it. The <b>L5 posterior arch</b> (lamina and spinous process) is held back by the S1 facets. So the L5 spinous process stays prominent and <b>L4 sits forward of it</b>: the shelf is felt between <b>L4 and L5</b>, one level above the slip.<br>A. That happens when the whole vertebra slips with an intact arch (degenerative type); here the arch is broken.<br>C. The sacrum does not move in an L5–S1 slip.<br>D. L3 and L4 move forward together, so there is no step between them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":110,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":163,"f":"O107"}]},{"id":"O092-4-1","s":"With passive range of motion testing of engaging flexion of the spinous processes of the lumbar spine, you find a step-off or a palpable shelf at the level of L5. You notice the spinous process is difficult to feel. The most likely diagnosis of L5 is:","o":["A. Osteoarthritis","B. Spondylolysis","C. Spondylosis","D. Extension somatic dysfunction","E. Spondylolisthesis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spondylolisthesis","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylolisthesis.</b> A <b>step-off (palpable shelf)</b> at L5 that <b>stays with flexion</b>, with a spinous process that is hard to feel, means one vertebra has slipped forward on the next.<br>A. Osteoarthritis: stiff, no spring, spinous processes close; no step.<br>B. Spondylolysis: pars fracture with no slip, so no step-off.<br>C. Spondylosis: degeneration; no step.<br>D. Extension somatic dysfunction: a motion restriction that changes with flexion, not a fixed shelf.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":34,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":169,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":136,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O095-165-1","s":"Ms.Brown, 49yr old Female has pain in the lower back, often worse when standing or walking and relieved when sitting or bending forward. She experiences a “slipping sensation” when returning to an upright position, such as standing up from a sitting position or from bending over. The symptoms can be one sided within the low back itself. Has pins and needles and numbness, into the back of the legs and feet. You palpate tight hamstrings on physical exam. Lateral X-ray is shown. What condition do these findings point to?","o":["A. Piriformis Syndrome","B. Spondylolisthesis","C. Spondylolysis","D. Spondylitis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Spondylolisthesis","a":"","why":"<b>B. Spondylolisthesis.</b> Clues: pain <b>worse standing and walking (extension)</b>, eased by sitting or bending forward, a <b>slipping sensation</b> on standing up, and <b>tight hamstrings</b>. The lateral x-ray shows L5 forward on S1.<br>A. Piriformis syndrome: buttock pain after sitting; normal x-ray.<br>C. Spondylolysis: pars fracture with no slip, so no slipping sensation or shift.<br>D. Spondylitis: inflammatory; morning stiffness that eases with activity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":165,"f":"O095"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":60,"label":"Degenerative L4-on-L5 slip causing stenosis (MRI)","slides":[{"src":"sl/O108-41.jpg","cap":"MRI: L4 slipped on L5 with spinal stenosis","where":"2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 41"},{"src":"sl/O079-106.jpg","cap":"Isthmic (under 50, L5–S1) versus degenerative (over 50, L4–L5) slip","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 106"}],"q":[{"id":"O108-41-1","s":"A 55 year old female presents with bilateral lower extremity weakness. What’s the problem?","o":["A. Isthmic spondylolisthesis of L5 on S1","B. Posterolateral L4–L5 disc herniation compressing the L5 root","C. Compression fracture of L4 from osteoporosis","D. Spondylolysis of L5 without displacement","E. Spondylolisthesis of L4 on L5 with spinal stenosis"],"g":["E"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Spondylolisthesis of L4 on L5 with spinal stenosis.","a":"","why":"<b>E. Spondylolisthesis of L4 on L5 with spinal stenosis.</b> The green line along the backs of the vertebral bodies <b>steps forward at L4</b> over L5, and the canal behind it is narrowed (arrow). A <b>degenerative</b> slip (over 50, usually L4–L5) narrows the canal, so both legs weaken; a shelf is felt at the L4 spinous process.<br>A. An isthmic L5–S1 slip shows the step between L5 and the sacrum, usually under 50.<br>B. A posterolateral L4–L5 disc gives one-sided L5 signs, not a forward slip.<br>C. A compression fracture wedges the body; it does not slide.<br>D. Spondylolysis is a pars break with no displacement.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O108-41-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":41,"f":"O108"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":60,"label":"Spondylolisthesis care: conservative, no HVLA","slides":[{"src":"sl/O079-105.jpg","cap":"Spondylolisthesis facts; manage by reducing the lumbar lordosis","where":"2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 105"},{"src":"sl/O080-9.jpg","cap":"85 to 90% conservative; HVLA relatively contraindicated","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 9"}],"q":[{"id":"O079-105-3","s":"Spondylolisthesis: How would this patient be managed?","o":["A. Prone extension (press-up) exercises to deepen the lumbar lordosis","B. HVLA thrust at the slipped segment to push the vertebra back into line","C. OMT above the slip and to the sacrum, plus pelvic-tilt exercise to reduce lordosis","D. Emergency surgical decompression within 48 hours of diagnosis"],"g":["C"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"Conservatively. Treat the somatic dysfunction above the slip and the sacral dysfunctions with OMT (HVLA is relatively contraindicated), and reduce the lumbar lordosis with exercises that tilt the pelvis and flatten the lordosis.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> Most patients (<b>85–90%</b>) do well with conservative care. Treat the somatic dysfunction <b>above the slip</b> and the <b>sacral</b> dysfunctions with OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment), and <b>reduce the lordosis</b> with pelvic-tilt exercise; add weight loss and no high heels.<br>A. Extension adds shear at the slip and worsens pain.<br>B. HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude) is <b>relatively contraindicated</b> at an unstable slip.<br>D. Emergency decompression within 48 hours is for cauda equina syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":105,"f":"O079"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":157,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 lecture","n":"Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine","sl":40,"f":"O108"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":61,"label":"Type I ME: concavity down, posterior TPs up","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-196-1.png","cap":"Type I set-up: concavity down, posterior TPs up","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 196","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1840}],"q":[{"id":"O096-108-1","s":"A patient has a diagnosis of L1-5NSlRr. How would you position the patient if you wanted to treat them using muscle energy?","o":["A. Patient will lay on the side with the convexity of the curve towards the table and the posterior transverse processes are facing up","B. Patient will lay on the side with the concavity of the curve towards the table and the posterior transverse processes are facing up","C. Patient will lay on the side with the concavity of the curve towards the table and the posterior transverse processes are facing down","D. Patient will lay on the side with the convexity of the curve towards the table and the posterior transverse processes are facing down"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Patient will lay on the side with the concavity of the curve towards the table and the posterior transverse processes are facing up","a":"","why":"<b>B.</b> L1 to L5 NSlRr is a Type I (neutral) group curve: sidebent left, rotated right. The <b>concavity is on the left</b> and the <b>posteriorly rotated transverse processes (TPs) are on the right</b>. The muscle energy set-up: side-lying with the <b>concavity toward the table</b> and the posterior TPs <b>up</b> (here, lying on the left side). Lifting the ankles then sidebends him right, into the barrier.<br>A. Convexity down would put the right-sided posterior TPs down, not up.<br>C. With the concavity down, the posterior TPs face up, not down.<br>D. Not the standard set-up; Type I goes concavity down, TPs up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":108,"f":"O096"}],"t":1},{"id":"O095-194-1","s":"Upon further inspection, you also see in the patient from the previous question that they have a type I curve somatic dysfunction (L1-5NSlRr)! How would you position and treat them to relax the tissues involving the vertebrae?","o":["A. MET → Posterior TP at apex is down, flex hips/knees to segment above apex, sidebend pts legs towards ceiling, pt brings legs to floor as you resist","B. HVLA → Posterior TP at apex is up, flex hips/knees to dysfx segment, pts straightens lower leg as top foot hooked behind, rotate shoulder away from you to segment above, small thrust on pt at end of exhalation","C. MET → Posterior TP at apex is up, flex hips/knees to segment above apex, sidebend pts legs towards ceiling, pt brings legs to floor as you resist","D. MET → Posterior TP at apex is up, flex hips/knees to apex, sidebend pts legs towards ceiling, pt brings legs to floor as you resist","E. Counterstain → Put pt in lateral recumbent, dysfx side up, pt hikes hip up to shoulder as you assess tender point at apex for 90 seconds"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"MET → Posterior TP at apex is up, flex hips/knees to apex, sidebend pts legs towards ceiling, pt brings legs to floor as you resist","a":"","why":"<b>D.</b> Type I muscle energy (MET) for L1 to L5 NSlRr: side-lying with the <b>posterior transverse process (TP) at the apex up</b> (concavity down), hips and knees flexed until motion reaches the <b>apex</b>, ankles lifted <b>toward the ceiling</b> to sidebend into the barrier. The patient pushes the feet <b>toward the floor</b> for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance; repeat 3 or more times.<br>A. Posterior TP down is the wrong side for this curve.<br>B. HVLA (high-velocity low-amplitude) is a thrust, not a patient-powered technique.<br>C. Localize to the apex itself, not the segment above it.<br>E. Counterstrain treats a tender point held 90 seconds; this is a group curve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":194,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":61,"label":"Lumbar ME: SUE for extended, FDR for flexed","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-174-1.png","cap":"Two side-lying lumbar muscle energy set-ups","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 174","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":503},{"src":"sl/ta-O096-132-1.png","cap":"Sims position for an extended dysfunction","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 132","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":2048},{"src":"sl/O081-3.jpg","cap":"Lumbar muscle energy SUE and FDR; counterstrain points","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"O104-29-1","s":"Which of the following positions would you put the patient in for muscle energy for a lumbar somatic dysfunction diagnosis of L2 extended, rotated and sidebent left?","o":["A. Lateral recumbent on the left side of the patient","B. Lateral Sims on the right side of the patient","C. Lateral Sims on the left side of the patient","D. Lateral recumbent on the right side of the patient.","E. Lateral Sims and prone."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral Sims on the right side of the patient","a":"","why":"<b>B. Lateral Sims on the patient's right side.</b> L2 ERSL (extended, rotated left, sidebent left) is an <b>extended</b> Type II dysfunction, so use <b>SUE</b>: <b>S</b>ims position, posterior transverse process (TP) <b>U</b>p, for <b>E</b>xtended. The posterior TP is left, so the left side faces up: she lies on her right side, chest rolled toward the table.<br>A. Lateral recumbent on the left: the FDR set-up (Flexed, TP Down, lateral Recumbent) for L2 FRSL.<br>C. Sims on the left side puts the posterior TP down.<br>D. Lateral recumbent on the right: FDR for L2 FRSR.<br>E. Prone is not part of this technique.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":29,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":117,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":126,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":180,"f":"O095"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":206,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O096-132-1","s":"Patient comes in with lower back pain. After an Osteopathic exam, you decide to perform Muscle Energy on the patient. You put the patient in the position below. What was the initial diagnosis of the patient?","o":["A. L1-L3 NSrRl","B. L2-L5 FRrSr","C. L2 ERrSr","D. L2 FRlSl","E. L2 ERlSl"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L2 ERrSr","a":"","why":"<b>C. L2 ERrSr.</b> The photo shows <b>Sims position</b> (side-lying, chest rolled toward the table), used only for <b>extended</b> Type II dysfunctions: <b>SUE</b> = Sims, posterior transverse process (TP) Up, Extended. She lies on her left side, so the posterior TP is <b>right</b>: extended, rotated right, sidebent right.<br>A. L1 to L3 NSrRl: a neutral (Type I) group curve, treated plain side-lying.<br>B. L2 to L5 FRrSr: flexed and a group; flexed uses lateral recumbent (FDR).<br>D. L2 FRlSl: flexed, so lateral recumbent with the posterior TP down.<br>E. L2 ERlSl: Sims too, but she would lie on her right side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O096-132-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2023","sl":132,"f":"O096"}]},{"id":"O095-172-1","s":"Dr.Burns has a pt come in with Lower Back Pain. She performs the displayed treatment. What is the most likely spinal diagnosis?","o":["A. T5 RSL","B. L3 FRSR","C. L4 ERSL","D. L1-3 NRLSR","E. L4 FRSL"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"L4 FRSL","a":"","why":"<b>E. L4 FRSL.</b> He lies on his side (<b>lateral recumbent</b>); the physician lifts the leg toward the ceiling and he pushes it <b>down</b>. That is <b>FDR</b>: <b>F</b>lexed dysfunction, posterior transverse process (TP) <b>D</b>own, lateral <b>R</b>ecumbent, patient pushes down. He lies on his left side, so the posterior TP is left: flexed, rotated left, sidebent left.<br>A. T5 RSL: thoracic; this is a lumbar technique.<br>B. L3 FRSR: FDR too, but he would lie on his right side.<br>C. L4 ERSL: extended, so Sims position (SUE).<br>D. L1 to L3 NRLSR: a neutral group curve; he would lie on his right side (concavity down).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O095-172-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":172,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O111-117-1","s":"You are evaluating your partner in OMM lab and find several somatic dysfunctions of their lumbar spine. After describing how an osteopathic treatment would benefit them in lay language and obtaining their consent. You perform the treatment depicted in the image below. Which of the following was their initial diagnosis?","o":["A. L4 FRlSl","B. L5 NSlRr","C. L2 FRrSr","D. L3 ERrSr"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L3 ERrSr","a":"","why":"<b>D. L3 ERrSr.</b> The patient lies in <b>Sims position</b> (side-lying, chest rolled toward the table) with the legs off the table, so this is <b>SUE</b>: Sims, posterior transverse process <b>Up</b>, <b>Extended</b> dysfunction. She lies on her left side, so the posterior transverse process is on the right: extended, rotated right, sidebent right.<br>A. L4 FRlSl (flexed, rotated and sidebent left): lateral recumbent (FDR), lying on the left.<br>B. L5 NSlRr: a neutral (Type I) pattern, treated plain side-lying.<br>C. L2 FRrSr: flexed, so lateral recumbent on the right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-117-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":117,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O111-111-1","s":"A 68-year-old male presents with right-sided low back pain that started after shoveling snow over the weekend. His pain extends from the right side of his low back to his right groin, and he additionally notes some pain in his left gluteal region. His posture appears overall bent forward and towards his right. Neurologic testing in the office including straight leg raise, lower extremity strength/sensation/reflexes is all intact bilaterally. Thomas test is positive on the right. What is the correct initial position for muscle energy for his most likely somatic dysfunction at L1?","o":["A. Sims position on his right","B. Lateral recumbent on his right","C. Lateral recumbent on his left","D. Sims position on his left"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral recumbent on his right","a":"","why":"<b>B. Lateral recumbent on his right.</b> Groin pain, a posture bent forward and to the right, opposite gluteal pain and a positive right Thomas test mean <b>right psoas syndrome</b>. Its key lesion is <b>L1 flexed, rotated and sidebent right</b> (FRSR). A flexed dysfunction uses <b>FDR</b>: Flexed, posterior transverse process <b>Down</b>, lateral <b>Recumbent</b>. The posterior transverse process is right, so he lies on his right side.<br>A. Sims on the right: SUE for L1 extended, rotated and sidebent left.<br>C. Lateral recumbent on the left: FDR for L1 FRSL (left psoas).<br>D. Sims on the left: SUE for L1 ERSR.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":111,"f":"O111"}]},{"id":"O111-119-1","s":"You are evaluating your partner in OMM lab and find several somatic dysfunctions of their lumbar spine. After describing how an osteopathic treatment would benefit them in lay language and obtaining their consent. You perform the treatment depicted in the image below. What is the patient's role in this treatment?","o":["A. Push their legs against the physician's resistance towards the ceiling","B. Push their legs against the physician's resistance towards the ground","C. Wait for 90 seconds","D. Exhale deeply before a thrust is delivered"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Push their legs against the physician's resistance towards the ceiling","a":"","why":"<b>A. Push the legs toward the ceiling against resistance.</b> The picture shows lumbar <b>muscle energy</b> in <b>Sims position</b> (SUE, for an extended dysfunction). The legs hang off the table toward the floor, taking the segment into its barrier. The patient then pushes the legs <b>up</b> for 3–5 seconds against equal resistance, relaxes, and the physician takes up the slack.<br>B. Pushing toward the ground is the patient's role in FDR (flexed dysfunction, legs lifted toward the ceiling).<br>C. Waiting 90 seconds is counterstrain.<br>D. Exhaling before a thrust is HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-119-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":119,"f":"O111"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":62,"label":"Lumbar counterstrain: anterior L1, spinous and TP points","slides":[{"src":"sl/O081-9.jpg","cap":"Anterior L1 tender point medial to the ASIS","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-183-1.png","cap":"Anterior L1 counterstrain, supine with hips hyperflexed","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 183","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":877},{"src":"sl/O081-11.jpg","cap":"Prone leg extension for spinous and transverse points","where":"2026 week 14 Lab Clinical Lumbar, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/ta-O095-185-1.png","cap":"Both legs extended for a spinous process point","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 185","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":322}],"q":[{"id":"O095-182-1","s":"27-year-old Female walks into you office with LBP. While prone, you feel a point that is tender just medial to the ASIS. How would you set up the Counterstrain treatment?","o":["A. Pt supine, extend both legs at hip","B. Pt supine, Flex neck and upper torso, SB toward+Rot away from tender point","C. Pt supine hyperflex hips, rotate legs away from tender point, SB toward tenderpoint.","D. Pt supine hyperflex hips, rotate and SB legs toward tenderpoint"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pt supine hyperflex hips, rotate legs away from tender point, SB toward tenderpoint.","a":"","why":"<b>C.</b> A tender point <b>just medial to the ASIS</b> (anterior superior iliac spine) is <b>anterior L1</b>. Anterior points are treated in <b>flexion</b>: patient supine, hips and knees markedly flexed, legs sidebent <b>toward</b> and rotated <b>away</b> from the tender point (flex, sidebend toward, rotate away). Hold 90 seconds, then return slowly.<br>A. Extending both legs treats a posterior spinous-process point, and that is done prone.<br>B. Flexing the neck and upper torso treats anterior thoracic points.<br>D. Rotating the legs toward the point is the wrong rotation for anterior L1.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":182,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O099-31-1","s":"A patient presents with a posterior L1 tenderpoint on the spinous process. How would you position the patient up to treat them using counterstrain?","o":["A. Pt is supine, flex the patient’s legs","B. Pt is prone, lift both legs","C. Pt is prone lift leg closest to the doctor and lean back.","D. Pt is prone and hanging off the edge of the table, flex their leg."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pt is prone, lift both legs","a":"","why":"<b>B. Prone, lift both legs.</b> A posterior tender point <b>on a lumbar spinous process</b> is treated with the patient <b>prone</b> and <b>both legs passively extended</b> (lifted, resting on the physician's thigh). Extension shortens the tissue at a midline point. Hold 90 seconds, return slowly.<br>A. Supine with the legs flexed: anterior L1 point.<br>C. Prone, lifting the near leg with rotation: a posterior <b>transverse process</b> point.<br>D. Hanging the leg off the table: not a lumbar counterstrain position.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2020","sl":31,"f":"O099"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":184,"f":"O095"}]},{"id":"O111-107-1","s":"A 24-year-old gymnast presents for evaluation of low back pain. She has had issues with back pain since her teenage years, which has typically been associated with periods of more intense gymnastics training. Prior workup including imaging has been negative. On evaluation, you palpate several tender points around her lumbar and pelvic regions. You decide to perform the treatment in the image depicted below. Which of the following is the location of the tender point being treated?","o":["A. Medial to the ASIS","B. Medial to the AIIS","C. Inferior to the AIIS"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Medial to the ASIS","a":"","why":"<b>A. Medial to the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine).</b> The photo shows the patient <b>supine</b> with hips and knees <b>markedly flexed</b> over the physician's thigh: the anterior L1 counterstrain position (flexion, legs sidebent toward and rotated away from the point). The anterior L1 tender point lies just medial to the ASIS.<br>B. Medial to the AIIS (anterior inferior iliac spine): the anterior L2 point.<br>C. Inferior to the AIIS: the anterior L4 point.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/O111-107-1.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":107,"f":"O111"}],"t":1},{"id":"O111-105-1","s":"A 24-year-old gymnast presents for evaluation of low back pain. She has had issues with back pain since her teenage years, which has typically been associated with periods of more intense gymnastics training. Prior workup including imaging has been negative. On evaluation, you palpate several tender points around her lumbar and pelvic regions. The most significantly tender point you palpate is found on her right transverse process at the level of the her iliac crest. Which of the following correctly describes the counterstrain treatment for this point?","o":["A. Physician stands on the patient's right side, extension and abduction of the right hip","B. Physician stands on the patient's left side, extension, adduction, and external rotation of the right hip","C. Physician stands on the patient's left side, flexion and abduction of the right hip","D. Physician stands on the patient's right side, flexion, sidebending, rotation of both lower extremities to the right"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Physician stands on the patient's left side, extension, adduction, and external rotation of the right hip","a":"","why":"<b>B. Physician on the patient's left; right hip extended, adducted and externally rotated.</b> A posterior lumbar <b>transverse process</b> tender point (here about L4, level with the iliac crest) is treated <b>prone in extension</b> with induced rotation. The physician stands opposite the point and draws the same-side leg into extension, adduction and external rotation, which rotates the pelvis; hold 90 seconds.<br>A. Extension with abduction from the same side does not add the needed rotation.<br>C. Flexion is for anterior points.<br>D. Flexing both legs with sidebending and rotation is the supine anterior L1 position.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":105,"f":"O111"}]}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":62,"label":"Counterstrain contraindications: fracture absolute, hypermobility HVLA","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O095-187-1.png","cap":"Relative contraindications, posterior lumbar counterstrain","where":"2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 187","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":338}],"q":[{"id":"O095-186-1","s":"All of these are relative contraindications to lumbar counterstrain, except:","o":["A. Acute lumbar fracture","B. Acute lumbar strain+sprain","C. Hip dislocation","D. Severe hip osteoarthritis","E. Joint hypermobility"],"g":["A","E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Joint hypermobility","a":"","why":"<b>A and E.</b> <b>Joint hypermobility</b> is a relative contraindication to <b>HVLA</b> (high-velocity low-amplitude), not to counterstrain. And a <b>fracture at the tender point</b> is an <b>absolute</b> contraindication to counterstrain, so an acute lumbar fracture is not 'relative'.<br>B. Acute lumbar strain and sprain: relative, for anterior lumbar points.<br>C. Hip dislocation: relative for posterior lumbar points, which move the legs.<br>D. Severe hip osteoarthritis: relative for the same reason.","ex":"","fl":["A is also correct: a fracture at the tender point is an absolute counterstrain contraindication, so an acute lumbar fracture is not a relative one. E stays correct: hypermobility is an HVLA contraindication."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review (2025 folder, undated)","sl":186,"f":"O095"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":63,"label":"Kidney viscerosomatic level: T10–T11","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O097-91-1.png","cap":"Sympathetic levels: kidneys and upper ureters at T10–T11","where":"Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 91","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":786},{"src":"sl/O108-15.jpg","cap":"Viscera and the lumbar spine; kidney pain to the flank","where":"2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 15"},{"src":"sl/O108-17.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic levels for head, heart, gut, pelvic organs and legs","where":"2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 17"}],"q":[{"id":"O110-1-2","s":"Which vertebral level is typically associated with kidney dysfunction?","o":["A. T10–T11","B. L3–L5","C. T1–T4","D. S2–S4","E. T5–T9"],"g":["A"],"k":[],"rv":false,"at":"T10-T11 (the study guide names T11). Kidney pain presents in the low back and flank.","a":"","why":"<b>A. T10–T11.</b> Sympathetic supply puts the <b>kidneys</b> and upper ureters (with the gonads, the rest of the small intestine and the right colon) at <b>T10–T11</b>, the superior mesenteric level. Kidney disease shows a viscerosomatic reflex there and pain from the low back to the flank.<br>B. L3–L5: no sympathetic outflow below L2; pain may be felt there, but the reflex level is higher.<br>C. T1–T4: head and neck; T1–T6 heart and lungs.<br>D. S2–S4: parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves to the pelvic organs.<br>E. T5–T9: upper abdominal organs (liver, stomach, pancreas).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 study guide (SGA)","n":"Montana OMM Exam 2 Study Guide 2024 (sample questions)","sl":null,"f":"O110"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"OMM-W14","u":63,"label":"Lumbar strain vs disc: neuro deficits decide","slides":[{"src":"sl/ta-O096-84-1.png","cap":"Low back pain differential; strain and disc are both mechanical","where":"Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 84","tb":1,"bl":"TA review","w":1140},{"src":"sl/O080-5.jpg","cap":"Lumbosacral strain: local ache and spasm, no deficits","where":"2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 5"}],"q":[{"id":"O092-6-1","s":"A 30-year-old athletic man presents with localized low back pain. The pain begin shortly after playing baseball. He denies any radiation of pain, paresthesias or weakness in the lower extremities. There is no previous history of low back pain. Physical exam reveals no focal neurological deficits. X-rays of the lumbar spine are normal. There is marked spasm of the paraspinal musculature on the right with L1-5 markedly rotated right and sidebent left. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Cauda equina syndrome","B. Herniated nucleus pulposus","C. Spondylolisthesis","D. Lumbar spinal stenosis","E. Acute lumbar strain"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute lumbar strain","a":"","why":"<b>E. Acute lumbar strain.</b> Pain right after sport, <b>localized</b>, with <b>no radiation and no neuro deficit</b>, and normal x-rays. The paraspinal spasm holds L1 to L5 in a group curve (neutral, rotated right, sidebent left). Treat with OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment).<br>A. Cauda equina syndrome: saddle numbness, bladder loss, low reflexes.<br>B. Herniated nucleus pulposus: dermatomal leg pain and a root deficit.<br>C. Spondylolisthesis: visible slip on x-ray; step-off.<br>D. Spinal stenosis: elderly patient; leg pain with standing and walking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Canvas pre-lab quiz","n":"Pre-Lab 14 Quiz - Clinical Lumbar Spine (results, 12 of 12)","sl":null,"f":"O092"},{"t":"older interactive (SGA)","n":"Master IA PPT","sl":36,"f":"O104"},{"t":"2026 interactive (poll)","n":"IA 12-13-14 Functional & Clinical Lumbar Spine (final)","sl":177,"f":"O107"},{"t":"2026 quizzes & polls (class compilation)","n":"Clickers + Quizzes OMM2 (2026 class compilation)","sl":140,"f":"O111"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":104,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":103,"f":"O098"}],"t":1},{"id":"O097-106-1","s":"Case 52-56: A 30-year-old athletic male presents to your office complaining of low back pain. He states that the pain started soon after playing baseball. The pain is localized to his low back. He denies any radiation of pain, paresthesias or weakness in the lower extremities. There is no previous history of low back pain. Physical exam reveals no focal neurologic deficits. X-rays of the lumbar spine are normal. There is marked spasm of the paraspinal musculature on the right. There appears to be a group dysfunction from L1-L5 that is markedly rotated right and sidebent left. There is a positive seated flexion test on the right, and the right sulcus is deeper. 53. If the patient had reported decreased sensation on the medial aspect of the ankle, absent patellar reflex and marked weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and knee extension, what would the most likely diagnosis be?","o":["A. Spondylolisthesis","B. Lumbar spinal stenosis","C. Herniated nucleus pulposus","D. Acute lumbar strain","E. None of the above are likely diagnoses"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Herniated nucleus pulposus","a":"","why":"<b>C. Herniated nucleus pulposus.</b> Now there are <b>focal root signs</b>: numb medial ankle, absent patellar reflex, weak dorsiflexion and knee extension. That is the <b>L4 root</b>, most likely from an L3 to L4 disc.<br>A. Spondylolisthesis: the x-rays were normal.<br>B. Spinal stenosis: an elderly patient with degenerative changes.<br>D. Acute lumbar strain: no neurologic deficit.<br>E. A herniated disc fits, so 'none' is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2022","sl":106,"f":"O097"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"OMM I E2 TA Review Fall 2021","sl":105,"f":"O098"}]}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Lymphoid tissue, vessels and one-way flow","p":"<p>The lymphatic system is called a <b>second circulatory system</b>. Blood capillaries leak fluid and protein into the tissues, and lymph vessels collect that fluid and return it to the veins. Unlike the blood, lymph has no central pump like the heart. The system is largely <b>passive</b>, so forces from outside the vessels drive much of its flow. When the flow fails, fluid stays in the tissues and the patient develops <b>edema</b> (swelling). A.T. Still, the founder of osteopathy, warned that fluid held too long in the tissues brings “fermentation, fever, sickness and death”. His student Frederic Millard described lymphatic techniques in 1922 that are still used today.</p>\n<h4>Three jobs</h4>\n<p>First, lymph carries protein that has leaked into the <b>interstitium</b> (the fluid space between cells) back to the blood. Second, it carries antigens (foreign molecules) to immune cells in the nodes, which starts an immune response. Third, it drains microbes, toxins, inflammatory mediators and inflammatory cells away from an injury. Acute inflammation resolves only when this clearing works; without it, inflammation lingers.</p>\n<h4>Lymphoid tissue</h4>\n<p>Lymphoid tissue is tissue packed with lymphocytes and other immune cells. The <b>spleen</b> is the <b>largest single mass</b> of it. It lies on the left, deep to ribs 9, 10 and 11, just below the abdominal diaphragm. It destroys damaged and misshapen red blood cells, makes antibodies (immunoglobulins) and clears encapsulated bacteria. The <b>thymus</b> sits in the superior mediastinum, the upper chest behind the sternum. T lymphocytes are processed there. It shrinks (involutes) in adults until it does little.</p>\n<p><b>Lymph nodes</b> are many small, organized filters spread along the vessels. They filter lymph and make lymphocytes. Superficial nodes run with the veins under the skin, and deep nodes lie beneath fascia and muscle. The <b>liver</b>, together with the intestines, makes one-half to two-thirds of all lymph fluid, but it is not the largest lymphoid mass. Smaller collections include the ring of tonsils at the back of the throat (palatine, lingual and pharyngeal) and the appendix. In the gut, Peyer's patches and lacteals (lymph vessels that absorb fat) lie beside the gut's own nerve plexuses, which suggests lymphatic treatment may help functional bowel disorders such as irritable bowel. Lung lymphoid tissue filters inhaled toxins.</p>\n<p>Lymph itself is a clear fluid with proteins and salts. Lymph vessels first appear in the fifth embryonic week as paired jugular lymph sacs. Lymphoid tissue keeps growing after birth until about age 6–9. It then shrinks to stable adult levels by age 15–16.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-4.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic organs (tonsils, thymus, liver, spleen) and a lymph node\"></div><figcaption>Lymphatic organs (tonsils, thymus, liver, spleen) and a lymph node <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where lymph vessels reach</h4>\n<p>Lymph channels run through <b>every tissue except three</b>. The three are the central nervous system (CNS, the brain and spinal cord), the <b>epidermis</b> (the outer layer of skin) and the <b>endomysium</b> (the thin sheath around each single muscle fiber). Small interstitial conduits drain those three instead. Every other tissue, the lungs included, has lymph vessels.</p>\n<h4>How lymph moves one way</h4>\n<p>Flow starts in <b>lymph capillaries</b>. These are blind-ended tubes made of a single leaky layer of endothelial cells. Fine anchoring filaments tie them to the surrounding connective tissue, so tissue movement tugs them open and fluid flows in. The capillaries drain into vessels built from short segments called <b>lymphangions</b>. Each lymphangion has smooth muscle in its wall and <b>one-way valves</b>. The segments contract <b>one after the other</b>, like a wave, not all at once. Each contraction pushes lymph into the next segment, and the valves stop it from flowing back. Flow is therefore <b>unidirectional</b> (one way only). Once lymph reaches the main transport vessels, the vessel itself propels it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O082-14.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphangions: one-way valves, segments contracting in sequence\"></div><figcaption>Lymphangions: one-way valves, segments contracting in sequence <small>· 2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System &amp; Diaphragms, page 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Lymph = passive second circulation; failure → edema</li>\n<li>Jobs: return interstitial protein, bring antigens to immune cells, clear inflammation</li>\n<li>Spleen = largest lymphoid mass; left, deep to ribs 9–11, below diaphragm</li>\n<li>Thymus: T-cell processing, involutes; nodes: many small filters; liver + gut make ½–⅔ of lymph</li>\n<li>Lymphoid tissue grows until age 6–9; adult level by 15–16</li>\n<li>No lymph channels in CNS, epidermis, endomysium; lungs have them</li>\n<li>Blind leaky capillary → lymphangions with smooth muscle + one-way valves</li>\n<li>Segments contract in sequence → unidirectional flow</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The lymphatic system and its ducts","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"The two ducts and who drains where","p":"<h4>The route of lymph</h4>\n<p>The path of lymph follows a fixed sequence. Lymph always starts in the tissues and ends in the great veins of the neck:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fluid enters a leaky terminal lymph capillary, made of a single layer of cells.</li>\n<li>It moves into a lymph vessel built of lymphangions.</li>\n<li>Vessels join into larger lymph trunks.</li>\n<li>Lymph passes through lymph nodes, which sit all along the way.</li>\n<li>The trunks empty into one of two ducts: the right lymphatic duct or the left (thoracic) duct.</li>\n<li>Each duct empties into the great veins at the base of the neck.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Two ducts, two territories</h4>\n<p>The two ducts do not split the body into left and right halves. The <b>right lymphatic duct</b> is the small (minor) duct. It drains the right arm, the right side of the head, face and neck (the right hemicranium, meaning the right half of the head), the right chest, the <b>heart</b>, and <b>every lung lobe except the left upper lobe</b>. The <b>thoracic duct</b>, also called the left or major duct, drains everything else. That includes <b>both legs</b>, the pelvis, every abdominal organ (gut, spleen, kidneys), the left arm, the left head and neck, the left chest and the left upper lobe. The main idea: everything below the diaphragm, on both sides, reaches the thoracic duct. Heart lymph goes mainly to the right duct. Poor heart lymph drainage has been linked to atherosclerosis, high blood pressure and weaker collateral circulation after a heart attack.</p>\n<p>The ducts also end differently. The thoracic duct empties consistently at the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The right duct varies: it ends in the right brachiocephalic vein or at the right jugular–subclavian junction. The table sets the two side by side.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Right lymphatic duct (minor)</th><th>Thoracic duct (left, major)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Drains</td><td>Right arm, right head and neck, right chest, heart, lungs except left upper lobe</td><td>Everything else, including both legs and all abdominal organs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Empties into</td><td>Right brachiocephalic vein, or right jugular–subclavian junction (variable)</td><td>Left jugular–subclavian junction (consistent)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Crosses the thoracic inlet</td><td>Once</td><td>Twice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume</td><td>Small</td><td>Most of the body's lymph</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-8-1.png\" alt=\"Right duct territory (dark) vs thoracic duct territory (green) at the neck veins\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Right duct territory (dark) vs thoracic duct territory (green) at the neck veins <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The thoracic duct, from bottom to top</h4>\n<p>Trunks from the abdomen, pelvis and legs empty into the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, a sac-like widening at the start of the thoracic duct. It lies <b>in front of and to the right of the L1–L2 vertebral bodies</b> (first and second lumbar vertebrae), behind the crus of the diaphragm. From there the thoracic duct, the largest lymph vessel at about 18 inches, enters the chest through the <b>aortic hiatus at T12</b> (twelfth thoracic vertebra), beside the aorta. It climbs the chest and crosses from right to left at about T4–T5. At the top it rises through Sibson's fascia, the fascial roof of the thoracic inlet, to the level of C7 (seventh cervical vertebra). Then it turns back down through that fascia into the left veins. It therefore crosses the inlet twice, while the right duct crosses once. For comparison, the aorta divides into the common iliac arteries at L4–L5, well below the cisterna.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-5.jpg\" alt=\"Cisterna chyli, thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct reaching the neck veins\"></div><figcaption>Cisterna chyli, thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct reaching the neck veins <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What a blockage does</h4>\n<p>Lymph flows one way, toward the neck. A block therefore backs fluid up in everything <b>upstream</b> of it, meaning every tissue whose lymph must pass the blocked point. Tissues that drain by the other duct keep flowing normally. In the same way, a restriction mainly on one side of the thoracic inlet backs up mainly the duct that ends on that side.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Route: leaky capillary → lymphangion vessel → trunk → nodes → duct → great veins</li>\n<li>Right (minor) duct: right arm, head, neck, chest; heart; lungs except left upper lobe</li>\n<li>Thoracic (left, major) duct: everything else, incl. both legs, all abdomen, left upper lobe</li>\n<li>Thoracic duct ends at left jugular–subclavian junction (consistent); right duct ending varies</li>\n<li>Cisterna chyli: anterior and right of L1–L2, behind the crus; collects abdomen, pelvis, legs</li>\n<li>Thoracic duct ≈18 in: T12 aortic hiatus → crosses ≈T4–T5 → up to C7 → down to left veins</li>\n<li>Thoracic duct crosses inlet twice (right duct once); carries most lymph</li>\n<li>Block → backs up its upstream territory; other duct's territory drains normally</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The lymphatic system and its ducts","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"How fluid enters lymph capillaries","p":"<p>Blood capillaries constantly leak fluid into the interstitium, the space between cells. Most of it is drawn back into the blood capillaries. The lymph capillaries collect what is left, at least 3 liters a day. Four pressures, the <b>Starling forces</b>, set the balance at the capillary wall.</p>\n<h4>The four Starling forces</h4>\n<p>Two forces move fluid out of the blood capillary. The <b>capillary (hydrostatic) pressure</b> is the blood pressure inside the capillary, and it pushes fluid out. The <b>interstitial colloid osmotic pressure</b> is the pull of proteins sitting outside the vessel, and it draws fluid out. Two forces hold fluid in or pull it back. The <b>plasma colloid osmotic pressure</b> is the pull of plasma proteins such as albumin, and it draws fluid back into the blood. The <b>interstitial fluid pressure</b> is the pressure of the tissue fluid itself, pressing against the vessel from outside. (Colloid osmotic pressure is also called oncotic pressure.)</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-21-1.png\" alt=\"The four Starling forces across a blood capillary wall\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>The four Starling forces across a blood capillary wall <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What lets more fluid into lymph capillaries</h4>\n<p>Six factors let extracellular fluid enter the lymph capillaries. Four of them shift the Starling balance so that more fluid or protein stays outside the blood vessels. <b>Increased</b> arterial capillary pressure pushes more fluid out. <b>Decreased</b> plasma colloid osmotic pressure leaves less pull to draw it back. <b>Increased</b> protein in the interstitium holds more fluid outside. <b>Increased</b> capillary permeability lets more fluid and protein leak out. The other two factors are motion: movement of connective tissue and muscle, and the fluid swings caused by arterial pulsations and breathing. The extra fluid left outside becomes lymph. The reverse of each change works the other way. More plasma protein, lower capillary pressure or tighter capillaries keep fluid in the blood, so less lymph forms.</p>\n<h4>Interstitial pressure helps only up to 0 mmHg</h4>\n<p>Normal interstitial fluid pressure is slightly negative, about <b>−6.3 mmHg</b> (millimeters of mercury), with lymph flow of about 120 cc (mL) per hour. As this pressure rises toward zero, more fluid is pushed into the lymph capillaries. Reaching 0 mmHg raises lymph flow about twentyfold. Once the pressure rises <b>above 0 mmHg</b>, though, the tissue squeezes the thin-walled lymph capillaries shut. They <b>collapse</b>, and lymph flow falls.</p>\n<p>Four conditions push interstitial pressure above 0 mmHg. Each is a disease-sized version of one of the first four entry factors. <b>Systemic hypertension</b> means too much pressure in the vessels. In <b>cirrhosis</b>, the scarred liver makes less plasma protein. <b>Hypoalbuminemia</b> (low blood albumin) occurs, for example, in starvation. <b>Toxins</b> such as rattlesnake venom make capillaries leak. Muscle movement, arterial pulsations and breathing belong only to the normal-entry list, not to this disease list.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O082-13.jpg\" alt=\"Blind lymph capillary held open by anchoring filaments among blood vessels\"></div><figcaption>Blind lymph capillary held open by anchoring filaments among blood vessels <small>· 2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System &amp; Diaphragms, page 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Out: capillary pressure, interstitial protein pull; back in: plasma protein pull</li>\n<li>Entry ↑ with ↑ capillary pressure, ↓ plasma colloid osmotic pressure</li>\n<li>Entry ↑ with ↑ interstitial protein, ↑ capillary permeability</li>\n<li>Also: tissue/muscle movement, arterial pulsations, breathing</li>\n<li>Normal interstitial pressure ≈ −6.3 mmHg; lymph flow ≈ 120 cc/hr</li>\n<li>Rising toward 0 → more lymph (≈20× at 0); above 0 → capillaries collapse, flow falls</li>\n<li>Above 0 causes: hypertension, cirrhosis, hypoalbuminemia (starvation), toxins (snake venom)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"What moves lymph","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Intrinsic and extrinsic forces","p":"<p>Once fluid is inside the lymph vessels, two kinds of force move it toward the neck. <b>Intrinsic</b> forces come from the vessel and its own fluid. <b>Extrinsic</b> forces act on the vessel from outside.</p>\n<h4>Intrinsic versus extrinsic</h4>\n<p>There are only <b>two intrinsic forces</b>. The first is contraction of the <b>smooth muscle</b> in the lymph vessel wall, the wave-like squeeze of the lymphangions (lymphatic peristalsis). The second is <b>interstitial fluid pressure</b>, the pressure of the tissue fluid that feeds the lymph capillaries. Neither one needs anything outside the vessel. Everything else on the list is extrinsic. Osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT), exercise and skeletal muscle contraction all squeeze the vessels from outside. So does the pulsation of nearby arteries. Breathing works from outside too, because inhalation makes the pressure inside the chest (intrathoracic pressure) more negative and draws lymph upward. A simple test helps: if the patient or physician does something to the body, such as moving, flexing a muscle, breathing deeply or receiving a pump technique, the force reaches the vessel from outside.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O082-20.jpg\" alt=\"Extrinsic versus intrinsic forces on lymph flow\"></div><figcaption>Extrinsic versus intrinsic forces on lymph flow <small>· 2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System &amp; Diaphragms, page 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The sympathetic nervous system and lymph</h4>\n<p>Lymph vessels are under the control of the <b>sympathetic nervous system (SNS)</b>, the fight-or-flight system, just as blood vessels are. Sympathetic fibers make the smooth muscle of the lymph vessel wall constrict and contract. They act on the vessel's own muscle pump, not on the tissues around it. A short, <b>acute</b> burst of sympathetic firing, as in brief stress or exercise, speeds the rate of lymphangion contraction through <b>alpha receptors</b>, and lymph flow rises. The SNS changes the rate of this peristalsis; it does not start it. Beta receptors, by contrast, relax lymphatic smooth muscle.</p>\n<p><b>Chronic</b> high sympathetic tone has the opposite overall effect. The vessels stay constricted and flow falls (part 5.2). Parasympathetic (cholinergic) fibers also reach lymph vessels, but their effect is barely known. Lymph nodes are under sympathetic influence too, and they release more lymphocytes as sympathetic tone rises.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-51.jpg\" alt=\"Acute sympathetic firing raises lymph flow via alpha receptors\"></div><figcaption>Acute sympathetic firing raises lymph flow via alpha receptors <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 51</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Exercise versus pump techniques</h4>\n<p>A 2005 study (Knott and colleagues) measured thoracic duct flow in five dogs. A lymphatic pump technique raised flow from 1.57 to a peak of 4.8 mL/min. Physical activity raised it from 1.47 to 5.81 mL/min, a bigger rise. The rule: <b>physical activity</b> gives the greatest increase in lymph return. For patients with lymphedema, gentle, rhythmic activity combined with deep breathing is best. Pump techniques (pedal, thoracic and splenic) and <b>effleurage</b> (light stroking of the skin in the direction of lymph flow) all help, but less. The splenic pump works mainly through the immune system.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Intrinsic (only 2): lymph vessel smooth muscle contraction; interstitial fluid pressure</li>\n<li>Extrinsic: OMT, exercise, skeletal muscle, artery pulsation, breathing (negative intrathoracic pressure)</li>\n<li>SNS acts on the vessel's own smooth muscle pump</li>\n<li>Acute SNS burst → faster lymphangion contraction (alpha receptors) → ↑ flow</li>\n<li>SNS modifies the rate, does not start peristalsis; beta → relaxation</li>\n<li>Chronic high SNS tone → vessels constricted → ↓ flow</li>\n<li>Nodes release more lymphocytes as SNS tone ↑</li>\n<li>Physical activity &gt; any pump (dogs: 1.47→5.81 vs 1.57→4.8 mL/min)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"What moves lymph","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"The five transverse diaphragms","p":"<p>A <b>diaphragm</b> is a thin partition of muscle and connective tissue that separates two neighboring regions. The body's <b>transverse</b> (horizontal) diaphragms matter in osteopathy for two reasons. First, as they move with each breath, they create the <b>pressure differences and tissue motion</b> that push lymph along. Second, they brace the <b>vertically running</b> muscles and fascia. They work by pressure, not by peristalsis, which is the lymph vessel's own smooth muscle pump (part 2.2). Gordon Zink, DO (doctor of osteopathic medicine), built his respiratory-circulatory model around these diaphragms. Fascia is continuous through the whole body, with “no beginning and no end” (Hollingshead), so the diaphragms and the vertical fascia work as one system.</p>\n<h4>The five, from top to bottom</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The <b>tentorium cerebelli</b>, with the diaphragma sellae, is the uppermost. It is a sheet of dura mater inside the skull, treated with cranial osteopathy.</li>\n<li>The <b>thoracic inlet</b> (cervicothoracic diaphragm) is the ring of rib 1, T1, the manubrium and the clavicles, roofed by Sibson's fascia. Both lymph ducts empty here (part 3.4).</li>\n<li>The <b>abdominal</b> (thoraco-abdominal or respiratory) diaphragm is the <b>largest</b> and the main pump (part 3.2).</li>\n<li>The <b>pelvic</b> diaphragm is the muscular floor of the pelvis, and it works in step with the abdominal diaphragm.</li>\n<li>The <b>popliteal</b> diaphragm is the fascia of the fossa behind the knee, and leg lymph passes through it (part 3.5).</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-8-1.png\" alt=\"The five transverse diaphragms and what they do\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>The five transverse diaphragms and what they do <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Structures that are not on the list</h4>\n<p>Several structures sound like diaphragms but are not among the five. There is no axillary diaphragm. The falx cerebri is a vertical fold of dura between the two cerebral hemispheres, and the mediastinum is a vertical space between the lungs, so neither is transverse. The urogenital diaphragm is an old name for the perineal membrane, which lies below the pelvic floor; the pelvic diaphragm is the muscular floor itself. The broad ligament of the uterus and the greater omentum are folds of peritoneum. The hard and soft palate and the tongue are also not on the list.</p>\n<h4>Tentorium cerebelli and diaphragma sellae</h4>\n<p>The tentorium cerebelli is a tent-like sheet of dura that roofs the cerebellum. The sella turcica is a saddle-shaped hollow in the sphenoid bone, between the clinoid processes, that holds the pituitary gland. Its dural roof, the <b>diaphragma sellae</b>, is a continuation of the tentorium.</p>\n<h4>Pelvic diaphragm</h4>\n<p>The pelvic diaphragm is a solid but elastic floor that supports the pelvic outlet. It is made of two paired muscles, the <b>levator ani</b> and the <b>coccygeus</b>, with the fascia above and below them. The levator ani is itself made of the <b>pubococcygeus</b> and <b>iliococcygeus</b> (with the puborectalis). The coccygeus is also called the <b>ischiococcygeus</b>, so “levator ani and ischiococcygeus” names the same two muscles.</p>\n<p>The pelvic organs rest on this floor. With every breath it absorbs the pressure swing sent down by the abdominal diaphragm. Moving in step with that diaphragm, it pumps venous blood and lymph out of the pelvis, rectum and perineum. Most pelvic organs drain to lymph nodes just above the pelvic floor, so its free motion helps push their lymph upward.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O082-45.jpg\" alt=\"Pelvic diaphragm from below: levator ani and coccygeus\"></div><figcaption>Pelvic diaphragm from below: levator ani and coccygeus <small>· 2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System &amp; Diaphragms, page 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Diaphragm = thin muscle/connective tissue partition between regions</li>\n<li>Transverse diaphragms: pressure gradients + tissue motion move lymph; steady vertical fascia</li>\n<li>Five: tentorium cerebelli, thoracic inlet, abdominal (largest), pelvic, popliteal</li>\n<li>Not diaphragms: axillary, falx, mediastinum, urogenital, broad ligament, omentum, palate, tongue</li>\n<li>Diaphragma sellae = dural roof of sella turcica, continuous with tentorium</li>\n<li>Pelvic = levator ani (pubo- + iliococcygeus) + coccygeus (= ischiococcygeus)</li>\n<li>Pelvic floor moves with abdominal diaphragm → pumps pelvic, rectal, perineal lymph</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The five diaphragms","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Abdominal diaphragm: attachments and openings","p":"<p>The abdominal diaphragm, also called the thoraco-abdominal or respiratory diaphragm, is a <b>dome-shaped muscle</b> with two halves (hemidiaphragms). The organs below shape its dome. Its main job is breathing. Its second jobs are pumping blood and lymph, and helping with speech, urination, defecation and childbirth, which all use abdominal pressure.</p>\n<h4>Attachments</h4>\n<p>The diaphragm attaches all the way around the lower chest. In front it attaches to the <b>sternum</b> at the xiphoid process. At the sides it attaches to the inner surfaces of <b>ribs 6–12</b>. Behind, it attaches to T12 (twelfth thoracic vertebra) and, through two tendinous legs called <b>crura</b> (singular crus, Latin for leg), to the lumbar spine. The <b>right crus</b> is longer, broader and stronger and arises from the bodies and discs of <b>L1–L3</b> (first to third lumbar vertebrae). The <b>left crus</b> arises from <b>L1–L2</b>. All the muscle fibers rise and meet in the <b>central tendon</b> at the top of the dome, which has <b>no bony attachment</b>. The crura also help form the barrier against acid reflux. A hiatal hernia is part of the stomach pushing up into the chest through the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>The <b>phrenic nerve</b> (roots C3–C5, third to fifth cervical) supplies all of the diaphragm's muscle and the sensation of its central part. The lower intercostal nerves (T5–T11) and the subcostal nerve (T12) carry sensation from its outer rim.</p>\n<h4>Arcuate ligaments</h4>\n<p>Behind, the diaphragm's edge forms three arches, the arcuate ligaments, over structures that pass beneath it. The <b>median</b> arcuate ligament joins the two crura in the midline at T12 and arches over the <b>aorta</b>. On each side, the <b>medial</b> arcuate ligament arches over the <b>psoas</b>. Farther out, the <b>lateral</b> arcuate ligament arches over the <b>quadratus lumborum (QL)</b>, the flat muscle between rib 12 and the iliac crest. A memory aid: the QL lies lateral to the psoas, and so does its arch. The fascia of the psoas and the QL, and the arcuate ligaments, all attach to the <b>tip of rib 12</b>. A tight psoas, a tight QL or a rib 12 problem can therefore pull on the diaphragm. The deltoid ligament, by contrast, is at the ankle.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-30.jpg\" alt=\"Underside of the diaphragm: crura, central tendon and arcuate ligaments\"></div><figcaption>Underside of the diaphragm: crura, central tendon and arcuate ligaments <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three openings</h4>\n<p>Three large structures pass through the diaphragm, each at its own vertebral level. The memory aid “<b>I 8 10 Eggs At 12</b>” gives the order: inferior vena cava (IVC) at T8, esophagus at T10, aorta at T12. Each one responds differently when the diaphragm contracts on breathing in, as the table shows.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Level</th><th>Opening</th><th>On inspiration</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>T8</b></td><td>Inferior vena cava (IVC), through the central tendon</td><td>The IVC widens, so more venous blood flows from abdomen to chest</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>T10</b></td><td><b>Esophagus</b> (esophageal hiatus)</td><td>The muscle pinches the hiatus and helps prevent reflux</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>T12</b></td><td><b>Aorta</b> (aortic hiatus), with the <b>thoracic duct</b></td><td>Both pass behind the diaphragm, which protects them</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The aortic hiatus is a gap behind the diaphragm, under the median arcuate ligament, rather than a hole through the muscle. The aorta and the thoracic duct pass through it together and are protected there. A hypertonic (overly tight) hemidiaphragm can still slow duct flow. No opening lies at T6. L1–L2 is the level of the crura and the cisterna chyli, not of an opening.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-12-1.png\" alt=\"Diaphragm openings at T8, T10, T12, with psoas and QL arches\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Diaphragm openings at T8, T10, T12, with psoas and QL arches <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Dome with 2 hemidiaphragms; main job breathing; also pumps blood + lymph</li>\n<li>Attaches: xiphoid, inner ribs 6–12, T12, crura</li>\n<li>Right crus L1–L3 (longer, stronger); left crus L1–L2; central tendon has no bony attachment</li>\n<li>Phrenic C3–C5 = all motor; rim sensation T5–T11 intercostals, T12 subcostal</li>\n<li>Arcuate: median → aorta (T12); medial → psoas; lateral → QL</li>\n<li>Psoas + QL fascia and arcuate ligaments → tip of rib 12</li>\n<li>“I 8 10 Eggs At 12”: IVC T8, esophagus T10, aorta + thoracic duct T12</li>\n<li>Inspiration: IVC widens; hiatus pinched (anti-reflux); aorta + duct protected behind</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The five diaphragms","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Breathing as the pump; diaphragm dysfunction","p":"<h4>Normal mechanics: the diaphragm as a piston</h4>\n<p>The respiratory diaphragm works as a <b>piston</b>. Each breath runs through the same steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>On <b>inhalation</b> the diaphragm contracts and <b>descends</b>. The mediastinum above and the abdominal organs below limit how far it goes.</li>\n<li>As it descends, the pressure inside the chest (<b>intrathoracic pressure</b>) becomes <b>more negative</b>, while the pressure inside the abdomen rises.</li>\n<li>Fluids, like air, move toward lower pressure. The gradient therefore draws lymph and venous blood up out of the abdomen toward the chest and heart.</li>\n<li>On exhalation the diaphragm relaxes and rises, and chest pressure climbs back up, so exhalation does not draw lymph centrally.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This respiratory pump is an extrinsic force, separate from the smooth muscle pump inside the vessel wall. Once the central tendon is fixed against the organs below, further contraction lifts the lower ribs, and the upper ribs and sternum follow. The chest then grows in three directions: vertically as the tendon descends, side to side as the lower six ribs rise, and front to back as the sternum rises. Angus Cathie, DO (doctor of osteopathic medicine), noted that breathing moves more joints, more often, than any other musculoskeletal function. Joel Goldthwait, MD, added that each excursion lengthens and shortens the inferior vena cava and forces blood up to the heart. When the excursion is small, he wrote, expect congestion of the abdominal and pelvic organs and back pressure in the legs, as in varicose veins.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O082-27.jpg\" alt=\"The respiratory diaphragm and its crura act as a piston\"></div><figcaption>The respiratory diaphragm and its crura act as a piston <small>· 2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System &amp; Diaphragms, page 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Hypertonic diaphragm</h4>\n<p>A diaphragm with increased resting tone (<b>hypertonicity</b>) cannot relax fully into its dome. A chain of effects follows. The dome <b>flattens</b>. The side-to-side (transverse) diameter of the chest shrinks. Breathing becomes less efficient, the pressure gradient between chest and abdomen falls, and less lymph and venous blood return to the heart. To keep breathing, the patient recruits <b>accessory breathing muscles</b>, especially the <b>scalenes</b> in the neck, which lift the upper ribs. Paraspinal muscle spasm that deepens the lumbar lordosis (the inward curve of the low back) can also flatten the diaphragm. The posture linked to a flattened diaphragm is therefore an increased lordosis, not a flat back. A tight piriformis belongs to psoas syndrome, not to diaphragm dysfunction.</p>\n<h4>Lateral spinal curves</h4>\n<p>A side-to-side spinal curve, an S-curve or a C-curve, pulls on the diaphragm <b>unevenly</b>. A common example is a Fryette <b>Type 1</b> (neutral) group curve. In Type 1 mechanics a group of vertebrae in neutral sidebends to one side and rotates to the other, so the vertebral bodies turn toward the outer (convex) side of the curve. The uneven pull on the crura and lower ribs gives a <b>weaker effective contraction</b>. The pressure pump weakens, and less lymph moves through the <b>thoracic duct</b>, the duct that passes through the abdominal diaphragm. Tissue fluid then backs up, and the tissues feel boggy. The right lymphatic duct never passes the abdominal diaphragm.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O082-37.jpg\" alt=\"S-curve and C-curve put an uneven pull on the diaphragm\"></div><figcaption>S-curve and C-curve put an uneven pull on the diaphragm <small>· 2026 week 11 Lecture 11 Lymphatic System &amp; Diaphragms, page 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Chronic diseases that stiffen the chest</h4>\n<p>Some chronic diseases restrict the rib cage and change the mechanics of the chest and abdomen. Examples are chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), asthma, obesity and kyphosis (a forward-rounded upper back), for example from ankylosing spondylitis or osteoporosis. Each weakens the chest's ability to generate negative pressure, so lymph and venous return suffer.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Inhalation: diaphragm descends → intrathoracic pressure more negative, abdominal ↑</li>\n<li>Gradient draws lymph + venous blood toward chest; exhalation does not</li>\n<li>Chest expands vertically (tendon), side to side (lower 6 ribs), front to back (sternum)</li>\n<li>Hypertonic diaphragm flattens → narrower chest, ↓ gradient → ↓ lymph, venous return</li>\n<li>Flattened diaphragm → scalenes recruited; ↑ lumbar lordosis flattens it</li>\n<li>Lateral curve (S/C, e.g. Type 1) → uneven pull → weaker contraction → ↓ thoracic duct flow</li>\n<li>COPD, asthma, obesity, kyphosis → weaker pressure gradients</li>\n<li>Small excursion → abdominal/pelvic congestion, leg back pressure (Goldthwait)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The five diaphragms","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Thoracic inlet, Sibson's fascia, cervical fascia","p":"<h4>Borders</h4>\n<p>The <b>thoracic inlet</b> is the bony ring at the top of the chest. Its borders are the <b>first pair of ribs, T1</b> (first thoracic vertebra), the <b>manubrium</b> (the top piece of the sternum) and the <b>clavicles</b>. Motion of T1–T4 and of rib 2 also affects it. The same region is also called the thoracic outlet.</p>\n<h4>Sibson's fascia</h4>\n<p><b>Sibson's fascia</b>, also called the <b>suprapleural membrane</b>, is the fascial roof of the inlet. It runs from the transverse process of C7 (seventh cervical vertebra) to rib 1 and caps the pleura over the apex of the lung. It is built from two fasciae. The <b>scalene fascia</b> covers the scalene muscles. The <b>prevertebral fascia</b> covers the deep muscles on the front of the spine, including the <b>longus colli</b>. The longus colli is a deep, paired neck muscle that runs along the front of the vertebral bodies from C1 to T3. The prevertebral fascia also encloses the sympathetic trunk, the brachial plexus and the phrenic nerve.</p>\n<p><b>Both lymph ducts pass through Sibson's fascia</b>. The thoracic duct climbs through it to the level of C7, then turns back down into the left veins, so it crosses twice. The right duct crosses once. Tight scalene or longus colli fascia therefore pulls directly on the roof that both ducts cross.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-12-1.png\" alt=\"Sibson&#x27;s fascia from the C7 transverse process to rib 1, over the lung apex\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Sibson&#x27;s fascia from the C7 transverse process to rib 1, over the lung apex <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why the inlet matters</h4>\n<p>Both ducts empty into the veins right here. A restriction of the inlet or of Sibson's fascia can therefore back up lymph from <b>the whole body</b>, not only from the head, the neck or one limb. A restriction mainly on one side backs up mainly the duct that ends on that side. Compression of Sibson's fascia has other effects too: poor neck posture, cervicothoracic joint dysfunction, and pressure on the nerves and vessels that pass the inlet. On inhalation, normal motion of rib 1 and the sternum helps both ducts empty. Inlet somatic dysfunction (SD), a restriction of motion with palpable tissue changes, is easy to diagnose and treat, and treating it has a large effect on lymph drainage.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-37.jpg\" alt=\"Longus colli and its fascia joining Sibson&#x27;s fascia\"></div><figcaption>Longus colli and its fascia joining Sibson&#x27;s fascia <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Fasciae and muscles found elsewhere</h4>\n<p>Several named fasciae belong to other regions. Scarpa's fascia is the membranous layer of the lower abdominal wall. Colles' fascia is the superficial fascia of the perineum, continuous with Scarpa's. Buck's fascia is the deep fascia of the penis. The tensor fasciae latae is a hip muscle on the iliotibial band. Likewise, the right crus of the diaphragm (L1–L3), the levator ani (pelvic floor), the pyramidalis (lower abdominal wall) and the pronator teres (forearm) lie far from the inlet.</p>\n<h4>Cervical fascia and breathing</h4>\n<p>The <b>anterior cervical fascia</b> attaches to the base of the skull, the mandible, the hyoid, the scapula, the clavicle and the sternum. It wraps the larynx, pharynx and thyroid and forms the carotid sheath. Through the prevertebral layer it surrounds the trachea and esophagus. Its pretracheal layer continues into the fascia of the pericardium (the sac around the heart) and attaches to the diaphragm. It lies close to the lymphatics of the head, neck, chest and arm. With breathing it moves front to back, not up and down. It moves <b>posterior</b> (toward the spine) on <b>inhalation</b> and <b>anterior</b> on <b>exhalation</b>. Osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) to the cervical fascia eases congestion in the chest and helps the head and neck drain. More broadly, veins, arteries, lymph vessels and nerves all travel inside fascia, so any fascial strain pattern slows all of them.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Inlet borders: rib 1, T1, manubrium, clavicles; T1–T4 and rib 2 affect it</li>\n<li>Sibson's fascia (suprapleural membrane): C7 transverse process → rib 1, caps lung apex</li>\n<li>Sibson's = prevertebral (over longus colli) + scalene fascia</li>\n<li>Both ducts cross it (thoracic twice, right once) → restriction can swell whole body</li>\n<li>Tight scalenes/longus colli → ↓ lymph return; also nerve and vessel compression</li>\n<li>Inhalation: rib 1 + sternum motion helps ducts empty; inlet SD easy, high-yield</li>\n<li>Elsewhere: Scarpa's, Colles', Buck's fascia, tensor fasciae latae</li>\n<li>Cervical fascia: posterior on inhalation, anterior on exhalation; OMT aids head/neck drainage</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The five diaphragms","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"The popliteal diaphragm","p":"<p>The <b>popliteal fossa</b> is the diamond-shaped hollow behind the knee. Its fascia forms the <b>popliteal diaphragm</b>, the lowest of the five transverse diaphragms. Lymph and venous blood from the foot and lower leg pass through it on their way up to the groin.</p>\n<h4>Borders and contents</h4>\n<p>Muscles frame the diamond. The <b>biceps femoris</b> forms the upper lateral border. The <b>semimembranosus and semitendinosus</b> form the upper medial border. The two heads of the <b>gastrocnemius</b> form the lower borders. Inside run the popliteal artery and vein, the tibial and common fibular (peroneal) nerves, and the popliteal lymph nodes and vessels. The popliteal artery does not block lymph. Its pulsation helps push lymph along, as an extrinsic force. The posterior cruciate ligament lies inside the knee joint, not in the fossa.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-14-1.png\" alt=\"Popliteal fossa: borders, vessels, nerves, with soleus below\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Popliteal fossa: borders, vessels, nerves, with soleus below <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The crowding point: the soleus arch</h4>\n<p>At the bottom of the fossa the vessels pass under the <b>soleus arch</b>, the tendinous arch at the top of the soleus muscle in the calf. The main point about this diaphragm: the lymph capillaries here can be <b>crowded by the soleus arch</b>. A tight soleus therefore slows drainage from the leg and foot. The muscles that form the walls of the fossa are not the crowding point. Fullness and bogginess behind the knee point to lymph congestion of the leg, for example in thrombophlebitis (an inflamed, clotted vein).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-10-1.png\" alt=\"Popliteal vessels passing under the soleus arch\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Popliteal vessels passing under the soleus arch <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Popliteal spread</h4>\n<p><b>Popliteal spread</b> is a <b>direct myofascial release</b>. Myofascial release treats restricted fascia, and direct means the tissue is taken into its restriction (its barrier). The physician's fingers sit in the popliteal fossa and spread its fascia apart. The aim is to free the fascial restrictions of the fossa so that lymph and venous blood can drain from the knee, ankle and foot. It is used to drain the leg in edema and in lower-extremity cellulitis (skin and soft tissue infection).</p>\n<p>Its target is the fascia of the fossa and nothing else. It does not treat the knee joint capsule, which is the target of articulatory technique. It does not loosen the collateral ligaments of the knee, and it does not aim at the sympathetic tone of the popliteal artery. It is also not effleurage, the light stroking used elsewhere in lymphatic treatment.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Popliteal fossa fascia = popliteal diaphragm, lowest of the five</li>\n<li>Borders: biceps femoris (upper lateral), semimembranosus/semitendinosus (upper medial), gastrocnemius heads (lower)</li>\n<li>Contents: popliteal artery, vein, tibial and fibular nerves, nodes; cruciates inside joint</li>\n<li>Popliteal artery pulsation helps lymph flow</li>\n<li>Soleus arch crowds the lymph capillaries → tight soleus slows leg drainage</li>\n<li>Popliteal fullness/bogginess → leg lymph congestion</li>\n<li>Popliteal spread = direct myofascial release of fossa fascia → leg edema, cellulitis</li>\n<li>Not aimed at: joint capsule, collateral ligaments, artery tone; not effleurage</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The five diaphragms","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Treatment order: central first","p":"<h4>The rule: open the drains, then push</h4>\n<p>Lymphatic techniques do two different things. Some <b>remove restrictions</b> that block flow, and others <b>augment</b> (boost) flow. Restrictions come off first; flow is augmented afterward. Treatment starts at the <b>central terminal drainage sites</b>, where lymph empties into the veins, and works outward to the periphery (proximal to distal). Pumping fluid toward a drain that is still closed only backs it up further, like pushing water into a blocked pipe. The rule holds even when the swelling is far out, as in a sprained ankle: the inlet still comes first, and a local technique at the injured joint comes last. Direct techniques (into the restriction) and indirect techniques (toward ease) both work.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-2-1.png\" alt=\"Treat central drainage sites first, then work outward\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Treat central drainage sites first, then work outward <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 2</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The treatment plan</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Diagnose and treat <b>thoracic inlet</b> somatic dysfunction (SD, a restriction of motion with palpable tissue changes), because the inlet is the terminal drain where both ducts enter the veins.</li>\n<li><b>Re-dome</b> (relax) the abdominal diaphragm so it can again create effective pressure gradients.</li>\n<li>Diagnose and treat <b>pelvic diaphragm</b> SD.</li>\n<li>Apply a <b>lymphatic pump</b> to push lymph through the opened path.</li>\n<li>Decongest specific target areas, such as the arm with the posterior axillary fold technique or the head and neck with the Galbreath technique.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The order follows the anatomy. Each step clears the segment just downstream of the next: the final drain first, then the large central pump, then the pelvic floor below it, and only then the pumps and the periphery. The same idea applies in more detail. The myofascial pathways at Zink's four transition zones open first (craniocervical, cervicothoracic, thoracolumbar and lumbopelvic junctions). T1–T6 (first to sixth thoracic vertebrae) are treated early, because the inlet includes T1. Pumps come once the diaphragms are free and sympathetic tone is normal, and most run at about 100 per minute for up to 2 minutes. Measuring diaphragm excursion is part of assessment, not a treatment step. The goals are vertebral symmetry, relaxed soft tissue, a compliant rib cage, normal pressure gradients and less edema.</p>\n<h4>The nine-technique sequence</h4>\n<p>In practice the plan runs as nine techniques. It starts with seated rib raising, which calms the sympathetic ganglia beside the thoracic spine. It then opens the inlet and re-domes the diaphragm before any pump.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Step</th><th>Technique</th><th>Role</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Seated rib raising</td><td>Frees the ribs; calms the sympathetic chain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Thoracic inlet myofascial release</td><td>Opens the terminal drain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Re-doming the diaphragm</td><td>Restores the main pump</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Pectoral lift</td><td>Augments flow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Thoracic pump</td><td>Augments flow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Cervical lymphatic effleurage</td><td>Moves head and neck lymph</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>Air sinus drainage</td><td>Moves sinus fluid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>Pedal pump</td><td>Whole-body pump from the feet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9</td><td>Self-lymphatic techniques</td><td>Home care</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Effleurage</b> is gentle stroking of the skin in the direction of lymph flow. It moves peripheral lymph, so it waits until the inlet is open and the diaphragm re-domed. Lymphatic congestion is what it treats.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-3-1.png\" alt=\"The nine-technique lymphatic sequence\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>The nine-technique lymphatic sequence <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 3</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Assessment</h4>\n<p>Before treating, the physician examines the three central diaphragms (thoracic inlet, abdominal, pelvic). The exam also covers the cervical, thoracic and lumbar spine, the rib cage, the front abdominal wall (transversus abdominis), the back abdominal wall (iliopsoas), the sacrum and pelvis, and the cranium. Lymphatic congestion feels <b>full and boggy</b>, and its location shows which region is congested. Above the clavicle points to the head and neck, for example sinusitis. The posterior axillary fold points to the arm. The epigastrium (upper middle abdomen) points to the chest and abdominal organs. The groin and the back of the knee point to the leg, and the Achilles tendon to the ankle and foot. Somatic dysfunctions that slow lymph include a tense pelvic diaphragm, restricted rib cage motion, a torsioned thoracic inlet, and paraspinal spasm that flattens the diaphragm.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Remove restrictions first, then augment flow</li>\n<li>Central terminal drainage sites first → periphery (proximal → distal), even for ankle swelling</li>\n<li>Plan: inlet → re-dome abdominal diaphragm → pelvic diaphragm → lymphatic pump → targets</li>\n<li>Nine techniques: rib raising → inlet → re-doming → pectoral lift → thoracic pump → effleurage → sinus → pedal → self</li>\n<li>Effleurage = light strokes with lymph flow; only after inlet + re-doming</li>\n<li>Transition zones and T1–T6 early; pumps ≈100/min, up to 2 min</li>\n<li>Diaphragm excursion measurement = assessment, not treatment</li>\n<li>Congestion = fullness/bogginess: supraclavicular, posterior axillary fold, epigastric, inguinal, popliteal, Achilles</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lymphatic treatment, step by step","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Opening the drains: inlet and re-doming","p":"<h4>Seated rib raising</h4>\n<p>The patient sits facing the physician with arms crossed, resting them on the physician's shoulders. The physician reaches around the patient and places the fingertips <b>just medial to the rib angles</b>, beside the thoracic spine. The physician then lifts the ribs <b>anterior and lateral</b> (forward and outward). This frees rib motion and calms the sympathetic chain ganglia that lie beside the thoracic spine. Lower sympathetic tone lets the lymph vessels pump normally.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-4-1.png\" alt=\"Seated rib raising: fingertips at the rib angles, lifting up and out\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Seated rib raising: fingertips at the rib angles, lifting up and out <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Thoracic inlet myofascial release</h4>\n<p>Myofascial release treats restricted fascia by holding it in a chosen position until it lets go. The inlet release is done <b>indirectly</b>, in five steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Look and palpate first: check T1 (first thoracic vertebra) for somatic dysfunction (SD), feel rib 1, and look for swelling above the clavicles.</li>\n<li>Place the thumbs on <b>T1 and its transverse process</b> and the fingers on both <b>clavicles</b>; a variant puts one hand behind on the cervicothoracic junction and one in front on the sternoclavicular joints.</li>\n<li>Test three pairs of motion: <b>rotation clockwise and counterclockwise</b>, sidebending left and right, and compression versus distraction (bringing the hands together versus pushing or pulling them apart).</li>\n<li>In each pair, find the direction the tissue moves into easily (its freedom) and hold it there.</li>\n<li>Have the patient breathe in and out, wait for the release (often about 20 seconds), then reassess.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Flexion and extension, and plain front-to-back or up-and-down glides, are not among the three pairs. The hand position also sets this technique apart. Hands along or under the costal margin belong to re-doming and to checking diaphragm excursion, and hands on the back of the rib cage belong to rib raising and rib motion testing.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-5-1.png\" alt=\"Inlet release: rotation, sidebending, and hands together or apart\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Inlet release: rotation, sidebending, and hands together or apart <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Direct, indirect and other technique names</h4>\n<p>A technique's name tells which way the tissue is taken. In an <b>indirect</b> technique the physician moves the tissue <b>toward its ease</b>, in the same directions as the diagnosis, away from the restrictive barrier. In a <b>direct</b> technique the tissue is taken <b>into the barrier</b>, opposite to the diagnosis. For example, if the inlet prefers rotation to the right, an indirect release holds it rotated right, and a direct release takes it left. The table sets the inlet release beside other technique names.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Technique</th><th>What the physician does</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Indirect</b></td><td>Takes the tissue toward its ease, the same directions as the diagnosis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direct</td><td>Takes the tissue into the barrier, opposite the diagnosis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>High-velocity, low-amplitude (HVLA)</td><td>A quick, short direct thrust through the barrier</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Balanced ligamentous tension (BLT)</td><td>Balances ligament tension around a joint</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Balanced membranous tension</td><td>Cranial technique for the dural membranes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Facilitated positional release (FPR)</td><td>Position of ease plus compression or torsion, held a few seconds</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Inlet release suits problems located at the inlet: rib 1 and T1 dysfunction, tight scalenes (whose fascia is part of Sibson's fascia), and head and neck congestion such as an upper respiratory infection, where it is usually the first treatment. Problems lower down, such as a mid-rib dysfunction, a spinal curve, the sacroiliac joint or the thoracolumbar junction, are treated at their own site. The bony parts of the drains are treated too. T1 and rib 1 get muscle energy (ME) or HVLA if needed. For the abdominal diaphragm, L1–L3 and rib 12 are checked. L1–L3 get myofascial release, ME or HVLA, and rib 12 gets myofascial release or ME using the patient's breathing.</p>\n<h4>Re-doming the diaphragm (thoraco-abdominal diaphragm release)</h4>\n<p>A hypertonic diaphragm stays <b>flattened</b>, sitting low as if in inhalation, so its excursion is small. Re-doming aims to restore its <b>upward movement in exhalation</b>, which brings back the dome, the full excursion and the pressure gradient. It can be done seated, with the physician behind the patient, or supine, and the procedure is the same. The physician places the fingers <b>under the costal margin</b> (the lower edge of the rib cage), pointing toward the head. On each exhalation the physician follows the diaphragm up. On each inhalation the physician resists its descent. This repeats until a release is felt.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-6-1.png\" alt=\"Re-doming: fingers under the costal margin, seated or supine\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Re-doming: fingers under the costal margin, seated or supine <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Seated rib raising: fingertips medial to rib angles, lift anterior + lateral; calms sympathetic chain</li>\n<li>Inlet release: thumbs on T1/transverse process, fingers on clavicles; assess first, reassess after</li>\n<li>Three motion pairs: rotation, sidebending, compression/distraction; not flexion/extension</li>\n<li>Hold each in ease = indirect; patient breathes; release ≈20 s</li>\n<li>Indirect = toward ease (same as diagnosis); direct = into barrier; HVLA = direct thrust</li>\n<li>Inlet release for rib 1/T1 SD, tight scalenes, upper respiratory infection</li>\n<li>Bony drains: T1/rib 1 with ME or HVLA; rib 12 with myofascial release or ME</li>\n<li>Re-doming: fingers under costal margin; follow up on exhale, resist on inhale; seated or supine</li>\n<li>Re-doming goal: restore upward diaphragm motion in exhalation → dome, excursion, gradient</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lymphatic treatment, step by step","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Pumps: pectoral lift, thoracic, pedal","p":"<p>Once the drains are open, pump techniques <b>augment</b> flow. Each one creates a rhythmic pressure change that pushes lymph toward the open ducts.</p>\n<h4>Pectoral lift (pectoral traction)</h4>\n<p>The patient lies supine (face up). The physician sits or stands at the head of the table and firmly grasps the lateral edge of the pectoralis muscles at the <b>anterior axillary folds</b>, the front walls of the armpits. The physician then leans back slowly, stretching the pectorals toward the head. The patient breathes deeply. On each inhalation the physician increases the stretch, and on each exhalation holds the traction steady, until the tissue stops giving. The traction holds the front of the rib cage in an inhaled position. To breathe deeply, the patient must therefore use the diaphragm more, and that extra diaphragm motion boosts lymph return. The mechanism is <b>respiratory assist</b>: the patient's own deep breathing does the work. It is not one of the muscle energy reflexes, such as reciprocal inhibition (contracting a muscle relaxes its opposite), the crossed extensor reflex (contracting the opposite limb relaxes the treated muscle) or the oculocephalogyric reflex (eye movement that helps neck and trunk muscles contract). Its indications include peripheral edema, for example in pregnancy. The hands hold only the pectoral muscles. They do not articulate the clavicles, press the sternum or reach the cisterna chyli deep in the abdomen. The <b>posterior</b> axillary fold technique is a separate technique, used to drain the arm. Cautions include aspiration and acute rib fracture.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-7-1.png\" alt=\"Pectoral lift: grasp the anterior axillary folds and lean back\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Pectoral lift: grasp the anterior axillary folds and lean back <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Thoracic pump (of Miller)</h4>\n<p>The patient lies <b>supine</b>. The physician stands at the <b>head</b> of the table with the palms on the <b>upper chest</b>, thumbs near the sternum. In the basic version the physician pushes the chest down and back in a steady rhythm, about 100 times a minute, firmly enough to make the abdomen move, for half a minute to 2 minutes. In the rebound version the physician pushes on each <b>exhalation</b> and <b>resists</b> the chest on each inhalation for several breaths. Then, early in a final inhalation, the physician <b>lets go suddenly</b>. The chest's <b>elastic recoil</b> springs it open, and intrathoracic pressure (the pressure inside the chest) drops abruptly. That sudden negative pressure draws lymph and venous blood centrally. The thoracic pump is done supine only; the prone-or-supine option belongs to the pedal pump.</p>\n<p>The thoracic pump was the core of the osteopathic <b>1918 influenza</b> protocol. It increased lymph drainage, eased the work of breathing and made the chest more compliant, so it could generate negative pressure. DOs (doctors of osteopathic medicine) reported influenza deaths of 0.25%, against 5–6% with standard medical care, and pneumonia deaths of 10% against 33%. Its indications are atelectasis (collapsed areas of lung), bronchitis, pneumonia and peripheral edema. Its contraindications are acute rib fracture, severe osteoporosis, aspiration, <b>lung cancer</b>, pulmonary embolism, acute congestive heart failure, peritonitis and recent abdominal surgery.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-8-1.png\" alt=\"Thoracic pump, with the rebound expansion version\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Thoracic pump, with the rebound expansion version <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Pedal pump (of Dalrymple)</h4>\n<p>The physician stands at the <b>foot</b> of the table with the palms on the soles. The physician <b>dorsiflexes both feet</b> (pushes the toes toward the shins) to the barrier, then leans in rhythmically, about 100 times a minute, so the abdomen moves. An alternative grips the tops of the feet and pulls them into plantar flexion. The rule for the rate is not too fast, not too slow. The motion travels up the fascia, so it moves lymph from <b>the whole body</b>, and it is handy in children. It may be done <b>prone or supine</b>. The patient stays passive throughout, so nothing is being tested or resisted. The straight leg raise (SLR), by contrast, lifts one straight leg to stretch the lumbar nerve roots. Acute ankle sprain and acute congestive heart failure call for caution, and deep vein thrombosis (DVT, a clot in a deep leg vein) rules it out; leg edema itself is the main indication.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O093-11-1.png\" alt=\"Pedal pump: rhythmic dorsiflexion of both feet\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Pedal pump: rhythmic dorsiflexion of both feet <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Pumps augment flow once drains are open</li>\n<li>Pectoral lift: supine, grasp anterior axillary folds, lean back; mechanism = respiratory assist</li>\n<li>Posterior axillary fold technique = separate arm-drainage technique</li>\n<li>Thoracic pump (Miller): supine, physician at head, palms on upper chest, ≈100/min</li>\n<li>Rebound: push on exhale, resist inhale, sudden release → elastic recoil → abrupt negative pressure</li>\n<li>1918 influenza: mortality 0.25% (DO) vs 5–6%; pneumonia 10% vs 33%</li>\n<li>Pedal pump (Dalrymple): foot of table, rhythmic dorsiflexion; whole-body lymph; prone or supine</li>\n<li>Thoracic pump: for atelectasis, bronchitis, pneumonia; avoid with rib fracture, osteoporosis, lung cancer</li>\n<li>Pedal pump: caution in acute ankle sprain, acute heart failure; DVT rules it out</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lymphatic treatment, step by step","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Head and neck drainage","p":"<p>Once the central drains are open, techniques at the face, jaw and neck move fluid out of the sinuses and the middle ear. The fluid travels toward the <b>jugulodigastric node</b>, which sits just <b>below and behind the angle of the mandible</b>, where the digastric muscle crosses the internal jugular vein. It is the main upper node of the deep cervical chain, and from it lymph runs down the neck to the two ducts. Other head nodes drain smaller areas: the mastoid nodes behind the ear, the submental nodes under the chin and the occipital nodes at the back of the skull base.</p>\n<h4>Effleurage and sinus drainage</h4>\n<p><b>Effleurage</b> is light stroking of the skin in the direction of lymph flow. In cervical effleurage the strokes run down the neck toward the jugulodigastric node and the deep cervical chain. In sinus drainage effleurage the physician sits at the head and makes three to four sweeps over each of six areas in turn. The frontal sweep crosses the forehead. The supraorbital sweep runs along the brow above the eyes, and the infraorbital sweep runs below the eyes beside the nose. The inframalar sweep runs below the cheekbone, and the mandibular sweep runs along the jaw. For the submandibular sweep, one hand steadies the head at the forehead while the other hand's fingers stroke under the jaw. Every stroke heads toward the neck.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O066-15.jpg\" alt=\"Cervical effleurage follows lymph flow toward head and neck nodes\"></div><figcaption>Cervical effleurage follows lymph flow toward head and neck nodes <small>· 2026 week 11 Lab Lymphatic &amp; Sinus Drainage, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Sinus inhibitory pressure</h4>\n<p><b>Sinus inhibitory pressure</b> uses steady pressure instead of stroking. The physician presses on the three facial openings where branches of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V) leave the skull. These are the <b>supraorbital</b> foramen above the eye (V1, ophthalmic branch), the <b>infraorbital</b> foramen below the eye (V2, maxillary branch) and the <b>mental</b> foramen on the chin (V3, mandibular branch). The pressure eases sinus congestion by reflex, and it is used for sinusitis. Stroking is the effleurage version; inhibitory pressure means steady pressure on the foramina.</p>\n<h4>Galbreath technique</h4>\n<p>The <b>Galbreath technique</b> (mandibular drainage) works on the jaw. With a hand along the mandible, the physician pulls the jaw slowly and rhythmically downward and toward the midline. Each pull opens the <b>Eustachian (auditory) tube</b>, the channel from the middle ear to the throat, so fluid trapped in the middle ear can drain. It helps any ear, nose and throat congestion, and it is especially useful in <b>otitis media</b> (middle ear infection), including recurrent cases. Its target is the Eustachian tube, so it is not a treatment for the eye, the tonsils or a nerve pain such as trigeminal neuralgia. Drainage moves fluid. It cannot shrink a structural growth such as a nasal polyp.</p>\n<h4>Other neck and head techniques</h4>\n<p><b>Anterior cervical mobilization</b> gently moves the hyoid bone, the thyroid and cricoid cartilages and the trachea from side to side, to drain the throat and neck. Some other head techniques sound similar but use different contacts. The <b>frontal lift</b> is a cranial technique that lifts the frontal bone. The <b>vomer pump</b> uses a contact inside the mouth on the hard palate. <b>Pterygoid fossa decongestion</b> works deep in the pterygoid region, not on the facial foramina. <b>Anterior cervical counterstrain</b> treats tender points on the front of the neck. Cranial osteopathy eases strain in the tentorium, the uppermost diaphragm, and helps venous blood leave the head.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Head/neck fluid → jugulodigastric node (below and behind mandible angle) → deep cervical chain → ducts</li>\n<li>Effleurage = light strokes in direction of lymph flow; cervical + sinus versions</li>\n<li>Sinus effleurage: 3–4 sweeps each—frontal, supraorbital, infraorbital, inframalar, mandibular, submandibular (under jaw)</li>\n<li>Sinus inhibitory pressure: steady pressure on supraorbital (V1), infraorbital (V2), mental (V3) foramina</li>\n<li>Galbreath: slow rhythmic down-and-medial mandible pull → opens Eustachian tube → otitis media</li>\n<li>Anterior cervical mobilization: side-to-side hyoid, thyroid, cricoid, trachea</li>\n<li>Look-alikes: frontal lift, vomer pump (palate), pterygoid decongestion, anterior cervical counterstrain</li>\n<li>Drainage moves fluid; cannot remove polyps</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lymphatic treatment, step by step","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Contraindications to lymphatic techniques","p":"<p>A <b>contraindication</b> is a reason not to use a treatment. An <b>absolute</b> contraindication means the technique is never used in that situation. A <b>relative</b> contraindication means it is used only with caution, after weighing risk against benefit. Lymphatic techniques have one list of each.</p>\n<h4>The two lists and why</h4>\n<p>Each absolute contraindication has a clear mechanism. In <b>deep vein thrombosis (DVT)</b> a clot sits in a deep vein, and pumping could dislodge it and send it to the lungs. In <b>infectious mononucleosis</b> the spleen is swollen and fragile, so a <b>splenic pump</b> could rupture it. A <b>local fracture</b> must not be compressed or moved. In <b>anuria</b> (no urine output) in a patient who is not on dialysis, the fluid that lymph returns to the blood has no way to leave the body. The relative list has three entries. <b>Unstaged cancer</b> is cancer whose spread has not yet been mapped. <b>Coagulopathies</b> are bleeding disorders, and the group includes patients taking <b>anticoagulants</b> (blood thinners). An <b>abscess</b> is a walled-off pocket of pus. The table sets the two lists side by side.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Absolute</th><th>Relative</th></tr>\n<tr><td>DVT</td><td>Unstaged cancer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Splenic pump in infectious mononucleosis</td><td>Coagulopathies, including anticoagulant use</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Local fracture</td><td>Abscess</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anuria in a patient not on dialysis</td><td></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-14-1.png\" alt=\"Absolute contraindications to lymphatic techniques\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Absolute contraindications to lymphatic techniques <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Splenic and liver pumps</h4>\n<p>The patient lies supine. The physician places one hand on the costal margin over the organ and the other hand under the ribs behind it. The left lower ribs cover the spleen, and the right lower ribs cover the liver. The physician compresses the ribs during exhalation and releases quickly early in inhalation so the ribs recoil, repeating 3–5 times. The effect is mainly immune. The pump brings antigens and toxins into contact with the spleen and with the liver's macrophages (Kupffer cells). Further cautions are acute hepatitis or acute cholecystitis for the liver pump, and an unexplained large spleen or splenic trauma for the splenic pump.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-22-1.png\" alt=\"Liver and spleen pump, with acute mononucleosis flagged for the spleen\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Liver and spleen pump, with acute mononucleosis flagged for the spleen <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Mononucleosis</h4>\n<p>Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) causes infectious mononucleosis: fever, fatigue, sore throat, swollen lymph nodes and often an enlarged liver and spleen. The danger is to the enlarged spleen. Techniques that do not compress the spleen, such as counterstrain, muscle energy or myofascial release elsewhere in the body, are not affected.</p>\n<h4>Local infection and indications</h4>\n<p>No technique is applied directly on an infected site, such as an abscess, cellulitis (skin and soft tissue infection) or osteomyelitis (bone infection). In a diabetic foot wound, a positive <b>probe-to-bone test</b>, where a probe placed in the wound touches bone, points to osteomyelitis. A pump that grips and moves the infected part itself is ruled out. Techniques far from the site, such as the thoracic pump, the liver pump or the Galbreath technique, stay available. For the pedal pump in particular, a suspected DVT rules it out, while chronic leg edema is the reason to use it. A past history of heart failure with stable swelling is not a contraindication; caution applies only in acute heart failure. Many infections are reasons to use lymphatic techniques, not to avoid them. Influenza, bronchitis, pneumonia and a productive cough are indications. Other indications are edema, venous or lymphatic congestion, sprains and strains, acute somatic dysfunction, pregnancy and inflammation.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Absolute: DVT, splenic pump in mono, local fracture, anuria without dialysis</li>\n<li>Why: clot → lungs; swollen spleen ruptures; fracture moved; fluid can't leave</li>\n<li>Relative: unstaged cancer, coagulopathy incl. anticoagulants, abscess</li>\n<li>Splenic/liver pump: compress on exhale, quick release on inhale; immune effect (Kupffer cells)</li>\n<li>Mono (EBV): danger = spleen compression only; distant techniques fine</li>\n<li>No technique on an infected site; positive probe-to-bone → osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Pedal pump: suspected DVT → no; chronic edema → yes; stable heart failure history not a contraindication</li>\n<li>Indications: flu, bronchitis, pneumonia, productive cough, edema, sprains, pregnancy, inflammation</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Contraindications and a clinical case","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"OMM-W11","h":"Post-mastectomy lymphedema: the whole plan","p":"<p>Removing a breast together with its armpit (axillary) lymph nodes, a <b>mastectomy</b>, can leave the arm on that side swollen. This is <b>lymphedema</b>: protein-rich fluid trapped in the tissues because the lymph cannot drain. The right arm drains to the right lymphatic duct, and the left arm to the thoracic duct. A typical case is a 54-year-old woman with mild, non-pitting swelling of both arms that began about a month after a bilateral mastectomy. Her clothes and wedding ring no longer fit.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-48.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphedema of the arm on the side of a mastectomy\"></div><figcaption>Lymphedema of the arm on the side of a mastectomy <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Acute versus chronic sympathetic tone</h4>\n<p>Lymph vessels answer mainly to the sympathetic nervous system (SNS), which sets the rate at which their smooth muscle pumps. The effect depends on how long the SNS stays switched on. A brief, <b>acute</b> burst, as in short stress or exercise, makes the lymphangions contract faster through alpha receptors. Flow rises for a while, and immune cells are mobilized. <b>Chronic</b> high tone is different. Prolonged exposure to stress hormones (noradrenaline and cortisol) keeps the vessels <b>constricted</b>, like a traffic jam, and lymph flow <b>falls</b>. Chronic tone also brings shallow chest breathing, so the diaphragm pumps less. It tightens the neck, shoulder and hip muscles, which press on nodes and channels, and it adds inflammatory debris to the tissue fluid. A classic example of this chronic state is post-mastectomy lymphedema. Treatment therefore aims to <b>lower</b> sympathetic tone, for example with rib raising over the sympathetic chain. The table compares the two states.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Acute burst (stress, exercise)</th><th>Chronic high tone</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymph vessel</td><td>Contracts faster (alpha receptors)</td><td>Stays <b>constricted</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymph flow</td><td>Rises briefly</td><td><b>Falls</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other effects</td><td>Mobilizes immune cells</td><td>Shallow breathing, tight muscles on nodes, more inflammatory debris</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O064-50.jpg\" alt=\"How chronic sympathetic tone stalls lymph: vessels, breathing, muscle tone, inflammation\"></div><figcaption>How chronic sympathetic tone stalls lymph: vessels, breathing, muscle tone, inflammation <small>· 2026 week 11 IA Lymphatics, slide 50</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Examining the arm</h4>\n<p>The arm is examined in three ways. First, the circumference of both arms is measured and compared. Second, axillary cording is checked: the shoulder is abducted and the elbow extended while the armpit is felt for a taut cord. Third, the <b>Stemmer sign</b> is tested: if a skin fold at the base of the second finger cannot be lifted, lymphedema is present. Fullness at the posterior axillary fold also points to lymph congestion of the arm. Before any osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT), the physician screens for contraindications and obtains the patient's informed consent.</p>\n<h4>The treatment plan</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Reduce <b>hypersympathetic</b> activity, for example with rib raising.</li>\n<li>Diagnose and treat the <b>thoracic inlet</b>, where both ducts empty.</li>\n<li><b>Re-dome</b> the abdominal diaphragm to restore the pressure gradient.</li>\n<li>Diagnose and treat the pelvic diaphragm.</li>\n<li>Apply <b>lymphatic pump</b> techniques to push lymph through the opened path.</li>\n<li>Drain the arm locally (posterior axillary fold technique, effleurage) and encourage daily gentle activity with deep breathing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every step either lowers sympathetic tone, opens a drain or pushes lymph through an open path.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-6-1.jpg\" alt=\"Right duct drains the right arm and head; thoracic duct drains the rest\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Right duct drains the right arm and head; thoracic duct drains the rest <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Mastectomy with node removal → arm lymphedema on that side</li>\n<li>Acute SNS burst → faster lymphangion contraction (alpha) → brief ↑ flow</li>\n<li>Chronic hypersympathetic tone → constricted vessels → ↓ lymph flow (e.g., post-mastectomy)</li>\n<li>Chronic tone also: shallow breathing, tight muscles on nodes, inflammatory debris</li>\n<li>Arm exam: compare circumference, axillary cording, Stemmer sign (fold at 2nd finger can't lift)</li>\n<li>Screen contraindications, get informed consent before OMT</li>\n<li>Plan: ↓ sympathetic tone → inlet → re-dome → pelvic diaphragm → pump → local arm drainage</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Contraindications and a clinical case","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Vertebrae, curves and range of motion","p":"<h4>The vertebrae</h4>\n<p>The lumbar spine carries the weight of the upper body down to the pelvis. Its design therefore has to balance <b>stability</b>, so it can bear load, with <b>mobility</b>, so it can bend and turn. Anatomically it has <b>5 vertebrae</b>, L1 to L5 (L = lumbar). Functionally it runs from T11 to L5 (T = thoracic), because the two lowest thoracic segments move the way lumbar segments do.</p>\n<p>The vertebral bodies get larger as you go down, because each one carries more weight than the one above it. <b>L5</b> is the largest in height and circumference, and it also has the most congenital anomalies. Two of these anomalies change the lumbosacral (L/S) junction, where L5 meets the sacrum. In <b>sacralization</b>, L5 is fused to the sacrum. In <b>lumbarization</b>, S1 (the first sacral segment) stays separate and acts like a sixth lumbar vertebra. Both alter how load passes through the bottom of the spine, and both are linked with earlier degeneration there, not with protection from it.</p>\n<p>Neighboring vertebrae are joined in two ways. The paired <b>zygapophyseal (facet) joints</b> at the back are synovial joints, with cartilage surfaces and a joint capsule. The <b>intervertebral disc joints</b> at the front are fibrocartilaginous.</p>\n<h4>How the lumbar curve forms</h4>\n<p>A newborn's spine is one long C-shaped curve, convex backward. This is the primary, kyphotic curve. When the infant learns to hold up the head, the neck extends and the cervical lordosis forms; a lordosis is a curve that is convex forward. The lumbar lordosis comes later, in <b>childhood</b>. As the child stands and walks, the lumbar spine extends. The iliopsoas, the hip flexor that runs from the lumbar spine to the femur, resists this extension and does not lengthen fully. It keeps holding the lumbar spine forward, and the result is the lumbar lordosis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-6.jpg\" alt=\"Sagittal spinal curves from day 1 to age 10\"></div><figcaption>Sagittal spinal curves from day 1 to age 10 <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Range of motion</h4>\n<p>Range of motion (ROM) is how far a region can move in each direction. In the lumbar spine, flexion is the largest motion and rotation the smallest; part 1.2 explains why. The normal values are shown below. Sidebending (SB) is 20–25° per side, the same normal used in the standing sidebending screen.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Motion</th><th>Normal range</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Flexion</td><td><b>40–50°</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extension</td><td><b>20–30°</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sidebending (lateral flexion)</td><td><b>20–25° per side</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rotation</td><td>The least, about 5–7° per side</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Where flexion happens</h4>\n<p>Total lumbar flexion is about 40–50°, but the joints do not share it evenly. Most of it happens at the very bottom. <b>L5–S1 gives 60–75%</b>, and L4–L5 adds another 20–25%. Together the two lowest joints supply 80–90% of lumbar flexion, while L1 to L4 together add only 5–10%.</p>\n<p>Bending fully forward also needs the pelvis. As the trunk goes down, the <b>pelvis rotates forward about the hip joints</b>. When a patient has lost forward bending, L5–S1 is the first lumbar level to examine, since it carries most of the motion and L5 has the most anomalies. The cause may also lie at the hips or in tight hamstrings.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-62-1.png\" alt=\"Share of lumbar flexion at each segment, with the pelvis tilting below\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Share of lumbar flexion at each segment, with the pelvis tilting below <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 62</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Anatomic lumbar spine L1–L5; functional T11–L5</li>\n<li>Bodies enlarge downward; L5 largest, most congenital anomalies</li>\n<li>Sacralization, lumbarization → altered L/S junction → earlier degeneration</li>\n<li>Facet joints synovial; disc joints fibrocartilaginous</li>\n<li>Newborn: one kyphotic curve; lumbar lordosis forms in childhood (iliopsoas doesn't lengthen)</li>\n<li>ROM: flexion 40–50°, extension 20–30°; rotation least</li>\n<li>SB 20–25° per side, rotation 5–7° per side</li>\n<li>Flexion: L5–S1 60–75%, L4–L5 20–25%, L1–L4 5–10%</li>\n<li>Lost flexion → look first at L5–S1; full flexion also needs pelvic rotation at the hips</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Shape and motion of the lumbar spine","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Facets decide the main motion","p":"<h4>How the facets fit together</h4>\n<p>Each lumbar vertebra has two superior and two inferior articular processes, and each process carries a smooth joint surface called a facet. The inferior processes of the vertebra above drop down and sit inside the superior processes of the vertebra below. Each point of contact is a facet (zygapophyseal) joint.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-34-1.png\" alt=\"Two lumbar vertebrae from behind: articular processes and facet joints\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Two lumbar vertebrae from behind: articular processes and facet joints <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 34</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The two surfaces of each joint have opposite shapes, so one cups the other. The <b>superior facets</b>, on the vertebra below, are <b>concave</b> and face backward, up and medially. The <b>inferior facets</b>, on the vertebra above, are <b>convex</b> and face forward, down and laterally. The concave superior facet therefore wraps around the convex inferior facet from the outside.</p>\n<p>The mnemonic is <b>BUM</b>: lumbar superior facets face <b>B</b>ack, <b>U</b>p and <b>M</b>edial. Cervical superior facets are also BUM. Thoracic superior facets are BUL instead: back, up and lateral. The lumbar facets are also turned about 45° from the sagittal plane. Facet orientation is fixed anatomy, so a group curve or a posture does not change it.</p>\n<p><b>Facet tropism</b> means the left and right facets at one level have different angles. It is the most common lumbar anomaly. The two sides then move unequally, and the joints wear out early. It is developmental; posture does not create it.</p>\n<h4>Why flexion–extension wins</h4>\n<p>Lumbar facet surfaces lie close to the <b>sagittal plane</b>, the front-to-back plane that divides left from right, and they face each other across the joint. Surfaces set this way slide easily forward and back past each other. Flexion and extension are therefore the <b>main lumbar motion</b>. When the spine tries to twist, the facing surfaces jam into each other, so <b>rotation is the most limited</b> lumbar motion. Sidebending (SB) lies in between.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-9.jpg\" alt=\"Lumbar facets lie near the sagittal plane\"></div><figcaption>Lumbar facets lie near the sagittal plane <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Planes and axes</h4>\n<p>Every motion happens in a plane and turns around an axis at right angles to that plane. Flexion and extension occur in the sagittal plane around a transverse (left-to-right) axis. Sidebending occurs in the coronal plane around an anterior–posterior (A–P, front-to-back) axis. Rotation occurs in the horizontal plane around a vertical axis.</p>\n<p>Some words sound like spinal motions but are not main lumbar motions. Abduction is a limb motion at the hip or shoulder. Translation is a small sideways glide used in passive motion testing. Compression is a load pressing down on the spine, not a motion.</p>\n<h4>Standing motion tests</h4>\n<p>In lumbar motion test 1 (lordosis), you stand behind the patient, get to eye level and palpate the lumbar curve while the patient bends forward. Bending forward flexes the lumbar spine, so the lordosis should flatten out. In motion test 2 (rotation), your palms rest on the paraspinal tissue on both sides while the patient bends forward. A prominence on one side is abnormal; it means the segments there are rotated. Repeat the test seated: if the prominence shrinks, the cause lies in the lower extremities. The same forward bend is <b>Adam's test</b>, which makes a scoliosis (a sideways curve, named by its convexity, such as right thoracic and left lumbar) stand out.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O071-6.jpg\" alt=\"Lumbar motion test 1: the lordosis flattens on bending forward\"></div><figcaption>Lumbar motion test 1: the lordosis flattens on bending forward <small>· 2026 week 12 Lab Lumbar Spine Diagnosis &amp; HVLA, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Superior facets: concave; BUM = back, up, medial (thoracic BUL)</li>\n<li>Inferior facets: convex; face forward, down, lateral</li>\n<li>Facets about 45° from sagittal; orientation fixed, not changed by curves</li>\n<li>Facet tropism: L ≠ R facet angles; most common anomaly → early degeneration</li>\n<li>Near-sagittal facets → flexion–extension greatest, rotation least, SB between</li>\n<li>Flexion–extension: sagittal plane, transverse axis; SB: coronal, A–P axis; rotation: horizontal, vertical axis</li>\n<li>Abduction, translation, compression: not main lumbar motions</li>\n<li>Motion tests: lordosis flattens on bending forward; one-sided prominence = rotation (Adam's test)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Shape and motion of the lumbar spine","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Ferguson's angle and the sacral base","p":"<h4>The sacral base sets the curve</h4>\n<p>The lumbar lordosis stands on the sacrum. The top surface of the sacrum, the <b>sacral base</b>, is not level; it tilts forward. The steeper it tilts, the more the lumbar spine above must curve backward to bring the trunk upright again. The lordosis therefore depends on the sacral base. The capsule of the hip joint also influences the angle.</p>\n<h4>Ferguson's (lumbosacral) angle</h4>\n<p><b>Ferguson's angle</b>, also called the lumbosacral or sacral base angle, measures that tilt on a standing lateral (side) X-ray. One line runs along the top of the sacrum, the upper endplate of S1 (the first sacral segment). A second line is drawn truly <b>horizontal</b>. The angle between them is Ferguson's angle. It is a standard radiographic measurement that any physician uses. Other named angles measure other things: the Cobb angle measures a scoliosis curve on a front-to-back X-ray, the Q angle lies at the knee and the carrying angle at the elbow.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-15-1.png\" alt=\"Lumbosacral angle: a line along the sacral base meets a horizontal line\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Lumbosacral angle: a line along the sacral base meets a horizontal line <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The normal range is <b>25–35°</b>. Anything larger is increased, so an angle of <b>45° or more</b> is <b>hyperlordotic</b>.</p>\n<h4>What a bigger angle does</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The sacral base tips forward and becomes steeper.</li>\n<li>The lumbar spine must curve more to keep the trunk upright, so the lordosis increases (<b>hyperlordosis</b>).</li>\n<li>L5 tends to slide forward down the steep sacral base, which raises the <b>shear force at the lumbosacral (L/S) junction</b>, L5–S1.</li>\n<li>These shear forces cause the most degeneration at the lumbosacral angle, and with it back pain. A bigger angle also puts more compressive load on the lumbosacral facets and raises the risk of spondylolisthesis, a forward slip of L5.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A smaller angle means a flatter sacral base and a <b>hypolordotic</b> (flat) lumbar spine.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-10.jpg\" alt=\"Ferguson&#x27;s angle normal 25–35°; larger angle, more L/S shear\"></div><figcaption>Ferguson&#x27;s angle normal 25–35°; larger angle, more L/S shear <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What tilts the sacral base</h4>\n<p>The sacrum is part of the pelvis, so rotating the pelvis changes the angle. In an <b>anterior pelvic tilt</b>, the top of the pelvis rolls forward, the sacral base steepens and the angle rises. In a <b>posterior pelvic tilt</b>, the top rolls back and the angle falls. High-heeled shoes tip the pelvis forward and so raise the angle, so patients with spondylolisthesis should avoid them.</p>\n<h4>Reading the curves on observation</h4>\n<p>Picture three spines. Curve A has normal front-to-back (A/P) curves. Curve B is <b>kypholordotic</b>: a deeper thoracic kyphosis and lumbar lordosis, a steeper sacral base and a larger Ferguson's angle. Curve C is <b>flattened</b>, with a smaller angle. When the lordosis deepens, the back of each lumbar segment closes: the spinous processes (SPs) and facet joints are pressed together, and the anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL) on the front of the bodies is stretched. A flexed, flat back does the reverse: the fronts of the bodies and discs are pressed together, and the posterior ligaments are stretched.</p>\n<p>The normal lumbar lordosis is about 30–60°, ideally 40–50°; this angle measures the curve itself, not the sacral base. A lordosis flattened by paravertebral muscle spasm can be a sign of ankylosing spondylitis. A <b>gibbus deformity</b> is a sharp local kyphosis where one or more vertebrae have collapsed; worldwide, spinal tuberculosis (Pott's disease) is the most common cause.</p>\n<p>Ferguson's angle describes a front-to-back curve. A higher iliac crest, lost sidebending and a positive hip drop test (4.2) are side-to-side (coronal) findings.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Ferguson's angle: S1 top (sacral base) vs horizontal, standing lateral X-ray</li>\n<li>Normal 25–35°; larger is increased, e.g. ≥45° hyperlordotic</li>\n<li>↑ angle → steeper sacral base → hyperlordosis → ↑ L5–S1 shear → degeneration, pain</li>\n<li>↓ angle → flat, hypolordotic spine</li>\n<li>Anterior pelvic tilt, high heels → ↑ angle; posterior tilt → ↓</li>\n<li>Curve B kypholordotic: SPs, facets compressed; ALL stretched. Curve C flattened</li>\n<li>Lordosis itself 30–60° (ideal 40–50°); gibbus = sharp local kyphosis (Pott's)</li>\n<li>Crest height, hip drop, lost sidebending = coronal findings, not A/P</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lordosis and Ferguson's angle","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Muscles that change the curve","p":"<p>Muscles that tilt the pelvis or pull on the lumbar spine change the lordosis and Ferguson's angle (2.1). Picture the pelvis as a bowl of water. Tipping its front rim down (anterior tilt) steepens the sacral base and deepens the lumbar curve. Tipping its back rim down (posterior tilt) levels the sacral base and flattens the curve.</p>\n<h4>Muscles that deepen the lordosis</h4>\n<p>The <b>psoas</b> runs from the bodies and transverse processes (TPs) of the lumbar vertebrae down to the lesser trochanter of the femur. When a person stands, the femur is fixed, so a short or contracting psoas pulls on its upper end instead. It draws the upper lumbar vertebrae forward, like arching the back. It also pulls the sacral base forward and rotates the pelvis anteriorly. A <b>hypertonic psoas</b>, one with too much resting tone, is one of the major causes of increased lumbar lordosis.</p>\n<p>The <b>quadriceps</b>, mainly the rectus femoris, arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) on the front of the pelvis. When it is hypertonic, it pulls the front of the pelvis down into an anterior tilt. That steepens the sacral base and increases both the lordosis and the angle.</p>\n<h4>Muscles that flatten it</h4>\n<p>The <b>gluteus maximus</b> arises from the back of the ilium and the sacrum, and the <b>hamstrings</b> arise from the ischial tuberosity. Both pull the back of the pelvis down into a posterior tilt. The sacral base levels out, the angle falls and the lumbar spine becomes hypolordotic.</p>\n<p>The <b>abdominal muscles</b> are the external and internal obliques and transversus abdominis, which act through the lumbodorsal fascia, plus rectus abdominis. They stabilize the lumbar spine and flatten the lordosis. Good abdominal tone protects the low back, because it adds stability and helps keep the curve from becoming excessive.</p>\n<p>The table gathers the four groups. Read across: where each muscle pulls, then what happens to the curve.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Pull</th><th>Lordosis and angle</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Psoas</b></td><td>Upper lumbar vertebrae forward; pelvis tilts anteriorly</td><td><b>Increase</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Quadriceps</b> (rectus femoris), hypertonic</td><td>Front of the pelvis down: anterior tilt</td><td><b>Increase</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gluteus maximus</b>, <b>hamstrings</b></td><td>Back of the pelvis down: posterior tilt</td><td><b>Decrease</b> (hypolordotic)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Abdominals</b></td><td>Stabilize the lumbar spine</td><td><b>Decrease</b> (flatten)</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-39-1.png\" alt=\"Muscle groups that increase or flatten the lordosis\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Muscle groups that increase or flatten the lordosis <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Using the balance in treatment</h4>\n<p>The groups oppose each other, so overworking one shifts the curve. Very strong abdominals, glutes or hamstrings can flatten the back too far; treatment then trains the opposing group, the lordosis-raisers. An excessive curve calls for abdominal strengthening and pelvic tilt exercises instead (6.2).</p>\n<h4>Muscles with other jobs</h4>\n<p>The <b>latissimus dorsi</b> joins the lumbodorsal fascia to the arm, so its problems cause arm and shoulder dysesthesias (abnormal sensations). The <b>quadratus lumborum (QL)</b> sidebends the trunk and hikes the hip, a side-to-side action (3.1). Pectoralis major is a chest and arm muscle with no effect on the lumbar curve.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-61-1.png\" alt=\"Short psoas pulls the lumbar spine into extension and tips the pelvis forward\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Short psoas pulls the lumbar spine into extension and tips the pelvis forward <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 61</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Anterior pelvic tilt → ↑ lordosis, ↑ Ferguson's angle; posterior tilt → ↓</li>\n<li>Psoas (lumbar bodies, TPs → lesser trochanter): pulls upper lumbar forward → ↑</li>\n<li>Hypertonic psoas = a major cause of ↑ lordosis</li>\n<li>Hypertonic quadriceps (rectus femoris, AIIS) → anterior tilt → ↑</li>\n<li>Gluteus maximus, hamstrings → posterior tilt → ↓ (hypolordotic)</li>\n<li>Abdominals stabilize, flatten lordosis; good tone protects the low back</li>\n<li>Correct a curve by training the opposing group</li>\n<li>Latissimus → arm/shoulder dysesthesias; QL → sidebend, hip hike; pectoralis major → none</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lordosis and Ferguson's angle","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Long and short back muscles","p":"<p>A back muscle's length predicts the kind of somatic dysfunction (SD) it holds. SD is impaired or altered motion of a body part, here a single vertebra or a group of vertebrae.</p>\n<h4>Two kinds of somatic dysfunction</h4>\n<p>Part 4.1 gives the full rules; here is the short version. A <b>type I (neutral)</b> SD is a <b>group</b> of vertebrae that curve together, with sidebending (SB) and rotation to <b>opposite</b> sides. A <b>type II (non-neutral)</b> SD is a <b>single segment</b> stuck flexed or extended, with rotation and SB to the <b>same</b> side.</p>\n<h4>Long muscles hold group curves</h4>\n<p>The <b>erector spinae</b> are three long columns. From medial to lateral they are spinalis, longissimus and iliocostalis. They attach along the lumbar spine and into the sacrum, and they work with the gluteal muscles and hamstrings, so those are examined too. When both sides contract, the lumbar spine extends. When one side contracts, the spine extends and sidebends toward that same side.</p>\n<p>The <b>quadratus lumborum (QL)</b> is the deepest muscle of the posterior abdominal wall. It runs from the posterior iliac crest and the iliolumbar ligament up to rib 12 and the transverse processes (TPs) of L1–L4. One side sidebends the trunk and hikes the hip. Both sides together extend the spine, and the muscle also steadies rib 12.</p>\n<p>Each of these muscles spans many segments. When one becomes hypertonic (too much tone), it pulls several vertebrae at once into one sidebent curve. A group curve in neutral rotates the opposite way, so long muscles hold <b>type I</b> group curves.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-36-1.png\" alt=\"Deep back muscles: erector spinae columns, rotatores, intertransversarii, quadratus lumborum\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Deep back muscles: erector spinae columns, rotatores, intertransversarii, quadratus lumborum <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Short, deep muscles hold single segments</h4>\n<p>The <b>multifidus</b> is an intrinsic back muscle. It lies deep to the erector spinae, under spinalis (their medial column), beside the spinous processes. It is a postural muscle, and when both sides contract it extends the segment locally. The <b>rotatores</b> run from a TP to the spinous process one level up (rotator brevis) or two levels up (rotator longus). They stabilize, extend and rotate single vertebrae. The <b>intertransversarii</b> run between neighboring TPs.</p>\n<p>These short muscles each control an individual vertebra, and each gets its nerve supply from the fewest spinal segments. When one becomes hypertonic, it locks just one joint. They are the muscles mainly involved in <b>segmental</b> (one-level) SD, the <b>type II</b> pattern.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O069-28.jpg\" alt=\"Rotatores, multifidus and intertransversarii span one or two levels\"></div><figcaption>Rotatores, multifidus and intertransversarii span one or two levels <small>· 2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Superficial (extrinsic) back muscles, such as latissimus dorsi and serratus posterior, connect the trunk to the arm and ribs. They do not control single segments. Trigger points in the deep muscles refer pain away from the muscle itself; the referral maps below show the patterns for the QL, piriformis, iliopsoas, and the rotatores and multifidi.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O094-5-1.png\" alt=\"Referred pain maps: QL, piriformis, iliopsoas, rotatores and multifidi\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Referred pain maps: QL, piriformis, iliopsoas, rotatores and multifidi <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Long muscles span many segments → type I (neutral) group curve, SB and rotation opposite</li>\n<li>Erector spinae, medial → lateral: spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis</li>\n<li>Erector spinae: both sides → extension; one side → same-side extension + SB</li>\n<li>QL: iliac crest, iliolumbar ligament → rib 12, L1–L4 TPs; long, type I</li>\n<li>Short deep muscles: one vertebra, fewest spinal segments → type II segmental SD</li>\n<li>Multifidus deep to erector spinae (under spinalis); rotatores brevis 1 level, longus 2</li>\n<li>Latissimus dorsi, serratus posterior: superficial, not segment controllers</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Back muscles and ligaments","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Longitudinal and spinous ligaments","p":"<p>Ligaments hold neighboring vertebrae together and stop each motion at its end. The rule is simple: a ligament limits the motion that stretches it. A ligament in front of the vertebral bodies is stretched when you bend backward. Ligaments behind the bodies are stretched when you bend forward.</p>\n<h4>The longitudinal ligaments</h4>\n<p>The <b>anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL)</b> runs down the front of the vertebral bodies and discs. It is <b>broad</b>, and bending backward stretches it, so it limits <b>extension</b>. In an extreme backbend (hyperextension) it is the main check, while the posterior ligament goes slack.</p>\n<p>The <b>posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL)</b> runs down the back of the bodies and discs, inside the vertebral canal. It is <b>narrow</b>, and it gets narrower low in the lumbar spine (6.1). Bending forward stretches it, so it limits <b>flexion</b>. In short: ALL broad, limits extension; PLL narrow, limits flexion.</p>\n<h4>The ligaments between the spinous processes</h4>\n<p>The <b>interspinous ligament</b> (also called intraspinal) is a thin sheet between neighboring spinous processes. The <b>supraspinous ligament</b> is a cord along their tips. Forward bending spreads the spinous processes apart and stretches both. So every ligament behind the disc, the PLL included, helps limit flexion. The lumbosacral ligament is different: it runs sideways from the L5 transverse process to the sacral ala and anchors L5; it is not a flexion check.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-42-2.png\" alt=\"Side view: ALL in front; interspinous and supraspinous ligaments behind\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Side view: ALL in front; interspinous and supraspinous ligaments behind <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-48-1.png\" alt=\"Ligaments: ALL broad, limits extension; PLL narrow, limits flexion\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Ligaments: ALL broad, limits extension; PLL narrow, limits flexion <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Segmental tenderness</h4>\n<p>In the palpation exam, you press on one spinous process and the interspinous ligament beside it. Tenderness at a single segment suggests a <b>possible disc problem</b> at that level. Sharp tenderness on palpation, or on tapping with a reflex hammer, is a red flag for vertebral fracture or infection.</p>\n<p>Tenderness elsewhere means other things. Tenderness lateral to the spine, between the L4–L5 transverse processes and the iliac crest, points to the iliolumbar ligament (3.3). Tenderness beside the posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS) points to the sacroiliac joint. The psoas lies in front of the spine and is checked with the Thomas test. Facet tropism is an anatomic finding, not a tenderness sign.</p>\n<h4>The disc and these ligaments</h4>\n<p>The annulus, the outer ring of the disc, attaches to both longitudinal ligaments. Flexion squeezes the front of the disc and raises pressure on the <b>posterior</b> annulus and the PLL. Extension squeezes the back of the disc and raises pressure on the <b>anterior</b> annulus and the ALL. A <b>diseased or aging disc is more compressible</b>, and its annulus tears. A thinner disc shifts more of the body's weight onto the facet joints.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-60-1.png\" alt=\"The same load compresses a diseased disc more than a normal disc\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>The same load compresses a diseased disc more than a normal disc <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>A ligament limits the motion that stretches it</li>\n<li>ALL: front of bodies, broad → limits extension (main check in hyperextension)</li>\n<li>PLL: back of bodies, narrow (narrower low) → limits flexion</li>\n<li>Interspinous (“intraspinal”), supraspinous: behind the disc → also limit flexion</li>\n<li>Lumbosacral ligament: L5 transverse process → sacral ala; anchors L5</li>\n<li>Single spinous process + interspinous tenderness → possible disc problem</li>\n<li>Sharp or percussion tenderness → red flag: fracture, infection</li>\n<li>Flexion loads posterior annulus + PLL; extension loads anterior annulus + ALL</li>\n<li>Diseased/aging disc: more compressible, annulus tears → more load on facets</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Back muscles and ligaments","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Multifidus triangle and iliolumbar ligament","p":"<p>Most low back pain (LBP) arises in one small region, the <b>multifidus triangle</b>. Palpating it first gives a baseline for the rest of the exam.</p>\n<h4>Borders and contents</h4>\n<p>Three reference points mark the triangle: the <b>L4 spinous process</b> and the iliac crests above, and the <b>tip of the sacrum</b> below. It contains the multifidus muscles, the most commonly ruptured lumbar discs (<b>L4 and L5</b>), the intertransverse ligaments, the iliolumbar ligaments, the sites of facet syndromes and the sacroiliac (SI) joint. The region also takes in the interspinous ligament, spondylolisthesis, sacral shear and an unlevel sacral base. Other causes of low back pain in this region include somatic dysfunction (SD), short leg syndrome, facet tropism and congenital anomalies.</p>\n<p>It helps to picture the contents from medial to lateral. The interspinous ligament sits in the midline, between the spinous processes. Next come the multifidi, then the facets, then the intertransverse ligaments, and finally the iliolumbar ligaments reaching out to the iliac crests. The SI joints lie lower, at the edges of the sacrum. The quadratus lumborum (QL) lies <b>above</b> the triangle, running from the iliac crest up to rib 12.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-42-1.png\" alt=\"Multifidus triangle: iliolumbar ligament, multifidus and SI ligaments inside; QL above\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Multifidus triangle: iliolumbar ligament, multifidus and SI ligaments inside; QL above <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O094-3-1.png\" alt=\"Triangle map: interspinous ligament midline, iliolumbar ligament lateral, L4–L5 levels\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Triangle map: interspinous ligament midline, iliolumbar ligament lateral, L4–L5 levels <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 3</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The iliolumbar ligament</h4>\n<p>The <b>iliolumbar ligament</b> runs from the <b>transverse processes of L4 and L5</b> to the <b>iliac crest</b>, just above and lateral to the posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS). It ties L5, and possibly L4, to the pelvis and restricts forward and rotary motion of L5. Its fibers blend with the upper part of the anterior SI ligament.</p>\n<p>Because it holds the lowest lumbar vertebra on the pelvis, it takes the strain whenever the lumbosacral junction is out of balance. It is the <b>first ligament to be strained</b>, and the first to become tender, in a lumbosacral imbalance (postural decompensation). Lifting injuries also often strain it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-43-1.png\" alt=\"Iliolumbar ligaments: L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crests\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Iliolumbar ligaments: L4–L5 transverse processes to the iliac crests <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Iliolumbar ligament syndrome</h4>\n<p>To show that the ligament causes the pain, you use a <b>spring test</b>: press firmly over the ligament on each side, between the L4–L5 transverse processes and the iliac crest. Reproduced tenderness confirms it. Palpating an irritated ligament often refers pain <b>anteriorly into the groin</b> on the same side. Its attachment beside the PSIS and its blend with the SI ligament put its pain over the SI region, so it <b>mimics SI joint SD or inflammation</b>. Same-side lumbosacral or SI dysfunction often comes with it.</p>\n<p>Several other low back problems look similar. The table lists each with the clue that sets it apart and its test.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Look-alike</th><th>Main clue</th><th>Test</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Iliolumbar ligament syndrome</td><td>Tender in the triangle; pain to the groin</td><td>Spring test</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Psoas (iliopsoas) syndrome</td><td>Flexed posture; low back and anterior thigh pain</td><td>Thomas test: the thigh lifts off the table</td></tr>\n<tr><td>QL spasm</td><td>Pain along the iliac crest, hip and buttock</td><td>Hip drop test (4.2): crest drops less than 20°</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Piriformis syndrome</td><td>Buttock pain down the back of the thigh, worse after sitting</td><td>Tender piriformis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>L5 or S1 nerve root (disc)</td><td>Radiating leg pain (L5: lateral leg, top of the foot; S1: back of the calf to the heel)</td><td>Straight leg raise: leg pain at 30–70°</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The annulus fibrosus is part of the disc, not a ligament of the triangle. The sacrospinous ligament runs from the sacrum to the ischial spine, deep in the pelvis.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Multifidus triangle: L4 spinous process, iliac crests, tip of sacrum; most LBP here</li>\n<li>Contents: multifidi, L4/L5 discs, intertransverse + iliolumbar ligaments, facets, SI joint</li>\n<li>Medial → lateral: interspinous lig → multifidi → facets → intertransverse → iliolumbar</li>\n<li>QL lies above the triangle (iliac crest → rib 12)</li>\n<li>Iliolumbar: L4–L5 transverse processes → iliac crest above/lateral to PSIS; stabilizes L5</li>\n<li>First ligament strained/tender in lumbosacral imbalance; lifting injuries</li>\n<li>Spring test; refers to same-side groin; mimics SI SD or inflammation</li>\n<li>Annulus = disc; sacrospinous = sacrum → ischial spine</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Back muscles and ligaments","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Fryette's three principles, freedoms and barriers","p":"<h4>Reading rotation</h4>\n<p>A vertebra's rotation is named for where the <b>front (anterior surface) of its body</b> turns. When the body turns right, the right transverse process (TP) swings <b>back</b>, the left TP swings forward, and the spinous process (SP) at the back swings <b>left</b>. You can therefore feel rotation from behind: the <b>posterior TP is on the side of rotation</b>, and the SP points the other way. Rotation turns around a vertical axis; sidebending turns around a front-to-back (A–P) axis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-60.jpg\" alt=\"Body rotated right: right transverse process goes back, spinous process left\"></div><figcaption>Body rotated right: right transverse process goes back, spinous process left <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Naming somatic dysfunction</h4>\n<p>Somatic dysfunction (SD) is named for its <b>freedoms</b>: the position the segment rests in and moves into easily. An injury leaves a segment stuck in the position it was in at the moment of injury, so the name records that position. The <b>barriers</b>, the restricted motions, are the opposites of every named component.</p>\n<p>A segment is always named for how it sits <b>relative to the vertebra below</b>. In L4 ERrSr, L4 is extended (E), rotated right (Rr) and sidebent right (Sr) on L5. F means flexed and N neutral.</p>\n<p>Four signs show that SD is present, remembered as TART: <b>T</b>issue texture change, <b>A</b>symmetry, <b>R</b>estriction of motion and <b>T</b>enderness. Warm, boggy tissue suggests an acute problem; ropy, fibrotic tissue suggests a chronic one.</p>\n<h4>Fryette's principles</h4>\n<p>Fryette's principles describe how sidebending (SB) and rotation pair up. The pairing depends on whether the facets are engaged.</p>\n<p>The <b>first principle (type I)</b> applies when the spine is <b>neutral</b>: not flexed or extended enough to engage the facets. Sidebending comes first, and rotation follows to the <b>opposite</b> side. It involves a group of segments, and the group rotates into the convexity of its curve. An example is L1–4 NSlRr: neutral, sidebent left, rotated right.</p>\n<p>The <b>second principle (type II)</b> applies when the spine is <b>non-neutral</b>: flexed or extended enough to engage the facets. Now rotation of at least one segment comes first, and sidebending follows to the <b>same</b> side. It is a single segment that rotates into its concavity, usually with neutral mechanics above and below it. An example is L4 FRrSr: flexed, rotated right, sidebent right.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Type I (first principle)</th><th>Type II (second principle)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Spine</td><td><b>Neutral</b></td><td><b>Flexed or extended</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Order</td><td>Sidebending, then rotation</td><td>Rotation, then sidebending</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coupling</td><td><b>Opposite sides</b></td><td><b>Same side</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extent</td><td>Group</td><td>Single segment</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The <b>third principle</b> applies to both types: motion in one plane changes, and reduces, motion in all the other planes. For example, a flexed segment has less sidebending and rotation available.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-64.jpg\" alt=\"Non-neutral RrSr segment rotates into the concavity; neutral mechanics above, below\"></div><figcaption>Non-neutral RrSr segment rotates into the concavity; neutral mechanics above, below <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 64</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Working out the barriers</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Write down the freedoms, for example E, Rl, Sl.</li>\n<li>Reverse each one to get the barriers: F, Rr, Sr.</li>\n<li>Check the pattern: in a type II segment the reversed rotation and sidebending again fall on one side, while a mixed pair (one left, one right) belongs to a neutral, type I group.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Rotation named for the anterior body; posterior TP = side of rotation; SP opposite</li>\n<li>SD named for freedoms (position of injury, relative to the vertebra below); barriers = opposites</li>\n<li>TART: tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, tenderness; boggy = acute, ropy = chronic</li>\n<li>Fryette I: neutral → SB then rotation, opposite sides; group; rotates into convexity</li>\n<li>Fryette II: flexed/extended → rotation then SB, same side; single segment; into concavity</li>\n<li>Fryette III: motion in one plane ↓ motion in the others (both types)</li>\n<li>Type II barriers again same-sided; mixed pair = type I</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Fryette's principles and group curves","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Group curves and the hip drop test","p":"<h4>Finding the level</h4>\n<p>Two landmarks set the lumbar levels. Follow the <b>12th rib</b> in toward the spine: the L1 transverse process (TP) lies just below it, so tissue directly below rib 12 is at L1. Rest your hands on the tops of the <b>iliac crests</b> and follow them back toward the spine: that line marks <b>L4</b>. A segment slightly above the crest line is L4, and L5 lies below it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-33-1.png\" alt=\"Pelvis from behind: the iliac crests, the landmark for L4\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Pelvis from behind: the iliac crests, the landmark for L4 <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Naming a neutral group curve</h4>\n<p>A group curve shows as posterior TPs, or tissue changes, over several segments in a row. The posterior TP marks the side of rotation (4.1). A <b>neutral</b> group is not held by a flexed or extended segment, so its asymmetry <b>does not change</b> when the patient flexes or extends. By Fryette's first principle, the group sidebends to the <b>opposite</b> side of its rotation; a group never shows same-side coupling.</p>\n<p>For example, left TPs posterior from L1 to L3 give L1–3 NSrRl: neutral (N), sidebent right (Sr), rotated left (Rl). The curve is concave on the side it is sidebent toward and convex on the other side. Ropy tissue over the group suggests it is chronic.</p>\n<h4>The hip drop test</h4>\n<p>The hip drop test is a <b>screening test</b> for lumbar and thoracolumbar <b>sidebending (SB)</b>. The patient stands, and your fingers rest on the top and side of each iliac crest to judge whether they are level. The patient puts all the weight on one leg and simply <b>unlocks the other knee, keeping that heel down</b>. The test sets no knee angle; the number you judge is the drop of the iliac crest.</p>\n<p>Normally the crest on the bent-knee side <b>drops 20–25°</b>, and the lumbar spine sidebends in a <b>smooth curve away</b> from the dropped side, convex toward it. The test is positive when the crest drops less than 20°, or when the curve is not smooth. Then find the level of any sharp angle or flat stretch. The <b>positive side is the side that drops less</b>. The test also evaluates gluteus medius weakness.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O094-6-1.png\" alt=\"Left hip drop: the left crest drops about 25°, lumbar spine sidebends right\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Left hip drop: the left crest drops about 25°, lumbar spine sidebends right <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O105-3-1.png\" alt=\"Normal spine dropping the right hip: the lumbar spine sidebends left\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Normal spine dropping the right hip: the lumbar spine sidebends left <small>· Fall 2021 lumbar review (SGA drive), slide 3</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>From a positive test to the spine</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Dropping one hip asks the lumbar spine to sidebend toward the other side.</li>\n<li>If that crest drops less, SB away from it is <b>restricted</b>.</li>\n<li>The spine therefore rests sidebent <b>toward</b> the positive side and moves freely that way. Its concavity faces the positive side, and its convexity faces the other side.</li>\n<li>In a group curve, rotation goes opposite to the SB (type I).</li>\n<li>A common cause is a hypertonic quadratus lumborum (QL) on the positive side, which holds that ilium up and the trunk sidebent toward it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When the two sides are compared, the positive side is the one whose crest drops less, even if neither fails completely. Name the restriction from it: the restricted sidebending is toward the opposite side, and the spine rests sidebent toward the positive side. The hip drop tests side-to-side motion only; it tells you nothing about flexion or the lordosis.</p>\n<p>A worked example runs the logic from the diagnosis. L2–5 NSlRr is sidebent left, so it resists SB to the right. A left hip drop asks for right SB, so the left crest drops less and the test is positive on the left.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Just below rib 12 = L1; iliac crest line = L4 (L5 below)</li>\n<li>Neutral group: posterior TPs over several levels, unchanged by flexion/extension</li>\n<li>Group = type I: SB opposite rotation, e.g. NSrRl; concave on SB side</li>\n<li>Hip drop: unlock one knee, heel down; no set knee angle</li>\n<li>Normal crest drop 20–25°, smooth lumbar SB away from dropped side</li>\n<li>Positive: &lt;20° drop or non-smooth curve; positive side = drops less</li>\n<li>Positive side → spine sidebent toward it (free); SB to the other side restricted</li>\n<li>Common cause: tight QL on the positive side; also tests gluteus medius</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Fryette's principles and group curves","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"The flexion–extension test","p":"<p>A type II (non-neutral) somatic dysfunction (SD) is a single segment stuck flexed or extended, with rotation and sidebending (SB) to the same side (4.1). Two findings name it: which transverse process is posterior, and whether flexion or extension evens it out.</p>\n<h4>Step 1: the posterior TP gives the rotation</h4>\n<p>Palpate the two transverse processes (TPs) of the segment. The posterior one is on the side the body is rotated toward. The spinous process (SP) deviates to the opposite side, because it sits behind the body and swings the other way. A right posterior TP means a segment rotated right, with its SP pointing left.</p>\n<h4>Step 2: flexion and extension give the F or E</h4>\n<p>Recheck the TPs with the segment in flexion and then in extension. The patient can bend forward and arch back. With the patient prone, you can also introduce the motions yourself: rolling your thumbs headward (cephalad) and forward over the segment flexes it, and pressing the SP forward extends it.</p>\n<p>The asymmetry gets <b>worse toward the barrier</b> and <b>evens out in the freedom</b>. Both the rotation and the sidebending stand out more toward the barrier. To read the result, note how the posterior TP behaves in each position; the last column of the table gives the name.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Posterior TP in flexion</th><th>Posterior TP in extension</th><th>Barrier</th><th>Name</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Evens out</td><td>More prominent</td><td>Extension</td><td><b>F</b>RS: flexed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>More prominent</td><td>Evens out</td><td>Flexion</td><td><b>E</b>RS: extended</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unchanged</td><td>Unchanged</td><td>None in this plane</td><td>N: a neutral group (type I)</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Why it works</h4>\n<p>Flexion opens (gaps) the facet joints and the intervertebral foramina, and extension closes them. A segment stuck in flexion cannot follow its neighbors into extension. When the spine extends, the stuck segment lags behind, and its rotation stands out against the rest. A segment stuck in extension cannot follow into flexion in the same way. Moving toward the free direction lets the stuck segment line up with its neighbors again, so the TPs even out.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O069-40.jpg\" alt=\"Facets and foramina open in flexion and close in extension\"></div><figcaption>Facets and foramina open in flexion and close in extension <small>· 2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 40</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Completing the name</h4>\n<p>Add sidebending to the same side as rotation, because a non-neutral segment follows type II mechanics. The barriers are the opposites of every component: a flexed segment rotated and sidebent right (FRSr) is restricted in extension, left rotation and left sidebending. The segments above and below usually show neutral mechanics. One non-neutral segment does not force dysfunction at the neighboring levels, or a high lumbar group curve. Psoas syndrome is a different pattern, centered on a non-neutral (flexed) L1 or L2.</p>\n<h4>Muscle energy in brief</h4>\n<p>Muscle energy (ME) of the post-isometric relaxation type is a direct technique, so the segment is first placed at its barrier. The patient then gently pushes back toward the freedom, away from the barrier, against the physician's equal counterforce. After the muscle relaxes, the physician takes up the new barrier.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Posterior TP = side of rotation; SP deviates opposite</li>\n<li>Thumbs cephalad + anterior → flexion; SP pressed forward → extension</li>\n<li>Asymmetry worse toward barrier, evens out in freedom (rotation and SB)</li>\n<li>Evens in flexion, worse in extension → FRS; reverse → ERS; unchanged → neutral group</li>\n<li>Flexion opens facets and foramina; extension closes them</li>\n<li>Type II: SB same side as rotation; barriers = opposites</li>\n<li>Neutral mechanics above/below; no forced group curve; psoas syndrome = flexed L1/L2</li>\n<li>ME: at the barrier, patient pushes toward the freedom</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Diagnosing a single segment","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Injury stories and springing","p":"<h4>From the story to the name</h4>\n<p>Somatic dysfunction (SD) is named for the position it is stuck in, and that is the position of the injury. A patient hurt while bending forward has a flexed (F) segment; one hurt while arching back has an extended (E) one. The side the patient reached or twisted toward gives the side of rotation and sidebending. A sudden injury of one segment in flexion or extension is type II (non-neutral), so rotation and sidebending go to the same side (4.1).</p>\n<p>The level comes from landmarks: directly below rib 12 is L1, and the iliac crest line is L4 (4.2). The exam findings then follow from the name. The posterior transverse process (TP) lies on the side of rotation. The TPs even out in the freedom and stand out more toward the barrier (5.1).</p>\n<h4>Springing, prone</h4>\n<p>Springing is a gentle screening test for segmental SD. The patient lies prone. You press briefly on one part of a segment and feel whether it gives or resists. A segment gives when you push it toward its freedom and resists when you push it toward its barrier. Each push therefore tests one motion, and resistance means the segment is stuck in the opposite position.</p>\n<p>Three pushes cover the three planes. An anterior push on the spinous process (SP) tips the segment back into extension. A headward (cephalad) push on the segment introduces flexion. An anterior push on one TP drives that side forward, which rotates the body toward the opposite side. The table turns each resisted push into part of the name.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Push</th><th>Motion it introduces</th><th>Resistance means</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Anterior, on the SP</td><td>Extension</td><td>The segment is <b>flexed</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cephalad, on the segment</td><td>Flexion</td><td>The segment is <b>extended</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anterior, on one TP</td><td>Rotation of the body to the <b>opposite</b> side</td><td>The segment is rotated <b>toward the pushed side</b></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Combine the answers, then add sidebending to the same side as rotation for a single resistant segment. Warmth, hypertonicity and bogginess over the segment are the tissue texture sign of TART (tissue texture change, asymmetry, restriction, tenderness), and they confirm the level. A neutral group curve would not resist an extension push, and it would spread over several levels rather than one.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-62.jpg\" alt=\"Body as the reference: a rotated body carries its TPs and spinous process\"></div><figcaption>Body as the reference: a rotated body carries its TPs and spinous process <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 62</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>SD named for the injury position: bent forward → F; arched back → E</li>\n<li>Side reached toward → side of rotation and sidebending (type II, same side)</li>\n<li>Below rib 12 = L1; iliac crest = L4</li>\n<li>Springing: gives toward freedom, resists toward barrier</li>\n<li>SP pushed anterior resists → flexed; cephalad push resists → extended</li>\n<li>Anterior push on one TP resists → rotated toward that side</li>\n<li>Warm, boggy, hypertonic tissue = TART tissue texture; marks the level</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Diagnosing a single segment","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"The moving nucleus and herniation","p":"<h4>Disc structure</h4>\n<p>Lumbar discs are large, built to take heavy loads and spread them out. Each is fixed to the endplates of the vertebrae above and below. A disc is <b>named for the vertebra above</b> it, so the L4 disc lies between L4 and L5.</p>\n<p>The disc has two parts. The <b>annulus fibrosus</b> is the outer ring, made of fiber layers that cross each other in a crosshatch. The crosshatch lets the disc twist and take shearing forces while it still holds the joint stable. The annulus is <b>thicker in front and thinner behind</b>, and it attaches to the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments. The <b>nucleus pulposus</b> is the gel center, sitting slightly behind the middle of the disc.</p>\n<h4>Squish and squirt</h4>\n<p>The nucleus moves <b>away from the side that is squeezed</b>, like toothpaste in a tube pressed at one end. In flexion the front of the disc is squeezed, so the nucleus moves <b>posteriorly</b> and loads the posterior annulus and the posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL). In extension the back is squeezed, so the nucleus moves <b>anteriorly</b> and loads the anterior annulus and the anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL). In sidebending, the side you bend toward (the concave side) is squeezed, so the nucleus moves toward the <b>convex</b> side, where the annulus is stretched.</p>\n<p>Because flexion pushes the nucleus back toward the nerve roots, it can enlarge a bulging or herniated disc and worsen symptoms.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-56-1.png\" alt=\"Flexion squeezes the front of the disc; the nucleus moves posteriorly\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Flexion squeezes the front of the disc; the nucleus moves posteriorly <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Herniation</h4>\n<p>In a <b>bulging disc</b>, the annulus sags outward but stays intact. In a <b>herniated disc</b>, the annulus tears, and nucleus material escapes toward the spinal canal or a nerve root.</p>\n<p>Herniations are usually <b>posterolateral</b>. The nucleus already sits slightly back, and the PLL reinforces only the midline of the back of the disc. The PLL also narrows as it descends; at L4–L5 it is about half its width at L1. <b>L4–L5</b>, the most common level, and <b>L5–S1</b> together account for about 95% of lumbar herniations. Upper lumbar levels, where the PLL is wide, rarely herniate. The thoracolumbar junction (T12–L1) is a transition zone for somatic dysfunction, not a common disc level.</p>\n<p><b>Central</b> herniations are rare. A large one compresses the cauda equina, the nerve roots below the end of the spinal cord, and causes cauda equina syndrome (saddle anesthesia, bladder or bowel dysfunction), a surgical emergency.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-64-1.png\" alt=\"Normal disc, posterolateral herniation on a root, central herniation compressing the cauda equina\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Normal disc, posterolateral herniation on a root, central herniation compressing the cauda equina <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 64</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Disc aging starts early. By the 30s, 30–50% of people show disc changes on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging), even with no pain. The disc is most vulnerable to herniation once its tissue changes type (metaplasia), in the third decade in men and the fourth in women. Disc cells turn <b>senescent</b> and release enzymes that break down the matrix. The endplates that feed the nearly bloodless disc <b>calcify</b> and starve its cells. The cells switch from type II to stiffer <b>type I collagen</b> and make fewer water-binding proteoglycans, so the disc dries out. Unrepaired stress then opens <b>micro-fissures</b> in the annulus in over half of people in their 30s. Staying active, keeping core strength and avoiding long static sitting help.</p>\n<h4>Which root is hit</h4>\n<p>A lumbar nerve root leaves the canal below the vertebra of the same number: L4 exits between L4 and L5, and L5 between L5 and S1. S1 leaves through the first sacral foramina. The X+1 rule: a posterolateral herniation of disc X compresses root X+1. The L4 root has already exited above the L4–L5 disc, so that disc hits L5.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-88-1.png\" alt=\"L4–L5 and L5–S1 discs beside the nerve roots that cross them\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>L4–L5 and L5–S1 discs beside the nerve roots that cross them <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 88</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Disc named for vertebra above; annulus crosshatched, thick front, thin back</li>\n<li>Nucleus moves away from the squeezed side (squish and squirt)</li>\n<li>Flexion → nucleus posterior, PLL loaded; extension → anterior, ALL loaded</li>\n<li>Sidebending → nucleus to the convex side</li>\n<li>Herniation posterolateral; PLL narrows (L4–L5 half its L1 width)</li>\n<li>L4–L5, L5–S1 ≈ 95%; central rare → cauda equina emergency</li>\n<li>30s: senescence, endplate calcification, type I collagen shift, annular micro-fissures</li>\n<li>Root exits below its vertebra; disc X hits root X+1 (L4–L5 → L5)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The disc and the lordotic back","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Disc load and the lordotic back","p":"<h4>Load across the day</h4>\n<p>Under load, the disc slowly loses fluid and gets thinner. Unloaded, as when lying down at night, it reabsorbs fluid and regains its height. Age slows this recovery.</p>\n<h4>Load by position</h4>\n<p>Disc pressure depends on posture. Lying supine loads the disc least. Sitting loads it more than standing, and bending forward raises the load further in either position, so <b>sitting flexed</b> is the highest. For a 70 kg person, the total load on a lumbar disc is about:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Position</th><th>Load on a lumbar disc</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Supine</td><td>About 25 kg (lowest)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lateral recumbent (side-lying)</td><td>About 75 kg</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standing</td><td>About 100 kg</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sitting</td><td>About 140 kg</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standing flexed</td><td>About 150 kg</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sitting flexed</td><td>About 185 kg (<b>highest</b>)</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O069-111.jpg\" alt=\"Total disc load by position: supine lowest, seated flexed highest\"></div><figcaption>Total disc load by position: supine lowest, seated flexed highest <small>· 2026 week 12 IA Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 111</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The load on L3, in newtons (N), follows the same pattern and covers everyday actions too: supine 300, standing 700, walking 850, twisting 900, bending sideways 950, upright sitting 1000, coughing 1100, and straining and laughing 1200 each. Lifting 20 kg with the back straight and knees bent gives 2100; with the back bent and knees straight, 3400. Coughing, straining and laughing raise the pressure inside the abdomen and the spinal canal, as a Valsalva maneuver does.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O068-82.jpg\" alt=\"Load on L3 in newtons for everyday activities\"></div><figcaption>Load on L3 in newtons for everyday activities <small>· 2026 week 12 Lecture 12 Functional Lumbar Spine, slide 82</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Radicular (nerve root) pain from a disc gets worse with <b>flexion and sitting</b>, because the nucleus moves back toward the root and the root is stretched. Twisting adds load. Extension and lying down unload the disc. Pain that eases when the patient bends forward points instead to spinal stenosis.</p>\n<h4>Hyperlordosis (lordotic low back pain)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A hypertonic psoas or quadriceps, weak abdominals or a large Ferguson's angle (2.1, 2.2) hold the lumbar segments in <b>extension</b>.</li>\n<li>Extension brings the facet joints together and shifts body weight onto them, and over time their cartilage breaks down. This degeneration is <b>facet syndrome</b>, an arthritis-like condition of the facet joints.</li>\n<li>Extension also brings the pedicles and facets of neighboring vertebrae closer, so the <b>intervertebral foramen</b>, the opening where each nerve root and its sheath exit, gets smaller (5.1 figure).</li>\n<li>With narrow foramina and loaded facets crowding them, the nerve roots and their sheaths are irritated more easily.</li>\n<li>The nucleus is pushed anteriorly, and the anterior longitudinal ligament is stretched.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Posture does not change the facets' orientation or create facet tropism; both are fixed anatomy (1.2). Exercises for excess lordosis tilt the pelvis back and flex the lumbar spine: the supine pelvic tilt, and active resistive flexion, in which the patient rolls into a ball and pushes the knees up toward the ceiling. Strengthening the lordosis-raisers (psoas, quadriceps) or extension work would deepen the curve instead.</p>\n<h4>Loss of lordosis (flat back)</h4>\n<p>Long periods spent bent forward hold the segments in flexion. The posterior ligaments, fascia and muscles lengthen, and the nucleus is pushed back, which encourages bulging and posterior herniation. The foramina open. Exercises for a flat back are extension-inducing, done prone.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Load → disc loses fluid, thins; lying down → regains height; age slows recovery</li>\n<li>Disc load (kg): supine 25, side 75, stand 100, sit 140, stand-flexed 150, sit-flexed 185</li>\n<li>L3 (N): supine 300, standing 700, sitting 1000, coughing 1100, laughing/straining 1200</li>\n<li>Radicular disc pain ↑ with flexion, sitting, twisting; ↓ with extension, lying</li>\n<li>Pain eased by bending forward → spinal stenosis</li>\n<li>Hyperlordosis → extension → facet loading, degeneration (facet syndrome)</li>\n<li>→ narrowed foramina → roots, sheaths more easily irritated; nucleus anterior</li>\n<li>Excess lordosis: pelvic tilt, active resistive flexion; flat back: prone extension</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The disc and the lordotic back","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"Lumbar HVLA","p":"<p>The lumbar spine is treated in a set order: myofascial techniques first, to relax the tissues, then HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust) for a type II segment.</p>\n<h4>Myofascial techniques first</h4>\n<p>Myofascial techniques stretch and relax muscle and fascia before a joint is treated. Four are used. The passive perpendicular myofascial stretch is done with the patient lateral recumbent (lying on the side). The thoracolumbar bidirectional stretch pulls the tissues in two directions at once. The active, direct myofascial lumbar technique adds the patient's own motion. The fourth works on the quadratus lumborum (QL) with the patient prone:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The physician stands on the side opposite the QL being treated.</li>\n<li>The cephalad (headward) hand steadies rib 12, where the QL attaches.</li>\n<li>The caudad (footward) hand grasps the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and lifts it toward the physician in slow, rhythmic motions.</li>\n<li>At the end, the physician reassesses QL tone.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O071-13.jpg\" alt=\"Prone QL myofascial: steady rib 12, lift the ASIS rhythmically\"></div><figcaption>Prone QL myofascial: steady rib 12, lift the ASIS rhythmically <small>· 2026 week 12 Lab Lumbar Spine Diagnosis &amp; HVLA, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What HVLA is</h4>\n<p>HVLA is a quick, short thrust through a very small distance. It is a <b>direct</b> technique: the segment is first taken into its <b>barrier</b>, the motion it cannot make, and the thrust then carries it through. The barrier is set for the segment <b>relative to the vertebra below</b>, the same way the segment is named (4.1). For example, L3 FRlSl (flexed, rotated left and sidebent left on L4) is taken into extension, right rotation and right sidebending of L3 on L4. Placing the segment in its freedom would make the technique indirect. HVLA is relatively contraindicated in a disc herniation and in spondylolisthesis.</p>\n<h4>Lumbar roll set-up</h4>\n<p>The main rule is <b>posterior transverse process (TP) up</b>. The posterior TP marks the side of rotation, so in a segment rotated right the right TP faces the ceiling and the patient lies on the left side.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient lies on the side with the posterior TP up. The physician stands in front and monitors that TP throughout.</li>\n<li>The physician flexes the patient's hips and knees until motion is felt just above the dysfunctional vertebra.</li>\n<li>The patient straightens the lower leg, and the toes of the upper foot hook behind the knee of the lower leg.</li>\n<li>The physician pulls the lower arm to rotate the torso toward the posterior TP, down to but not including the dysfunctional segment.</li>\n<li>One forearm rests over the iliac crest, and the other arm goes under the patient's upper arm against the rib cage; a slight springing confirms the localization.</li>\n<li>The patient breathes in, then out fully. At the end of exhalation, a rapid rotary thrust rolls the pelvis forward toward the table.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O094-7-1.png\" alt=\"Lumbar HVLA steps: monitor the posterior TP, flex hips, rotate torso, thrust\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Lumbar HVLA steps: monitor the posterior TP, flex hips, rotate torso, thrust <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O071-3.jpg\" alt=\"Myofascial techniques, then HVLA with posterior transverse process up\"></div><figcaption>Myofascial techniques, then HVLA with posterior transverse process up <small>· 2026 week 12 Lab Lumbar Spine Diagnosis &amp; HVLA, slide 3</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Order: myofascial first, then HVLA</li>\n<li>Prone QL myofascial: stand opposite, steady rib 12, lift ASIS rhythmically</li>\n<li>HVLA = direct: segment into its barrier, relative to the vertebra below</li>\n<li>e.g. L3 FRlSl → extension, right rotation, right sidebending of L3 on L4</li>\n<li>Lumbar roll: lateral recumbent, posterior TP up</li>\n<li>Flex hips to the level, rotate torso down to the segment, thrust at end-exhalation</li>\n<li>HVLA relatively contraindicated: disc herniation, spondylolisthesis</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Treatment and evidence","sn":7,"pn":"7.1"},{"l":"OMM-W12","h":"The 1999 NEJM trial","p":"<p>One landmark trial tested osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) in low back pain (LBP). Andersson and colleagues published it in the New England Journal of Medicine (NEJM) in 1999 as “A comparison of osteopathic spinal manipulation with standard care for patients with low back pain”.</p>\n<h4>How the trial was set up</h4>\n<p>The patients had <b>subacute</b> LBP, meaning pain that had lasted 3 weeks to 6 months, and 155 completed the study. The trial excluded patients whose pain might have another cause or who could not be treated safely: nerve-root compression (dermatomal pain or a neurologic deficit), systemic inflammatory disease, scoliosis, cancer, a recent heart attack, pregnancy, alcohol or drug abuse, active litigation, and manipulation in the previous three weeks.</p>\n<p>The control group received standard care: medications (analgesics including opioids, anti-inflammatory drugs and muscle relaxants) and physical therapy. The OMT group received <b>8 individualized OMT sessions over 12 weeks</b>.</p>\n<h4>What it found</h4>\n<p>Both groups improved, and they did not differ significantly in the main outcomes: pain scales, disability questionnaires, range of motion (ROM) and straight leg raising (SLR). More than 90% of patients in each group were satisfied with their care. The difference lay in what it took to get there. The OMT group used <b>significantly less medication</b> and less physical therapy.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-171-1.png\" alt=\"Abstract of the 1999 NEJM trial of OMT versus standard care\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Abstract of the 1999 NEJM trial of OMT versus standard care <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 171</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why it matters</h4>\n<p>Equal results with less medication mean fewer drug harms. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) cause about 103,000 hospitalizations and 16,500 deaths a year in the United States. They triple the risk of acute renal failure, and long-term use damages the small intestine.</p>\n<p>OMT is also being studied as a response to the opioid epidemic. Early studies of OMT in patients with chronic LBP who use opioids show initial promise. A two-year study funded by the American Osteopathic Association is testing it, and more research is needed.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-206-2.png\" alt=\"NSAID harms: hospitalizations, deaths and renal failure risk\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>NSAID harms: hospitalizations, deaths and renal failure risk <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 206</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>NEJM 1999 (Andersson): OMT vs standard care for subacute LBP (3 weeks–6 months)</li>\n<li>155 completed; nerve-root compression, cancer, pregnancy and others excluded</li>\n<li>Standard care: analgesics incl. opioids, NSAIDs, muscle relaxants, physical therapy</li>\n<li>OMT: 8 individualized sessions over 12 weeks</li>\n<li>Same outcomes (pain, disability, ROM, SLR); &gt;90% satisfied in both groups</li>\n<li>OMT group: significantly less medication and physical therapy</li>\n<li>NSAIDs: ~103,000 hospitalizations, 16,500 deaths/year; 3× acute renal failure</li>\n<li>Early promise for OMT in chronic LBP with opioid use</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Treatment and evidence","sn":7,"pn":"7.2"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Diaphragm: attachments, nerves and breathing","p":"<p>Four muscles tie the lumbar spine to the ribs, pelvis and hip: the diaphragm, the quadratus lumborum (QL), the psoas and the piriformis. When one of them is tight, it bends the lumbar spine in a predictable way. The diaphragm comes first, because its back end is anchored on the same upper lumbar vertebrae that the psoas and QL pull on.</p>\n<h4>Where the diaphragm attaches</h4>\n<p>The diaphragm is a dome of muscle between the chest and the abdomen. Around its rim it attaches to the <b>lower ribs</b> and their cartilages and to the back of the <b>sternum</b> at the xiphoid process. At the back it has two tendinous legs, the <b>crura</b> (one leg is a crus). They run down the front of the lumbar spine and anchor on the bodies of <b>L1, L2 and L3</b>, the first three lumbar vertebrae. The right crus is longer and reaches L3; the left one stops at L2. So the crura reach neither as high as T10–T12 nor as low as L4–L5.</p>\n<p>Beside the crura, the back edge of the diaphragm forms two arches, the medial and lateral <b>arcuate ligaments</b>. The psoas passes under the medial arch and the QL under the lateral arch, so both muscles run behind the diaphragm. The sympathetic trunk also runs under the medial arch. Two similar sets of attachments belong to other muscles. The bodies, discs and transverse processes of T12–L5 are where the psoas arises. Rib 12 and the transverse processes of L1–L4 are where the QL attaches.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-12-1.png\" alt=\"Diaphragm from below: crura on the lumbar bodies, arches over psoas and QL\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Diaphragm from below: crura on the lumbar bodies, arches over psoas and QL <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Its nerves</h4>\n<p>The <b>phrenic nerve</b> is the only motor nerve of the diaphragm. It forms from the ventral rami of <b>C3, C4 and C5</b>, the third to fifth cervical nerves, and runs down the neck on the anterior scalene. The mnemonic is “C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive.” The phrenic nerve also carries sensation from the central part of the dome. The outer rim sends its sensation through the lower <b>intercostal nerves (T5–T11)</b> and the <b>subcostal nerve (T12)</b>. These nerves carry no motor supply to the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>Three neighbors cause confusion. The C5–C7 roots form the long thoracic nerve, which supplies the serratus anterior. The vagus nerve passes through the diaphragm with the esophagus, but it neither moves the diaphragm nor carries its sensation. The crura sit on L1–L3, yet no lumbar nerve supplies the diaphragm.</p>\n<h4>Breathing moves the spine</h4>\n<p>Each breath changes the spinal curves. On <b>inhalation</b> the curves straighten: the thoracic kyphosis and the lumbar lordosis both decrease, and the sacral base moves posteriorly. On <b>exhalation</b> the curves deepen again. The lumbar curve therefore moves with every breath.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-45-1.png\" alt=\"Spinal curves straighten on inhalation and deepen on exhalation\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Spinal curves straighten on inhalation and deepen on exhalation <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why this matters for the low back</h4>\n<p>The link runs both ways. Upper lumbar somatic dysfunction (SD), meaning a segment with restricted, altered motion, pulls on the crura and can flatten the dome, so each breath works less well. A tight psoas or QL shortens the diaphragm and weakens its contraction, so breathing is restricted. A diaphragm in spasm, in turn, restricts lumbar motion. Posture adds to this. Slumped sitting with slack abdominal muscles crowds the diaphragm from below, and that posture soon becomes a habit.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Diaphragm: lower ribs + xiphoid; crura on L1–L3 bodies (right to L3, left to L2)</li>\n<li>Psoas under medial arcuate ligament; QL under lateral; sympathetic trunk under medial</li>\n<li>Phrenic C3–C5: all motor + central sensation (“C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive”)</li>\n<li>Rim sensation only: intercostals T5–T11 + subcostal T12</li>\n<li>Not the diaphragm's nerves: long thoracic (C5–C7), vagus, lumbar nerves</li>\n<li>Inhalation → curves straighten, lordosis ↓, sacral base posterior; exhalation → curves deepen</li>\n<li>Upper lumbar SD flattens diaphragm; tight psoas/QL weakens it; diaphragm spasm limits lumbar motion</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The diaphragm and the lumbar spine","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Attachments and actions","p":"<p>The quadratus lumborum (QL) is the deepest muscle of the posterior abdominal wall. It is a flat, four-sided sheet on each side of the lumbar spine that fills the space between the lowest rib and the pelvis.</p>\n<h4>Its two ends</h4>\n<p>The lower end is the origin: the <b>posterior iliac crest</b> (its inner lip) and the <b>iliolumbar ligament</b>. The upper end, the insertion, is the lower border of the <b>12th rib</b> and the <b>transverse processes of L1–L4</b>, the bony side arms of the upper four lumbar vertebrae.</p>\n<p>The <b>iliolumbar ligament</b>, the QL's lower anchor, runs from the transverse processes of L4 and L5 to the iliac crest. It steadies the lumbosacral junction and limits forward and rotary motion of L5. It is often strained in lifting injuries, and it is the first ligament to become tender when the lumbosacral area decompensates under postural strain (Week 12 covers it in detail).</p>\n<p>The anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS), the bony point at the front of the pelvis, is not a QL attachment. The sartorius and part of the iliacus attach there.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-121-1.png\" alt=\"Posterior abdominal wall: QL lateral to psoas, below the 12th rib\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Posterior abdominal wall: QL lateral to psoas, below the 12th rib <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 121</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where to feel it</h4>\n<p>With the patient prone, press into the soft gap <b>between the 12th rib and the posterior iliac crest</b>, lateral to the thick erector spinae mass. The QL lies deep there. Its trigger points (TrPs) sit along the <b>lateral edge</b> of the muscle. Other muscles fill the nearby spaces. The piriformis runs from the lateral sacrum to the greater trochanter. The psoas runs from the upper lumbar transverse processes and vertebral bodies to the lesser trochanter. The gluteus medius and tensor fasciae latae run from below the iliac crest to the lateral thigh.</p>\n<h4>What it does</h4>\n<p>A muscle pulls its two ends toward each other. When <b>one QL</b> contracts, it draws rib 12 and the iliac crest of that side together. If the pelvis is fixed, this <b>side-bends</b> the lumbar spine toward the contracting side. If the trunk is fixed, it lifts that half of the pelvis toward the ribs, which is called <b>hip hiking</b>. When <b>both</b> QLs contract together, their side pulls cancel and the lumbar spine <b>extends</b>. The QL also holds rib 12 down, which gives the diaphragm a firm base when it contracts.</p>\n<p>Because it sits on each side of the spine like a guy-wire, the QL steadies the low back and the lumbosacral junction during movement. It is the key <b>side-to-side (lateral) stabilizer</b> of the lumbar spine. Its neighbors have other jobs. The erector spinae extend the spine and control front-to-back motion. The iliopsoas flexes the hip and flexes the lumbar spine on the pelvis. The rotatores are tiny rotators between single vertebrae. The piriformis externally rotates the hip, and the hamstrings flex the knee. The QL crosses neither the hip nor the knee, so it cannot do either of those.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O074-9.jpg\" alt=\"QL origin, insertion and actions, with its referral zones\"></div><figcaption>QL origin, insertion and actions, with its referral zones <small>· 2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>QL: posterior iliac crest + iliolumbar ligament → rib 12 + L1–L4 transverse processes</li>\n<li>Origin = lower end (crest, ligament); insertion = upper end (rib 12, transverse processes)</li>\n<li>Iliolumbar ligament: L4–L5 transverse processes → crest; first tender in lumbosacral decompensation</li>\n<li>Palpate between rib 12 and posterior crest, lateral to erector spinae; TrPs at lateral edge</li>\n<li>ASIS = sartorius/iliacus, not QL</li>\n<li>One side → side-bends toward itself + hikes hip; both sides → lumbar extension</li>\n<li>Holds rib 12 down; key side-to-side lumbar stabilizer</li>\n<li>Erector spinae extend; iliopsoas flexes hip; piriformis externally rotates; hamstrings flex knee</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Quadratus lumborum","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"The tight QL: posture, short leg, referral","p":"<p>Quadratus lumborum (QL) problems are extremely common in people with low back pain. Janet Travell, who mapped trigger points (TrPs), called QL TrPs the most overlooked muscle cause of low back pain.</p>\n<h4>How QL trouble starts</h4>\n<p>There are two routes. The active route is <b>overload</b>: lifting or reaching while bent and twisted strains the muscle. The passive route is <b>prolonged malposition</b>: hours of slumped sitting, or a night lying twisted on a sagging couch. In the slumped position the QL is held on constant stretch. A muscle kept on stretch becomes <b>weak</b>, then <b>painful</b>, and sometimes goes into <b>spasm</b>, and TrPs form in it.</p>\n<h4>What a tight right QL does</h4>\n<p>Follow one example, a hypertonic right QL. It pulls the right 12th rib and the right iliac crest toward each other, so the lumbar spine is held <b>side-bent right</b>. Several segments bend together, which makes it a <b>Fryette type I group curve</b>. In type I mechanics (neutral spine, a group of segments) the vertebrae rotate opposite the side-bending, toward the convex side. The curve is concave right and convex left and is written L1–L5 N Sr Rl: neutral, side-bent right, rotated left. A tight left QL gives the mirror image, L1–L5 N Sl Rr. A single flexed segment, such as L2 flexed and side-bent left, is the psoas pattern.</p>\n<p>Motion testing follows from the same pull. Bending right moves toward the short muscle, so it stays free. Bending <b>left</b> would stretch the muscle, so <b>left side-bending is decreased</b>, and flexion may be limited too. A tight muscle never increases range.</p>\n<p>The pelvis changes as well. The QL <b>hikes the right innominate</b> (hip bone) upward, so in supine the right leg <b>looks short</b>. This is a functional short leg; the bones are equal. Gentle traction on both legs in supine meets <b>restriction on the right</b>, because traction tries to lengthen the tight QL. The QL also pulls the <b>right 12th rib inferiorly</b> and holds it there, so <b>breathing is restricted</b>. For the caliper ribs 11 and 12, moving down is the inhalation motion; in rib muscle energy the QL is the muscle that pulls rib 12 into inhalation. A tight QL therefore holds rib 12 in an <b>inhalation somatic dysfunction (SD)</b> on its own side. Standing, the trunk leans toward the tight side, so the right shoulder and scapula sit lower.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O100-136-1.png\" alt=\"QL spasm: hiked innominate, unlevel pelvis, trunk lean, short leg on spasm side\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>QL spasm: hiked innominate, unlevel pelvis, trunk lean, short leg on spasm side <small>· Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 136</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where QL trigger points send pain</h4>\n<p>A TrP refers pain to a fixed zone, so the pain map points back to the muscle. QL TrPs refer along the <b>iliac crest</b>, to the lateral hip and <b>greater trochanter</b>, to the sacroiliac (SI) region and lower buttock, and they can wrap around toward the <b>groin</b>. Their pain does not go to the umbilicus. The neighbors refer elsewhere. Psoas TrPs refer to a vertical strip beside the lumbar spine and the front of the thigh. Piriformis TrPs refer to the sacrum and buttock and spread down the back of the thigh. Gluteus medius TrPs refer to the posterior iliac crest, sacrum, buttock and upper lateral thigh, but not to the groin.</p>\n<p>No gluteal muscle hikes the pelvis; gluteal TrPs cause buttock and trochanter pain but leave leg length alone. A tight piriformis changes apparent leg length only indirectly, through a sacral torsion, and its spasm belongs to psoas syndrome (section 4).</p>\n<h4>A true short leg can hide behind the QL</h4>\n<p>Picture a true left short leg: the sacral base is low on the left and the lumbar spine curves convex left. A right QL spasm then hikes the right innominate, deepens the left convexity and makes the right leg look short, which confuses the exam. Treat the QL first. The right innominate drops and the true left short leg appears. Once the patient stands again, the left convexity returns unless a heel lift is used. Suspect QL TrPs when 12th rib and SI somatic dysfunctions (SDs) keep coming back, or when osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) does not help.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O105-23-1.png\" alt=\"Diagnosis of QL: observation, palpation, motion and special tests\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Diagnosis of QL: observation, palpation, motion and special tests <small>· Fall 2021 lumbar review (SGA drive), slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Causes: overload (lifting twisted) or prolonged malposition (slumped sitting, twisted on a couch)</li>\n<li>Constant stretch → weakness, pain, spasm, TrPs</li>\n<li>Tight right QL → type I curve L1–L5 N Sr Rl, concave right</li>\n<li>Left side-bending ↓ (± flexion ↓); right side-bending free</li>\n<li>Hiked innominate → same-side functional short leg; leg traction restricted that side</li>\n<li>Rib 12 held inferior = inhalation SD → breathing restricted; shoulder, scapula low</li>\n<li>QL TrPs → iliac crest, trochanter, SI region, lower buttock, groin</li>\n<li>Psoas TrPs → paraspinal strip + front of thigh; piriformis → buttock, back of thigh</li>\n<li>Treat QL first to unmask a true short leg; recurrent rib 12/SI SD → think QL</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Quadratus lumborum","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"The hip drop test","p":"<p>The hip drop test is a standing screening test of how well the lumbar and thoracolumbar spine side-bends. It is one of the two special tests for a tight quadratus lumborum (QL); the other is gentle leg traction in supine.</p>\n<h4>How it is done</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient stands straight. You rest your fingers on the top and side of both iliac crests, so you can judge whether the pelvis is level.</li>\n<li>The patient bends one knee without lifting that heel and puts all the weight on the other leg.</li>\n<li>The pelvis on the bent-knee side no longer bears weight, so it drops. The test is named for that side: bending the right knee is the right hip drop test.</li>\n<li>As that hip drops, the lumbar spine side-bends the other way, toward the weight-bearing leg, in a smooth curve that is convex on the dropped side.</li>\n<li>You repeat the test on the other side and compare.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-105-1.png\" alt=\"Hip drop test: one knee bends, that hip drops, lumbar spine side-bends away\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Hip drop test: one knee bends, that hip drops, lumbar spine side-bends away <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 105</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Reading the result</h4>\n<p>In a normal (negative) test the iliac crest drops smoothly by about <b>20–25°</b>, roughly an inch, and the lumbar curve is smooth. The test is positive when the crest drops less than 20°, drops more than 25°, or the lumbar curve is not smooth. When the curve is not smooth, find the level of the sharp angle or of the flat segment. The usual positive finding is a crest that drops too little. It points to lumbar or thoracolumbar somatic dysfunction (SD), such as a restricted joint or a hypertonic muscle, on the side being tested.</p>\n<p>Think through what the right hip drop asks of the spine. Dropping the right hip side-bends the lumbar spine to the <b>left</b>. A positive right hip drop therefore means that left side-bending is restricted, so the spine side-bends <b>freely to the right</b>. A neutral group curve held side-bent right, L1–L5 N Sr Rl (neutral, side-bent right, rotated left), gives a positive right hip drop. A curve held side-bent left, such as L2–L5 N Sl Rr, gives a positive left hip drop.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-107-1.png\" alt=\"Group curve side-bent right holds the right crest up: positive right hip drop\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Group curve side-bent right holds the right crest up: positive right hip drop <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 107</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The tight QL and its hip drop</h4>\n<p>A tight right QL holds the right iliac crest up toward rib 12 and keeps the lumbar spine side-bent right. When the patient bends the right knee, the right hip <b>stays up</b> or drops only a little, perhaps 10°: a positive right hip drop. The left hip still drops a normal 20–25°, because side-bending right is free. The curve is concave right and convex left. The same hiked innominate makes the right leg look short, and right QL trigger points may refer pain to the right greater trochanter.</p>\n<p>So everything lines up on one side: the tight QL, the positive hip drop, the short-looking leg and the concave side of the curve. A single flexed or extended L5 segment, such as L5 F Rr Sr (flexed, rotated and side-bent right), does not hike the innominate. Other problems can also make the test unequal; a disc herniation with a protective sideways curve is one (Week 14).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-127-1.png\" alt=\"Tight left QL holds the left ilium up: left hip drop is restricted\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Tight left QL holds the left ilium up: left hip drop is restricted <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 127</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Which test for which muscle</h4>\n<p>Each lumbar muscle problem has its own special test.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Test</th><th>Checks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Hip drop; leg traction in supine</td><td>QL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thomas test</td><td>Tight iliopsoas (4.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Straight leg raise (SLR)</td><td>Nerve root irritation; painful throughout in piriformis syndrome (5.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trendelenburg test</td><td>Weak hip abductors (gluteus medius): in single-leg stance the opposite hip sags</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Bend one knee → that crest should drop 20–25°; spine side-bends away, smooth curve</li>\n<li>Positive: drop under 20°, over 25°, or curve not smooth → SD on tested side</li>\n<li>+ right hip drop → left side-bending restricted; spine side-bends freely right</li>\n<li>L1–L5 N Sr Rl → + right hip drop; L2–L5 N Sl Rr → + left</li>\n<li>Tight QL = + hip drop, short-looking leg and concavity, all on one side</li>\n<li>Single L5 type II lesion does not hike the innominate</li>\n<li>Tests: hip drop/traction = QL; Thomas = psoas; SLR = root/piriformis; Trendelenburg = gluteus medius</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Quadratus lumborum","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Treating the QL","p":"<p>The plan for quadratus lumborum (QL) hypertonicity treats three things: its trigger points (TrPs), for which spray and stretch is one option (6.3); the 12th rib it pulls down; and the muscle itself. The 12th rib technique and passive myofascial release of the QL are both gentle enough for the acute stage. Muscle energy is also used for the QL.</p>\n<h4>How muscle energy works</h4>\n<p>Muscle energy (ME) is a direct, active technique. Direct means you move the tissue into its <b>barrier</b>, the point where the tight muscle will not lengthen any further. Active means the patient does the work. You hold the muscle at its barrier while the patient gently contracts it against your equal counterforce for 3–5 seconds, so nothing moves. The patient then relaxes. For a moment the muscle lets go a little more, and you take up the new slack to the next barrier. You repeat this about 3 times and finish with a passive stretch.</p>\n<p>The recipe is the same for any muscle: put it in its stretched position, then ask it to do its own action. The QL joins rib 12 to the iliac crest, so its barrier is a <b>longitudinal stretch</b> that pulls the two apart. Its own action brings them back together, so the patient <b>hikes the hip toward the head</b>, or tries to bring hip and shoulder together.</p>\n<h4>Two versions of QL muscle energy</h4>\n<p>In the <b>side-lying</b> version, the patient lies on the side with the <b>tight QL facing up</b>. For a tight right QL the patient lies on the left side (left lateral recumbent). The hips and knees are bent about 90°, set up like the thoracolumbar bidirectional stretch. Your forearms rest on the iliac crest and on the lower ribs or shoulder and spread them apart to the barrier. The patient then tries to bring the hip and shoulder together against you. The tight side must face up; pressed against the table, it cannot be lengthened.</p>\n<p>In the <b>supine</b> version you hold the ankle on the tight side, abduct the leg slightly and pull gentle traction to the barrier. The patient hikes that hip toward the head against your hold. Both versions use 3 contractions and a final passive stretch.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-124-1.png\" alt=\"Side-lying QL muscle energy: tight side up, hands on hip and ribs\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Side-lying QL muscle energy: tight side up, hands on hip and ribs <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 124</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Passive myofascial release, prone</h4>\n<p>Here the patient does not contract at all, so it is not muscle energy. The patient lies prone and you stand on the side opposite the tight QL. Your cephalad hand (the one toward the head) fixes the 12th rib, the QL's upper attachment. Your caudad hand (toward the feet) grasps the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and lifts it toward you, which stretches the QL. You repeat this in slow, rhythmic motions and reassess the QL's tone at the end.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O078-9.jpg\" alt=\"Passive myofascial release of the QL, prone\"></div><figcaption>Passive myofascial release of the QL, prone <small>· 2026 week 13 Lab Lumbar Myofascial Psoas &amp; Quadratus, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Telling the muscle energy setups apart</h4>\n<p>Each setup follows the muscle's barrier.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Position</th><th>Barrier</th><th>Patient pushes toward</th></tr>\n<tr><td>QL</td><td>Side-lying, tight side up (or supine with leg traction)</td><td>Longitudinal stretch</td><td>Hip toward the head</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Psoas</td><td>Supine, other knee to the chest, affected leg hanging off the table edge</td><td>Hip extension</td><td>Hip flexion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Piriformis</td><td>Prone, knee bent 90°, foot swung outward</td><td>Hip internal rotation</td><td>External rotation</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Other efforts belong to other muscles: pushing the hip backward is hip extension (gluteus maximus), lifting the top leg is hip abduction (gluteus medius), and pressing the leg into your pelvis uses other hip muscles. None brings rib 12 toward the crest.</p>\n<h4>Managing the patient</h4>\n<p>First rule out anything more serious. Red flags for low back pain include trauma, a history of cancer or steroid use, first onset after age 50, fever or weight loss, and bladder or bowel loss with saddle numbness, which signals cauda equina syndrome and needs the emergency department. Without red flags no imaging is needed. Management is in-office osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) plus a home program of stretching and self-care, and it needs no medication. A corticosteroid injection is not first-line care for a tight muscle.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Treat QL TrPs, rib 12 and muscle; rib 12 technique + passive myofascial suit acute stage</li>\n<li>ME: patient contracts 3–5 s against you at barrier; relax, new barrier; ×3; stretch</li>\n<li>QL barrier = longitudinal stretch (rib 12 away from crest)</li>\n<li>Side-lying: tight QL up (right QL → left side down); patient brings hip and shoulder together</li>\n<li>Supine: ankle held, slight abduction, traction; patient hikes hip toward head</li>\n<li>Prone passive myofascial: opposite side, hand on rib 12, rhythmic lift of ASIS; no contraction</li>\n<li>Psoas ME supine leg off table; piriformis ME prone, hip internally rotated</li>\n<li>No red flags → no imaging; in-office OMT + home stretching and self-care first</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Quadratus lumborum","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Anatomy, actions and the pull on lordosis","p":"<p>Janet Travell called the iliopsoas the “hidden prankster”: it does many vital jobs, often causes pain and is hard to reach. Two muscles, the psoas major and the iliacus, share one tendon and work as a unit, the <b>iliopsoas</b>.</p>\n<h4>The muscles</h4>\n<p>The <b>psoas major</b> arises by tendinous arches from the bodies, discs and transverse processes of <b>T12 through L5</b>. It runs down beside the lumbar spine, passes under the inguinal ligament and joins the iliacus to insert on the <b>lesser trochanter</b> of the femur, a small knob on the inner back of the upper femur. Lumbar nerves L1–L2, with some L3, supply it. The <b>psoas minor</b>, absent in about 40% of people, runs from the bodies of T12 and L1 to the pecten pubis. A branch of L1 supplies it, and it is only a weak flexor. The <b>iliacus</b> fills the iliac fossa, the inner bowl of the ilium. It also arises from the inner lip of the iliac crest, the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS), the sacroiliac and iliolumbar ligaments and the lateral sacrum. It shares the psoas tendon to the lesser trochanter and is supplied by branches of the femoral nerve.</p>\n<p>Some look-alike attachments belong elsewhere. Rib 12 and the posterior iliac crest are quadratus lumborum (QL) attachments. The greater trochanter, the big knob on the outside of the hip, takes the piriformis and gluteal muscles. There is no L7.</p>\n<p>The psoas has many neighbors, which matters for psoas syndrome (4.4). Its top lies behind the diaphragm. The kidney, ureter, renal and gonadal vessels, colon, QL, sympathetic trunk, aorta and inferior vena cava all touch it, and branches of the lumbar plexus emerge from it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O074-20.jpg\" alt=\"Psoas major beside the lumbar spine, lumbar plexus emerging\"></div><figcaption>Psoas major beside the lumbar spine, lumbar plexus emerging <small>· 2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What the iliopsoas does</h4>\n<p>Together the psoas and iliacus are the <b>strongest flexor of the thigh</b> at the hip. They add some external rotation and flex the spine on the pelvis. The iliopsoas has four main functions: <b>walking</b>; flexion of the femur, pelvis and lumbar spine; keeping <b>erect posture at the hip joints</b>; and keeping the pelvis balanced in the midline. Side-bending of the lumbar spine is not on that list. A psoas spasm does bend the trunk toward itself (section 4), but that is a dysfunction, not one of its functions. Hip abduction belongs to the gluteus medius and minimus. Knee flexion belongs to the hamstrings; the psoas does not cross the knee.</p>\n<h4>Which end moves</h4>\n<p>A muscle pulls its two ends together, and the free end is the one that moves. Lying supine, the spine is fixed, so the insertion moves toward the origin: the psoas lifts the femur (hip flexion). Standing, the femur is fixed, so the origin moves toward the insertion. A short psoas then pulls the lumbar vertebrae down and forward. The lumbar spine goes into <b>extension, with increased lordosis</b>. The lumbar spine, sacrum and pelvis are linked, so the <b>sacral base moves forward</b> and the pelvis <b>rotates anteriorly</b>. A hypertonic psoas is one of the major causes of increased lumbar lordosis; this extension is a dysfunction, not a normal function.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-61-1.png\" alt=\"Femur fixed: a short psoas pulls the lumbar spine into lordosis\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Femur fixed: a short psoas pulls the lumbar spine into lordosis <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 61</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Other muscle groups change the lordosis too. Whatever pulls the spine into extension or tips the pelvis forward deepens the curve, and whatever pulls the pelvis backward flattens it.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Muscle group</th><th>Effect on the lumbar lordosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Psoas, hypertonic</td><td><b>Increases</b> it by pulling directly on the vertebrae: a major cause</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quadriceps, hypertonic</td><td>Increases it only indirectly, by tipping the pelvis; never attaches to the spine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gluteus maximus, hamstrings</td><td>Pull the pelvis posteriorly; decrease it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Abdominals</td><td>Flatten it; weak ones only allow it to increase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Erector spinae</td><td>Extend the spine; weak ones reduce extension</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-39-1.png\" alt=\"How the lumbar muscle groups change the lordosis\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>How the lumbar muscle groups change the lordosis <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Psoas major: T12–L5 bodies, discs, transverse processes → lesser trochanter; L1–L2 (some L3)</li>\n<li>Psoas minor (absent 40%): T12–L1 → pecten pubis; iliacus: iliac fossa → lesser trochanter</li>\n<li>Not psoas: greater trochanter, rib 12/posterior crest (QL), L7 (doesn't exist)</li>\n<li>Neighbors: kidney, ureter, colon, sympathetic trunk; lumbar plexus emerges from psoas</li>\n<li>Iliopsoas = strongest hip flexor; some external rotation; flexes spine on pelvis</li>\n<li>Four functions: walking, flexion, erect posture at hips, pelvis balanced; not side-bending</li>\n<li>Standing, femur fixed: short psoas → lumbar extension (↑ lordosis), sacral base forward, pelvis anterior</li>\n<li>Hypertonic psoas = major direct cause of ↑ lordosis; quadriceps only indirect; hamstrings flatten</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The iliopsoas","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Cause, pain and posture","p":"<p><b>Psoas syndrome</b> is a painful low back condition caused by hypertonicity of the psoas, with a typical cluster of related signs and symptoms.</p>\n<h4>How it starts</h4>\n<p>The usual story is a long flexion stress followed by a sudden stretch. First the psoas is held <b>short for a long time</b>: sitting with the pelvis tucked under, hours of desk work, bending over from the waist, weeding or raking, or repeated sit-ups. Overuse of hip flexion can also set it off. Then the muscle is <b>suddenly lengthened</b>, for example by standing up quickly or nearly falling backward. What happens next is a reflex chain.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The muscle spindles, the stretch sensors inside the muscle, signal “overstretch” to the central nervous system (CNS).</li>\n<li>The CNS answers with a reflex contraction: alpha motor output to the muscle rises, and gamma firing, which sets spindle sensitivity, rises too.</li>\n<li>Contraction causes pain, and pain causes more contraction. This positive feedback loop is the pain–spasm cycle.</li>\n<li>The lasting spasm produces the syndrome. This neurological imbalance also leads to psoas hypertrophy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Rising slowly from a flexed position avoids the sudden stretch.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-199-1.png\" alt=\"Flexed positions that shorten the psoas: tucked sitting, desk work, slouching\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Flexed positions that shorten the psoas: tucked sitting, desk work, slouching <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 199</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The pain</h4>\n<p>The typical pain is <b>low back pain</b> with pain on the <b>lateral or anterior thigh that stops at the knee</b>. It is low back pain, not upper back pain. Psoas trigger points (TrPs), tight tender spots that send pain to a distant zone, refer to a vertical strip beside the lumbar spine and down the front of the thigh. One psoas TrP is reached by deep pressure through the abdominal wall <b>beside the umbilicus</b>, just lateral to the rectus abdominis. Others lie in the iliacus inside the iliac crest and in the upper inner thigh near the insertion. Rib 12, the iliotibial band and the suprapubic area are not psoas TrP sites. Multifidus TrPs, by contrast, stay close to the spine and sacrum. Once the pain settles, the home exercise is a psoas stretch (4.5).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-76-1.png\" alt=\"Psoas trigger points and referral: low back strip and front of thigh\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Psoas trigger points and referral: low back strip and front of thigh <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 76</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What the exam shows</h4>\n<p>The patient stands and walks <b>flexed at the hip and side-bent toward</b> the tighter psoas and cannot stand up straight. The gait limps, bent forward and listing to that side. The tight psoas lets the hip flex but holds it back from extending, so range of motion shows <b>increased hip flexion and decreased hip extension</b>; resisted or limited extension on one side points to that psoas. Palpation finds the muscle hypertonic. The typical somatic dysfunctions (SDs) are <b>flexion of the upper lumbar segments (L1, L2)</b>, an <b>extended L5</b>, and variable sacral and innominate dysfunctions. Tender points sit in the <b>same-side iliacus</b>, about 1 cm medial to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS), and in the <b>opposite piriformis</b>. The pelvis shifts to the opposite side (4.3). The <b>neurologic exam is normal</b>. The special tests are the <b>Thomas test</b> (4.2), the straight leg raise (SLR) and the well-leg raise.</p>\n<p>The normal neuro exam separates psoas syndrome from nerve root compression; an S1 root lesion weakens the ankle reflex and numbs the lateral foot. Other conditions look different. Piriformis syndrome gives buttock pain down the back of the thigh, worse with sitting. A tight quadratus lumborum (QL) gives crest, hip and buttock pain with a short-looking leg. Iliotibial (IT) band syndrome gives lateral thigh and knee pain in runners, without back pain. The sciatic nerve runs through the buttock, not under the inguinal ligament. Spondylolysis is a crack in the bony pars, usually at L5, with no tender point beside the ASIS.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Psoas syndrome = low back pain from psoas hypertonicity + typical sign cluster</li>\n<li>Psoas held short long (desk, tucked sitting, weeding, raking, sit-ups) → sudden stretch</li>\n<li>Spindles → reflex contraction (alpha + gamma ↑) → pain–spasm cycle</li>\n<li>Pain: low back + lateral/anterior thigh, not past knee; TrP beside umbilicus</li>\n<li>Posture: flexed, side-bent toward psoas; hip flexion ↑, extension ↓</li>\n<li>SD: L1–L2 flexed, L5 extended, variable sacrum/innominate</li>\n<li>Tender points: same-side iliacus (1 cm medial to ASIS), opposite piriformis</li>\n<li>Neuro normal; tests: Thomas, SLR, well-leg raise</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Psoas syndrome","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Thomas test and the L1–L2 key lesion","p":"<p>Two findings anchor the diagnosis of psoas syndrome: a positive Thomas test on the psoas side and the key upper lumbar lesion.</p>\n<h4>The Thomas test</h4>\n<p>The Thomas test checks for a contraction or contracture (fixed shortening) of the iliopsoas. It works by flattening the lumbar spine so that a tight psoas can only show itself by lifting the thigh.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient lies supine, clasps both hands behind one knee and pulls that thigh toward the chest while you assist.</li>\n<li>Full flexion of one hip flattens the lumbar lordosis and fixes the pelvis, so the other psoas can no longer hide its tightness by arching the back.</li>\n<li>You watch the <b>other</b> leg, the one lying straight, because it is the side being tested.</li>\n<li>Normally the straight thigh stays down and the back of the knee touches the table, which is a negative test.</li>\n<li>If the straight thigh lifts and leaves a gap under the knee (the popliteal fossa), that iliopsoas is tight, which is a positive test.</li>\n<li>You test both sides, because unequal gaps mean unequal spasm.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other tight muscles look different. Tight hamstrings limit the straight leg raise but do not lift the thigh off the table. A tight quadratus lumborum (QL) shows on the hip drop test and leg traction. A tight piriformis turns the leg outward. Several tests have similar names: the Thompson test squeezes the calf for an Achilles tendon rupture, the Spurling test compresses the neck for a cervical nerve root, and the Phalen test flexes the wrist for carpal tunnel syndrome.</p>\n<h4>The key lesion</h4>\n<p>The psoas arises from T12–L5. In spasm it pulls the upper lumbar spine forward, into flexion, and bends and turns it toward its own side. The result is the <b>key lesion</b> of psoas syndrome. It is a <b>non-neutral (Fryette type II) flexion somatic dysfunction (SD) at L1 or L2</b>, sometimes T12 or L3, <b>side-bent and rotated toward the psoas spasm</b>. L5 is involved too, but it is <b>extended</b> and secondary. L3 and L4 are not the key levels.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-56-1.png\" alt=\"Psoas syndrome exam: upper lumbar flexion, L5 extended, opposite piriformis tender points\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Psoas syndrome exam: upper lumbar flexion, L5 extended, opposite piriformis tender points <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Naming a type II lesion</h4>\n<p>Fryette type II mechanics apply to one segment that is flexed or extended, and its rotation and side-bending go to the <b>same side</b>. The name lists the motion first, then rotation, then side-bending. Rotation is named for the side of the posterior transverse process. Take a left psoas. L1 is flexed, rotated left and side-bent left: L1 FRSL, also written L1 F Rl Sl. A right psoas gives L1 or L2 F Rr Sr. So an extended upper segment (ERS) or a flexed one turned away from the psoas does not fit. A neutral group curve (type I: several segments, rotation opposite the side-bending, such as L1–L5 N Sl Rr) is the QL pattern, not the psoas pattern.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-114-1.png\" alt=\"L1–L2 side-bent and rotated to the psoas side; piriformis and shift opposite\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>L1–L2 side-bent and rotated to the psoas side; piriformis and shift opposite <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 114</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Sorting the findings by side</h4>\n<p>A simple rule sorts all the findings: what the psoas pulls directly is on its own side, and the compensations below it are on the other side.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Same side as the psoas spasm</th><th>Opposite side (4.3)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Flexed, side-bent posture; Thomas test positive; L1–L2 rotated and side-bent; iliacus tender point; hip extension decreased</td><td>Piriformis spasm and tender point; pelvic shift; buttock and back-of-thigh pain</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Thomas test: hug one knee → lordosis flat; watch the straight leg</li>\n<li>Thigh lifts, gap under knee → that iliopsoas tight (positive); test both sides</li>\n<li>Not Thompson (Achilles), Spurling (cervical root), Phalen (carpal tunnel)</li>\n<li>Key lesion: type II flexion SD at L1 or L2 (sometimes T12/L3)</li>\n<li>Rotated + side-bent toward the psoas: left psoas → L1 FRSL; right → F Rr Sr</li>\n<li>L5 extended and secondary; L3–L4 not key; group curve = QL pattern</li>\n<li>Psoas side: posture, Thomas, L1–L2, iliacus; opposite: piriformis, shift, leg pain</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Psoas syndrome","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"The opposite side: piriformis and pelvic shift","p":"<p>Psoas syndrome does not stay in the low back. It spreads step by step, downward and across to the other side.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It starts as somatic dysfunction (SD) with spasm of both psoas muscles.</li>\n<li>One side becomes dominant, and the sacrum twists into a sacral torsion.</li>\n<li>The piriformis on the opposite side goes into spasm.</li>\n<li>The sciatic nerve under that piriformis becomes irritated, which causes sciatica.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Full-blown left psoas syndrome</h4>\n<p>A full-blown left psoas syndrome brings six findings together. On the left, the psoas side, there is the key non-neutral SD at L1 or L2, side-bent left, and marked left psoas spasm with a positive Thomas test. The sacrum rotates on the left oblique axis, often forward. On the right there is a right pelvic shift, a right piriformis spasm with its tender point, and pain in the right hip running down the back of the thigh to the knee.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-57-1.png\" alt=\"Full-blown left psoas syndrome: the six numbered findings\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Full-blown left psoas syndrome: the six numbered findings <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 57</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How each finding arises</h4>\n<p>Start with the lumbar curve. The spine side-bends toward the tight psoas, so it is <b>concave on the psoas side and convex on the other</b>. A left psoas therefore gives a convexity facing right.</p>\n<p>The pelvis is the unstable base under that bent spine. With the spine bent one way, the pelvis slides the other way, away from the psoas: a <b>pelvic shift</b> to the opposite side. A shift, like any SD, is named for the direction it moves freely. The pelvic side-shift test is therefore positive on the side opposite the psoas, so a left psoas gives a right pelvic shift and a positive right side-shift test.</p>\n<p>Then the <b>opposite piriformis</b> goes into spasm. A tight piriformis holds its hip turned out, so the exam finds piriformis tenderness with <b>restricted internal rotation</b> of that opposite hip. The spasm can also irritate the sciatic nerve that runs beneath it and cause pain from the hip down the back of the thigh to the knee on that side. The leg pain of psoas syndrome therefore sits opposite the psoas, while any front-of-thigh pain from the psoas itself is on the psoas side. The psoas's own pain begins in the midline low back. It may spread to the sacroiliac (SI) and gluteal region, or to the hip on the side of the shift.</p>\n<h4>What does not belong</h4>\n<p>Some findings look plausible but are not part of the pattern. L5 is extended, never flexed; the flexed levels are L1–L2. The sacrum rotates forward on an oblique axis, so a backward-rotated or posterior sacrum and a sacral shear do not fit. An elevated iliac crest on one side is a QL sign, not a psoas sign. A neutral group curve such as L1–L5 N Sl Rr (neutral, side-bent left, rotated right) is also the QL pattern. A piriformis spasm on the psoas side is not expected; the piriformis spasm is always contralateral.</p>\n<p>Memory line: <b>psoas side</b> = posture, Thomas test, L1–L2 rotation and side-bending, iliacus tender point; <b>opposite side</b> = piriformis, pelvic shift, leg pain.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Stages: both psoas spasm → one dominant + sacral torsion → opposite piriformis → sciatica</li>\n<li>Left psoas: L1/L2 side-bent left, Thomas +, sacrum on left oblique axis (often forward)</li>\n<li>Right side: pelvic shift, piriformis spasm (internal rotation ↓), hip pain to back of knee</li>\n<li>Spine concave on psoas side, convex opposite</li>\n<li>Pelvic shift and + side-shift test opposite the psoas</li>\n<li>Psoas pain: midline low back → SI/gluteal or hip on shift side</li>\n<li>Not in pattern: flexed L5, posterior sacrum, sacral shear, high crest, group curve</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Psoas syndrome","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Organic causes: the kidney stone","p":"<p>Before you call psoas spasm mechanical, rule out disease of the organs that lie on or near the psoas. An irritated organ can make the psoas spasm through a viscerosomatic reflex (organ to muscle), and a nearby strained structure can do it through a somatosomatic reflex (body tissue to muscle).</p>\n<h4>Organic causes to rule out</h4>\n<p>The bowel is one group: disease of the sigmoid colon, diverticulosis, appendicitis, descending or sigmoid colon cancer, irritable bowel syndrome, <b>Crohn's disease</b> and ulcerative colitis. The urinary and reproductive organs are a second group: ureter disease, <b>kidney stones</b>, kidney disease or tumor, prostatitis, <b>ovarian disease</b> and <b>fibroid tumors</b> of the uterus. A third group covers other problems: metastatic cancer, lumbar disc impingement, femoral bursitis, arthritis of the hip and a psoas abscess. If an organ cause is found, it must be treated too.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-80-1.png\" alt=\"Kidney and ureter running down over the psoas major\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Kidney and ureter running down over the psoas major <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 80</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The kidney stone</h4>\n<p>The <b>ureter runs down over the front of the psoas</b>. A stone passing down the ureter irritates the psoas beneath it, so the <b>psoas on the same side</b> often goes into spasm. The link also runs the other way: psoas spasm can disturb the ureter. The patient then shows psoas signs on the side of the stone, such as a positive Thomas test and more hip flexion than extension. Over time the opposite piriformis may join in (4.3). The stone adds its own signs: severe flank or low back pain, blood in the urine, sweating and urinary urgency. The kidney also rests on the quadratus lumborum (QL), but stones are linked to the psoas.</p>\n<h4>Reading the paraspinal tissue</h4>\n<p>A sick organ sends sympathetic signals into its own spinal segments, and the paraspinal tissue at those levels changes on the same side. This is the <b>viscerosomatic reflex</b>. An acute reflex makes the tissue <b>warm and boggy</b> (moist and swollen), with a red reflex, a redness that lingers after you stroke the skin. A chronic reflex makes the tissue cool, ropy, thin and fibrotic. So the texture tells you how long the problem has lasted.</p>\n<p>The level tells you which organ. The kidneys, upper ureters and gonads send their sympathetics to about <b>T10–T11</b>, and so do the appendix, cecum and ascending colon on the right. The lower ureter, the colon from mid-transverse to rectum, the bladder, the uterus and the prostate use T12–L2. Acute changes at T10–T11 on one side therefore point to that kidney or upper ureter.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-91-1.png\" alt=\"Sympathetic levels of the viscera, T1 to L2\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Sympathetic levels of the viscera, T1 to L2 <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 91</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Other causes do not fit this picture. A urinary tract infection (UTI) brings urgency but does not cause psoas spasm. Irritable bowel is chronic, so its reflex feels ropy and cool, not warm and boggy. A lifting strain or poor posture strains muscle but makes no segmental organ reflex. Thoracic radiculitis gives band-like chest wall pain without psoas signs. A piriformis spasm is somatic and sits in the buttock.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Rule out organ causes before treating psoas spasm as mechanical</li>\n<li>Bowel: sigmoid, diverticulosis, appendicitis, colon cancer, irritable bowel, Crohn's, ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Urinary/pelvic: ureter, kidney stone or tumor, prostatitis, ovarian disease, fibroids; also abscess, hip, disc</li>\n<li>Ureter runs over psoas → stone → same-side psoas spasm (Thomas +)</li>\n<li>Stone signs: flank pain, blood in urine, sweating, urgency</li>\n<li>Acute viscerosomatic reflex = warm, boggy, red; chronic = cool, ropy, fibrotic</li>\n<li>Kidney/upper ureter T10–T11; lower ureter, bladder, pelvic organs T12–L2</li>\n<li>UTI, irritable bowel (chronic), lifting strain, thoracic radiculitis don't fit</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Psoas syndrome","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Treating psoas syndrome","p":"<p>The goal of treatment is to restore the normal lumbar front-to-back (anteroposterior, AP) curve, because long-term management depends on it. The plan has four parts: osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT), drugs in the acute phase, a list of do's and don'ts, and a home stretching program. Any organ cause must be treated as well.</p>\n<h4>Where OMT begins</h4>\n<p>Treat the <b>key lesion first</b>: the flexion somatic dysfunction (SD) of the upper lumbar spine, usually L1–L2 and sometimes T12 or L3. Correcting it may restore function and relieve the symptoms by itself. Only then treat the psoas itself with muscle energy (ME), counterstrain and direct stretching. The sacral torsion and the opposite piriformis are downstream compensations.</p>\n<p>The phase decides how hard you treat. In the <b>acute</b> phase the tissue is inflamed, so the dose is limited and you use only techniques that bring the upper lumbar spine into <b>extension</b>. These are passive extension, counterstrain, ME (especially good for acute spasm), and active and passive myofascial release of the lumbar and lumbosacral regions. High-velocity low-amplitude (<b>HVLA</b>) thrust is not tolerated at this stage, and the unstable <b>lumbosacral</b> (L5–S1) area is left alone. Ice helps an acute spasm; heat is not used at first. Drugs for the acute phase are analgesics, namely nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and acetaminophen (Tylenol), plus muscle relaxants. In the <b>chronic</b> phase treatment can be more vigorous. Forceful articulation may be needed to break fibrotic restrictions, along with ME and myofascial work, but repeated HVLA at the unstable lumbosacral junction is still avoided.</p>\n<p>The key lesion is corrected with direct or indirect techniques. For a left psoas the lesion is L1–L2 FRSl (flexed, rotated and side-bent left), and muscle energy, counterstrain or HVLA can each be aimed at it; HVLA waits until the patient can tolerate it. Quadratus lumborum techniques, such as the 12th rib technique or prone myofascial release, do not treat it. Passive extension can be as simple as a rolled towel under the thoracolumbar junction. Counterstrain is an indirect technique: you find the tender point, here the iliacus point just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS), and fold the body into its position of ease until the tenderness falls by at least 70%. You hold that about 90 seconds and return slowly to neutral.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O074-52.jpg\" alt=\"Passive extension over a roll for flexed L1–L2\"></div><figcaption>Passive extension over a roll for flexed L1–L2 <small>· 2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 52</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Psoas muscle energy</h4>\n<p>The psoas is mainly a hip flexor, so its barrier is <b>hip extension</b>. The patient lies <b>supine</b> at the edge of the table and hugs the other knee to the chest. The affected leg hangs off the table, which takes that hip into extension. You steady the bent leg and press the hanging thigh toward the floor. The patient lifts it toward the ceiling (hip flexion) against you for 3–5 seconds, 3 times, and you finish with a passive stretch. Marked hip flexion shortens the psoas, so it is the muscle's ease, not its barrier.</p>\n<h4>Home program</h4>\n<p>Home stretching starts once the condition is no longer acute and is vital to prevent recurrence. The patient does passive extension over a rolled towel 3 times a day and active psoas stretches 5–6 times a day. Supine leg lifts of 6 inches, push-ups and swimming help, and the patient stays active. Extension exercises are indicated. During a flare the patient should not sleep on the stomach, slump, bend forward to touch the toes or tie shoes, lean toward the painful side, do sit-ups, lean backward when standing, or stay inactive.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O100-33-1.jpg\" alt=\"Lancelot stretch and psoas stretch: the back hip goes into extension\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Lancelot stretch and psoas stretch: the back hip goes into extension <small>· Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A stretch moves a muscle's two ends apart, so each stretch position tells you its muscle.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stretch position</th><th>Muscle lengthened</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Kneeling or standing lunge with the back hip extended (Lancelot)</td><td>Psoas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heel on a table, knee straight, bending forward at the hips</td><td>Hamstrings (they cross the back of hip and knee)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heel pulled to the buttock with the hip extended</td><td>Quadriceps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trunk arched backward</td><td>Rectus abdominis</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Downward dog flexes the hips and stretches the hamstrings and calves. Cat-cow moves the spine, and planks strengthen the core. None of them extends the hip.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Goal: restore lumbar AP curve; treat organ cause too</li>\n<li>OMT first to key lesion: L1–L2 (T12/L3) flexion SD; then psoas (ME, counterstrain, stretch)</li>\n<li>Acute: extension-only techniques, limited dose; no HVLA, avoid L5–S1; ice not heat</li>\n<li>Acute drugs: NSAIDs, acetaminophen, muscle relaxants</li>\n<li>Key lesion by direct or indirect technique (left psoas → L1–L2 FRSl); HVLA once tolerated</li>\n<li>Chronic: articulation for fibrosis, ME, myofascial; no repeated lumbosacral HVLA</li>\n<li>Psoas ME: supine, other knee to chest, leg off table (hip extension), patient lifts up</li>\n<li>Home: towel extension 3×/day, stretches 5–6×/day, swim; flare: no sit-ups, slumping, toe touching</li>\n<li>Lancelot lunge = psoas; straight knee + hip flexion = hamstrings; heel to buttock = quadriceps</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Psoas syndrome","sn":4,"pn":"4.5"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Anatomy, actions and the sciatic nerve","p":"<p>The piriformis is a pear-shaped muscle (that is what its name means) deep in the buttock. It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen and crosses behind the hip joint.</p>\n<h4>Attachments and nerve</h4>\n<p>It arises from the <b>anterior-lateral surface of the sacrum at S2–S4</b>, at or near the sacroiliac (SI) joint capsule, the greater sciatic foramen and the sacrotuberous ligament. It inserts by a round tendon on the <b>superior-medial aspect of the greater trochanter</b>, the top inner part of the big knob on the outside of the femur. Small direct branches of the <b>S1 and S2</b> spinal nerves supply it, occasionally with help from L5. The lumbar spinous processes and the lower part of the trochanter are not its attachments.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-96-1.png\" alt=\"Sacrum, front view: piriformis origin outlined around the S2–S4 foramina\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Sacrum, front view: piriformis origin outlined around the S2–S4 foramina <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 96</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Its action depends on hip position</h4>\n<p>The piriformis crosses the back of the hip, so the hip's position changes what its pull does. With the lower limb straight, it <b>externally rotates</b> the thigh and may help extend it. With the hip flexed 90°, it <b>abducts</b> the thigh and may also internally rotate it. Past 90° of hip flexion it acts as an internal (medial) rotator. It never adducts the thigh. It belongs to the group of <b>six lateral (external) rotators</b> of the hip: piriformis, gemellus superior, obturator internus, gemellus inferior, quadratus femoris and obturator externus.</p>\n<h4>The sciatic nerve</h4>\n<p>The sciatic nerve forms from the <b>ventral rami of L4–S3</b>. Its roots gather on the <b>anterior surface</b> of the piriformis, and the nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen just beneath the muscle. It is the largest nerve in the body, about 2 cm wide. It passes the piriformis but does not supply it. Hip internal rotation, adduction and flexion tighten the muscle over the nerve, so most hip motions hurt when the piriformis is irritated. In a study of 1,510 limbs, 88% of nerves passed whole below the muscle and 11% split, with one part piercing the muscle. Rarely one part ran above it or the whole nerve pierced it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-96-2.png\" alt=\"Piriformis from sacrum to greater trochanter, sciatic nerve emerging below\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Piriformis from sacrum to greater trochanter, sciatic nerve emerging below <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 96</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-102-1.png\" alt=\"Sciatic nerve and piriformis: usual course and variants\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Sciatic nerve and piriformis: usual course and variants <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 102</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Nerves not to confuse</h4>\n<p>Many nerves of this region have overlapping roots. The table pairs each with its roots and its job, so you can match a root range to its nerve.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Roots</th><th>Supplies or course</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Superior gluteal</td><td>L4–S1</td><td>Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fasciae latae</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inferior gluteal</td><td>L5–S2</td><td>Gluteus maximus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Femoral</td><td>L2–L4</td><td>Between psoas and iliacus to the front of the thigh; supplies the iliacus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Obturator</td><td>L2–L4</td><td>Obturator foramen to the medial thigh</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pudendal</td><td>S2–S4</td><td>Perineum</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lateral femoral cutaneous</td><td>L2–L3</td><td>Trapped under the inguinal ligament in meralgia paresthetica</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Genitofemoral</td><td>L1–L2</td><td>Pierces the psoas</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Dorsal rami supply the back muscles and the skin over them, not the limb.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Piriformis: anterior-lateral sacrum S2–S4 → superior-medial greater trochanter</li>\n<li>Nerve: S1–S2 (occasionally L5); sciatic passes it but doesn't supply it</li>\n<li>Leg straight → external rotation (+ some extension); hip flexed 90° → abduction</li>\n<li>Past 90° flexion → internal rotator; never adducts</li>\n<li>One of six lateral rotators of the hip</li>\n<li>Sciatic: ventral rami L4–S3, on anterior piriformis, exits just below it; 2 cm wide</li>\n<li>1,510 limbs: 88% below the muscle, 11% split through it</li>\n<li>Look-alikes: inferior gluteal L5–S2, femoral/obturator L2–L4, pudendal S2–S4</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The piriformis","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Piriformis syndrome: causes, history, exam","p":"<p><b>Piriformis syndrome</b> is a peripheral neuritis (nerve irritation) of the sciatic nerve caused by an abnormal piriformis that is tight, in spasm or swollen. It is classed as a deep gluteal syndrome. About 6% of patients with low back pain have it, and it is often overlooked because it mimics disc disease, a pinched nerve root, sacral dysfunction, sacroiliitis, sciatica and trochanteric bursitis. The piriformis trigger point (TrP) is the one most often mistaken for sciatica.</p>\n<h4>Sciatica is a symptom, not a diagnosis</h4>\n<p><b>Sciatica</b> is sharp or burning pain along the sciatic nerve, down the back of the thigh and sometimes to the foot. It names a symptom, not a cause. Most sciatica comes from a herniated lumbar disc pressing on the L5 or S1 root; somatic dysfunction, foraminal stenosis, a pelvic mass, diabetic neuropathy and sacroiliitis are other causes.</p>\n<h4>Primary and secondary causes</h4>\n<p><b>Primary</b> piriformis syndrome (under 15%) has an anatomical cause: a split piriformis, a <b>split sciatic nerve</b>, or a nerve on an unusual path. <b>Secondary</b> piriformis syndrome is more common and has a precipitating cause. Macrotrauma (a blow or fall on the buttock) brings inflammation and spasm that squeeze the nerve. Microtrauma comes from overuse (running, walking) or direct compression: “wallet neuritis” from a wallet in the back pocket, or long sitting on hard seats.</p>\n<h4>History</h4>\n<p>The patient often reports trauma to the <b>same-side buttock</b>: a fall, long sitting or driving, items in a back pocket, or overuse in track or golf. The most common symptom is pain that <b>increases after sitting 15–20 minutes</b>, along with pain on rising from a chair. The dull pain lies over the piriformis, especially at the <b>sacrum and greater trochanter</b>, and radiates down the back of the thigh to the knee. Many have <b>contralateral sacroiliac (SI) pain</b>, because the spasm pulls the sacrum into a forward torsion to the opposite side. Strain on the pudendal nerve can add groin and pelvic pain.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-129-1.png\" alt=\"Piriformis syndrome history: buttock trauma, long sitting, back-pocket items, overuse\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Piriformis syndrome history: buttock trauma, long sitting, back-pocket items, overuse <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 129</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Physical exam</h4>\n<p>On observation the gait is altered and painful, the <b>leg is turned out</b> (externally rotated) on the same side, and the spine is held in <b>increased flexion</b>. Palpation finds tenderness over the SI joint, the greater sciatic notch and the piriformis, sometimes a palpable mass in the buttock (below the iliac crest) from the contracted muscle, iliac restrictions, the forward sacral torsion and an apparent leg length difference from that torsion. Active external rotation and passive internal rotation, flexion and adduction of the hip are painful. The lumbar spine is restricted, especially in <b>extension</b>. The <b>neurologic exam is normal</b>: sensation, strength and deep tendon reflexes (DTRs), with no upper motor neuron sign such as a Babinski reflex. The straight leg raise (SLR) is <b>painful throughout</b> its range. Its <b>tender points</b> lie along the muscle: at the <b>midpole of the lateral sacral border</b>, in the belly of the muscle and at the <b>posteromedial greater trochanter</b>. They are also its counterstrain points (5.3).</p>\n<p>In the well-leg raise you lift the painless leg; if a disc presses on a nerve root, pain appears on the painful side. A negative well-leg raise and a normal neuro exam point away from the disc and toward the piriformis. Electromyography (EMG), a test of nerve signals in muscle, also separates them: a disc injures the nerve above the piriformis, while piriformis syndrome makes the EMG abnormal only below it. X-rays help little, and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) mainly rules out spine and disc disease.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-129-2.png\" alt=\"Piriformis exam: neuro normal, straight leg raise painful throughout\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Piriformis exam: neuro normal, straight leg raise painful throughout <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 129</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three muscle problems side by side</h4>\n<p>The three syndromes differ in pain, posture and test. A tender piriformis and a normal neuro exam do not separate psoas from piriformis syndrome, because psoas syndrome also has a tender piriformis (on the opposite side). The Thomas test does: it is positive only with the tight psoas.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Psoas syndrome</th><th>Tight quadratus lumborum</th><th>Piriformis syndrome</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Pain</td><td>Low back; front or side of thigh, not past the knee</td><td>Low back; crest, hip, buttock, groin</td><td>Buttock; back of thigh to knee; worse sitting</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Look</td><td>Flexed, side-bent toward the psoas; cannot stand up straight</td><td>Side-bent toward the QL; short-looking leg</td><td>Leg turned out; spine flexed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Test</td><td>Thomas</td><td>Hip drop; leg traction</td><td>SLR</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Sciatic neuritis from abnormal piriformis; ~6% of low back pain; mimics disc, bursitis</li>\n<li>Sciatica = symptom; usually L5/S1 disc; piriformis TrP most mistaken for it</li>\n<li>Primary (under 15%): split muscle/nerve; secondary: buttock trauma, overuse, wallet neuritis</li>\n<li>History: pain after sitting 15–20 min, sacrum/trochanter to knee, contralateral SI pain</li>\n<li>Exam: leg externally rotated, spine flexed, extension restricted, piriformis tender</li>\n<li>Neuro normal; SLR painful throughout; well-leg raise negative</li>\n<li>EMG abnormal only below piriformis; MRI mainly rules out disc</li>\n<li>Tender points: midpole lateral sacral border, muscle belly, posteromedial trochanter</li>\n<li>Thomas test separates psoas from piriformis syndrome; QL = hip drop/traction</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The piriformis","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Hip rotation and treating the piriformis","p":"<h4>From action to barrier</h4>\n<p>Every direct technique starts from one question: which motion stretches the tight muscle? With the leg straight, the piriformis rotates the hip <b>externally</b>. A hypertonic piriformis therefore holds the hip turned out. External rotation stays free; it is the <b>freedom</b>, or ease. Internal rotation is restricted; it is the <b>barrier</b>. A direct technique takes the hip into internal rotation, and with the hip flexed, adduction adds to the stretch. Abduction and external rotation shorten the muscle instead. Hip flexion alone does not engage the piriformis barrier; rotation does. Hip flexion belongs mainly to the iliopsoas, and hip extension to the gluteus maximus and hamstrings.</p>\n<h4>Piriformis muscle energy</h4>\n<p>Muscle energy (ME) holds the muscle at its barrier while the patient pushes toward the freedom against your equal counterforce.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient lies <b>prone</b>, and you bend the knee on the affected side to 90°.</li>\n<li>You monitor the piriformis with one hand and with the other swing the foot outward, away from the midline. With the knee bent, this turns the hip into <b>internal rotation</b>, and you stop at the barrier.</li>\n<li>The patient pushes the foot back toward the midline, toward external rotation, against your equal counterforce for 3–5 seconds.</li>\n<li>The patient relaxes, you take up the slack, and you repeat about 3 times before a final passive stretch.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>ME has no absolute contraindication.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-116-1.png\" alt=\"Prone piriformis muscle energy: knee bent, hip turned into internal rotation\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Prone piriformis muscle energy: knee bent, hip turned into internal rotation <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 116</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The setups for the three lumbar muscles are easy to mix up, so compare them by position.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Setup</th><th>Belongs to</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Supine at the edge, other knee to the chest, affected leg hanging off; patient lifts toward the ceiling</td><td>Psoas ME</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Side-lying, tight side up, forearms on hip and shoulder; patient brings them together</td><td>Quadratus lumborum (QL) ME</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prone, hand on rib 12, slow rhythmic lift of the anterior superior iliac spine</td><td>QL passive myofascial release</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Treating piriformis syndrome</h4>\n<p>Early conservative treatment works in about 79% of patients. It means nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), <b>ice</b>, rest and muscle relaxants, plus stretching the piriformis and strengthening the hip abductors and adductors. Opioids are not first line, and ice is used, not heat. Osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) aims to restore motion and reduce pain by decreasing the spasm. If these fail, physical therapy, acupuncture and injection come next; the injection is a local anesthetic with a corticosteroid, or botulinum toxin. Surgical release of the muscle is the last resort.</p>\n<p>OMT techniques split by direction. <b>Indirect</b> techniques move the tissue into its ease. They are gentle, suit an acutely painful hip and are the ones reported most often. They are <b>counterstrain</b>, with tender points at the midpole of the sacrum, in the piriformis muscle and at the posteromedial trochanter, and <b>facilitated positional release (FPR)</b>. <b>Direct</b> techniques move into the barrier. They are <b>muscle energy</b> and high-velocity low-amplitude thrust (<b>HVLA</b>). HVLA treats the sacral and iliac somatic dysfunction (SD) and needs caution in osteoporosis. Piriformis inhibition, which is steady pressure on the muscle, and home stretches are also used.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O074-83.jpg\" alt=\"Piriformis stretches\"></div><figcaption>Piriformis stretches <small>· 2026 week 13 Lecture 13A Lumbar - Psoas, slide 83</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Leg straight: piriformis = external rotator (+ some extension); abducts at 90° flexion</li>\n<li>Hypertonic → external rotation free; internal rotation = barrier (+ adduction if flexed)</li>\n<li>ME: prone, knee 90°, foot out (internal rotation) to barrier; patient pushes back</li>\n<li>3–5 s, ×3, passive stretch; ME has no absolute contraindication</li>\n<li>Conservative first (79%): NSAIDs, ice, rest, relaxants, stretch; opioids not first line</li>\n<li>Later: physical therapy, acupuncture, injection (anesthetic + steroid, botulinum); surgery last</li>\n<li>Indirect: counterstrain (midpole sacrum, piriformis, posteromedial trochanter), FPR</li>\n<li>Direct: ME, HVLA for sacral/iliac SD (caution osteoporosis); plus piriformis inhibition</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The piriformis","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Trigger, tender and Chapman's points","p":"<h4>What a trigger point is</h4>\n<p>Janet Travell, later President Kennedy's physician, coined the term “trigger point” in 1942. A <b>trigger point (TrP)</b> is a hyperirritable spot in skeletal muscle: a hypersensitive, palpable nodule in a <b>taut band</b> of hypercontracted muscle fibers. Pressing it gives characteristic <b>referred pain</b>, meaning pain felt in a zone away from the spot, along with motor dysfunction and autonomic effects. Rolling the band under your fingers (snapping palpation) or needling it makes the band jump; this is the <b>local twitch response</b>. TrP pain is common and often missed.</p>\n<p>Tenderness alone does not make a TrP. A TrP needs a cluster: a taut band, a hypersensitive spot and referred symptoms when the spot is pressed. The twitch response is typical but is not always elicited.</p>\n<h4>Three kinds of points</h4>\n<p>Three palpable points are easy to confuse. A <b>Chapman's point</b> is a small nodule in deep fascia or periosteum that signals an organ problem. A <b>TrP</b> lies in muscle and refers pain. A <b>Jones tender point</b>, the point used in counterstrain, is a small, tense spot about the size of a fingertip or a dime. It lies in muscle, tendon, ligament or fascia. It is only locally tender, often exquisitely so, and it has no taut band, no twitch, no referral and no typical pain pattern. It may lie away from where the patient hurts. In counterstrain you fold the body into the position of ease until its tenderness drops by at least 70%, hold about 90 seconds and return slowly.</p>\n<p>Some features are shared and so do not separate a TrP from a tender point. Both are locally tender, both may give a jump sign (the patient flinches), both sit in predictable places, and both occur on the trunk and the limbs. Only the TrP has a taut band and a twitch, and only the TrP refers pain. Dermographia, a raised red line after stroking the skin, may occur over thoracic and lumbar TrPs, not tender points.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Chapman's point</th><th>Trigger point (Travell)</th><th>Tender point (Jones)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Location</td><td>Deep fascia, periosteum</td><td><b>Muscle</b></td><td>Muscle, tendon, ligament or fascia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Feel</td><td>Smooth, firm nodule, 2–3 mm; chronic ones up to almond size</td><td>Nodule in a taut band; twitch response</td><td>Small, tense spot; no taut band, no twitch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pain</td><td>Slight to unbearable, local</td><td><b>Refers to a predictable zone</b></td><td>Local only; <b>no referral</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reflex</td><td>Viscerosomatic</td><td>Viscerosomatic, somatovisceral or somatosomatic</td><td>Somatic dysfunction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Firm circular pressure, 20–30 seconds</td><td>Spray and stretch, injection, manipulation, stretching</td><td>Counterstrain</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-70-1.png\" alt=\"Trigger point versus counterstrain tender point\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Trigger point versus counterstrain tender point <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 70</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Chapman's reflexes, briefly</h4>\n<p>Chapman's reflexes are pea-sized nodules thought to come from lymph congestion under sympathetic influence, a form of viscerosomatic reflex. They come in pairs: an <b>anterior</b> point in an intercostal space beside the sternum and a <b>posterior</b> point beside the spinous processes. Colon points lie on the lateral thigh, and the appendix point is at the tip of the right 12th rib. They help confirm a diagnosis. Innominate dysfunction is treated first, and either point of a pair may be treated. The full chart comes next year.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O052-51.jpg\" alt=\"Chapman&#x27;s anterior and posterior points\"></div><figcaption>Chapman&#x27;s anterior and posterior points <small>· 2026 week 8 Lecture 8 Autonomics (New ANS Lecture), slide 51</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Trigger points and the organs</h4>\n<p>TrPs behave much like facilitation. A strain sends constant sensory input into a spinal segment and sensitizes it, and the muscle of that segment tightens into a taut band. An organ can do the same: in one report 61% of patients with heart disease had chest-muscle TrPs (viscerosomatic). The reverse also happens, and a TrP can drive an organ (somatovisceral). A TrP in the <b>right pectoralis major</b>, between the <b>5th and 6th ribs near the sternum</b>, is linked to <b>supraventricular tachyarrhythmia (SVT)</b>, a fast heart rhythm that starts above the ventricles. Pectoralis TrPs can also mimic cardiac pain and cause palpitations. When the TrP is the cause, manual treatment has stopped the arrhythmia in case reports. Other spinal findings point elsewhere: a flattened upper thoracic kyphosis goes with mitral valve prolapse, and a Jones tender point reflects local somatic dysfunction, not an organ reflex.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>TrP (Travell): hyperirritable nodule in a taut muscle band; refers pain; twitch on snapping</li>\n<li>TrP needs cluster: taut band + hypersensitive spot + referred symptoms</li>\n<li>Jones tender point: muscle/tendon/ligament/fascia; dime-size; local only; no band, twitch, referral</li>\n<li>Counterstrain: ease position, tenderness ↓ ≥70%, hold 90 s</li>\n<li>Both locally tender, jump sign, predictable sites, trunk and limbs</li>\n<li>Chapman's: 2–3 mm nodule in deep fascia; viscerosomatic; appendix = right rib 12 tip</li>\n<li>Right pectoralis major TrP, ribs 5–6 by sternum → SVT (somatovisceral)</li>\n<li>Flattened upper thoracic kyphosis → mitral valve prolapse</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Trigger points","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"How trigger points form; active and latent","p":"<h4>How a trigger point forms</h4>\n<p>Muscle shortens when its sarcomeres, the tiny contractile units inside each fiber, contract; normally they then relax. The proposed trigger point (TrP) mechanism starts at a faulty motor end-plate, the junction where nerve meets muscle.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The dysfunctional end-plate releases <b>excess acetylcholine (ACh)</b>, the chemical signal from nerve to muscle.</li>\n<li>The ACh triggers calcium release inside the fiber, and the sarcomeres contract.</li>\n<li>Instead of relaxing, a patch of sarcomeres stays contracted as a <b>contraction knot</b>, while the fibers on either side are stretched thin.</li>\n<li>A group of knots lying close together forms the palpable <b>nodule</b> of the TrP.</li>\n<li>The knot uses more energy but squeezes its own small vessels, so oxygen falls and waste builds up.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The TrP muscle is therefore shortened, not overlengthened, and its oxygen demand rises while its circulation falls. More acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that clears ACh, would calm the end-plate rather than cause a TrP. Stretching a muscle that contains TrPs sometimes makes it even more hypertonic.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-69-2.png\" alt=\"Trigger point up close: contraction knots between stretched muscle fibers\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Trigger point up close: contraction knots between stretched muscle fibers <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 69</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The four stages</h4>\n<p>Trigger points develop in four stages. Stage 1 is <b>hyperirritability</b>: acute or chronic strain sensitizes the nerve endings and causes local tenderness and a twitch response. Stage 2 is <b>increased metabolism with reduced circulation</b>. The contracted fibers need more energy while the tight muscle squeezes its arteries and veins, so the TrP becomes a spot of <b>local ischemia</b> (too little blood flow). Stage 3 is the <b>palpable taut band</b>, a contracture: the damaged sarcoplasmic reticulum, the fiber's calcium store, leaks calcium, so the sarcomeres keep contracting. Stage 4 brings <b>secondary effects</b>. Joint motion is restricted, muscles in the referral zone fire more easily, and <b>satellite TrPs</b> form in muscles that lie inside the referral zone. In short, the stages are tenderness only, local pain, referred pain and then satellite TrPs. A taut band that refers pain while joint motion stays full is therefore stage (grade) 3; restricted motion or satellites mean stage 4.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O100-103-1.png\" alt=\"Stages 1–2 of trigger point development, with the local twitch response\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Stages 1–2 of trigger point development, with the local twitch response <small>· Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 103</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A muscle with a TrP shows four features: pain in the muscle, referred pain, weakness (decreased contractility) and decreased flexibility (hypertonicity).</p>\n<h4>Active and latent trigger points</h4>\n<p>An <b>active</b> TrP causes the patient's own pain complaint, felt during movement and daily activity. It is always tender, keeps the muscle from fully lengthening and weakens it. When pressed, it refers a pain pattern the patient <b>recognizes</b>. It also causes motor and autonomic effects and tenderness in its referral zone, can upset functionally related muscles, and can seed satellite TrPs. A <b>latent</b> TrP causes no spontaneous pain. It hurts <b>only when palpated</b>, and the patient may not recognize the pain as familiar. Its taut band still raises muscle tension and limits motion, so the patient feels <b>joint stiffness</b>. A latent TrP can do everything an active one does except cause spontaneous pain. Latent TrPs are <b>more common</b>. In one study of young, symptom-free Air Force recruits, about half had latent TrPs in the posterior shoulder muscles.</p>\n<p>Both types share the taut band and the twitch response. Both can cause <b>autonomic</b> effects: abnormal sweating, tearing, a persistently runny nose, excess salivation and gooseflesh. Both can also cause <b>proprioceptive</b> effects: imbalance, dizziness, tinnitus and misjudging the weight of lifted objects. TrPs are very common in pain clinics, found in 85–93% of patients in two studies.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Faulty end-plate → excess ACh → calcium → sarcomeres stay contracted (contraction knots)</li>\n<li>Group of knots = nodule; muscle shortened, oxygen demand ↑, circulation ↓</li>\n<li>Stage 1 hyperirritability (tender, twitch); 2 ↑ metabolism + ischemia</li>\n<li>Stage 3 taut band + referred pain (grade III); 4 restricted motion + satellite TrPs</li>\n<li>TrP muscle: pain, referred pain, weakness, ↓ flexibility</li>\n<li>Active: spontaneous pain complaint, patient-recognized referral, satellites</li>\n<li>Latent: more common; painful only on palpation; joint stiffness</li>\n<li>Both: taut band, twitch, autonomic (sweating, tearing) + proprioceptive (dizziness, tinnitus)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Trigger points","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Aggravating factors and treatment","p":"<h4>What keeps trigger points going</h4>\n<p>A trigger point (TrP) persists when something keeps loading or sensitizing its muscle. These perpetuating factors fall into three groups.</p>\n<p>The first group is <b>how the muscle is used</b>: strenuous use, pressure on the TrP, <b>prolonged shortening</b>, chronic postural or occupational strain, poor ergonomics and somatic dysfunction. The second group is the <b>body's state and the weather</b>: cold, damp weather, cold drafts, <b>viral infections</b>, marked nervous tension and <b>hypoglycemia</b>, which is low blood sugar, not high. The third group is <b>body build</b>. It includes a <b>leg length inequality</b> and a <b>small hemipelvis</b>, where one half of the pelvis is smaller, so the patient sits tilted and crosses a leg to level it. Short upper arms belong here too, because the elbows cannot reach the armrests and the shoulder muscles must hold the arms up. Lower extremity dysfunction completes the list. Each factor keeps a muscle working against a mechanical or metabolic handicap.</p>\n<h4>Conditions linked with TrPs</h4>\n<p>Several medical conditions make muscles prone to TrPs and myofascial pain: vitamin deficiency or insufficiency (C, D, B12, folate), <b>hypothyroidism</b>, hypoglycemia, <b>hyperuricemia</b> (high uric acid, as in gout), <b>iron deficiency</b> and Candida (yeast) infection. A pattern helps you remember them. Each is a shortage or a low state, except the high uric acid. Their opposites, such as hyperthyroidism, high blood sugar, low uric acid and vitamin excess, are not on the list.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-43-1.jpg\" alt=\"Perpetuating factors and associated conditions\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Perpetuating factors and associated conditions <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Treatment</h4>\n<p>All TrP treatments try to break the cycle of contraction and ischemia. There are three main options.</p>\n<p><b>Spray and stretch</b> uses ethyl chloride vapocoolant spray. You spray over the TrP <b>and its referral zone</b>. The cold is a distraction: it interrupts the pain cycle so that the muscle tolerates a stretch. The <b>stretch</b> and the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) that follow are the real treatment, so the spray is followed by stretching, not by rest. You rewarm the area afterward. This is the option to offer a patient whose pain makes stretching or OMT hard to tolerate, and it is one option for the quadratus lumborum.</p>\n<p><b>Injection or dry needling</b> goes into the TrP in the muscle, with a local anesthetic or with a dry needle. The referral zone is not injected. Steroid injection is not a TrP treatment. It is used for an inflamed trochanteric bursa (6.4) and as a late option in piriformis syndrome (5.3).</p>\n<p><b>OMT</b> includes counterstrain, muscle energy, myofascial release and more; no single technique is required. Ultrasound, reciprocal inhibition and ischemic compression are also used.</p>\n<p>Whatever the method, treatment must include <b>stretching</b> of the involved muscle and <b>self-care</b>, or the TrP returns. Self-care means home stretching, ergonomic education, postural strengthening and self-massage with a Theracane, a hooked massage stick.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-72-1.png\" alt=\"Vapocoolant spray over the muscle before stretching\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Vapocoolant spray over the muscle before stretching <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 72</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Use: strenuous use, pressure, prolonged shortening, postural/occupational strain, somatic dysfunction</li>\n<li>State: cold damp weather, drafts, viral infection, nervous tension, hypoglycemia (not hyper-)</li>\n<li>Build: leg length inequality, small hemipelvis, short upper arms, lower limb dysfunction</li>\n<li>Associated: vitamin deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypoglycemia, hyperuricemia, iron deficiency, Candida</li>\n<li>Spray and stretch: ethyl chloride over TrP + referral zone; spray distracts, stretch treats; rewarm</li>\n<li>Injection (anesthetic) or dry needling into the TrP, not the referral zone</li>\n<li>OMT: counterstrain, muscle energy, myofascial; steroid injection is not TrP treatment</li>\n<li>Always stretching + self-care (home stretches, ergonomics, posture, Theracane)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Trigger points","sn":6,"pn":"6.3"},{"l":"OMM-W13","h":"Pain at the greater trochanter","p":"<p>A patient points to the greater trochanter, the bony knob on the side of the hip. Two very different problems cause pain there, and palpating the spot itself sorts them.</p>\n<h4>Bursitis or referred pain</h4>\n<p>If the trochanter is <b>very tender</b> and the tissue shows acute inflammation, warm and boggy, the trochanteric bursa is inflamed. This is <b>trochanteric bursitis</b>, and it is often treated with a steroid injection into the bursa. If the pain is over the trochanter but there is <b>little tenderness</b> and no tissue texture change, the bursa is not inflamed. Then think of <b>referred pain</b>. It can come from the pelvis, the abdomen or the lumbar spine, or from myofascial trigger points (TrPs).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O075-30.jpg\" alt=\"Pain over the greater trochanter in trochanteric bursitis\"></div><figcaption>Pain over the greater trochanter in trochanteric bursitis <small>· 2026 week 13 Lecture 13B Intro to Trigger Points, slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three muscles whose TrPs reach the trochanter</h4>\n<p>The <b>quadratus lumborum (QL)</b> TrPs lie along its lateral edge between rib 12 and the iliac crest. You reach them by pressing just above (cephalad to) the posterior iliac crest. They refer along the iliac crest, to the lateral hip and greater trochanter, to the sacroiliac (SI) region and to the lower buttock.</p>\n<p>The <b>gluteus medius</b> has three TrPs just below the iliac crest. TrP1 and TrP3 refer mainly to the posterior iliac crest, sacrum and buttock. <b>TrP2</b> lies below the crest, behind the halfway mark between the anterior and posterior superior iliac spines (ASIS and PSIS). It refers to the greater trochanter region and the upper lateral thigh.</p>\n<p>The <b>piriformis</b> TrPs lie mid-buttock, between the sacrum and the trochanter. They refer to the sacrum, buttock and back of the hip and down the back of the thigh. Their pain is mistaken for SI joint pain, trochanteric bursitis or sciatic nerve impingement.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O094-5-1.png\" alt=\"Travell referral maps: QL, piriformis, iliopsoas, rotatores and multifidi\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Travell referral maps: QL, piriformis, iliopsoas, rotatores and multifidi <small>· 2026 Harlem TA review (SGA drive), slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-48-1.jpg\" alt=\"Palpating gluteus medius trigger points just below the iliac crest\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Palpating gluteus medius trigger points just below the iliac crest <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Telling them apart</h4>\n<p>Because all three can refer to the same spot, you sort them by examining the muscles, in this order.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Use the referral maps to choose which muscles to examine.</li>\n<li>Palpate each one for a taut band whose local twitch response reproduces the patient's hip pain.</li>\n<li>Add the rest of the exam. A piriformis TrP comes with buttock tenderness and restricted hip internal rotation. A QL TrP is found above the posterior crest. A gluteus medius TrP2 is found below the crest, in the upper buttock.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Shortcuts do not work. Imaging has little role in finding TrPs. A bursa injection cannot tell the three muscles apart. A jump sign also occurs with tender points, and a tender point has no taut band and no referral, so neither one settles the question.</p>\n<h4>Not a trigger point</h4>\n<p><b>Hip osteoarthritis</b> is wear of the hip joint itself. It hurts in the front of the groin and limits hip <b>extension and internal rotation</b>. Normal internal rotation and extension argue against it.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Trochanter very tender + warm, boggy → bursitis → steroid into bursa</li>\n<li>Little tenderness, no tissue change → referred pain (spine, pelvis, abdomen, TrP)</li>\n<li>QL TrPs: lateral edge, pressed above posterior crest → crest, trochanter, SI, buttock</li>\n<li>Gluteus medius TrP2: below crest, behind ASIS–PSIS midpoint → trochanter, lateral thigh</li>\n<li>Piriformis TrPs: mid-buttock; mimic SI pain, bursitis, sciatic impingement</li>\n<li>Sort: referral maps → taut band + twitch reproducing pain → rest of exam</li>\n<li>Imaging, bursa injection, jump sign don't distinguish them</li>\n<li>Hip osteoarthritis: groin pain, ↓ extension and internal rotation</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Trigger points","sn":6,"pn":"6.4"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Causes, timing and red flags","p":"<p>Low back pain (LBP) is very common. About 80% of adults have it at some point. It is the top reason people under 45 limit their activity, the top musculoskeletal reason to see a doctor, and the second most common pain complaint after headache.</p>\n<h4>How it usually goes</h4>\n<p>About 90% of acute episodes resolve within 6 weeks, and another 5% resolve by 12 weeks. How long the pain has lasted gives its label. <b>Acute</b> LBP has lasted under 6 weeks, <b>subacute</b> LBP 6 to 12 weeks, and <b>chronic</b> LBP more than 12 weeks (3 months).</p>\n<p>Osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) has evidence behind it. A 1999 New England Journal of Medicine (NEJM) study of subchronic LBP (3 weeks to 6 months) found OMT as effective as standard care, cheaper, and with far less medicine. Studies of spinal manipulation also show less pain, earlier return to work, shorter disability, fewer physical therapy visits and higher patient satisfaction.</p>\n<h4>What causes it</h4>\n<p>About 90% of LBP is <b>mechanical</b>, a problem in the spine's own moving parts: disc herniation, iliolumbar ligament strain or sprain, spondylolisthesis, compression fracture, degenerative disc, spinal stenosis or facet degeneration. <b>Viscerogenic</b> pain is referred from an organ, such as the kidney, the bowel (including inflammatory bowel disease), endometriosis or an aortic aneurysm; inflammatory arthritis is grouped here too. A kidney, for example, hurts in the low back and flank (7.1). <b>Neoplastic</b> pain comes from a primary tumor (myeloma, sarcoma) or from lung, breast or prostate metastases. <b>Infection</b> means discitis or vertebral osteomyelitis. Postural somatic dysfunction (SD) also hurts. Psychosocial factors (seeking disability or drugs) and psychiatric illness (depression) add to pain but rarely cause it alone.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-147-1.png\" alt=\"Differential diagnosis of low back pain, by category\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Differential diagnosis of low back pain, by category <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 147</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Risk factors and red flags</h4>\n<p>The history does most of the sorting. Suspicion rises with age under 20 or first onset after 50, intravenous (IV) drug use, manual labor, trauma, smoking, hormone replacement, systemic steroids and a history of cancer.</p>\n<p>A <b>red flag</b> points to a serious cause: cancer, infection, fracture or cauda equina syndrome. Red flags include pain radiating below the knee (true sciatica) or into the front of the thigh, trauma, and night or unrelenting pain. Two posture clues are on the list too: pain worse with sitting points to a disc, because flexion pushes disc material toward the root (2.1), and pain relieved by bending forward points to stenosis, because flexion opens the canal (4.2). So are bladder or bowel incontinence, first onset after 50, steroid use, a history of cancer (especially prostate, breast, lung, thyroid or kidney), and systemic signs such as fever, weight loss or swollen lymph nodes. HIV (human immunodeficiency virus), IV drug use and no improvement after 4–6 weeks are red flags too. <b>Order no imaging unless a red flag is present.</b></p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-81-1.png\" alt=\"Red flags: no imaging unless one is present\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Red flags: no imaging unless one is present <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 81</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three serious patterns</h4>\n<p><b>Vertebral osteomyelitis with discitis</b> is infection of bone and disc. In an IV drug user, bacteria in the blood seed the vertebral body and disc. The pain is constant, and pain medicine fails for weeks. An MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) with contrast shows eroded endplates and an enhancing disc.</p>\n<p>A <b>compression fracture</b> crushes the front of a vertebral body. That is its weak spot, where the bony struts (trabeculae) are sparse. It follows activity or a fall in a patient with osteoporosis or low calcium. Long untreated estrogen loss, as after a hysterectomy decades earlier with no hormone replacement, is a classic cause of that osteoporosis. The pain is out of proportion to the exam, worse with loading and better with unloading, and tapping the spinous process hurts sharply. The x-ray shows a wedge-shaped body.</p>\n<p><b>Metastasis</b> fits a cancer history, night pain and weight loss without trauma. Start with an x-ray plus an ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate, a blood marker of inflammation).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O108-43.jpg\" alt=\"Compression fracture: weak anterior trabeculae of the vertebral body\"></div><figcaption>Compression fracture: weak anterior trabeculae of the vertebral body <small>· 2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>LBP: 80% of adults; 2nd most common pain complaint after headache</li>\n<li>Acute &lt;6 wk, subacute 6–12 wk, chronic &gt;3 mo; 90% resolve by 6 wk</li>\n<li>NEJM 1999: OMT as effective as standard care, cheaper, far less medication</li>\n<li>~90% mechanical; also viscerogenic, neoplastic, infection, SD, psychosocial, psychiatric</li>\n<li>Red flags: below-knee pain, trauma, night pain, incontinence, onset &gt;50, cancer, steroids, fever</li>\n<li>No imaging unless a red flag is present</li>\n<li>IV drugs + pain unrelieved for weeks → osteomyelitis/discitis → contrast MRI</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis (e.g. untreated estrogen loss) + fall → wedge compression fracture; cancer → x-ray + ESR</li>\n<li>Worse sitting/flexion → disc; relieved by flexion → stenosis</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Approach to low back pain","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Cauda equina syndrome","p":"<h4>What lies in the lower canal</h4>\n<p>The spinal cord ends at about L1–L2 (the first and second lumbar vertebrae). Below that, the dural sac, also called the <b>thecal sac</b>, holds only nerve roots. They run down to leave through their own foramina, and together they look like a horse's tail, the <b>cauda equina</b>. Each root travels in its own dural sheath with its own small blood supply.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-64-1.png\" alt=\"Normal canal; posterolateral disc on one root; massive central disc on many\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Normal canal; posterolateral disc on one root; massive central disc on many <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 64</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Upper versus lower motor neuron</h4>\n<p>An upper motor neuron (UMN) runs from the brain down the cord. A lower motor neuron (LMN) runs from the cord out through the root and nerve to the muscle. UMN fibers normally hold the spinal reflexes in check. When a UMN is damaged (stroke, brain damage, trauma, multiple sclerosis, a tumor, or a cord mass above L1), that brake is lost. Reflexes become brisk and weakness is spastic. When an LMN is damaged (a root, the cauda equina or a nerve), the reflex arc itself is broken. Reflexes drop or disappear and weakness is flaccid.</p>\n<p>The <b>Babinski</b> test separates the two. The examiner scratches the sole with the pointed end of the reflex hammer, from the heel to the base of the great toe. Normally the toes curl down. With a UMN lesion the inhibition is gone, the toe extensors fire, and the great toe goes up. This upgoing toe is normal only in infants. A root or cauda equina lesion never gives a positive Babinski.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-82.jpg\" alt=\"Babinski: negative (toe down) and positive (toe up, UMN lesion)\"></div><figcaption>Babinski: negative (toe down) and positive (toe up, UMN lesion) <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 82</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Cauda equina syndrome (CES)</h4>\n<p>In CES many lumbosacral roots are squeezed at once inside the thecal sac. Most cases come from a disc: a large central herniation or an extruded disc fragment that fills much of the canal. Central herniations are uncommon, but one pressing on S2–S4 causes CES. Spinal stenosis, spondylolisthesis, fracture, infection and tumors (especially lung and breast metastases) can do it too.</p>\n<p>The patient has progressive low back pain, often with sciatica in one or both legs. Feeling changes between the legs: the perineum and buttocks go numb. This is <b>saddle anesthesia</b>, the S2–S4 skin; groin skin is L1, not saddle. The anal and urinary sphincters weaken. The patient struggles to start urine, then retains it and leaks (overflow incontinence), and may have constipation, fecal incontinence and sexual dysfunction. A complete syndrome has retention with overflow; an incomplete one has milder urinary symptoms plus other deficits. The rectal exam shows <b>decreased anal tone</b>. Because roots are LMNs, <b>knee and ankle reflexes drop</b>. Weakness and numbness climb up the legs, slowly or within hours.</p>\n<h4>Management</h4>\n<p>CES is a surgical emergency, so the patient goes straight to the emergency room (ER). Get an <b>emergency MRI</b> (magnetic resonance imaging) and an urgent neurosurgical or spine surgery consult for <b>decompression within 48 hours</b>. Delay can make the bladder, bowel and leg loss permanent. Medicines and OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) do not replace surgery, and a thrust (HVLA, high-velocity, low-amplitude) could worsen the herniation. Muscle relaxants, physical therapy or a day of waiting on pain medicine only delay decompression. Blood tests or a biopsy come later, only if imaging shows infection or a mass.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-38.jpg\" alt=\"Red flags for cauda equina syndrome\"></div><figcaption>Red flags for cauda equina syndrome <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Look-alikes</h4>\n<p>Meningeal inflammation causes fever, a stiff neck and a Kernig sign, without root deficits. Congested epidural veins, as with a blocked inferior vena cava, do not lower rectal tone. Meralgia paresthetica numbs only the lateral thigh (2.4).</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Cord ends L1–L2; below it the thecal sac holds roots (cauda equina)</li>\n<li>UMN lesion → brisk reflexes, spastic weakness, Babinski positive (toe up)</li>\n<li>LMN lesion (root, cauda equina) → reflexes ↓, flaccid weakness, toe down</li>\n<li>CES: many roots squeezed; usually large central disc or extruded fragment</li>\n<li>Other causes: stenosis, slip, fracture, infection, lung/breast metastases</li>\n<li>Saddle anesthesia (S2–S4), retention + overflow, ↓ rectal tone, ↓ knee + ankle reflexes</li>\n<li>Progressive, ascending weakness: slow or within hours</li>\n<li>ER → emergency MRI → decompression within 48 h; no HVLA, no waiting; delay → permanent loss</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Approach to low back pain","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"How and why discs herniate","p":"<h4>The disc and what hurts</h4>\n<p>Each disc has a jelly-like center, the <b>nucleus pulposus</b>, held inside rings of tough fiber, the <b>annulus fibrosus</b>. The nucleus sits slightly toward the back. In a <b>bulging disc</b> the disc sags outward but the annulus holds. In a <b>herniated disc</b> (herniated nucleus pulposus, HNP) the annulus tears and nucleus leaks out.</p>\n<p>Not every tissue can hurt. The posterior and anterior longitudinal ligaments, the vertebral body, the nerve root and the facet joint have pain nerve endings. The ligamentum flavum, the interspinous ligament and the annulus lack them. So a disc hurts mainly when it pushes on the posterior ligament or a root.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O108-50.jpg\" alt=\"Pain-sensitive (+) and pain-free (−) tissues of a spinal segment\"></div><figcaption>Pain-sensitive (+) and pain-free (−) tissues of a spinal segment <small>· 2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 50</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why discs age and tear</h4>\n<p>Herniation risk peaks when the disc cells change type (metaplasia): in the third decade (20s) in men and the fourth (30s) in women. By the 30s, 30–50% of people show disc changes on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging), even without pain. Four cell changes take place. Cells turn <b>senescent</b>, stop dividing, and release enzymes that digest the matrix. The <b>endplates calcify</b>, so the disc, which has almost no blood supply, starves of glucose and oxygen. Cells switch from elastic type II to stiff <b>type I collagen</b> and make fewer water-binding proteoglycans, so the disc dries out. Then tiny tears (<b>micro-fissures</b>) open in the annulus. Activity, core strength and avoiding long static sitting protect the disc.</p>\n<h4>Why the herniation goes posterolateral</h4>\n<p>The anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL) is broad. The <b>posterior longitudinal ligament</b> (PLL) is narrow, and it narrows further going down: at L4–L5 it is half as wide as at L1. It covers the middle of the back of the disc but not its sides. So the nucleus escapes <b>posterolaterally</b>, toward a nerve root, and posterolateral herniations far outnumber central ones. The commonest level is <b>L4–L5, then L5–S1</b>, together 98%. Heavy lumbar loading (more than in the neck), ligament laxity and degenerative joint disease add risk.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-67-1.png\" alt=\"Posterolateral herniation pressing on the nerve root\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Posterolateral herniation pressing on the nerve root <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 67</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Motion: the squish and squirt</h4>\n<p>Lumbar superior facets face back, up and medial (<b>BUM</b>), near the sagittal plane. So the lumbar spine moves mostly in flexion and extension, about a transverse axis; rotation is only about 5–7° each side. In <b>flexion</b> the front of the disc is squeezed and the nucleus squirts backward, like toothpaste, onto the root. So disc symptoms worsen with bending forward, sitting, coughing and straining. Sitting flexed and twisted, rotated away from the symptomatic side, is worst. The L5 root exits between L5 and S1, S1 through the first sacral foramen (2.2). A transitional vertebra does not protect the discs (3.1).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-56-1.png\" alt=\"Flexion drives the nucleus backward and stretches the posterior ligaments\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Flexion drives the nucleus backward and stretches the posterior ligaments <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How root entrapment looks</h4>\n<p>A trapped root gives leg pain worse than back pain and limited trunk flexion. Protective spasm shifts the pelvis and bends the spine sideways, a <b>functional scoliosis</b>. Sidebending is then restricted, so the <b>hip drop test</b> is asymmetric. In that test the standing patient bends one knee; that iliac crest should drop 20–25° as the lumbar spine sidebends away from it. The test is positive on the side whose crest cannot drop, the side the spine is stuck sidebent toward. Root entrapment also brings one root's weakness and altered feeling (2.3), a positive straight leg raise made worse by ankle dorsiflexion (the dural sign, 2.4), and multifidus triangle tenderness (7.1).</p>\n<p>About 90–95% of herniations settle with conservative care: bed rest for 2 days at most, OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment, indirect first) and medicines. HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust) is relatively contraindicated; surgery is for failure. A tight psoas, by contrast, gives groin and front-of-thigh pain that stops above the knee, with a normal neurologic exam.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Nucleus pulposus inside annulus fibrosus; bulge = annulus intact; HNP = annulus tears</li>\n<li>Pain-sensitive: PLL, ALL, vertebral body, root, facet; not ligamentum flavum, interspinous, annulus</li>\n<li>30s: senescent cells, calcified endplates, type II → I collagen, annular micro-fissures</li>\n<li>Narrow PLL → posterolateral HNP; L4–L5 &gt; L5–S1 (98% together)</li>\n<li>BUM facets → flexion/extension dominate; rotation 5–7°</li>\n<li>Flexion squirts nucleus backward → worse bending, sitting, coughing; worst sitting flexed + twisted</li>\n<li>Root entrapment: limited flexion, functional scoliosis, asymmetric hip drop, dural sign, multifidus tenderness</li>\n<li>Hip drop positive on the side the spine is sidebent toward</li>\n<li>90–95% resolve conservatively; HVLA relatively contraindicated</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Disc herniation and nerve roots","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Which root a disc hits","p":"<h4>Where each lumbar root leaves</h4>\n<p>The cord ends at L1–L2, so the lumbar and sacral roots run down inside the canal before they leave. Each lumbar root exits through the intervertebral foramen (IVF) <b>below its own vertebra</b>. The L4 root, for example, leaves between L4 and L5. It passes through the upper part of that foramen, <b>above the disc</b>, carrying both its sensory and motor fibers inside its dural sheath. Sacral roots leave through the sacral foramina, so S1 exits through the first sacral foramen.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-69-1.png\" alt=\"Each lumbar root exits below its vertebra, above the disc\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Each lumbar root exits below its vertebra, above the disc <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 69</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The X+1 rule</h4>\n<p>Take a disc between vertebra X and the one below it. Root X has already left the canal through the foramen above that disc, so the disc cannot reach it. The root crossing the back of the disc on its way down is the next one, X+1. A posterolateral herniation, the usual kind, presses on that crossing root. So a herniated nucleus pulposus (HNP) usually hits the <b>lower</b> root. An L3–L4 disc hits L4, an L4–L5 disc hits L5, and an L5–S1 disc hits S1. The L5 root still exits between L5 and S1, but a herniation of that disc catches S1.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-60.jpg\" alt=\"Disc versus deficit (X+1 rule)\"></div><figcaption>Disc versus deficit (X+1 rule) <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three kinds of compression</h4>\n<p>Where the disc material goes decides which root suffers. A posterolateral disc stays inside the canal and catches the crossing root, X+1. A <b>far-lateral (foraminal) herniation</b> pushes into the foramen itself. So does <b>foraminal stenosis</b>, a narrowed IVF. Both squeeze the root that is exiting through that foramen, which is root X. A large central disc or an extruded fragment presses on many roots on both sides and causes cauda equina syndrome (1.2). The table shows the three patterns.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Lesion</th><th>Root caught</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Posterolateral disc (most common)</td><td>X+1, the root crossing the disc</td><td>L2–L3 disc → L3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Far-lateral disc or foraminal stenosis</td><td>X, the root exiting that foramen</td><td>L2–L3 foramen → L2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Large central disc</td><td>Many roots, both sides</td><td>Cauda equina syndrome</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O108-51.jpg\" alt=\"Normal disc, bulge, foraminal herniation and extruded fragment\"></div><figcaption>Normal disc, bulge, foraminal herniation and extruded fragment <small>· 2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 51</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Reading the MRI</h4>\n<p>On a sagittal MRI (magnetic resonance imaging), first find the marked level. A herniated disc is disc material bulging backward from between two vertebral bodies into the canal. Next decide whether it presses into the canal, which calls for X+1, or narrows a foramen, which calls for X. A pars fracture (spondylolysis, 5.1) is a break in the bony arch behind the canal, not in the disc. A disc can catch a root anywhere between the cord's end and that root's foramen. On exams, use X+1 for a posterolateral disc and X for a foraminal lesion.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Lumbar root exits below its own vertebra, above the disc (L4 at L4–L5)</li>\n<li>L5 exits between L5 and S1; S1 exits the first sacral foramen</li>\n<li>X+1: posterolateral disc X hits root X+1 (L4–L5 → L5; L5–S1 → S1)</li>\n<li>Root X already left above the disc → spared</li>\n<li>Foraminal stenosis or far-lateral disc → exiting root X (L3–L4 foramen → L3)</li>\n<li>Large central disc or extruded fragment → many roots → cauda equina</li>\n<li>MRI: disc bulging back between bodies = HNP; arch break = spondylolysis</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Disc herniation and nerve roots","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Reading the root: L4, L5, S1","p":"<p>Each root supplies a group of muscles, a reflex and a strip of skin (its dermatome). A root is part of the lower motor neuron (LMN), so a squeezed root weakens its muscles, <b>lowers or abolishes</b> its reflex, and causes numbness or shooting pain in its dermatome. It never makes reflexes brisk and never gives a Babinski sign. The lumbar neurologic exam therefore checks motor strength, reflexes and sensation, then adds the straight leg raise and the Babinski.</p>\n<h4>The three roots to know</h4>\n<p>The <b>L4</b> root runs the tibialis anterior, which dorsiflexes and <b>inverts</b> the foot, and helps the quadriceps straighten the knee, as in rising from a squat. Its reflex is the <b>patellar</b> (knee jerk). Its skin covers the <b>medial</b> leg, ankle and foot.</p>\n<p>The <b>L5</b> root runs the extensor hallucis longus, which lifts the big toe (<b>great-toe extension</b>, also called great-toe dorsiflexion). L5 has <b>no reliable reflex</b>; great-toe extension is a strength test, not a reflex. Its skin covers the lateral leg and the <b>dorsum (top) of the foot</b>.</p>\n<p>The <b>S1</b> root runs the peroneus longus, which <b>everts</b> the foot, and the calf, which plantar flexes it, as in toe walking. Its reflex is the <b>Achilles</b> (ankle jerk). Its skin covers the back of the calf and the <b>lateral foot and sole</b>.</p>\n<p>The table lines the three up. Read across a row to link the disc, the root and its findings.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Root</th><th>Disc (X+1)</th><th>Motor</th><th>Reflex</th><th>Sensation</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>L4</b></td><td>L3–L4</td><td>Inversion (tibialis anterior); knee extension</td><td>Patellar</td><td>Medial leg and foot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>L5</b></td><td>L4–L5</td><td>Great-toe extension (extensor hallucis longus)</td><td>None reliable</td><td>Lateral leg, dorsum of foot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S1</b></td><td>L5–S1</td><td>Eversion (peroneus longus); plantar flexion</td><td>Achilles</td><td>Lateral foot and sole</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-191-1.png\" alt=\"Disc, root, motor, sensory and reflex loss for L4, L5, S1\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Disc, root, motor, sensory and reflex loss for L4, L5, S1 <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 191</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Details that separate them</h4>\n<p>Tibialis anterior gets fibers from L4 and L5, but it is the classic L4 test. Extensor hallucis longus (EHL) is L5 alone. So when electromyography (EMG, a recording of muscle electrical activity) shows both abnormal, the shared root is L5. The quadriceps is L3–L4; weak knee extension also points to L3, tested with the femoral nerve test. L3 also helps the patellar reflex, but its skin stops at the medial knee. L2 flexes the hip (iliopsoas) and covers the upper front of the thigh. S2–S4 run the small foot muscles, bladder and bowel. S2 skin covers the sole and back of the thigh, and S2–S5 form a bull's-eye around the anus.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-30-1.png\" alt=\"L4 level: tibialis anterior, patellar reflex, medial leg skin\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>L4 level: tibialis anterior, patellar reflex, medial leg skin <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-101-2.png\" alt=\"L5 level: extensor hallucis longus, no reflex, dorsum of foot\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>L5 level: extensor hallucis longus, no reflex, dorsum of foot <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 101</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Osteopathic findings with a root lesion</h4>\n<p>TART findings (tissue texture change, asymmetry, restriction of motion, tenderness) come with the neurologic signs. The muscles around the disc go hypertonic in protective spasm. Trunk flexion is limited, a functional scoliosis appears, and the multifidus triangle is tender.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Root lesion = LMN: weakness, reflex ↓ or absent, dermatomal numbness; never brisk or Babinski</li>\n<li>L4 (L3–L4 disc): inversion, quadriceps, patellar reflex, medial leg and foot</li>\n<li>L5 (L4–L5 disc): great-toe extension, no reliable reflex, dorsum of foot</li>\n<li>S1 (L5–S1 disc): eversion, plantar flexion, Achilles reflex, lateral foot and sole</li>\n<li>Tibialis anterior L4 + L5; EHL L5 only → both abnormal on EMG = L5</li>\n<li>Quadriceps L3–L4; L3 skin stops at medial knee; L2 = hip flexion</li>\n<li>S2–S4 bladder, bowel; S2–S5 perianal bull's-eye</li>\n<li>TART: hypertonic muscles around disc, limited flexion, functional scoliosis, multifidus tenderness</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Disc herniation and nerve roots","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Bedside screens and nerve stretch tests","p":"<h4>Quick 60-second neuro screen</h4>\n<p>Walking on the heels or toes loads one root's muscles at a time, so weakness shows quickly. Heel walking with the foot turned in (inverted) needs the tibialis anterior, so it tests <b>L4</b>. Heel walking needs the great-toe and foot extensors, so it tests <b>L5</b>. Toe walking needs the calf, so it tests <b>S1</b>. A squat and rise tests the quadriceps (L4).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-191-2.png\" alt=\"Pain, numbness, weakness, screening walk and reflex for L4, L5, S1\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Pain, numbness, weakness, screening walk and reflex for L4, L5, S1 <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 191</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Straight leg raise (SLR, Lasègue test)</h4>\n<p>The SLR stretches the sciatic nerve and its roots (L4–S3). Raising the straight leg is hip flexion with the knee extended. A root already irritated by a disc hurts when it is pulled, usually between about 30° and 70° of hip flexion; the nerve is still slack below 30°. The second step separates root pain from tight hamstrings.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient lies supine, and the examiner raises the affected leg with the knee straight until pain starts.</li>\n<li>If pain starts before 80° of hip flexion, the examiner lowers the leg about 5° until the pain eases.</li>\n<li>The examiner then dorsiflexes the foot (the <b>Bragard test</b>, also called the dural sign). Dorsiflexion pulls the nerve tight again; plantar flexion slackens it.</li>\n<li>If pain shoots down the leg again, below the knee toward the foot, the test is <b>positive</b>: nerve root irritation, usually from a disc.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Pain only in the back of the thigh means tight hamstrings, not a root. No pain is a negative test.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-154-1.png\" alt=\"Straight leg raise: hamstring pain versus nerve root pain\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Straight leg raise: hamstring pain versus nerve root pain <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 154</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Well-leg (crossed) straight leg raise</h4>\n<p>Here the examiner raises the unaffected leg the same way, into hip flexion with the knee straight, which also tugs on the roots of the other side. Pain shooting down the <b>symptomatic</b> leg means a large herniation, often central.</p>\n<h4>Other tests and what they check</h4>\n<p>The Milgram test (holding both legs 2–3 inches off the table for 30 seconds) and the Valsalva maneuver (bearing down) raise the pressure inside the dural sac. Pain suggests a disc or tumor in the canal. The Kernig sign tests for meningeal irritation. The Thomas test looks for a psoas (hip flexor) contracture. ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) compression tests sacroiliac joint motion. The leg traction test checks the quadratus lumborum (QL). An iliac crest that drops when the patient stands on the other leg is a hip drop or Trendelenburg finding, not a nerve test. The Thompson test (Achilles tendon rupture) and the Hoover sign (effort during leg raising) are not lumbar root tests.</p>\n<h4>A nerve, not a root: meralgia paresthetica</h4>\n<p>The <b>lateral femoral cutaneous nerve</b> comes from L2–L3 and is purely sensory. It passes through the psoas and iliacus, then under the inguinal ligament near the ASIS, to the skin of the anterolateral thigh. Pressure there causes <b>meralgia paresthetica</b>: inguinal pain with burning, tingling or numbness of the anterolateral thigh, reaching from the buttock toward the knee. Causes are local trauma, scarring of the inguinal ligament, diabetes, obesity, pregnancy, tight clothing or belts and a wallet in the front pocket. There is no weakness and no reflex change. The sciatic nerve, by contrast, serves the back of the thigh and the leg.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O108-48.jpg\" alt=\"Meralgia paresthetica: anterolateral thigh area of a nerve under the inguinal ligament\"></div><figcaption>Meralgia paresthetica: anterolateral thigh area of a nerve under the inguinal ligament <small>· 2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Heels + foot inverted → L4; heels → L5; toes → S1</li>\n<li>SLR: straight leg up (hip flexion); root pain ~30–70°; pain before 80° → lower 5°, dorsiflex (Bragard)</li>\n<li>Pain again below knee → positive → root irritation (L4–S3)</li>\n<li>Back-of-thigh pain only → tight hamstrings; no pain → negative</li>\n<li>Well-leg SLR pain down symptomatic leg → large, often central, disc</li>\n<li>Milgram/Valsalva → canal pressure; Kernig → meninges; Thomas → psoas; leg traction → QL</li>\n<li>Meralgia: lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (L2–L3) under inguinal ligament near ASIS</li>\n<li>Tight clothes, pregnancy, obesity, diabetes → anterolateral thigh numbness; no weakness</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Disc herniation and nerve roots","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Tropism, transitional vertebrae, spina bifida","p":"<p>Some lumbar spines are built differently from birth. These congenital variants change how load is shared, and some lead to early wear.</p>\n<h4>Facet tropism</h4>\n<p>Normally the left and right facets at one level face the same way, close to the sagittal plane. In <b>facet tropism</b> the two facets at one level sit at different angles, 8° or more apart. The two sides then move and carry load unevenly. Tropism is the <b>most common lumbar anomaly</b>, found in about 30% of patients, and it favours early degeneration.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-89.jpg\" alt=\"Facet tropism: left and right facet angles differ\"></div><figcaption>Facet tropism: left and right facet angles differ <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 89</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Transitional vertebrae</h4>\n<p>The last lumbar and first sacral segments can trade roles. In <b>sacralization</b>, L5 is fused with the sacrum, partly or fully. Often an enlarged L5 transverse process (TP) forms a joint with the sacrum or fuses to it. In <b>lumbarization</b>, S1 stays independent of the rest of the sacrum and acts like a sixth lumbar vertebra. Either can happen on one side or both; this is the batwing deformity. Sacralization is more common: about 17%, against 2–7% for lumbarization.</p>\n<p>The palpatory findings follow from the fusion. A sacralized L5 is one less mobile segment. The L5–S1 region loses its spring, the end feel is hard and abrupt, and the L5 TP feels bony and stiff. The disc above the fused level takes extra load and wears early. Lumbarization adds an extra mobile segment, but the altered lumbosacral junction also leads to early disc degeneration. So neither variant protects against degenerative joint disease (DJD). Two lumbar vertebrae fused together, such as L4 and L5, form a block vertebra. S1–S4 fused together is simply the normal sacrum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O108-25.jpg\" alt=\"Sacralization of L5: one-sided joint and complete fusion\"></div><figcaption>Sacralization of L5: one-sided joint and complete fusion <small>· 2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Spina bifida</h4>\n<p>In spina bifida the laminae fail to close in the midline, usually in the lumbar spine. The types differ by what comes through the gap. In <b>spina bifida occulta</b> (about 10% of people) nothing comes through. Often the only sign is a tuft of hair, the <b>faun tail nevus</b>, which may hide a tethered cord. In a <b>meningocele</b> only the meninges push out, making a sac on the back with no neural tissue. In a <b>meningomyelocele</b> the meninges <b>and neural tissue</b> (cord or roots) push out, so the child has neurologic deficits.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-13.jpg\" alt=\"Faun tail nevus and sacral spina bifida in infants\"></div><figcaption>Faun tail nevus and sacral spina bifida in infants <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Not congenital variants</h4>\n<p>Facet hypertrophy is degenerative. A disc herniation pushes into the canal and never forms a mass on the back. Spondylitis is inflammatory, with morning stiffness that eases with exercise (5.1). Piriformis syndrome causes buttock pain with no bony change.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Facet tropism: left/right facet angles differ ≥8°; most common lumbar anomaly (~30%)</li>\n<li>Sacralization: L5 fused with sacrum; ~17%; more common than lumbarization (2–7%)</li>\n<li>Lumbarization: S1 independent of sacrum, acts as a 6th lumbar</li>\n<li>Unilateral or bilateral (batwing deformity)</li>\n<li>Sacralized L5: loss of spring, hard end feel, bony stiff L5 TP; disc above wears</li>\n<li>Neither variant protects against DJD; L4–L5 fused = block vertebra</li>\n<li>Occulta: nothing herniates; hair tuft (faun tail nevus)</li>\n<li>Meningocele = meninges only; meningomyelocele = meninges + neural tissue → deficits</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lumbar variants","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Lumbar osteoarthritis","p":"<p>Osteoarthritis (OA), also called degenerative arthritis or disc degeneration, is age-related wear of the discs and facet joints. In the lumbar spine it starts in the most loaded discs, <b>L4–L5 and L5–S1</b>. A former name is degenerative hypertrophic spondylitis.</p>\n<h4>The chain of events</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>An aging disc dries out, compresses more under load, and its annulus tears.</li>\n<li>Disc height falls, so the neighbouring vertebrae and their spinous processes sit <b>closer together</b>.</li>\n<li>More weight shifts onto the facets, and the facet joints degenerate.</li>\n<li>Bone answers the stress with spurs (<b>osteophytes</b>) on the vertebral bodies and facets.</li>\n<li>Spurs, thick facets and bulging discs can narrow the canal, so OA often brings the symptoms of spinal stenosis (4.2).</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-60-1.png\" alt=\"Normal, diseased and aged disc under load\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Normal, diseased and aged disc under load <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What you find on exam</h4>\n<p>The patient feels stiff, slow and sore. On palpation the segment shows <b>loss of spring</b>: pressing on it feels hard, with rigid, abrupt end points. <b>Active and passive range of motion (ROM) are restricted in all planes</b>, because bone and lost disc height block every direction. The spinous processes feel close together.</p>\n<p>Somatic dysfunction (SD) behaves differently. A Type II (non-neutral) SD is restricted in only some directions, and its asymmetry changes with flexion or extension. One blocked direction with a normal springy end feel is a functional problem, not bony OA.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-151-1.png\" alt=\"Degenerative arthritis: thin discs and spurs on a lateral x-ray\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Degenerative arthritis: thin discs and spurs on a lateral x-ray <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 151</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>X-ray</h4>\n<p>The lateral film shows narrowed disc spaces, mostly at L4–L5 and L5–S1, often thinnest at the back of the disc. It also shows osteophytes and endplate sclerosis. Disc spaces never widen with degeneration. The imaging name for this picture is spondylosis (5.1).</p>\n<h4>Clues to OA elsewhere, and look-alikes</h4>\n<p>OA in the hands supports the diagnosis: Heberden nodes at the finger-tip joints, Bouchard nodes at the middle finger joints, and morning stiffness that lasts under 30 minutes. Other diseases look different. Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) causes inflammatory erosions and long morning stiffness, and it spares the lumbar spine. Felty syndrome is RA with a big spleen and low white cells. Fibromyalgia gives widespread tender points with a normal x-ray. Pott disease is spinal tuberculosis (TB): it destroys the vertebral body and disc, and the collapse makes a sharp hump (gibbus). Crystal arthritis (gout, pseudogout) affects hot, swollen peripheral joints. Spondylolysis is a pars fracture in a young athlete (5.1).</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>OA = disc + facet wear; starts at L4–L5 and L5–S1</li>\n<li>Disc height ↓ → spinous processes closer → facet load ↑ → osteophytes</li>\n<li>Spurs + thick facets → narrowed canal → stenosis symptoms</li>\n<li>Exam: loss of spring, rigid abrupt end points, active + passive ROM ↓ in all planes</li>\n<li>Type II SD: some directions only, changes with flexion/extension; OA does not</li>\n<li>X-ray: narrow discs, osteophytes, endplate sclerosis; discs never widen</li>\n<li>Heberden/Bouchard nodes + stiffness &lt;30 min → OA</li>\n<li>RA spares lumbar spine; Pott (TB) → gibbus; fibromyalgia → normal x-ray</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Degeneration and stenosis","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Spinal stenosis: why posture matters","p":"<p><b>Spinal stenosis</b> is narrowing of the spinal canal (central stenosis) or of an intervertebral foramen (foraminal stenosis). It comes with the aging spine, usually after 60, and is mostly degenerative. The canal holds all the roots of the cauda equina, each with its dural sheath and its own small blood supply. When the canal narrows, the roots are both compressed and starved of blood (<b>ischemic</b>). The result is chronic back pain with radicular (root) symptoms and intermittent <b>neurogenic claudication</b>.</p>\n<h4>What narrows the canal</h4>\n<p>Causes include hypertrophy and buckling of the posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL), thickening of the ligamentum flavum, osteoarthritis (OA) of the zygapophyseal (facet) joints with spurs, osteophytes, ruptured discs and tumors. Lost disc height, spondylolisthesis and spondylosis add to it. Soft tissue such as the ligamentum flavum shows on MRI (magnetic resonance imaging); x-ray shows the bony changes.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O099-67-1.png\" alt=\"Normal versus stenotic vertebral foramen\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Normal versus stenotic vertebral foramen <small>· Fall 2020 TA review (SGA drive), slide 67</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why posture matters</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Standing and walking hold the lumbar spine in <b>extension</b>.</li>\n<li>Extension buckles the ligamentum flavum inward and closes the facets, so the canal and foramina narrow further.</li>\n<li>The roots are squeezed and their blood flow drops. Pain spreads to the back, buttocks and thighs, often in both legs, and the legs feel heavy and weak. This is neurogenic claudication.</li>\n<li><b>Flexion</b> opens the canal. Sitting, bending forward, leaning on a shopping cart while walking, or lying with the knees to the chest relieves the pain within about 20–30 minutes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A disc behaves the opposite way and worsens with flexion (2.1). Stenosis squeezes roots, so reflexes fall rather than rise. Brisk reflexes and an extensor plantar response (Babinski) mean an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion. Fasciculations (muscle twitches) mean anterior horn disease such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). Symmetric weakness around both hips suggests a muscle disease. Sciatica, pain along the sciatic nerve, is a symptom, not a diagnosis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-160-1.png\" alt=\"Stenosis: aging canal, ischemic roots, neurogenic claudication\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Stenosis: aging canal, ischemic roots, neurogenic claudication <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 160</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Neurogenic versus vascular claudication</h4>\n<p>Both kinds of claudication bring leg pain with walking, calf pain included, and both ease with sitting. They differ in what the pain depends on. Neurogenic claudication depends on spinal posture: it needs flexion to settle, and it hurts whenever the spine is held in extension, even at rest. Vascular claudication (peripheral artery disease) depends on muscle work, so standing still in any posture relieves it. The ankle-brachial index (ABI) compares ankle and arm blood pressure; it is low in artery disease and normal in stenosis.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Neurogenic (stenosis)</th><th>Vascular (artery disease)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Squeezed, ischemic roots</td><td>Leg muscles short of arterial blood</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Relief standing still</td><td>No: needs flexion</td><td>Yes: rest in any posture</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spine held in extension at rest, as in posterior lumbar counterstrain (6.2)</td><td>Painful</td><td>Not painful</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leg weakness, numbness</td><td>Common</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pulses, ankle-brachial index (ABI)</td><td>Normal</td><td>Weak pulses, low ABI</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Confirming test</td><td>MRI</td><td>Arterial Doppler ultrasound</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-95.jpg\" alt=\"Normal and stenotic canal; osteophytes and narrowed disc on MRI\"></div><figcaption>Normal and stenotic canal; osteophytes and narrowed disc on MRI <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 95</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Stenosis = narrowed canal or foramen; usually &gt;60, degenerative</li>\n<li>Causes: PLL hypertrophy, thick ligamentum flavum, facet OA spurs, osteophytes, discs, tumors</li>\n<li>Roots compressed + ischemic → neurogenic claudication (back, buttock, thigh; heavy legs)</li>\n<li>Extension (standing, walking) narrows canal → worse</li>\n<li>Flexion (sitting, shopping cart, knees to chest) → relief in 20–30 min</li>\n<li>Disc = worse flexion; stenosis = worse extension</li>\n<li>Root compression → reflexes ↓; brisk + Babinski = UMN; fasciculations = ALS</li>\n<li>Vascular claudication: relief standing still, weak pulses, low ABI; stenosis: normal ABI, MRI confirms</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Degeneration and stenosis","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Testing, imaging and exercise in stenosis","p":"<h4>Kemp test</h4>\n<p>The Kemp test reproduces stenosis pain by narrowing the canal on purpose. The patient extends the lumbar spine and adds rotation and sidebending toward the painful side, which closes the zygapophyseal (facet) joints and foramen on that side. The position is held for 30 seconds. The test is positive when pain spreads from the back to <b>below the buttocks</b> and into the thigh, sometimes with heavy legs. That is neurogenic claudication, and it supports stenosis.</p>\n<p>Other tests answer other questions. The straight leg raise looks for a disc. The hip drop test screens lumbar sidebending. The Ober test looks for a tight iliotibial band. Pain shooting down the opposite leg belongs to the crossed straight leg raise (2.4). Rebound tenderness signals peritoneal irritation, and pain on breathing in points to a rib or the pleura.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-163-1.png\" alt=\"Kemp test, aggravating and relieving factors in stenosis\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Kemp test, aggravating and relieving factors in stenosis <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 163</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Neurologic exam and imaging</h4>\n<p>Check whether numbness follows one dermatome or covers both feet like a stocking; a stocking pattern with a lost Achilles reflex can also come from diabetic neuropathy. At rest the reflexes and sensation may be normal. The patient may need to walk until the claudication appears; only then do the reflex or sensory changes show.</p>\n<p>The x-ray shows <b>osteophytes and decreased disc space</b>, the degeneration behind most stenosis. This degenerative picture is <b>spondylosis</b>, and it often narrows the foramina too. A CT (computed tomography) scan or MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) confirms the narrowed canal or foramen. In an elderly patient spondylosis is far more likely than a slip, and without red flags a tumor or infection is unlikely. Other findings point elsewhere, as the table shows.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Finding</th><th>Means</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Lytic, punched-out lesions</td><td>Metastases: cancer history, night pain, weight loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blurred endplate, lifted periosteum</td><td>Vertebral osteomyelitis: fever, IV (intravenous) drug use</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pars fracture, no slip</td><td>Spondylolysis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Forward slip, usually L5 on S1 or L4 on L5</td><td>Spondylolisthesis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Herniated disc on MRI</td><td>One-sided dermatomal pain, worse with flexion and sitting</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Facet tropism, lumbarization</td><td>Congenital variants, not stenosis (3.1)</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O100-50-1.png\" alt=\"Degenerative disc disease: osteophytes and narrowed discs\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Degenerative disc disease: osteophytes and narrowed discs <small>· Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 50</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Treatment: open the canal</h4>\n<p>Treatment is usually conservative, and OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) helps control pain. The aim is to lengthen the spine by decreasing the lordosis and starting a lumbar kyphosis, which unkinks the cauda equina. The patient does exercises that tilt the pelvis and flatten the lordosis. One is the <b>supine pelvic tilt</b>. Another is <b>active resistive flexion</b>: the hips and knees are pulled to the chest while the patient pushes the knees into the hands. OMT increases range of motion (ROM), relaxes muscle tone, mobilizes into flexion, and keeps motion and blood supply. A corset can help. NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) and an epidural steroid injection come next. When the walking distance and standing time without pain keep shrinking, surgery (laminectomy) is justified.</p>\n<p>Prone extension exercises (press-ups) treat the opposite problem, a lost lordosis, and they narrow the canal. Bed rest beyond 2 days weakens the patient.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-131.jpg\" alt=\"Lumbar exercises: pelvic tilt, flexion, extension-inducing\"></div><figcaption>Lumbar exercises: pelvic tilt, flexion, extension-inducing <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 131</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Kemp: extension + rotation/sidebending to painful side, 30 s → pain below buttocks</li>\n<li>Check dermatomal vs stocking numbness; walk until claudication, then retest reflexes</li>\n<li>X-ray: osteophytes + ↓ disc space (spondylosis); confirm with CT or MRI</li>\n<li>Lytic lesions → metastasis; blurred endplate → osteomyelitis; slip → spondylolisthesis</li>\n<li>Goal: ↓ lordosis, start kyphosis → unkink cauda equina</li>\n<li>Pelvic tilt + active resistive flexion (knees to chest); OMT for ROM and blood supply</li>\n<li>Corset, NSAIDs, epidural steroid; shrinking walking distance → surgery</li>\n<li>No press-ups (extension); no bed rest &gt;2 days</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Degeneration and stenosis","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Four look-alike words","p":"<p>Spondyl- means vertebra. The ending tells you what happened to it: <b>-lysis</b> means a break, <b>-listhesis</b> means a slip, <b>-osis</b> means a degenerative condition, and <b>-itis</b> means inflammation.</p>\n<h4>The pars interarticularis</h4>\n<p>The pars interarticularis is the narrow bridge of bone in the vertebral arch between the superior and inferior articular processes. It joins the facets of one vertebra to those of the vertebra below. Repeated extension, as in gymnastics, loads it hard.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O100-48-1.png\" alt=\"Normal pars, spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Normal pars, spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis <small>· Fall 2016 TA review (SGA drive), slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Spondylolysis: the break</h4>\n<p><b>Spondylolysis</b> is a fracture of the pars interarticularis <b>without</b> forward displacement of the vertebral body. It is a stress fracture, thought to build from micro-fractures over time. It is the most common cause of low back pain (LBP) in adolescent athletes. It occurs at L5 most often, then L4, and is bilateral in 80%. The back muscles spasm to protect the fracture, so the patient has low back pain, stiffness, tight hamstrings and sometimes leg pain. It has three causes: inherited (a malformed, thin or weak pars), degenerative (osteoarthritis and disc degeneration) and traumatic (one large injury or many small ones).</p>\n<h4>Spondylolisthesis: the slip</h4>\n<p>As the stress fracture worsens, a gap opens at the pars and the vertebra loses its alignment. The slope of the lumbosacral angle, gravity and muscle pull then push the body forward down this slippery slope. <b>Spondylolisthesis</b> is this <b>forward slip</b> of a vertebral body on the one below, most often L5 on S1. A backward slip of the upper vertebra is a retrolisthesis (retrospondylolisthesis). Most patients are between 20 and 50, and many who stay physically active have no symptoms.</p>\n<p>The two main types differ in cause, age and level, as the table shows.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Isthmic</th><th>Degenerative</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Bilateral pars defects (spondylolysis)</td><td>Facet OA, disc degeneration, thick ligamentum flavum</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usual age</td><td>Under 50</td><td>Over 50</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usual level</td><td>L5–S1</td><td>L4–L5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Note</td><td>The broken arch stays behind (5.3)</td><td>More often compresses the cauda equina or exiting roots</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Spondylosis and spondylitis</h4>\n<p><b>Spondylosis</b> is the general term for age-related degeneration of the discs and vertebrae: osteoarthritis (OA) and degenerative disc disease. The disc degenerates, and the neighbouring vertebral bodies stiffen and bridge (ankylosis). Patients have constant pain and stiffness in the low back and buttocks. <b>Spondylitis</b>, also called spondyloarthritis, is inflammation of the spine and joints. Examples are ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic spondylitis, enteropathic arthritis and reactive arthritis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-98.jpg\" alt=\"Spondylosis (degeneration) versus spondylitis (inflammation)\"></div><figcaption>Spondylosis (degeneration) versus spondylitis (inflammation) <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 98</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Two other terms are easy to mix up with these. Spinal stenosis is the narrowed canal that a slip or spondylosis can cause. Spina bifida is failed closure of the laminae, with meninges coming through in a meningocele (3.1).</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>-lysis = break; -listhesis = slip; -osis = degeneration; -itis = inflammation</li>\n<li>Pars interarticularis: bony bridge between superior and inferior articular processes</li>\n<li>Spondylolysis: pars stress fracture, no slip; L5 &gt; L4; 80% bilateral</li>\n<li>#1 LBP cause in adolescent athletes; spasm, tight hamstrings; inherited, degenerative or traumatic</li>\n<li>Spondylolisthesis: forward slip, usually L5 on S1; backward = retrolisthesis</li>\n<li>Ages 20–50; active patients often asymptomatic</li>\n<li>Isthmic: pars defects, &lt;50, L5–S1; degenerative: &gt;50, L4–L5, compresses roots</li>\n<li>Spondylosis: disc degeneration + ankylosis; spondylitis: inflammatory (ankylosing spondylitis)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spondylo- family","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Reading the x-ray: the Scottie dog","p":"<h4>Which view shows what</h4>\n<p>A lumbar series starts with an anteroposterior (AP, front-to-back) view and a lateral view. The AP view shows the spinous processes, the paired pedicles, the transverse processes and the laminae. The <b>lateral view</b> shows disc height, osteophytes, and any forward slip, graded by how far the body has moved. A spondylolisthesis is seen best on the lateral x-ray or MRI (magnetic resonance imaging). The <b>oblique view</b> lines the posterior elements up into the shape of a <b>Scottie dog</b>. Order it when spondylolysis is suspected, because a pars fracture without a slip does not show on the lateral view. Flexion and extension views test stability, and MRI is for cauda equina symptoms or stenosis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-91-1.png\" alt=\"Pars defect on lateral and oblique x-rays\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Pars defect on lateral and oblique x-rays <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 91</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Each part of the dog is a part of the vertebra. The neck is the one to remember, because it is the pars.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Part of the dog</th><th>Part of the vertebra</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nose</td><td>Transverse process</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eye</td><td>Pedicle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ear</td><td>Superior articular process</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Front leg</td><td>Inferior articular process</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Neck</b></td><td><b>Pars interarticularis</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hind leg</td><td>Spinous process</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O079-112.jpg\" alt=\"The Scottie dog outlined; arrows point to the pars\"></div><figcaption>The Scottie dog outlined; arrows point to the pars <small>· 2026 week 14 PRE-IA 12-13-14 Functional &amp; Clinical Lumbar Spine, slide 112</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The signs</h4>\n<p>In spondylolysis the pars fracture is a dark (lucent) line across the dog's neck on the oblique view, a <b>collar on the Scottie dog</b>. The lateral view shows no slip. In spondylolisthesis the lateral view shows the body moved forward. On the oblique view the neck is not just collared but pulled apart, a <b>Scottie dog neck separation</b> (a decapitated dog). The isthmic type shows a pars lucency plus the slip.</p>\n<p>In spondylosis the film shows disc space narrowing, endplate sclerosis, osteophytes at the front edges (anterior lipping) and sometimes bony bridges. Ankylosing spondylitis gives a bamboo spine and fused sacroiliac joints. Osteomyelitis with discitis shows eroded endplates and an enhancing disc on contrast MRI. Somatic dysfunction, such as L5 flexed, rotated and sidebent, is a motion finding and does not show on x-ray.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-166-1.png\" alt=\"Lateral film: L5 sitting forward on the sacrum\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Lateral film: L5 sitting forward on the sacrum <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 166</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>AP + lateral first; lateral (or MRI) shows disc height, spurs and slip</li>\n<li>Oblique view = Scottie dog; order it for suspected spondylolysis</li>\n<li>Nose = transverse process; eye = pedicle; ear = superior articular process</li>\n<li>Front leg = inferior articular process; neck = pars; hind leg = spinous process</li>\n<li>Collar on the neck = spondylolysis; neck separation = spondylolisthesis</li>\n<li>Spondylosis: narrow disc, sclerosis, anterior lipping; ankylosing spondylitis: bamboo spine</li>\n<li>Somatic dysfunction is invisible on x-ray</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spondylo- family","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Spondylolisthesis: exam and care","p":"<h4>Who gets it</h4>\n<p>About 5% of people have a spondylolisthesis, and half have no symptoms. A large <b>Ferguson (lumbosacral) angle</b> means more lordosis and more forward shear at L5–S1, which favours a slip. The normal angle is 25–35°.</p>\n<h4>Symptoms and signs</h4>\n<p>The patient has an ache in the low back, buttock or back of the thigh, and may have root symptoms. It worsens with <b>extension</b>, as in standing and walking, and eases with sitting or bending forward. Many feel a slipping sensation when they straighten up from sitting or bending. An irritated root makes the leg tingle, but there is usually no deficit. Look for more lordosis (marked in children), flattened gluteal creases and a stiff-legged, waddling gait. The most common dysfunction is <b>spasm of the hamstrings</b> on both sides.</p>\n<p>On palpation the area feels boggy, with a <b>palpable shelf (step-off)</b>. One spinous process sits forward of its neighbour and is hard to feel. The shelf <b>stays when the patient flexes</b>, so it is structural. An extension somatic dysfunction, by contrast, changes with flexion.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-166-2.png\" alt=\"L5 slipped forward on the sacrum\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>L5 slipped forward on the sacrum <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 166</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where the shelf is felt</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>In an isthmic L5–S1 slip, the pars of L5 is broken on both sides.</li>\n<li>The L5 body slides forward on S1 and carries L4 and everything above it.</li>\n<li>The S1 facets hold back the L5 posterior arch (lamina and spinous process), and the sacrum does not move.</li>\n<li>The shelf is therefore felt where a spinous process that moved forward meets one that stayed behind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When a whole vertebra slips with its arch intact, as in the degenerative type, its own spinous process moves forward with it, so the shelf is felt at that vertebra's spinous process. A degenerative slip, usually L4 on L5 after 50, also narrows the canal: spinal stenosis with weakness in both legs. On a sagittal MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) the line along the backs of the vertebral bodies steps forward at the slipped level.</p>\n<h4>Other palpation findings</h4>\n<p>Spinous processes close together with loss of spring at every level mean osteoarthritis (4.1). Tenderness from the L4–L5 transverse processes (TPs) to the iliac crest is the iliolumbar ligament, often the first ligament to become tender when the lumbosacral junction decompensates. A tender point just medial to the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) is anterior L1 or the iliopsoas (6.2). Piriformis syndrome causes buttock pain after sitting with a normal x-ray.</p>\n<h4>Management</h4>\n<p>Most patients, 85–90%, do well with conservative care. OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) treats the somatic dysfunctions <b>above</b> the slip and the sacral dysfunctions. <b>HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust) is relatively contraindicated</b>, because the slipped level is unstable. The lordosis is reduced with <b>pelvic-tilt</b> exercise, weight loss and no high heels. Extension exercise adds shear at the slip. Emergency decompression is for cauda equina syndrome.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O080-9.jpg\" alt=\"Signs, treatment, and slip grades I–IV (each 25%)\"></div><figcaption>Signs, treatment, and slip grades I–IV (each 25%) <small>· 2026 week 14 Lumbar Spine Review Package with Board Questions, page 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>~5% of people, half asymptomatic; ↑ Ferguson angle → ↑ L5–S1 shear</li>\n<li>Ferguson normal 25–35°</li>\n<li>Pain worse with extension (standing, walking); eased by sitting, bending forward</li>\n<li>Slipping sensation on rising; ↑ lordosis, flat gluteal creases, bilateral hamstring spasm</li>\n<li>Boggy palpable shelf (step-off) that persists in flexion</li>\n<li>Isthmic L5–S1: shelf between L4 and L5 (L5 arch stays back)</li>\n<li>Degenerative slip: whole vertebra moves (L4 on L5 → shelf at L4)</li>\n<li>L4–L5 TPs to iliac crest tender → iliolumbar ligament</li>\n<li>85–90% conservative: OMT above slip + sacrum, pelvic tilt; HVLA relatively contraindicated</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spondylo- family","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Lumbar muscle energy","p":"<p>Muscle energy (ME) is a direct technique: it works into the restriction. The physician takes the segment to its restrictive barrier. The patient pushes gently away from the barrier against equal resistance for 3–5 seconds, then relaxes. The physician takes up the slack to the new barrier. This repeats 3–5 times, followed by a retest.</p>\n<h4>Naming lumbar somatic dysfunction</h4>\n<p>A somatic dysfunction (SD) is named for the directions it moves freely. Rotation is named for the side of the <b>posterior transverse process</b> (TP). A <b>Type I</b> (neutral) SD is a group curve, sidebent and rotated to opposite sides, such as L1–L5 NSRRL (neutral, sidebent right, rotated left). The concavity is on the sidebending side, and the posterior TPs are on the convex side. Long muscles such as the erector spinae and quadratus lumborum hold these curves. A <b>Type II</b> (non-neutral) SD is a single segment, flexed or extended, rotated and sidebent to the same side: FRS (flexed, rotated, sidebent) or ERS (extended, rotated, sidebent). Short, deep segmental muscles such as the rotatores brevis hold a Type II SD.</p>\n<p>The injury predicts the dysfunction. Bending forward and to the right to lift something can leave a segment stuck in flexion, right rotation and right sidebending (FRS right). It is then restricted in extension, left rotation and left sidebending. A psoas spasm does the same at L1 or L2: it pulls the segment into flexion, rotation and sidebending toward its own side, with groin pain, a bent-forward posture and a positive Thomas test on that side. An extended segment (ERS) cannot flex, so its asymmetry, sidebending included, is most obvious in flexion and fades in extension.</p>\n<h4>Type I group curve set-up</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient lies on the side with the <b>concavity toward the table</b>, so the posterior TPs face <b>up</b>.</li>\n<li>The physician monitors the apex with the hand nearer the head.</li>\n<li>The physician flexes the hips and knees (about 90°) until motion reaches the apex.</li>\n<li>The physician lifts the ankles toward the ceiling, which sidebends the lumbar spine the other way, into the barrier.</li>\n<li>The patient pushes the feet toward the floor for 3–5 seconds against resistance and relaxes, and the physician lifts further. This repeats at least 3 times and ends with a passive stretch.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O096-110-1.png\" alt=\"Type I muscle energy: concavity down, ankles lifted toward the ceiling\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Type I muscle energy: concavity down, ankles lifted toward the ceiling <small>· Fall 2023 TA review (SGA drive), slide 110</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Type II: FDR and SUE</h4>\n<p>Two mnemonics give the Type II set-ups, one letter per choice. <b>FDR</b> means a <b>F</b>lexed SD, posterior TP <b>D</b>own, lateral <b>R</b>ecumbent (plain side-lying). <b>SUE</b> means <b>S</b>ims position (side-lying with the chest rolled toward the table), posterior TP <b>U</b>p, for an <b>E</b>xtended SD. In each case the spine is taken into the motion it has lost: extension for a flexed SD, flexion for an extended one. The table compares them.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th><b>FDR</b></th><th><b>SUE</b></th></tr>\n<tr><td>For</td><td>Flexed SD (FRS): extension restricted</td><td>Extended SD (ERS): flexion restricted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Position</td><td>Lateral recumbent</td><td>Sims</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Posterior TP</td><td>Down: lies on the rotation side</td><td>Up: lies on the side opposite the rotation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spine taken into</td><td>Extension</td><td>Flexion; the Sims roll adds rotation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legs</td><td>Lifted toward the ceiling</td><td>Dropped off the table toward the floor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Patient pushes</td><td>Down</td><td>Up</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-174-1.png\" alt=\"Sims position, then legs off the table, for an extended dysfunction\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Sims position, then legs off the table, for an extended dysfunction <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 174</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The patient's job is a gentle push away from the barrier. In SUE the legs hang toward the floor, so the patient pushes them up toward the ceiling; in FDR the legs are lifted, so the patient pushes them down. A set-up can be read in three steps. Sims or plain side-lying tells flexed or extended. The side facing up or down tells where the posterior TP is. Sidebending matches rotation. HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust) and counterstrain (a 90-second hold on a tender point) are different techniques. Lumbar ME is relatively contraindicated in an undiagnosed lumbar radiculopathy.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>ME: direct; barrier → patient pushes 3–5 s → relax → new barrier; 3–5 times</li>\n<li>Named for freedom; rotation = side of posterior TP</li>\n<li>Type I: group, sidebending and rotation opposite; held by long muscles</li>\n<li>Type II: one segment, flexed or extended, same-side rotation + sidebending; held by rotatores</li>\n<li>Bend forward-right injury or right psoas spasm → L1/L2 FRS right → FDR lying on the right</li>\n<li>ERS asymmetry worst in flexion</li>\n<li>Type I: concavity down, posterior TPs up, lift ankles, patient pushes down</li>\n<li>FDR: flexed, posterior TP down, lateral recumbent, legs up, push down</li>\n<li>SUE: Sims, posterior TP up, extended, legs down, push up</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Treating the lumbar spine","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Lumbar counterstrain and contraindications","p":"<p>Counterstrain (Jones technique) is indirect and passive: the body moves away from the restriction and the patient does no work. First the physician finds a <b>tender point</b>, a small, dime-sized tender spot in muscle, tendon, ligament or fascia. Then the physician folds the body over the point into a position of ease, where tenderness drops by at least 70%. The position is held for <b>90 seconds</b>. The patient is returned slowly and passively to neutral, and the point is retested. Anterior points are mostly treated in <b>flexion</b> and posterior points in <b>extension</b>, because folding over a point shortens the tissue that holds it.</p>\n<h4>Anterior L1</h4>\n<p>The anterior L1 tender point lies just <b>medial to the ASIS</b> (anterior superior iliac spine). The patient lies supine. The physician flexes the hips and knees markedly, resting the legs on the physician's thigh, then sidebends the legs toward the tender point and rotates them away from it. Other anterior lumbar points sit around the AIIS (anterior inferior iliac spine): anterior L2 just medial to it and anterior L4 just below it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-43-1.png\" alt=\"Anterior lumbar tender points; anterior L1 medial to the ASIS\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Anterior lumbar tender points; anterior L1 medial to the ASIS <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Posterior L1–L5</h4>\n<p>The posterior tender points lie on the spinous processes or transverse processes of L1–L5. For a spinous process point the patient lies <b>prone</b>, and the physician raises <b>both legs</b> passively into pure extension, resting them on the physician's thigh. A midline point needs a straight, midline position. For a transverse process (TP) point, such as one level with the iliac crest, the patient also lies prone. The physician stands on the side opposite the point and draws the leg on the point's side into extension, adduction and external rotation, which rotates the pelvis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O097-43-2.png\" alt=\"Posterior lumbar tender points on spinous and transverse processes\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Posterior lumbar tender points on spinous and transverse processes <small>· Fall 2022 TA review (SGA drive), slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-185-1.png\" alt=\"Spinous process point: both legs extended on the physician&#x27;s thigh\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>Spinous process point: both legs extended on the physician&#x27;s thigh <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 185</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Contraindications</h4>\n<p>A <b>relative</b> contraindication means use caution or change the technique; an <b>absolute</b> one means do not use it. Counterstrain is gentle, so its limits come from the patient's ability to relax and from the positions themselves. Relative contraindications are a patient who cannot relax, children who cannot stay passive, lower-limb counterstrain with a deep vein thrombosis (DVT), and any position that brings on neurologic symptoms. Absolute ones are a <b>fracture in the region of the tender point</b>, a ligament tear the position could worsen, and vertebral artery disease.</p>\n<p>Two more relative contraindications apply to the lumbar points. Hip dislocation and severe hip osteoarthritis (OA) matter because the posterior positions move the hips, and an acute lumbar strain and sprain matters for the anterior points.</p>\n<p>Hypermobility belongs to a different technique. It is a relative contraindication to HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust), along with patient apprehension. HVLA is absolutely contraindicated with fracture, bone metastases, ligament disruption and severe osteoporosis. Lumbar muscle energy is relatively contraindicated in an undiagnosed radiculopathy and absolutely with a local fracture.</p>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>Counterstrain: indirect, passive; tender point → position of ease (≥70% less tender)</li>\n<li>Hold 90 s; return slowly and passively; retest</li>\n<li>Anterior points → flexion; posterior points → extension</li>\n<li>Anterior L1: medial to ASIS; supine, hips flexed, sidebend toward, rotate away</li>\n<li>Spinous point: prone, both legs extended; TP point: stand opposite, same-side hip extended, adducted, externally rotated</li>\n<li>Absolute: fracture at tender point, ligament tear, vertebral artery disease</li>\n<li>Relative: can't relax, DVT (lower limb), hip dislocation, severe hip OA, acute strain</li>\n<li>Hypermobility → relative contraindication to HVLA, not counterstrain</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Treating the lumbar spine","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"OMM-W14","h":"Which lumbar problem is it?","p":"<p>Most back pain is mechanical, and a careful history plus a neurologic exam usually sorts it out before any imaging.</p>\n<h4>Taking the history</h4>\n<p>The history covers the pain's location, quality and intensity, the mechanism of injury, what worsens and relieves it, trauma, family members with similar problems, and pending litigation. It separates pain over a spinous process from pain in the paravertebral (beside-the-spine) tissue, a dull throbbing ache from sharp pain, and pain shooting down the leg (a root) from a vague spread into the thigh or buttock. Lifestyle and occupation often give the diagnosis away.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-145-1.png\" alt=\"History: location, mechanism, aggravating and relieving factors\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>History: location, mechanism, aggravating and relieving factors <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 145</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Risk factors, prevention and emotion</h4>\n<p>Mechanical low back pain follows bending, twisting, poor lifting mechanics, trauma, prolonged repetitive work, and prolonged standing or sitting. Poor fitness, prior episodes, smoking, a family history of disc degeneration, whole-body vibration (a jackhammer) and heavy manual labor also predispose. Psychosocial strain (depression, job dissatisfaction, workers' compensation or litigation) predicts chronic disability more than workload does, and back belts did not prevent back pain. Prevention means a healthy weight, lifting with bent knees and a straight back, managing stress, staying fit, and omega-3 foods such as wild salmon. In Sarno's mind-body view, pent-up emotion such as unexpressed anger cuts oxygen to tissues and causes pain; OMT (osteopathic manipulative treatment) can release it.</p>\n<h4>When an organ is the source</h4>\n<p>Somatic dysfunction can have a non-mechanical origin. Sympathetic fibers leave the cord as low as L2. A diseased organ irritates the cord level that supplies it, and that level shows tissue changes in the back: a <b>viscerosomatic reflex</b>. T1–T4 serve the head and neck, T1–T6 the heart and lungs, and T5–T9 the upper abdominal organs. <b>T10–T11</b> serve the <b>kidneys</b>, upper ureters, gonads, small intestine and right colon. <b>T12–L2</b> serve the left colon, rectum, bladder, uterus and ovaries, prostate and lower extremity, so think of these levels in chronic back pain. Below L2 there is no sympathetic outflow. A kidney stone gives flank and low back pain, a warm, boggy area at T10–T12 and psoas spasm. Too much sympathetic tone slows the gut (ileus, constipation, distension). Too much parasympathetic tone through the sacral pelvic splanchnic nerves speeds it (colitis, Crohn disease, diarrhea); irritable bowel syndrome can involve both.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/O108-17.jpg\" alt=\"Sympathetic levels for the head, heart, gut, pelvic organs and legs\"></div><figcaption>Sympathetic levels for the head, heart, gut, pelvic organs and legs <small>· 2026 week 14 Lecture 14 Clinical Lumbar Spine, page 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Acute lumbar strain and sprain</h4>\n<p>A <b>strain</b> injures muscle and a <b>sprain</b> injures ligament, such as the iliolumbar ligament, often after lifting, twisting or sport. The pain is an ache with spasm, localized to the low back, sometimes the buttock or back of the thigh, never in a dermatomal line to the foot. It worsens with activity or long standing or sitting. There are no paresthesias, no weakness, normal reflexes and normal x-rays. Paraspinal spasm often holds a Type I group curve. Treatment is OMT: counterstrain, muscle energy or HVLA (high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust).</p>\n<h4>The multifidus triangle</h4>\n<p>The multifidus triangle runs from the L4 spinous process to both iliac crests and down to the tip of the sacrum. Most low back problems arise inside it. It holds the multifidi, the L4–L5 and L5–S1 discs, the intertransverse and iliolumbar ligaments, the facets and the sacroiliac (SI) joints.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/ta-O095-42-1.png\" alt=\"The multifidus triangle and its contents\"></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">TA review</span>The multifidus triangle and its contents <small>· 2025 TA review (SGA drive), slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Side by side</h4>\n<p>The table lines up the common problems. Age, the posture that worsens the pain and the neurologic exam separate most of them.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Problem</th><th>Typical patient</th><th>Worse or better</th><th>Neurologic exam</th><th>Main test or image</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Lumbar strain</td><td>Any age, after activity</td><td>Worse with activity</td><td>Normal</td><td>Normal x-ray</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disc herniation</td><td>Young and middle-aged adults</td><td>Worse flexion, sitting, coughing</td><td>One root: weakness, reflex loss, numbness</td><td>Straight leg raise with Bragard; MRI</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spinal stenosis</td><td>Over 60</td><td>Worse standing, walking; better flexion</td><td>Heavy legs on walking</td><td>Kemp; MRI</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Osteoarthritis</td><td>Middle-aged and elderly</td><td>Stiff in every direction</td><td>Usually normal</td><td>Loss of spring; narrow discs, spurs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spondylolysis</td><td>Young athlete</td><td>Worse extension</td><td>Usually normal</td><td>Oblique x-ray: collar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spondylolisthesis</td><td>Isthmic under 50, degenerative over 50</td><td>Worse extension</td><td>Usually normal</td><td>Step-off; slip on lateral x-ray</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cauda equina</td><td>Large central disc, tumor</td><td>Progressive</td><td>Many roots, both sides; saddle numbness</td><td>Emergency MRI</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"psum\"><h4>Short summary</h4><ul>\n<li>History: location, quality, mechanism, aggravating/relieving, trauma, family, litigation, occupation</li>\n<li>Risks: bending, twisting, poor lifting, repetition, prolonged sitting/standing, smoking, vibration</li>\n<li>Psychosocial factors predict chronic disability; Sarno: pent-up emotion → pain</li>\n<li>Viscerosomatic: T10–T11 kidneys, upper ureters, gonads; T12–L2 left colon, bladder, pelvic organs</li>\n<li>↑ Sympathetic → ileus, constipation; ↑ pelvic splanchnic → diarrhea, colitis</li>\n<li>Acute strain: local ache + spasm, no deficit, normal x-ray → OMT</li>\n<li>Multifidus triangle: L4 spinous, iliac crests, sacral tip; discs, facets, iliolumbar, SI</li>\n<li>Root deficit + flexion pain → disc; extension pain, &gt;60 → stenosis; step-off → slip</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Putting it together","sn":7,"pn":"7.1"}]};